ic', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neha Thirani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thirani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b681087-b4e8-56ee-89f7-991c6a88295b', 'word_count': 914, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b681087-b4e8-56ee-89f7-991c6a88295b'}, {'abstract': 'Also, The Daily lays off a third of its staff and Charles McGrath on the life and death of Gore Vidal.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/01/the-breakfast-meeting-special-olympic-anger-edition/', 'snippet': 'Also, The Daily lays off a third of its staff and Charles McGrath on the life and death of Gore Vidal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NBC and Twitter have relented and rescinded the order to suspend the account of Guy Adams, the journalist for The Independent who criticized the network’s Olympic coverage and broadcast the e-mail address of NBC’s Olympic chief. (“Did I miss much while I was away?” he immediately tweeted.) Twitter also admitted that an executive there had alerted NBC, Twitter’s business partner in games coverage. Both companies experienced a backlash on – where else? – Twitter. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Special Olympic Anger Edition', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lazarus, Mark', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McGrath, Charles', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, Rupert', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vidal, Gore', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Comcast Corporation', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Daily, The (News Corp)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NBC Sports', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'News Corporation', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-01T13:06:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d4b5818-b200-523b-a9da-c68c9132dc41', 'word_count': 329, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d4b5818-b200-523b-a9da-c68c9132dc41'}, {'abstract': 'To understand the difference between the two nations look at their respective reactions to the problems surrounding the 2010 Commonwealth Games, hosted by India, and this summer’s Olympic Games in London.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/01/indian-insecurity-vs-british-confidence-the-games-edition/', 'snippet': 'To understand the difference between the two nations look at their respective reactions to the problems surrounding the 2010 Commonwealth Games, hosted by India, and this summer’s Olympic Games in London.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Against the backdrop of problems at the Summer Olympics in London and at the 2010 Commonwealth Games in Delhi, and the distinct ways Britain and India reacted to their national embarrassments, my latest column is an attempt to explain why Indians magnify all their flaws. But what also interests me is the difference between national pride and the collective confidence of a country, even though they may appear to be the same emotion.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Indian Insecurity vs. British Confidence: The Games Edition', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'conten