'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-01T17:18:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Weisman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weisman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8821a99d-25d1-51df-8d0f-9bdf0734314a', 'word_count': 447, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8821a99d-25d1-51df-8d0f-9bdf0734314a'}, {'abstract': 'The uprising in Libya last year, especially the decades-old weapons let loose in the chaos, tell a cold-war tale of allies and arms deals.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/01/american-white-phosphorous-shells-in-libya-a-challenge-to-a-pentagon-chestnut/', 'snippet': 'The uprising in Libya last year, especially the decades-old weapons let loose in the chaos, tell a cold-war tale of allies and arms deals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The history of how Libya became a bunker state reduces readily to short-hand. After overthrowing King Idris in 1969, Muammar el-Qaddafi, a young army officer, used his nation’s oil revenue to underwrite a decades’ long arms-purchasing spree, stockpiling weapons from the West but principally from Russia, China and the Eastern bloc. As his holdings grew, he used his arsenal to supply his expanding army and internal security units, as well as a source of items to export to guerrilla or terrorist groups in Chad, the Palestinian territories, Ireland and beyond. This was one of the many ways that Libya became a menace and regional scourge. Libya’s growing arms stockpile was also a means by which the colonel’s hold on power remained secure, at least until the uprising that toppled him and cost thousands of lives, including his own.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'American White Phosphorus Shells in Libya: A Challenge to a Pentagon Chestnut', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-01T17:22:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By C. J. Chivers and John Ismay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'C.', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Chivers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ismay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f070b1d5-549c-59cd-b5d0-7109cccc3fa8', 'word_count': 3300, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f070b1d5-549c-59cd-b5d0-7109cccc3fa8'}, {'abstract': 'A spam attack on Dropbox users has been linked to a password stolen in a breach on another Web site. Dropbox declined to say which company’s breach was responsible. But the attack illustrates a common tactic used by hackers.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/01/dropbox-spam-attack-tied-to-stolen-employee-password/', 'snippet': 'A spam attack on Dropbox users has been linked to a password stolen in a breach on another Web site. Dropbox declined to say which company’s breach was responsible. But the attack illustrates a common tactic used by hackers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A spam attack on users of Dropbox, the cloud-storage service, has been linked to a password that was stolen in a breach on another Web site.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dropbox Spam Attack Tied to Stolen Employee Password', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberattacks and Hackers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Data Storage', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dropbox Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-01T17:23:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicole Perlroth', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicole', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perlroth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8b551e5-c2b8-506f-83e1-9abbedaec25a', 'word_count': 539, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8b551e5-c2b8-506f-83e1-9abbedaec25a'}, {'abstract': 'A little bit on the campaign, a lot about the Olympics.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/01/political-gymnastics/', 'snippet': 'A little bit on the campaign, a lot about the Olympics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gail Collins: David, I feel compelled to discuss Mitt Romney in Europe, but before we get to that, let’s try a happier topic. How are you feeling about the Olympics?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Political Gymnastics', 'kicker': 'Opinionator', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gymnastics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lochte, Ryan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Phelps, Michael', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Raisman, Aly', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strug, Kerri', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-01T17:37:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks and Gail Collins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Gail', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a8a3db4-a4c1-5cea-b280-7aa80c560325', 'word_count': 1297, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a8a3db4-a4c1-5cea-b280-7aa80c560325'}, {'abstract': 'Pennsylvania, whose 20 electoral votes have long been among the most closely watched states in the race for the White House, is now rated as leaning Democratic, according to the latest New York Times ranking of presidential battlegrounds.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/01/the-electoral-map-pennsylvania-now-leaning-democratic/', 'snippet': 'Pennsylvania, whose 20 electoral votes have long been among the most closely watched states in the race for the White House, is now rated as leaning Democratic, according to the latest New York Times ranking of presidential battlegrounds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pennsylvania, whose 20 electoral votes have long been among the most closely watched in the race for the White House, is now rated as Leaning Democratic, according to the latest New York Times ranking of presidential battlegrounds.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Electoral Map: Pennsylvania Now Leaning Democratic', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pennsylvania', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-01T17:45:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Zeleny', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zeleny', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/57a393fd-3c9b-578e-9de8-4860fc8d1970', 'word_count': 174, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57a393fd-3c9b-578e-9de8-4860fc8d1970'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama attacked Mitt Romney’s proposal to cut taxes for individuals and businesses by more than $5 trillion over the next decade.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/01/obama-in-ohio-attacks-romney-over-tax-cuts/', 'snippet': 'President Obama attacked Mitt Romney’s proposal to cut taxes for individuals and businesses by more than $5 trillion over the next decade.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MANSFIELD, Ohio – As polls in this battleground state and nationally continue to show voters split over President Obama’s stewardship of the economy, Mr. Obama on Wednesday attempted to contrast his vision for the nation’s future with that of his Republican rival Mitt Romney.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama, in Ohio, Attacks Romney Over Tax Cuts', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-01T18:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jackie Calmes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jackie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Calmes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f887b14a-92bf-574e-8149-8a69e0ceaca7', 'word_count': 1002, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f887b14a-92bf-574e-8149-8a69e0ceaca7'}, {'abstract': 'There’s a fine line between gliding to 51 percent and coming up just short.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/01/the-complacent-romney-campaign/', 'snippet': 'There’s a fine line between gliding to 51 percent and coming up just short.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My Campaign Stops column this week runs through Mitt Romney’s possible vice presidential picks, and concludes that everything we’ve seen from the Romney campaign suggests they’ll make the most cautious, first-do-no-harm, don’t-rock-the-boat choice possible\xa0— which probably means picking the competent, experienced, and completely unexciting Ohio Senator Rob Portman. For further evidence that Team Romney is happy with the course they’re on, here’s National Review’s Robert Costa on the plan to reintroduce the Republican candidate during the run-up to convention week:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Complacent Romney Campaign', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-01T18:01:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d51a074-b5c3-589b-aff9-382ab33c2730', 'word_count': 588, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d51a074-b5c3-589b-aff9-382ab33c2730'}, {'abstract': '“Electric Cord” was sent out for cleaning in 1970 and never returned.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/01/lichtenstein-painting-missing-for-42-years-surfaces-in-warehouse/', 'snippet': '“Electric Cord” was sent out for cleaning in 1970 and never returned.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Pop artist Roy Lichtenstein’s black and white “Electric Cord” painting, which disappeared 42 years ago, has turned up in a New York City warehouse, The Associated Press reported.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lichtenstein Painting, Missing for 42 Years, Surfaces in Warehouse', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Castelli, Leo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lichtenstein, Roy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-01T18:02:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0470275-0993-559f-b126-b7da86790966', 'word_count': 261, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0470275-0993-559f-b126-b7da86790966'}, {'abstract': 'In his new book, Mr. Macintyre illuminates the World War II espionage that helped the Allies at Normandy.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/01/the-spy-game-ben-macintyre-talks-about-double-cross/', 'snippet': 'In his new book, Mr. Macintyre illuminates the World War II espionage that helped the Allies at Normandy.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Double Cross” is Ben Macintyre’s third book about World War II espionage, following the acclaimed “Agent Zigzag” and “Operation Mincemeat.” When MI5, Britain’s domestic intelligence service, realized that it had turned all of Germany’s spies into double agents, it started\xa0 the Double Cross operation. The German spies fed an ornate series of lies to Berlin, helping the Allies win at Normandy by drawing the attention of the Nazis to other possible points of attack. In a recent interview via e-mail, Mr. Macintyre discussed whether he would have made a good spy, Churchill’s knowledge of Double Cross, the possibility that German intelligence officers may have been sympathetic to the plan and more. Below are edited excerpts from the conversation:', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Spy Game: Ben Macintyre Talks About ‘Double Cross’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A History Sleuth, Yes, but Please Don’t Ask Him to Do Any Spying', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage and Intelligence Services', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Macintyre, Ben', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Security Service (MI5)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-01T18:12:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c62821d2-1313-5132-a348-37c373fbb926', 'word_count': 1414, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c62821d2-1313-5132-a348-37c373fbb926'}, {'abstract': 'Ethnobotanists help Micronesian communities preserve medicinal plant knowledge and resources for future generations.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/01/ancestral-remedies-to-the-rescue/', 'snippet': 'Ethnobotanists help Micronesian communities preserve medicinal plant knowledge and resources for future generations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ethnobotanists are fond of the joke that pills don’t grow on trees. The punch line, of course, is that they actually do. Countless pharmaceutical products originate from trees or other plant species, many of which are probably still waiting to be discovered. This week around 1,300 natural products researchers and practitioners gathered in New York to discuss the latest findings in their field, including the ways in which nature, culture and conservation overlap to provide health benefits to millions and how those links can best be preserved.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ancestral Remedies to the Rescue', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Alternative and Complementary Medicine', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biodiversity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flowers and Plants', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Balick, Michael', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Botanical Garden', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Micronesia', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Palau', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-01T18:15:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Nuwer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nuwer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2030a408-9420-5674-befa-1ffc76527b11', 'word_count': 877, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2030a408-9420-5674-befa-1ffc76527b11'}, {'abstract': 'An analysis from the Brookings Institution says Romney’s tax plans would most likely cut taxes for the wealthy and raise them for the p