nctions that make life a little more comfortable at home.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2012/08/02/garden/20120802-SHOPPING.html', 'snippet': 'Shawn Henderson, an interior designer in Manhattan, goes shopping for console tables, which can serve functions that make life a little more comfortable at home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Shawn Henderson, an interior designer in Manhattan, goes shopping for console tables, which can serve functions that make life a little more comfortable at home.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shopping for Console Tables', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tables', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-01T22:52:43+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Home & Garden', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/10a94a8e-14c5-59ad-91b1-6d58be6e1881', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/10a94a8e-14c5-59ad-91b1-6d58be6e1881'}, {'abstract': 'Is well-meaning but overprotective environmental education scolding children away from developing their own love for the land?', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/01/please-dont-touch-the-nature/', 'snippet': 'Is well-meaning but overprotective environmental education scolding children away from developing their own love for the land?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our environment, we’re told by climate scientists, is fragile. But are children learning that their natural environment — the trees, dirt and grass that surround us — is “fragile,” too? Several educators, after observing years of children’s being taught to “look, but don’t touch,” have argued this summer that many programs and policies designed to protect the natural world are actually preventing a new generation from developing an interest in it. Don’t climb the trees, don’t dig holes, take nothing but pictures, leave nothing but footprints — do we keep nature and children too far apart in the interest of protecting (or overprotecting) them both?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Please, Don’t Touch the Nature', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-01T22:53:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/edf1a015-bfe5-5ff3-962e-a35efbeb2c61', 'word_count': 524, 'uri': 'nyt://article/edf1a015-bfe5-5ff3-962e-a35efbeb2c61'}, {'abstract': 'These tables frequently take a back seat to other furniture, but Shawn Henderson sees them as unsung heroes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/02/garden/shopping-for-console-tables-with-shawn-henderson.html', 'snippet': 'These tables frequently take a back seat to other furniture, but Shawn Henderson sees them as unsung heroes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CONSOLE tables frequently take a back seat to other furniture, hidden behind the living room sofa or tucked into an inconspicuous corner of the entry hall.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/201