ek with Critical Thinking questions. Can you identify the stylistic device this author uses?', 'lead_paragraph': 'As we announced on Monday, we’re experimenting with a new kind of Test Yourself question this week. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | Critical Thinking, Aug. 2, 2012', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-02T08:47:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51d76312-77ad-5172-a332-8bf940a4bb73', 'word_count': 111, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51d76312-77ad-5172-a332-8bf940a4bb73'}, {'abstract': 'Megabanks are defending themselves by perpetuating myths and are trying to dismiss their increasing number of critics as populists, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/02/under-pressure-megabanks-rely-on-three-myths/', 'snippet': 'Megabanks are defending themselves by perpetuating myths and are trying to dismiss their increasing number of critics as populists, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Simon Johnson is the Ronald A. Kurtz Professor of Entrepreneurship at the M.I.T. Sloan School of Management and co-author of “White House Burning: The Founding Fathers, Our National Debt, and Why It Matters to You.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Under Pressure, Biggest Banks Rely on 3 Myths', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}