 'Minder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b6ced839-a91a-529f-a078-220750957a76', 'word_count': 489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b6ced839-a91a-529f-a078-220750957a76'}, {'abstract': 'Coal-fired power contributes the most to Facebook’s energy mix, but renewable energy sources are close behind.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/02/on-our-radar-facebooks-carbon-footprint/', 'snippet': 'Coal-fired power contributes the most to Facebook’s energy mix, but renewable energy sources are close behind.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a move toward greater transparency, Facebook discloses its carbon footprint. Coal-fired power accounts for the biggest share of the energy consumed by its data centers — 27 percent — followed by renewable energy sources, natural gas and nuclear power. [The Guardian]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Our Radar: Facebook’s Carbon Footprint', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-02T14:02:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b43080ba-82b9-5e07-a0b8-76a515f3677b', 'word_count': 318, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b43080ba-82b9-5e07-a0b8-76a515f3677b'}, {'abstract': 'In its first Olympic Games after throwing off Soviet shackles, Lithuania fielded its own team of stars — financed with the help of the Grateful Dead — and capped a magical run with a victory over the Russian team to capture the bronze medal.', 'web_url': 'https://london2012.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/02/lithuania-the-other-dream-team-at-the-92-games/', 'snippet': 'In its first Olympic Games after throwing off Soviet shackles, Lithuania fielded its own team of stars — financed with the help of the Grateful Dead — and capped a magical run with a victory over the Russian team to capture the bronze medal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Between Jack McCallum’s stellar retrospective (the aptly titled “Dream Team,” released last month), a 90-minute NBA TV documentary and myriad articles raising pointed cross-decades comparisons, memories of the Dream Team’s dominance, personality and global influence continue to ring true two decades on.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lithuania, the ‘Other Dream Team’ at the ’92 Games', 'kicker': '2012 London Olympics', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sabonis, Arvydas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Basketball Assn', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NBA TV', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lithuania', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-02T14:07:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Cavan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cavan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/30967235-6848-5396-9a5f-a95e032eb15a', 'word_count': 840, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30967235-6848-5396-9a5f-a95e032eb15a'}, {'abstract': 'The conductor Pablo Heras-Casado, 34, has only recently begun engaging with the most popular classical music, having concentrated before on both early and contemporary compositions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/05/arts/music/pablo-heras-casado-with-orchestra-of-st-lukes.html', 'snippet': 'The conductor Pablo Heras-Casado, 34, has only recently begun engaging with the most popular classical music, having concentrated before on both early and contemporary compositions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN studying music, few young conductors begin at the beginning. The Gustavo Dudamels of the world cut their teeth on a 200-year orchestral canon, starting with Mozart and ending with Shostakovich. Rarely does a conductor with an eye on a mainstream career tackle music written before Bach’s time, works typically reserved for specialists and scholars.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05PABLO1_SPAN/05PABLO1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05PABLO1_SPAN/05PABLO1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05PABLO1_SPAN/05PABLO1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05PABLO1_SPAN/05PABLO1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05PABLO1_SPAN/05PABLO1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05PABLO1_SPAN/05PABLO1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05PABLO1_SPAN/05PABLO1_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 100, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Renaissance Man, and Many Eras Besides', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Renaissance Man, And Many Eras Besides', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Heras-Casado, Pablo', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Berlin Philharmonic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Orchestra of St Luke's", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-02T14:11:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Robin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/64297f9e-ad5d-54b3-8fc0-ebdedd0dcb27', 'word_count': 1639, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64297f9e-ad5d-54b3-8fc0-ebdedd0dcb27'}, {'abstract': 'Overall sales rose 4.3 percent in July, according to a Thomson Reuters survey, although stores that cater to teenagers reported a drop.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/03/business/economy/retailers-beat-expectations-helped-by-midpriced-stores.html', 'snippet': 'Overall sales rose 4.3 percent in July, according to a Thomson Reuters survey, although stores that cater to teenagers reported a drop.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In July, higher-than-expected sales at low- and midpriced stores helped push sales past analysts’ estimates.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Retailers Beat Expectations, Helped by Midpriced Stores', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Retailers Beat Forecasts Over All, But Teenagers Cut Their Shopping', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Behavior', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-02T14:11:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephanie Clifford', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clifford', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4bd58cc-870b-5c8b-8f54-bd5d96f4265c', 'word_count': 572, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4bd58cc-870b-5c8b-8f54-bd5d96f4265c'}, {'abstract': 'At the Mostly Mozart Festival audiences for concerts featuring works inspired by bird calls can join bird-watching tours in Central Park to hear the real thing first.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/05/arts/music/at-mostly-mozart-a-menagerie-of-bird-related-works.html', 'snippet': 'At the Mostly Mozart Festival audiences for concerts featuring works inspired by bird calls can join bird-watching tours in Central Park to hear the real thing first.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN the busier areas of Central Park, dodging hordes of tourists and would-be Tour de France racers can take precedent over wildlife spotting. But Olivier Messiaen, a French composer who disliked cities and said, “Nature never displays anything in bad taste,” would surely have approved as two New York City Audubon recently led a small group of music lovers on a fascinating bird-watching tour through the southern section of the park.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05BIRD_SPAN/05BIRD_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05BIRD_SPAN/05BIRD_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05BIRD_SPAN/05BIRD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 626, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05BIRD_SPAN/05BIRD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1253, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05BIRD_SPAN/05BIRD_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05BIRD_SPAN/05BIRD_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05BIRD_SPAN/05BIRD_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 92, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mockingbirds of the Concert Hall', 'kicker': 'Into the Music', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mockingbirds of the Concert Hall', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lincoln Center for the Performing Arts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mostly Mozart Festival', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Messiaen, Olivier', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Contemporary Ensemble', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-02T14:13:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivien Schweitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6aed2f58-e6aa-5e54-96ca-37b1fedf2c46', 'word_count': 995, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6aed2f58-e6aa-5e54-96ca-37b1fedf2c46'}, {'abstract': 'The new physics prize from Yuri Milner, the Russian tech investment magnate, throws a new light on how we might correct some of the perverse priorities of capitalism.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/02/what-other-professions-deserve-3-million-prizes/', 'snippet': 'The new physics prize from Yuri Milner, the Russian tech investment magnate, throws a new light on how we might correct some of the perverse priorities of capitalism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The monster $3 million prize for theoretical physics Russian billionaire Yuri Milner created this week raises a lot of intriguing questions. One of them is the structure of incentives in modern capitalism.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Other Professions Deserve $3 Million Prizes?', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Physics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Milner, Yuri', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-02T14:21:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chrystia Freeland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chrystia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Freeland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/933f8ec5-f7b8-5742-8538-3af61a9f6165', 'word_count': 243, 'uri': 'nyt://article/933f8ec5-f7b8-5742-8538-3af61a9f6165'}, {'abstract': 'A selection of events.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/05/arts/aug-5-11.html', 'snippet': 'A selection of events.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DETAILS have been scant about “Heartless,” the new play by SAM SHEPARD that begins an Off Broadway run this week, but for Shepard fans it hardly matters. It’s new, and it’s by the man who won a Pulitzer Prize in 1979 for “Buried Child.” In that play he dealt with submerged secrets, and in “Heartless” dark secrets are at issue too, but Mr. Shepard is not simply playing in an old sandbox here. This writer known for many a testosterone-fueled battle (see “True West,” “The Tooth of Crime” and others) has — perhaps surprisingly — written a play predominantly populated by female characters.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05SUBGUIDE3_SPAN/05SUBGUIDE3_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05SUBGUIDE3_SPAN/05SUBGUIDE3_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05SUBGUIDE3_SPAN/05SUBGUIDE3_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, '