ank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberwarfare', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCain, John', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Collins, Susan M', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-02T16:30:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Washington', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael S. Schmidt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Schmidt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a1be851-4919-525a-8394-cb153efdada3', 'word_count': 655, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a1be851-4919-525a-8394-cb153efdada3'}, {'abstract': 'In this week’s links: A confused opening to the London Games; how to write joke algorithms; and an inquiry to the nature of guns.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/02/stone-links-boyles-olympic-incoherence/', 'snippet': 'In this week’s links: A confused opening to the London Games; how to write joke algorithms; and an inquiry to the nature of guns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At The Paris Review, Rafil Kroll-Zaidi parses the opening ceremony of the London Games and finds it to be a semiotic mess — a jumble of signifiers never exceeding haphazard pastiche. We should have expected no less, he argues, as its director, Danny Boyle, has always shown himself, as a filmmaker, to be more of an “executive technician” than an artist of coherent vision. The erraticism of the opening ceremony — indeed, its mild insanity — follows from the striking inconsistency (in many senses) of his oeuvre: Boyle’s films range from the high of “Trainspotting” to the (very) low of “Sunshine,” with the treacly, manipulative “Slumdog Millionaire” falling somewhere in between.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stone Links: Boyle’s Olympic Incoherence', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-02T16:35:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark de Silva', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'de Silva', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab9de4c4-6a12-55d0-bf2e-32fd7baf6a0a', 'word_count': 452, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab9de4c4-6a12-55d0-bf2e-32fd7baf6a0a'}, {'abstract': 'The recall is of 778,000 RAV4 and Lexus HS 250h models sold in the United States is to fix a problem in the rear suspension that Toyota says arises when the vehicles are serviced.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/02/toyota-recalls-rav4-and-lexus-hs-250h-to-fix-rear-suspension-issue/', 'snippet': 'The recall is of 778,000 RAV4 and Lexus HS 250h models sold in the United States is to fix a problem in the rear suspension that Toyota says arises when the vehicles are serviced.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Toyota is recalling a total of 778,000 RAV4 and Lexus HS 250h models sold in the United States to fix a problem in the rear suspension that it says arises when the vehicles are serviced.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Toyota Recalls RAV4 and Lexus HS 250h to Fix Rear Suspension Issue', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, '