zations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Francisco (Calif)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-02T19:28:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emmarie Huetteman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emmarie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Huetteman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/94c27450-e1c6-51b6-adba-28b12dd41c14', 'word_count': 259, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94c27450-e1c6-51b6-adba-28b12dd41c14'}, {'abstract': 'Republicans — but very likely not Mitt Romney — have a rare opportunity for success in Hawaii this year.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/02/in-blue-hawaii-senate-race-offers-a-bit-o-suspense/', 'snippet': 'Republicans — but very likely not Mitt Romney — have a rare opportunity for success in Hawaii this year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We continue our Presidential Geography series, a one-by-one examination of the peculiarities that drive the politics in all 50 states and the District of Columbia. Here is Hawaii, the Aloha State. FiveThirtyEight spoke with Neil Milner, a former political science professor at the University of Hawaii, and Andrew Winer, a Democratic consultant and adviser to the Senate campaign of Representative Mazie Hirono, a Democrat representing Hawaii’s Second District. Mr. Winer was also the campaign manager for Senator Daniel K. Akaka, of Hawaii, in 2006.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Blue Hawaii, Senate Race Offers a Bit of Suspense', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-02T19:34:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Micah Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Micah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9bd737d-63a7-5de9-88e6-be9b1da2b2c9', 'word_count': 1331, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9bd737d-63a7-5de9-88e6-be9b1da2b2c9'}, {'abstract': 'A conversation with Adam Sharp, Twitter’s Man in Washington, on a new way to measure public opinion.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/02/video-a-new-election-index-from-twitter/', 'snippet': 'A conversation with Adam Sharp, Twitter’s Man in Washington, on a new way to measure public opinion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A conversation with Adam Sharp, Twitter’s Man in Washington, on a new way to measure public opinion.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Video: A New Election Index from Twitter', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-02T19:36:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f8f5cbb-bb82-582b-9656-204fb8d06b5c', 'word_count': 24, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f8f5cbb-bb82-582b-9656-204fb8d06b5c'}, {'abstract': '“Joan Miró,” with 160 or so works from 1917 to 1974, is a beautiful and exciting show at the National Gallery of Art.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/03/arts/design/joan-miro-the-ladder-of-escape-at-national-gallery.html', 'snippet': '“Joan Miró,” with 160 or so works from 1917 to 1974, is a beautiful and exciting show at the National Gallery of Art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Was Joan Miró a political artist? A much-beloved Surrealist, he is not commonly thought of as such. On its face, his oeuvre appears remarkably apolitical, especially considering that he lived through two world wars and a murderous civil war in his homeland, Spain. From the hallucinogenic vision of “The Farm” in the 1920s to his mural-scale fields of color punctuated by wispy signs in the 1960s, evidence of worldly political engagement is hard to find. A reluctant joiner and manifesto signer, Miró (1893-1983) disliked Social Realism. The artists of the past who inspired him were mystic visionaries like Hieronymus Bosch and William Blake.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/03/arts/03MIRO1/03MIRO1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 896, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/03/arts/03MIRO1/03MIRO1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1791, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/03/arts/03MIRO1/03MIRO1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/03/arts/03MIRO1/03MIRO1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/03/arts/03MIRO1/03MIRO1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Filtering Miró’s Work Through a Political Sieve', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Filtering Miró’s Work Through a Political Sieve', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miro, Joan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Gallery of Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-02T19:39:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5d8f262-c7f3-515c-bc68-b7413463fe6a', 'word_count': 1022, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5d8f262-c7f3-515c-bc68-b7413463fe6a'}, {'abstract': 'Prepaid phone plans, where you pay the full price for a cellphone and then pay cheaper monthly rates without a contract, seem to offer what most budget-conscious people want. So why haven’t they really caught on?', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/02/prepaid-phone-plans/', 'snippet': 'Prepaid phone plans, where you pay the full price for a cellphone and then pay cheaper monthly rates without a contract, seem to offer what most budget-conscious people want. So why haven’t they really caught on?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Daniel Acker/Bloomberg NewsPrepaid mobile phone products on display at a Family Dollar Stores location.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Prepaid Cellphones Are Cheaper. Why Aren’t They Popular?', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cellular Telephones', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wirel