ampaign announced it wanted the credit to die.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/02/senate-panel-passes-extension-of-wind-energy-tax-credit/', 'snippet': 'Senate Republicans rebuffed their presidential nominee and won Senate Finance Committee passage of a one-year extension of the tax credit for wind energy, just four days after Mitt Romney’s campaign announced it wanted the credit to die.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Senate Republicans rebuffed their presumptive presidential nominee as the Senate Finance Committee passed a one-year extension of the tax credit for wind energy, just four days after Mitt Romney’s campaign announced that it wanted the credit to die.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Senate Panel Passes Extension of Wind Energy Tax Credit', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Credits, Deductions and Exemptions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wind Power', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grassley, Charles E', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hatch, Orrin G', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thune, John R', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-02T19:50:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Weisman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weisman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c6443ad3-67cb-57b5-9c08-f40aeccbab16', 'word_count': 380, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c6443ad3-67cb-57b5-9c08-f40aeccbab16'}, {'abstract': 'Behind the scenes of the artist’s 6,700 square foot installation at the Hirshhorn Museum.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/02/strong-not-so-silent-type-barbara-krugers-beliefdoubt/', 'snippet': 'Behind the scenes of the artist’s 6,700 square foot installation at the Hirshhorn Museum.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In pretty much any museum, real estate is at a premium — every inch of exhibition space counts. The Hirshhorn Museum has been getting particularly creative of late, successfully activating spaces where no art was ever intended to hang. This spring, Doug Aitken’s “Song1” transformed the exterior of the Gordon Bunshaft-designed building into a giant, wraparound movie screen. Next up: “Belief+Doubt,” a site-specific installation by the artist Barbara Kruger. Even as it’s tucked into the museum’s lower lobby, the piece, which will be unveiled on Aug. 20 and remain up through 2014, will be impossible to miss: 6,700 square feet of text-printed vinyl in Kruger’s signature palette of red, white and black will occupy the walls, the escalators, even the floors of this highly trafficked public space, confronting visitors with questions (“Who Is Free to Choose? Who Prays Loudest?”) and declarations (“Plenty Should Be Enough”), forcing them to reexamine their faith in the democratic process — or their need to purchase a souvenir in the museum’s newly renovated bookstore.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Strong, Not So Silent Type | Barbara Kruger’s ‘Belief+Doubt’', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-02T20:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alix Browne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alix', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Browne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/da9260f8-dac6-560e-bbd2-9bd67d99c79c', 'word_count': 169, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da9260f8-dac6-560e-bbd2-9bd67d99c79c'}, {'abstract': 'A Robert Adams trove and a Whistler land at the National Gallery, while the Brooklyn Museum plans an El Anatsui show.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/03/arts/design/robert-adams-chooses-photos-for-national-gallery.html', 'snippet': 'A Robert Adams trove and a Whistler land at the National Gallery, while the Brooklyn Museum plans an El Anatsui show.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Robert Adams, the 75-year-old photographer who has devoted more than four decades to capturing the lush beauty and stark desolation of the American West, has been working with curators at the National Gallery of Art in Washington, selecting 169 prints from his own holdings for the museum to acquire. His choices not only chart his entire career, they also complement the 25 images by him that the gallery already owns.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/03/arts/03VOGEL/VOGEL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1020, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/03/arts/03VOGEL/VOGEL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1506, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/03/arts/03VOGEL/03VOGEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/03/arts/03VOGEL/03VOGEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'th