an gymnast Ksenia Afanasyeva fell on her face during her floor exercise in the women’s team all-around Tuesday, NBC edited her out of its prime-time broadcast, as if dispatched to a video gulag.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Was NBC When the Russian Gymnast Fell?', 'kicker': '2012 London Olympics', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gymnastics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Douglas, Gabby', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lazarus, Mark', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trautwig, Al', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wieber, Jordyn', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-02T21:25:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Sandomir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandomir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/65d03906-a948-5983-9f42-99d6fcdf1ee7', 'word_count': 519, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65d03906-a948-5983-9f42-99d6fcdf1ee7'}, {'abstract': '“Celeste and Jesse Forever” explores a relationship between a man and a woman that’s no longer predicated by mutual desire.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/03/movies/celeste-and-jesse-forever-stars-rashida-jones.html', 'snippet': '“Celeste and Jesse Forever” explores a relationship between a man and a woman that’s no longer predicated by mutual desire.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A wistful romantic comedy about love, marriage and never wanting to say you’re sorry, “Celeste and Jesse Forever” doesn’t try to rock the world — just gently nudge it. Set in Los Angeles, it begins as a mildly, self-consciously quirky story about a young married couple, played by an appealingly matched Rashida Jones and Andy Samberg, who have split up without moving on or out. Best friends, Celeste and Jesse share the same adolescent humor, fondness for stupid voices and even property lines: a hard-driving trend forecaster, she lives in their house while he’s a laid-back artist who now flops in the backyard studio. You see what keeps them together, even when it shouldn’t. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/03/arts/03CELESTE_SPAN/03CELESTE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/03/arts/03CELESTE_SPAN/03CELESTE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/03/arts/03CELESTE_SPAN/03CELESTE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 574, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/03/arts/03CELESTE_SPAN/03CELESTE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 841, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/03/arts/03CELESTE_SPAN/03CELESTE_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/03/arts/03CELESTE_SPAN/03CELESTE_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/03/arts/03CELESTE_SPAN/03CELESTE_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Splitting but Not Leaving', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Splitting but Not Leaving', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Celeste and Jesse Forever (Movie)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Samberg, Andy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jones, Rashida', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Krieger, Lee Toland', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-02T21:32:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8728cbe1-86ad-5d79-802e-b18b6a47addb', 'word_count': 787, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8728cbe1-86ad-5d79-802e-b18b6a47addb'}, {'abstract': 'We use computer projections to bring you the likely results if New York City rather than London had been awarded the 2012 Olympic Games.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/03/arts/imagining-the-2012-olympic-games-in-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'We use computer projections to bring you the likely results if New York City rather than London had been awarded the 2012 Olympic Games.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hi, everybody. Bob Costas here at the International Broadcast Center, welcoming you to our sixth night of Olympic coverage on NYCNBC. To those who have been watching our exhaustive, turgid coverage of the London Games on NBC, MSNBC, CNBC, Bravo and any other unclaimed bandwidth we could find, thank you for joining us. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/03/arts/03GAMES1/03GAMES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 693, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/03/arts/03GAMES1/03GAMES-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1385, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Now for Some Real Hurdles', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Now for Some Real Hurdles', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-02T21:35:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/36fc4d85-25a1-585a-b4db-ee1a4197da16', 'word_count': 580, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36fc4d85-25a1-585a-b4db-ee1a4197da16'}, {'abstract': '“He pays less. You pay more” is the theme of a new Obama campaign ad, which seeks to trumpet the results of a new study of Mitt Romney’s tax plan.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/02/new-obama-ad-takes-shot-at-romney-tax-plan/', 'snippet': '“He pays less. You pay more” is the theme of a new Obama campaign ad, which seeks to trumpet the results of a new study of Mitt Romney’s tax plan.', 'lead_paragraph': '5:40 p.m. | Updated The Obama campaign is wasting no time getting an ad on the air touting a new study showing that Mitt Romney’s plans would mean additional large tax cuts for millionaires at the expense of other Americans. The spot, titled “Stretch” takes a double-barreled approach, beginning by reminding viewers of Mr. Romney’s own tax history, before pivoting to an analysis from the nonpartisan Tax Policy Center of the Brookings Institution and the Urban Institute, two centrist Washington-based policy research organizations. The ad will air in eight battleground states.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ad Watch: Obama Campaign Seeks to Recast Romney as Tax Raiser', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brookings Institution', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-02T21:39:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f441004d-d5a0-5b69-bd0c-a7799e0e94a4', 'word_count': 538, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f441004d-d5a0-5b69-bd0c-a7799e0e94a4'}, {'abstract': 'A selected, critical guide to comedy shows scheduled for around town.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/03/arts/comedy-listings-for-aug-3-9.html', 'snippet': 'A selected, critical guide to comedy shows scheduled for around town.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Comedy', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Comedy Listings for Aug. 3-9', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Listings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-02T21:40:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/c01b3bad-b231-58b9-bf33-81f5c4db858a', 'word_count': 522, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c01b3bad-b231-58b9-bf33-81f5c4db858a'}, {'abstract': 'A Hamptons house becomes a summer gallery for the group show “Creature From the Blue Lagoon.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/03/arts/design/creature-from-the-blue-lagoon-in-bridgehampton.html', 'snippet': 'A Hamptons house becomes a summer gallery for the group show “Creature From the Blue Lagoon.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'BRIDGEHAMPTON, N.Y. — In July and August, Chelsea art dealers typically set off to Europe and the Hamptons while leaving their galleries in the hands of the junior staff. Often these are working vacations, with business conducted on the dinner-party circuit. But Jose Martos has gone a step further, establishing a seasonal branch of his Martos Gallery right in his summer residence. For the last three years he has enlisted the independent curator and critic Bob Nickas to organize lively group shows at a rented 1860s farmhouse here.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/03/arts/03CREATURE1_SPAN/03CREATURE1_S