': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage and Intelligence Services', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate Committee on Intelligence', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House Committee on Intelligence', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T01:38:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/771b1d00-b1d4-5958-be0e-005e72c0e2dd', 'word_count': 417, 'uri': 'nyt://article/771b1d00-b1d4-5958-be0e-005e72c0e2dd'}, {'abstract': 'A malpractice case about the 2007 death of a young woman who had been treated at a Brooklyn emergency room raises troubling questions about the quality of care.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/03/opinion/was-this-death-really-unavoidable.html', 'snippet': 'A malpractice case about the 2007 death of a young woman who had been treated at a Brooklyn emergency room raises troubling questions about the quality of care.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An article in The Times on Sunday recounted in jarring detail the tragic death of a young woman, an aspiring novelist named Sabrina Seelig, at a hospital in Brooklyn five years ago. Ms. Seelig’s family is convinced she was a victim of medical lapses and errors, and troubling details emerged during a malpractice trial this spring. Nevertheless, the trial jury decided that neither the hospital nor an emergency room doctor or nurse had been negligent. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '