times.com/2012/08/04/us/politics/in-iowa-grass-roots-republicans-are-still-not-sold-on-romney.html', 'snippet': 'The lack of zeal for Mitt Romney may affect turnout in a state that is considered up for grabs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OSKALOOSA, Iowa — Presidential candidates are again marshaling their forces in Iowa, which is up for grabs in November. Mitt Romney, eager to capture a state President Obama carried four years ago, is planning another visit on Tuesday as he bulks up his 11 field offices to compete with Mr. Obama’s 17.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/04/us/04iowa1/04iowa1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/04/us/04iowa1/04iowa1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/04/us/04iowa1/04iowa1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 647, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/04/us/04iowa1/04iowa1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1293, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/04/us/04iowa1/04iowa1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/04/us/04iowa1/04iowa1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/04/us/04iowa1/04iowa1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Tight Iowa Race, Romney Struggles to Excite G.O.P. Base', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In a Tight Iowa Race, Romney Fails to Excite the Republican Party’s Base', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T14:33:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Trip Gabriel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Trip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gabriel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/baebbf25-82c0-5713-aa0e-37fd53fffc9e', 'word_count': 1240, 'uri': 'nyt://article/baebbf25-82c0-5713-aa0e-37fd53fffc9e'}, {'abstract': 'Times film critics on “Total Recall,” “Celeste & Jesse Forever” and “You’ve Been Trumped.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/03/this-weeks-movies-august-3/', 'snippet': 'Times film critics on “Total Recall,” “Celeste & Jesse Forever” and “You’ve Been Trumped.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this episode of This Week’s Movies, Manohla Dargis writes that the wistful romantic comedy “Celeste & Jesse Forever” doesn’t try to rock the world, just gently nudge it. Stephen Holden takes on Anthony Baxter’s documentary “You’ve Been Trumped,” a portrait of the billionaire developer Donald Trump in action. And A. O. Scott reviews a remake of “Total Recall” with Colin Farrell. Full reviews of all of this week’s movies can be found here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Week’s Movies: August 3', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reviews', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T14:36:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/02a0546c-982d-50e1-bef4-905a5ee36ad6', 'word_count': 74, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02a0546c-982d-50e1-bef4-905a5ee36ad6'}, {'abstract': 'Over the last several months, two California men have watched their wives end their lives after a long, drawn-out illness. Were the husbands’ acts informed by love or despair? Were they a reasonable response to unbearable suffering?', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/03/a-desperate-act-born-of-depression/', 'snippet': 'Over the last several months, two California men have watched their wives end their lives after a long, drawn-out illness. Were the husbands’ acts informed by love or despair? Were they a reasonable response to unbearable suffering?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the last several months, two California men have watched their wives end their lives after a long, drawn-out illness. Afterward, each of these old men was placed in handcuffs by the police, led from his home and questioned extensively, and faced the possibility of being charged with assisting in a suicide.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Desperate Act, Born of Depression', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Depression (Mental)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Delury, George', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T14:44:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judith Graham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Graham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7a636b2-6859-5d0f-9432-7ff2303651fc', 'word_count': 852, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7a636b2-6859-5d0f-9432-7ff2303651fc'}, {'abstract': 'If you can look past anti-Semitism, Steven Gimbel suggests, maybe relativity is “Jewish science” after all.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/05/books/review/einsteins-jewish-science-by-steven-gimbel.html', 'snippet': 'If you can look past anti-Semitism, Steven Gimbel suggests, maybe relativity is “Jewish science” after all.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s no wonder Nazis hated relativity. They lived in a world of absolutes. There was a master race with one true religion and one true language, with a music and literature that celebrated its glory. There was a true German empire, sliced up by the arbitrary boundaries of concoctions called nation-states. With absolute might the Fatherland would regain its proper position in space and time.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Cover/0805Cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Cover/0805Cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Cover/0805Cover-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 878, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Cover/0805Cover-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1755, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Cover/0805Cover-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Cover/0805Cover-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Cover/0805Cover-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Quantum Leaps', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quantum Leaps', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Physics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Einstein, Albert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gimbel, Steven', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T14:44:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa9833f6-b490-5c60-b1c4-adba68303838', 'word_count': 1144, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa9833f6-b490-5c60-b1c4-adba68303838'}, {'abstract': 'A feminist historian explores the contradictions of Marilyn Monroe’s character.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/05/books/review/marilyn-by-lois-banner.html', 'snippet': 'A feminist historian explores the contradictions of Marilyn Monroe’s character.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1972, on the 10th anniversary of Marilyn Monroe’s death, Gloria Steinem wrote an essay for Ms. magazine titled “The Woman Who Died Too Soon.” As a teenager, Steinem had relished the celluloid darkness of the matinee: the sci-fi flicks, the serials, the stubborn charm of Doris Day. She loved them all, however improbable the plots or poor the acting. But she walked out of “Gentlemen Prefer Blondes.” The sight of Marilyn as the diamond-obsessed showgirl Lorelei Lee, “huge as a colossus doll, mincing and whispering and simply hoping her way into total vulnerability,” enraged her.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Slutzsky/0805Slutzsky-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 385, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Slutzsky/0805Slutzsky-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 385}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Slutzsky/0805Slutzsky-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Slutzsky/0805Slutzsky-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1334, '