nal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bridgehampton (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T14:46:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Lewine', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lewine', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a1e4d1f-690e-5514-a3d2-ce913d911aec', 'word_count': 666, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a1e4d1f-690e-5514-a3d2-ce913d911aec'}, {'abstract': 'Karl Taro Greenfeld’s novel observes the details of downtown life, circa 2008.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/05/books/review/triburbia-by-karl-taro-greenfeld.html', 'snippet': 'Karl Taro Greenfeld’s novel observes the details of downtown life, circa 2008.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One view of the novel, the one that E. M. Forster advanced, says it’s “a fiction in prose of a certain extent” — meaning more than 50,000 words, in Forster’s opinion. By that definition Karl Taro Greenfeld’s “Triburbia” qualifies as a novel, which is what his publisher calls it, though I’d be inclined to call it linked stories. I suspect that if “Dubliners” had been published in recent years it would have been marketed as a novel. If Tom Rachman’s “Imperfectionists” and Jennifer Egan’s “Visit From the Goon Squad” are novels, if Flann O’Brien’s “At Swim-Two-Birds” is a novel, then so, perhaps, is “Triburbia.” It lacks the sense of closure and the linear narrative drive of more conventional examples of the form, but it’s an artful and casually cohesive work of fiction imbued with anthropological insight.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805SiloMcInerney/0805SiloMcInerney-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1015, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805SiloMcInerney/0805SiloMcInerney-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2031, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805SiloMcInerney/0805SiloMcInerney-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805SiloMcInerney/0805SiloMcInerney-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805SiloMcInerney/0805SiloMcInerney-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bobos in Paradise', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bobos in Paradise', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greenfeld, Karl Taro', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T14:46:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jay McInerney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jay', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McInerney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e63d5da-d0d9-517c-aa0d-efcbf08407d5', 'word_count': 1223, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e63d5da-d0d9-517c-aa0d-efcbf08407d5'}, {'abstract': 'An astronaut’s wife is pushed to the brink in Lydia Netzer’s novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/05/books/review/shine-shine-shine-by-lydia-netzer.html', 'snippet': 'An astronaut’s wife is pushed to the brink in Lydia Netzer’s novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The opening lines of Lydia Netzer’s first novel, “Shine Shine Shine,” read like a cyber-alloy of astro\xadphysical and metaphysical poetry — imagine Wordsworth recast by Carl Sagan. It’s tempting to arrange them as a stanza:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Schillinger/0805Schillinger-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 739, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Schillinger/0805Schillinger-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1479, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Schillinger/0805Schillinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Schillinger/0805Schillinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Schillinger/0805Schillinger-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Moonstruck', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Moonstruck', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Netzer, Lydia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T14:47:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liesl Schillinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liesl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schillinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a316b58-92fe-5aa6-b8a4-af4227b2ce6e', 'word_count': 1193, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a316b58-92fe-5aa6-b8a4-af4227b2ce6e'}, {'abstract': 'John Brandon’s novel, set outside Albuquerque, delves into the metaphysic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/05/books/review/a-million-heavens-by-john-brandon.html', 'snippet': 'John Brandon’s novel, set outside Albuquerque, delves into the metaphysic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'John Brandon’s unpretentious, deeply heartfelt voice speaks to, and for, a certain type of reader — one who’s inhaled the gateway drugs of Hogwarts and Katniss, and is ready for more adult pleasures. Centering on unmoored young men and women taking fraught voyages of self-discovery, Brandon’s first two novels — “Arkansas” and “Citrus County” — shuddered through strip-mall landscapes leafy with damaged adults, broken families, disintegrating plans and unrealized hopes. “Citrus County” was about the tentative love between a middle schoolboy and the girl whose younger sister that boy had kidnapped; its every page was bright with the detailed observations, aesthetic vision and moral complexities we look for in the big leagues of serious literature. Thus, like Salinger before him and contemporaries including Hannah Tinti, Tony Earley and Mark Haddon, Brandon is an author who expands the tent, delivering capital-letter good stuff to young readers and adults alike.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Bock/0805Bock-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 336, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Bock/0805Bock-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 336}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Bock/0805Bock-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 573, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Bock/0805Bock-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1146, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Bock/0805Bock-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Bock/0805Bock-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Bock/0805Bock-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Desert Vigil', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Desert Vigil', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brandon, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T14:48:23+0000', 'docu