 and a half campaigning across the country', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corruption’s Reach in India Spawns New Political Party', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corruption (Institutional)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hazare, Anna', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Delhi (India)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T14:49:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Niharika Mandhana', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Niharika', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mandhana', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/152f900e-7ca8-59d3-8b9f-4836948c7d0e', 'word_count': 607, 'uri': 'nyt://article/152f900e-7ca8-59d3-8b9f-4836948c7d0e'}, {'abstract': 'Make-believe is more than fun and games, Jonathan Gottschall says; it helps us navigate life’s complex social problems.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/05/books/review/the-storytelling-animal-by-jonathan-gottschall.html', 'snippet': 'Make-believe is more than fun and games, Jonathan Gottschall says; it helps us navigate life’s complex social problems.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We love a good story. Narrative is stitched intrinsically into the fabric of human psychology. But why? Is it all just fun and games, or does storytelling serve a biological function? ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Eagleman/0805Eagleman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 860, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Eagleman/0805Eagleman-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1720, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Eagleman/0805Eagleman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Eagleman/0805Eagleman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Eagleman/0805Eagleman-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 184, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Moral of the Story', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Moral of the Story', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gottschall, Jonathan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T14:49:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Eagleman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eagleman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/954093a3-e40e-5610-9fb4-161a137b68e9', 'word_count': 1162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/954093a3-e40e-5610-9fb4-161a137b68e9'}, {'abstract': 'In Padgett Powell’s novel, “two weirdly agreeable dudes” share their observations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/05/books/review/you-me-by-padgett-powell.html', 'snippet': 'In Padgett Powell’s novel, “two weirdly agreeable dudes” share their observations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Padgett Powell’s sixth novel extends a slender and complicated body of work that over almost 30 years has been occasionally brilliant and just as often stubbornly opaque. “You & Me” is presented by its publisher as a “Southern sendup” of “Waiting for Godot” and is introduced by the author, despite its being a novel, with a piece of stage direction: “Two weirdly agreeable dudes are on a porch in a not upscale neighborhood, apparently within walking distance of a liquor store, talking a lot.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Mallon/0805Mallon-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 963, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Mallon/0805Mallon-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1925, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Mallon/0805Mallon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Mallon/0805Mallon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Mallon/0805Mallon-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'That’s Life', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'That’s Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Powell, Padgett', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T14:50:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Mallon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mallon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/db04765d-4dac-583c-b126-8b16ac894ce6', 'word_count': 1243, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db04765d-4dac-583c-b126-8b16ac894ce6'}, {'abstract': 'In Simon Rich’s novel, God is C.E.O. of Heaven Inc.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/05/books/review/what-in-gods-name-by-simon-rich.html', 'snippet': 'In Simon Rich’s novel, God is C.E.O. of Heaven Inc.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Simon Rich’s first novel, the prep-school sendup “Elliot Allagash,” drew comparisons to Evelyn Waugh and P. G. Wodehouse. His new novel, “What in God’s Name,” evokes another titan of English comedy: Douglas Adams. Like Adams in “The Hitchhiker’s Guide to the Galaxy,” Rich drags heaven down to Earth. His paradise is a mismanaged corporation, Heaven Inc. — full of departments like Prayer Intake and Geyser Regulation — whose chief executive, God, decides on a whim to retire, destroy Earth, kill all the humans (by fire or ice, he’s still deciding) and fulfill his lifelong dream of opening an Asian-\xadAmerican fusion restaurant.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images