ons', 'value': 'Chick-fil-A', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Graham, Billy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T14:53:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Oppenheimer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Oppenheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1aabb60b-8d89-552e-8005-76db26239250', 'word_count': 1048, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1aabb60b-8d89-552e-8005-76db26239250'}, {'abstract': 'Fifteen tumultuous years in the life of San Francisco, from Altamont to the Super Bowl.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/05/books/review/season-of-the-witch-by-david-talbot.html', 'snippet': 'Fifteen tumultuous years in the life of San Francisco, from Altamont to the Super Bowl.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Upon seeing the subtitle appended to “Season of the Witch,” the latest book by David Talbot, this reader went into a defensive crouch. She hoped it was merely something dreamed up by an overzealous publicist.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Ullman/0805Ullman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 981, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Ullman/0805Ullman-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1961, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Ullman/0805Ullman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Ullman/0805Ullman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Ullman/0805Ullman-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Peace, Love and Crime', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Peace, Love and Crime', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Talbot, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Francisco (Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T14:54:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ellen Ullman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ellen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ullman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a751dbe-4013-5728-b3cb-7fd56bf317ee', 'word_count': 1247, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a751dbe-4013-5728-b3cb-7fd56bf317ee'}, {'abstract': 'Bill Maher says America’s drug policy is “a glaring hypocrisy.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/05/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'Bill Maher says America’s drug policy is “a glaring hypocrisy.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a 2009 episode of his HBO show, “Real Time,” Bill Maher and his panel of guests — Mos Def, Salman Rushdie and Christopher Hitchens (Scotch in hand) — pilloried President Obama for his dismissive answer to an “online town hall” question about whether it would make economic sense for America to legalize, regulate and tax marijuana. “I don’t know what this says about the online audience,” Obama said. “But the answer is no, I don’t think that is a good strategy.” Yet Maher writes in his review of Doug Fine’s new book, “Too High to Fail: Cannabis and the New Green Economic Revolution,” that there are fiscal and moral arguments to be made over how the United States polices pot. In a recent e-mail, he said of Obama: “If I could tell him just one thing, it would be to remind him that each of the last three presidents could have gone to jail in their youth for doing drugs that other Americans are still being punished for. Just getting caught smoking pot once can follow you around for the rest of your life, blocking career and even housing opportunities. Three presidents in a row now have acknowledged partying in their youth. Our drug policy is a glaring hypocrisy.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805UpFront/0805UpFront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 647, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805UpFront/0805UpFront-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2031, 'width': 1283, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T14:54:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51deb819-fc20-5d0f-bd3b-d6c469778765', 'word_count': 315, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51deb819-fc20-5d0f-bd3b-d6c469778765'}, {'abstract': 'Mitt Romney counters Harry Reid’s tax accusation in exactly the wrong way.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/03/put-up-or-shut-up/', 'snippet': 'Mitt Romney counters Harry Reid’s tax accusation in exactly the wrong way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Senate Majority Leader Harry Reid scored a magnificent own goal the other day, easing pressure on Mitt Romney to release his tax returns by throwing around wild accusations. He told The Huffington Post on Tuesday that, according to a Bain investor, Mr. Romney didn’t pay taxes for ten years. Realizing that gossip from an unnamed source would hardly convince a skeptical public, he conceded: “Now, do I know that that’s true? Well, I’m not certain.” Thus he added to the impression that the allegations were baseless.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Put Up or Shut Up’', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reid, Harry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T14:56:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/868b7c19-8ec8-5afd-a2f3-d1fa7ef0a2ce', 'word_count': 293, 'uri': 'nyt://article/868b7c19-8ec8-5afd-a2f3-d1fa7ef0a2ce'}, {'abstract': 'According to a complaint filed by the Securities and Exchange Commission, an employee of Bristol-Myers Squibb did little to cover his tracks when he engaged in an insider trading scheme that generated over $300,000 in profits.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/08/03/insider-trading-case-reveals-an-unsophisticated-scheme/', 'snippet': 'According to a complaint filed by the Securities and Exchange Commission, an employee of Bristol-Myers Squibb did little to cover his tracks when he engaged in an insider trading scheme that generated over $300,000 in profits.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Stupid Pet Tricks” has always been a favorite David Letterman sketch. The criminal insider trading charges filed against a Bristol-Myers Squibb executive, Robert D. Ramnarine, may well qualify for “Stupid Insider Trading Tricks.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Insider Trading Case Reveals a Dubious Scheme', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insider Trading', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bristol Myers Squibb Company', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T14:57:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter J. Henning', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Henning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/06083083-d84e-5aba-ad3c-42e4c37f7734', 'word_count': 902, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06083083-d84e-5aba-ad3c-42e4c37f7734'}, {'abstract': 'The playwright Daisy Foote says her father, Horton, was her biggest champion, but she couldn’t help indulging in some moments of self-pity as she tried to make her own career.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/05/theater/daisy-foote-on-being-a-playwright-like-her-father.html', 'snippet': 'The playwright Daisy Foote says her father, Horton, was her biggest champion, but she couldn’t help indulging in some moments of self-pity as she tried to make her own career.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ONE morning in 2006, well into the run of my play “Bhutan” at the Cherry Lane Theater, my father called and suggested we take in a matinee. He said he had woken up thinking of the play and just needed to see it. When the play was over, he turned to me and said: “Well, sister, it’s grown, don’t you think so? It’s just gotten better.” He was my biggest fan, believed in me completely — he was my dad.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05DAISY2_SPAN/05DAISY2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 365, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05DAISY2_SPAN/05DAISY2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 365}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05DAISY2_SPAN/05DAISY2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 676, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05DAISY2_SPAN/05DAISY2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1352, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05DAISY2_SPAN/05DAISY2_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05DAISY2_SPAN/05DAISY2_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/arts/05DAISY2_SPAN/05DAISY2_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 99, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Pleasure and Pain of Doing Like Dad', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Pleasure and Pain of Doing Like Dad', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Foote, Horton', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T15:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daisy Foote', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daisy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Foote', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0b4ee79-c0b5-5b8c-a9ab-e5f4bdcc70eb', 'word_count': 1197, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0b4ee79-c0b5-5b8c-a9ab-e5f4bdcc70eb'}, {'abstract': 'Randy Blythe was freed after spending a month in jail in connection with the death of a fan at a 2010 concert in Prague.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/03/heavy-metal-singer-is-released-from-a-czech-jail/', 'snippet': 'Randy Blythe was freed after spending a month in jail in connection with the death of a fan at a 2010 concert in Prague.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Randy Blythe, the lead singer of the American metal band Lamb of God, was released on bail from a Czech jail on Thursday a month after he was a arrested for manslaughter, The Associated Press reported.\xa0Mr. Blythe, 41, posted $400,000 bond and left Prague for the United States on Friday, said his Czech lawyer, Martin Radvan.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Heavy-Metal Singer Is Released From a Czech Jail', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lamb of God', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Prague (Czech Republic)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T15:04:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d927133-8177-5692-bee9-1d7e38a8983e', 'word_count': 169, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d927133-8177-5692-bee9-1d7e38a8983e'}, {'abstract': 'Ryan Lochte could not match the level of performance he had in the 2011 world championships in Shanghai, where he won five events and twice defeated Michael Phelps.', 'web_url': 'https://london2012.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/03/lochte-caught-in-wake-of-schedule-and-expectations/', 'snippet': 'Ryan Lochte could not match the level of performance he had in the 2011 world championships in Shanghai, where he won five events and twice defeated Michael Phelps.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — What shifted the hype machine into overdrive, helping to broadside Ryan Lochte at these London Games, were the world swimming championships last year in Shanghai.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lochte Caught in Wake of Schedule and Expectations', 'kicker': '2012 London Olympics', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Swimming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bowman, Bob', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clary, Tyler', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lochte, Ryan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Phelps, Michael', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T15:04:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Crouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5fd2cf21-2abb-5aed-b03d-2450266e3734', 'word_count': 395, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5fd2cf21-2abb-5aed-b03d-2450266e3734'}, {'abstract': 'Runners want to do well enough to advance to the next round, but not go so fast that they burn out too quickly.', 'web_url': 'https://london2012.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/03/jockeying-for-position-in-womens-400/', 'snippet': 'Runners want to do well enough to advance to the next round, but not go so fast that they burn out too quickly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON – The rain came down hard as the American sprinter DeeDee Trotter raised her arms high in the air before starting her first 400-meter race at the Olympic Games. Because of an injury, she failed to make the final at the Beijing Games. She is hoping that on Sunday she will be in the final here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jockeying for Position in the Women’s 400', 'kicker': '2012 London Olympics', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Running', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Track and Field', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Day, Christine', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Montsho, Amantle', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ohuruogu, Christine', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Richards, Sanya', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T15:07:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mary Pilon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pilon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed99e2ac-e26a-5d67-9ad7-2b41d8638cbb', 'word_count': 554, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed99e2ac-e26a-5d67-9ad7-2b41d8638cbb'}, {'abstract': 'The former Olympic swimmer Aaron Peirsol discusses the next step for college-age Olympic athletes.', 'web_url': 'https://london2012.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/03/success-as-a-double-edged-sword/', 'snippet': 'The former Olympic swimmer Aaron Peirsol discusses the next step for college-age Olympic athletes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Aaron Peirsol competed in three Olympics and won seven medals. He won three gold medals at the Athens Games in 2004, sweeping the backstroke events. In Beijing in 2008, he won the gold medal in the 100-meter backstroke. As he says, “I’m a former swimmer who enjoyed what he did and got to do it for a long time.” He will be writing for the 2012 London blog throughout the Olympics. This is his third post.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Success as a Double-Edged Sword', 'kicker': '2012 London Olympics', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Swimming', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Peirsol, Aaron', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Collegiate Athletic Assn', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T15:07:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aaron Peirsol', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aaron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Peirsol', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8767ec5-407f-5f32-a34d-b5f122921e07', 'word_count': 1145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8767ec5-407f-5f32-a34d-b5f122921e07'}, {'abstract': 'Joseph E. Stiglitz considers the cultural and political consequences of concentrated wealth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/05/books/review/the-price-of-inequality-by-joseph-e-stiglitz.html', 'snippet': 'Joseph E. Stiglitz considers the cultural and political consequences of concentrated wealth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Joseph E. Stiglitz’s new book, “The Price of Inequality,” is the single most comprehensive counter\xadargument to both Democratic neoliberalism and Republican laissez-faire theories. While credible economists running the gamut from center right to center left describe our bleak present as the result of seemingly unstoppable developments — globalization and automation, a self-\xadreplicating establishment built on “meritocratic” competition, the debt-driven collapse of 2008 — Stiglitz stands apart in his defiant rejection of such notions of inevitability. He seeks to shift the terms of the debate.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Edsall/0805Edsall-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 281, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Edsall/0805Edsall-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 281}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Edsall/0805Edsall-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 480, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Edsall/0805Edsall-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 959, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Edsall/0805Edsall-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Edsall/0805Edsall-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/books/review/0805Edsall/0805Edsall-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Separate and Unequal', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Separate and Unequal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "The Price of Inequality: How Today's Divided Society Endangers Our Future (Book)", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stiglitz, Joseph E', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T15:13:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas B. Edsall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/75d7c722-0c28-5ccf-9753-4e7ab5b3e281', 'word_count': 1220, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75d7c722-0c28-5ccf-9753-4e7ab5b3e281'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/05/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE CANDIDATE: What It Takes to Win — and Hold — the White House,  by Samuel L. Popkin. (Oxford University, $27.95.) Campaigns really do matter, a political scientist argues in this management bible for the business of presidential campaigning.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T15:14:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/f8daca59-85ad-598c-b5c1-dcac6bb641a2', 'word_count': 285, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f8daca59-85ad-598c-b5c1-dcac6bb641a2'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/05/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ZONE ONE,  by Colson Whitehead. (Anchor, $15.) Whitehead takes on a pulp staple in this savvy novel, set in a post-apocalyptic Manhattan after a plague has turned much of the world’s population into zombies: either flesh-eating predators or harmless catatonics. Our guide to this new reality is a survivor who works on a quasi-military crew tasked with clearing Zone One (geographically, everything south of Canal Street) of the remaining undead.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T15:14:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8dc0f84-a8a7-539d-8bfa-0c305de4a3a9', 'word_count': 499, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8dc0f84-a8a7-539d-8bfa-0c305de4a3a9'}, {'abstract': '“Days of Destruction, Days of Revolt,” a collaboration between the reporter Chris Hedges and the comics artist Joe Sacco — like an updated version of James Agee’s “Let Us Now Praise Famous Men” — hits the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 8.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/12/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': '“Days of Destruction, Days of Revolt,” a collaboration between the reporter Chris Hedges and the comics artist Joe Sacco — like an updated version of James Agee’s “Let Us Now Praise Famous Men” — hits the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 8.', 'lead_paragraph': 'COMBAT JOURNALIST: “Days of Destruction, Days of Revolt,” a collaboration between the reporter Chris Hedges and the comics artist Joe Sacco, is new on the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 8. The book takes readers into abandoned cities, depleted coal-mining towns and other “sacrifice zones” where the authors say corporate greed has ruined life for the locals — like an updated version of James Agee’s “Let Us Now Praise Famous Men,” with Sacco’s comics in place of Walker Evans’s photographs.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/12insidelist/12insidelist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 225, 'width': 222, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/12insidelist/12insidelist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 225, 'width': 222, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/12insidelist/12insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/12insidelist/12insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/12insidelist/12insidelist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'TBR: Inside the List', 'nam