', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fdc2a7cc-d8fd-51cc-8dac-78f55797d4fd', 'word_count': 153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fdc2a7cc-d8fd-51cc-8dac-78f55797d4fd'}, {'abstract': 'James K. Galbraith and Jim Kessler continue the debate around Social Security.', 'web_url': 'https://keller.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/03/boomers-and-entitlements-the-next-round/', 'snippet': 'James K. Galbraith and Jim Kessler continue the debate around Social Security.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My proposal that the baby boomer generation take on the cause of rescuing entitlements from their unsustainable course – setting aside a share of the savings to invest in infrastructure, education and basic research – continues to draw a lot of mail, pro and con, as I noted in my last post. Today I’m kicking up my feet and cranking up the Beach Boys while two more authoritative advocates go at it. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Boomers and Entitlements: The Next Round', 'kicker': 'Bill Keller', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Security (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Galbraith, James K', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schumer, Charles E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Third Way', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T17:58:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Keller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1dc3773b-5579-56a5-951e-51c9865a5e9c', 'word_count': 2108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1dc3773b-5579-56a5-951e-51c9865a5e9c'}, {'abstract': 'The jewelry designer is now making paintings inspired by pieces in her most recent collection, called Sut’ana.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/03/monique-peans-new-pieces-as-paintings/', 'snippet': 'The jewelry designer is now making paintings inspired by pieces in her most recent collection, called Sut’ana.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After experimenting with photographs of fossilized ivory, acrylic paint, gesso and really big sheets of canvas, the jewelry designer Monique Péan is now painting, too. Her inspiration: the pieces in her most recent collection, called Sut’ana. “I’m just so fascinated by fossilized woolly mammoth and walrus ivory; no two pieces are the exact same,” says Péan about the thousands-of-years-old Peruvian treasures that star in Sut’ana. “I thought it would be exciting to magnify them, to show their intricate natural patterns.” And that she did. With the help of the photographer Marshall Troy, Péan put a selection of her jewelry — a necklace made of olive-tinted ivory and earrings made of cognac fossils, opals, black jade and diamonds, for example — under the camera lens to capture their constellation-like compositions. She then magnified and printed a total of five pictures onto large canvases that underwent a 72-hour Mark Rothko-like top coat of paint, to mimic the fossils’ reflective and translucent textures. “The human eye doesn’t necessarily recognize the natural beauty of the fossils within jewelry’s small scale,” Péan says. “There are many people that see different patterns in the paintings, and make guesses, like, ‘Are they bursts of sun?’ But once I show them the actual jewelry pieces, they completely understand.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monique Péan’s New Pieces as Paintings', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jewels and Jewelry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pean, Monique', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T18:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/da91b0c0-d9eb-5a14-b703-e15e6bb17813', 'word_count': 210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da91b0c0-d9eb-5a14-b703-e15e6bb17813'}, {'abstract': 'Summer is tomato time. Melissa Clark shows how to peel, remove seeds and grate them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/dining/100000001698429/tomato-time.html', 'snippet': 'Summer is tomato time. Melissa Clark shows how to peel, remove seeds and grate them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Summer is tomato time. Melissa Clark shows how to peel, remove seeds and grate them.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/03/dining/video-clarktomato/video-clarktomato-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/03/dining/video-clarktomato/video-clarktomato-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/03/dining/video-clarktomato/video-clarktomato-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 507, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'ima