, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-03T21:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Branch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Branch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/701c1d8e-ab3e-55b7-94cf-712ec6204fd0', 'word_count': 403, 'uri': 'nyt://article/701c1d8e-ab3e-55b7-94cf-712ec6204fd0'}, {'abstract': 'Mocked and misunderstood by outsiders, and largely an afterthought even in the world of track and field, racewalking is a serious pursuit in many parts of Latin America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/04/sports/olympics/olympic-racewalking-is-more-than-just-a-stroll.html', 'snippet': 'Mocked and misunderstood by outsiders, and largely an afterthought even in the world of track and field, racewalking is a serious pursuit in many parts of Latin America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Eider Arevalo is just 19, but he lives in a comfortable apartment in Bogotá, Colombia. With a monthly income of about $2,500, he earns about twice as much as his father, a construction worker in Pitalito, 233 miles to the south. Arevalo’s ticket to a better life has been racewalking.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/04/sports/RACEWALK/RACEWALK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 657, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/04/sports/RACEWALK/RACEWALK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1314, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type':