ne': {'original': 'By Christoph Niemann and Jon Huang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christoph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Niemann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Huang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e61251ca-7ff4-505b-a862-97dc69a97878', 'word_count': 48, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e61251ca-7ff4-505b-a862-97dc69a97878'}, {'abstract': 'In the 56-board semifinals at the 14th World Youth Teams Championships on Thursday, there was one very exciting match for American fans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/04/crosswords/bridge/in-bridge-14th-world-youth-teams-championships.html', 'snippet': 'In the 56-board semifinals at the 14th World Youth Teams Championships on Thursday, there was one very exciting match for American fans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TAICANG, China — In the 56-board semifinals at the 14th World Youth Teams Championships here on Thursday, there was one very exciting match for American fans. In the Youngsters (under-21) division, USA-1 (Zachary Brescoll, Adam Kaplan, the brothers Adam and Zachary Grossack, and the brothers Andrew and Richard Jeng) were playing against Israel. With 14 boards remaining, Israel led by 30 international match points. This lead rose to 42 on the first board of the last session. But then USA-1 won the remaining deals by 74 imps to 0, to win by 32.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/04/crosswords/bridge/04card/04card-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 612, 'width': 292, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/04/cros