'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Nocera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nocera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e1df66e-0629-57fe-a62a-89873fcaf0df', 'word_count': 832, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e1df66e-0629-57fe-a62a-89873fcaf0df'}, {'abstract': 'Instead of remorse, Representative Laura Richardson displayed “utter disdain” for attempts to investigate her disgraceful behavior during her re-election campaign.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/04/opinion/the-house-reprimands-one-of-its-own.html', 'snippet': 'Instead of remorse, Representative Laura Richardson displayed “utter disdain” for attempts to investigate her disgraceful behavior during her re-election campaign.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the very least, Representative Laura Richardson could have shown a bit of remorse when the House voted this week to reprimand her for requiring that her staff aides give donations and work for her re-election campaign and for destroying evidence when the Ethics Committee sought the truth.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The House Reprimands One of Its Own', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The House Reprimands One of Its Own', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Richardson, Laura', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Institutional)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-04T02:02:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d075962e-a458-51b9-8c96-9d88045958cf', 'word_count': 265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d075962e-a458-51b9-8c96-9d88045958cf'}, {'abstract': 'For-profit schools are generally a bad deal for taxpayers and for the underprivileged students they often recruit through deceptive means. It is imperative that Washington regulate this industry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/04/opinion/closer-scrutiny-of-for-profit-schools.html', 'snippet': 'For-profit schools are generally a bad deal for taxpayers and for the underprivileged students they often recruit through deceptive means. It is imperative that Washington regulate this industry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The last several weeks have not been particularly restful for the lucrative for-profit education industry.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Closer Scrutiny of For-Profit Schools', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Closer Scrutiny of For-Profit Schools', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'For-Profit Schools', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Education Department (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Contreras, Rudolph', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-04T02:02:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/1be269c6-51a2-5206-b708-ebd5421b1be3', 'word_count': 357, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1be269c6-51a2-5206-b708-ebd5421b1be3'}, {'abstract': 'The Supreme Court must stay a Texas execution and stop the state from breaching the Constitution.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/04/opinion/mentally-retarded-and-on-death-row.html', 'snippet': 'The Supreme Court must stay a Texas execution and stop the state from breaching the Constitution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Marvin Wilson, with an I.Q. of 61, is scheduled to be put to death in Texas on Tuesday. His execution would directly contradict the Supreme Court’s 2002 ruling in Atkins v. Virginia that “the mentally retarded should be categorically excluded from execution” because of “their disabilities in areas of reasoning, judgment and control of their impulses.” The court should accept Mr. Wilson’s case for review and end Texas’s illegal defiance of its explicit holding that the death penalty for the mentally retarded is unconstitutional. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mentally Retarded and on Death Row', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mentally Retarded and on Death Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Marvin Lee', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Retardation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-04T02:03:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/a296e574-a58c-5cec-b7ba-9f85600a1936', 'word_count': 402, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a296e574-a58c-5cec-b7ba-9f85600a1936'}, {'abstract': 'Amid unruly proceedings inside and outside the courtroom, prosecutors rested their case against the band, accused of hooliganism over a protest at Moscow’s main cathedral.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/04/world/europe/russian-punk-bands-trial-inspires-protests.html', 'snippet': 'Amid unruly proceedings inside and outside the courtroom, prosecutors rested their case against the band, accused of hooliganism over a protest at Moscow’s main cathedral.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — A day after President Vladimir V. Putin appeared to urge leniency in the trial of three young women who staged a “punk prayer” in Moscow’s main church, the prosecution rested its case after showing the judge the dark blue balaclavas that the band members wore during demonstrations.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/04/world/moscow/moscow-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 692, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/04/world/moscow/moscow-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1384, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Unruly Proceedings in Trial of Russian Punk Group', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unruly Proceedings in Trial Of Russian Punk Group', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pussy Riot', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-04T02:04:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Roth', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/218807dc-dec6-5efa-9cbd-ec59f6311d2c', 'word_count': 472, 'uri': 'nyt://article/218807dc-dec6-5efa-9cbd-ec59f6311d2c'}, {'abstract': 'Job growth in July wasn’t enough to lower the unemployment rate. The market lacks forward momentum that the government could and should provide.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/04/opinion/the-economy-is-stuck-in-place.html', 'snippet': 'Job growth in July wasn’t enough to lower the unemployment rate. The market lacks forward momentum that the government could and should provide.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With 163,000 new jobs created, July’s employment growth topped both analysts’ expectations and the meager job gains in May and June. While that growth was not enough to reduce the jobless rate — now 8.3 percent — it was enough to boost the stock market. For investors, the job tally was just high enough to be a pleasant surprise and low enough to give them hope that the Federal Reserve would soon intervene to juice the economy. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stuck in Place', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stuck in Place', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs'