ckey team defeated the U.S. 3-2, putting the U.S. out of medal contention at the London Olympics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Disappointment hit the United States women’s field hockey team on Saturday, as it lost to New Zealand, 3-2. The Americans had had high hopes of at least contending for a medal here, based on an improving offense, a notably rugged defense and one of the world’s best goalkeepers in Amy Tran-Swensen.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Zealand Field Hockey Eliminates U.S.', 'kicker': '2012 London Olympics', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Field', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Taylor, Shannon', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-04T17:30:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lawrie Mifflin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lawrie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mifflin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6daa3565-bd29-5662-9c84-4af2d0762303', 'word_count': 1684, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6daa3565-bd29-5662-9c84-4af2d0762303'}, {'abstract': 'How Serena Williams overwhelmed Maria Sharapova to win the gold medal in the women’s singles in London.', 'web_url': 'https://london2012.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/04/serena-williams-close-to-perfect/', 'snippet': 'How Serena Williams overwhelmed Maria Sharapova to win the gold medal in the women’s singles in London.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a bright, blustery day on Centre Court in London, Serena Williams dominated Maria Sharapova 6-0, 6-1 to win the Olympic gold medal in women’s singles. The breezy conditions scarcely bothered Williams, as she served and returned with power, disguise, and fearlessness. Williams pounded a thunderous ace up the T on match point, then let out a celebratory yell as the ball thumped into the backstop. It was her 60th ace of the tournament.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Serena Williams, Close to Perfect', 'kicker': '2012 London Olympics', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sharapova, Maria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Serena', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-04T17:36:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoff Macdonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macdonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5fca38a3-1523-5510-8bc8-9a228ea910a5', 'word_count': 394, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5fca38a3-1523-5510-8bc8-9a228ea910a5'}, {'abstract': 'With the planet, and particularly cities, becoming ever more crowded, there’s nowhere to grow but up.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/04/living-thinking-houses/', 'snippet': 'With the planet, and particularly cities, becoming ever more crowded, there’s nowhere to grow but up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the sizzling summer heat I’ve been thinking about igloos. To chill out in, of course, but also because I admire their elemental simplicity. Inuits traditionally used bone knives to carve bricks from quarries of hardened snow. A short, low tunnel led to the front door, trapping heat in and keeping out fierce cold and critters. Mortar wasn’t needed, because the snow bricks were shaved to fit, and at night the dome ossified into a glistening ice fort. The human warmth inside melted the ice just enough to seal the seams.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Living, Thinking Houses', 'kicker': 'Diane Ackerman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Living, Thinking Houses', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roofs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sustainable Living', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-04T17:38:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Diane Ackerman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Diane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ackerman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0d26a80-1630-5d32-ad1c-2da41df9f3ae', 'word_count': 853, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0d26a80-1630-5d32-ad1c-2da41df9f3ae'}, {'abstract': 'How some crimes of the elite are not prosecuted, and why.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/05/opinion/sunday/sunday-dialogue-harm-no-foul.html', 'snippet': 'How some crimes of the elite are not prosecuted, and why.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How some crimes of the elite are not prosecuted, and why.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/05/sunday-review/05LETTERS/05LETTERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 379, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge':