'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By James R. Oestreich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Oestreich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fbdec42b-fe2c-537e-9af6-66eb1f8b6732', 'word_count': 611, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fbdec42b-fe2c-537e-9af6-66eb1f8b6732'}, {'abstract': 'With a $600,000 bounty on the line, Lee Chong Wei of Malaysia took an early lead against Lin Dan of China in the badminton men’s final, but in the end he fell to his archrival.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/sports/olympics/lin-dan-of-china-beats-rival-to-win-badminton-gold.html', 'snippet': 'With a $600,000 bounty on the line, Lee Chong Wei of Malaysia took an early lead against Lin Dan of China in the badminton men’s final, but in the end he fell to his archrival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Just 2 points stood between Lee Chong Wei and gold in the men’s badminton finals — and not just the modest amount of precious metal in the Olympics’ most coveted prize. He was also on the verge of winning a bar of gold worth more than $600,000.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/06/sports/BADMINTON1/BADMINTON1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 550, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/06/sports/BADMINTON1/BADMINTON1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 550}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/06/sports/BADMINTON1/BADMINTON1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 628, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image',