finally beating two-time gold medalist Yelena Isinbayeva of Russia, who settled for a bronze. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'London Live: Follow Monday’s Action at the Olympics', 'kicker': '2012 London Olympics', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T11:53:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/be291c78-260b-5987-a1c3-5df5f9aad130', 'word_count': 8135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be291c78-260b-5987-a1c3-5df5f9aad130'}, {'abstract': 'A summary of what’s on today’s editorial page.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/opinion-report-the-vehicular-olympics/', 'snippet': 'A summary of what’s on today’s editorial page.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From the editorial page', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Opinion Report: The Vehicular Olympics', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T12:01:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/538315ad-5c28-51b4-8039-418c5b267160', 'word_count': 81, 'uri': 'nyt://article/538315ad-5c28-51b4-8039-418c5b267160'}, {'abstract': 'Attack on the Sikh community in America raises global concerns.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/for-many-sikhs-wisconsin-attack-has-troubling-echoes/', 'snippet': 'Attack on the Sikh community in America raises global concerns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For Sikhs in the United States, India and elsewhere, the killing of six people at a gurudwara, or Sikh temple, in Wisconsin on Sunday afternoon has brought grief and sorrow but also concern that the rampage might be the latest example of a hate crime against Sikhs on American soil in the decade since the 9/11 attacks.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Many Sikhs, Wisconsin Attack Has Troubling Echoes', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sikhs and Sikhism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wisconsin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T12:06:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/59cd5a9f-d51c-5d19-8267-38845b2eec0a', 'word_count': 813, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59cd5a9f-d51c-5d19-8267-38845b2eec0a'}, {'abstract': 'Both critics and proponents of the E.U. airline emissions reduction scheme acknowledge that emissions from aviation, representing 3 percent of total global carbon emissions, are environmentally significant. The question is what to do about it.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/u-s-fight-against-european-airline-emissions-plan-heats-up/', 'snippet': 'Both critics and proponents of the E.U. airline emissions reduction scheme acknowledge that emissions from aviation, representing 3 percent of total global carbon emissions, are environmentally significant. The question is what to do about it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A meeting of 17 non-European nations hosted by the U.S. State and Transportation departments in Washington last week ended with assurances that the participants were working toward curbing their emissions from aviation.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/07/blogs/us-airlines-eu-emissions-plan-rdv/us-airlines-eu-emissions-plan-rdv-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/07/blogs/us-airlines-eu-emissions-plan-rdv/us-airlines-eu-emissions-plan-rdv-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. Fight Against E.U. Airline Emissions Plan Heats Up', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T12:25:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher F. Schuetze', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Schuetze', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7fa8b787-ba73-5ca0-b3a3-a7c585ba1015', 'word_count': 676, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7fa8b787-ba73-5ca0-b3a3-a7c585ba1015'}, {'abstract': 'Knight Capital confirmed on Monday that it had struck a rescue deal with a group of investors, staving off collapse after a recent trading mishap, even as the New York Stock Exchange temporarily revoked its market-making responsibilities.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/knight-capital-confirms-lifeline-loses-market-making-duties/', 'snippet': 'Knight Capital confirmed on Monday that it had struck a rescue deal with a group of investors, staving off collapse after a recent trading mishap, even as the New York Stock Exchange temporarily revoked its market-making responsibilities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Knight Capital Group confirmed on Monday that it had struck a $400 million rescue deal with a group of investors, staving off collapse after a recent trading mishap, even as the New York Stock Exchange temporarily revoked the firm’s market-making responsibilities. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'As Knight Capital Gains a Lifeline, It Loses Market-Making Duties', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Knight Capital Group Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Stock Exchange', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T12:29:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Protess', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90459107-b33a-5c48-a9f0-e8d20c886dd4', 'word_count': 853, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90459107-b33a-5c48-a9f0-e8d20c886dd4'}, {'abstract': 'The clear line between Internet companies that cater to consumers and those that serve businesses is beginning to blur, reports Nick Wingfield on Monday in The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/daily-report-consumer-sites-broaden-mission-to-include-businesses/', 'snippet': 'The clear line between Internet companies that cater to consumers and those that serve businesses is beginning to blur, reports Nick Wingfield on Monday in The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The clear line between Internet companies that cater to consumers and those that serve businesses is beginning to blur, reports Nick Wingfield on Monday in The New York Times. Sites like Zillow, LinkedIn, Groupon and GrubHub — consumer services that help people find homes, advertise their talents, find daily deals and order takeout food — are among the companies seeking to burrow more deeply into the operations of businesses in their respective markets.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-jumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-superJumbo-v3.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbStandard-v5.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbStandard-v5.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbLarge-v5.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Daily Report: Consumer Sites Broaden Mission to Include Businesses', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T12:45:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/280faf49-c95e-5836-b387-0f32585aac8c', 'word_count': 199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/280faf49-c95e-5836-b387-0f32585aac8c'}, {'abstract': 'English Heritage said that because of budget cuts it had removed the names of Graham Chapman, Brian Epstein, Vladimir Nabokov and Jacqueline du Pré, among others, from a list of famous figures whose homes were to be marked with special plaques.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/not-cool-britannia-plans-are-dropped-to-honor-homes-of-some-great-britons/', 'snippet': 'English Heritage said that because of budget cuts it had removed the names of Graham Chapman, Brian Epstein, Vladimir Nabokov and Jacqueline du Pré, among others, from a list of famous figures whose homes were to be marked with special plaques.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During his career with the Monty Python comedy troupe, Graham Chapman was set upon by the Spanish Inquisition (which nobody expected), pitted in combat against the fearsome Black Knight and crucified in a musical number, accomplishments for which he surely deserves some recognition. Instead, Mr. Chapman, who died in 1989, is one of several noteworthy Britons and people who lived in Britain whose names have been dropped from a list to have their homes distinguished by ceremonial markers because of budget cuts at the organization that grants them. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Not Cool, Britannia: Plans Are Dropped to Honor Homes of Some Great Britons', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chapman, Graham', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gardner, Ava', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nabokov, Vladimir', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Niven, David', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sellers, Peter', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'English Heritage', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Monty Python', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T12:49:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0af63399-92e2-5857-a126-ea7e9e9e3424', 'word_count': 252, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0af63399-92e2-5857-a126-ea7e9e9e3424'}, {'abstract': 'For months, party elders and current senior officials have been negotiating quietly in Beijing to fill the seats of the party’s top governing bodies, the 25-member Politburo and its elite Standing Committee.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/07/world/asia/reports-signal-gathering-of-chinese-leaders-ahead-of-transition.html', 'snippet': 'For months, party elders and current senior officials have been negotiating quietly in Beijing to fill the seats of the party’s top governing bodies, the 25-member Politburo and its elite Standing Committee.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — At least five senior Communist Party officials have arrived at the beachside resort of Beidaihe, including Xi Jinping, the man expected to be the next leader of China, setting the stage for what some analysts expect to be the climactic negotiations over the coming once-a-decade power transition.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/07/world/SUB-SUB-CHINA/SUB-SUB-CHINA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 263, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/07/world/SUB-SUB-CHINA/SUB-SUB-CHINA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 263}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/07/world/SUB-SUB-CHINA/SUB-SUB-CHINA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 449, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/07/world/SUB-SUB-CHINA/SUB-SUB-CHINA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 898, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Signs in China Point to Meeting on Transition', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Signs in China Point To Meeting on Transition', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Xi Jinping', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T12:56:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f8aed589-9551-5580-a9cf-234d819d3162', 'word_count': 790, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f8aed589-9551-5580-a9cf-234d819d3162'}, {'abstract': 'An August checklist for rising high school seniors, simplifying mortgage loan documents, criticism grows as more check-cashing stores expand in poorer areas and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times Sunday and Monday editions.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/monday-reading-august-checklist-for-rising-high-school-seniors/', 'snippet': 'An August checklist for rising high school seniors, simplifying mortgage loan documents, criticism grows as more check-cashing stores expand in poorer areas and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times Sunday and Monday editions.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and on our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monday Reading: August Checklist for Rising High School Seniors', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T12:58:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Siegel Bernard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': 'Siegel', 'lastname': 'Bernard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4248f615-616e-594c-aca4-ebe6f4f933e4', 'word_count': 221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4248f615-616e-594c-aca4-ebe6f4f933e4'}, {'abstract': 'The romantic clothes of Mimi Plange have obvious first-lady appeal.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/vote-of-confidence/', 'snippet': 'The romantic clothes of Mimi Plange have obvious first-lady appeal.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I kept asking myself, ‘Is that really my skirt?’ ” Mimi Plange recalls of the time she saw Michelle Obama wearing a periwinkle gabardine skirt from her spring collection during an appearance on “The View” in May. “And it was. And it looked great. And I felt really, really blessed.” In her fifth season, the 34-year-old Plange, who was born in Ghana and grew up in California, has joined the ranks of White House-approved designers, including Thakoon Panichgul, Jason Wu and Carly Cushnie and Michelle Ochs of Cushnie et Ochs, with clothes that have obvious first-lady appeal: they are romantic (corseted organza dresses in an hourglass silhouette, top left, $2,900; Esti’s, 718-645-2600), mature (Victorian silk blouses with velvet trim) and practical (lots of A-line skirts). “I am catering to every woman’s body from a female perspective,” Plange says. “It’s not about me or what I want to wear; it’s about wearable, real fantasy for real women.” And although Plange admits that she had never really watched “The View” — “I’m still not sure what the episode was all about because I couldn’t pay attention,” she says — she’s now a fan of the show and its star guest for life.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '86', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vote of Confidence', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Vote Of Confidence', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Michelle', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T13:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/24b9e717-33fb-57b6-bb25-eb9f1c0e639a', 'word_count': 200, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24b9e717-33fb-57b6-bb25-eb9f1c0e639a'}, {'abstract': 'Mitt Romney and the Republican National Committee raised $101.3 million in July, his campaign announced Monday, as Republican donors rallied behind their nominee with the national convention only a few weeks away.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/romney-committees-raised-more-than-100-million-in-july/', 'snippet': 'Mitt Romney and the Republican National Committee raised $101.3 million in July, his campaign announced Monday, as Republican donors rallied behind their nominee with the national convention only a few weeks away.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The fund-raising machine behind Mitt Romney and the Republican Party once again bested President Obama’s effort last month, raising $25 million more in July than the president and his Democratic allies did.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney’s Fund-Raising Outpaces Obama’s Again in July', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Election Commission', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican National Committee', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T13:05:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear and Nicholas Confessore', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a31028e1-dd1b-528c-8d0a-8fad2b88af90', 'word_count': 376, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a31028e1-dd1b-528c-8d0a-8fad2b88af90'}, {'abstract': 'Richard Schulze, who resigned from Best Buy’s board in June, has offered to take the company private in a deal that would value its equity as much as $8.8 billion.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/best-buy-founder-offers-8-8-billion-to-buy-out-company/', 'snippet': 'Richard Schulze, who resigned from Best Buy’s board in June, has offered to take the company private in a deal that would value its equity as much as $8.8 billion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Richard Schulze, the founder of Best Buy, offered to buy the electronics retailer on Monday in a deal that would value it as much as $8.8 billion.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Best Buy Founder Offers $8.8 Billion to Buy Out Company', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schulze, Richard M', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Best BUY Company Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T13:08:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2b93159-208d-534e-af49-e378d373ae62', 'word_count': 387, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2b93159-208d-534e-af49-e378d373ae62'}, {'abstract': 'Also, higher-than-expected ratings mean NBC may earn a profit with its Olympics broadcasts, and understanding the potential and cacophony of online radio.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/the-breakfast-meeting-sticking-the-mars-landing-and-tvs-latino-woes/', 'snippet': 'Also, higher-than-expected ratings mean NBC may earn a profit with its Olympics broadcasts, and understanding the potential and cacophony of online radio.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While incredible feats were being accomplished in London, another example of human excellence was occurring millions of miles away: the Curiosity rover landed in a 96-mile-wide crater on Mars at 1:32 a.m. Eastern time. The Mars mission’s final stage was a thriller — the rover, the size of a small car, was lowered at the end of 25-foot-long cables from a hovering rocket stage, Kenneth Chang writes. One wit on Twitter noted that the 14-minute time delay for images from Mars to reach Earth was nothing compared to the hours of delay from NBC in showing Usain Bolt’s Olympic-record-setting dash in the 100-meter race.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Sticking the Mars Landing, and TV’s Latino Woes', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T13:19:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9d63887-06df-536a-bd7a-7f50307a7924', 'word_count': 592, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9d63887-06df-536a-bd7a-7f50307a7924'}, {'abstract': 'The Day: There was every reason to assume Cathy Watkins would spend most of her life, if not all of it, in prison. But now federal investigators say she was innocent of a 1995 killing.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/a-proclamation-of-innocence-for-a-prisoner-who-never-gave-up/', 'snippet': 'The Day: There was every reason to assume Cathy Watkins would spend most of her life, if not all of it, in prison. But now federal investigators say she was innocent of a 1995 killing.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Sometimes it turns out that people do tell the truth about themselves. Sorry if that qualifying “sometimes” sounds cynical. But when you meet someone in prison who says she did not commit the murder that earned her a sentence of 25 years to life, you have reason to take her claims with a shaker of salt.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Proclamation of Innocence for a Prisoner Who Never Gave Up', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'False Arrests, Convictions and Imprisonments', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Diop, Baithe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Watkins, Cathy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T13:24:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66e2820d-7b50-5f17-ada4-e48aa1848750', 'word_count': 741, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66e2820d-7b50-5f17-ada4-e48aa1848750'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/what-were-reading-494/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Epicurious: Stop yanking back the husks on that greenmarket corn; whether you keep it or throw it back in the pile, it’s ruined. (As one farmer asks, do you break your eggs before you buy them?) Instead, follow this simple tip for finding the best ears: Feel the outside. — Patrick Farrell', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T13:29:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/846c31d9-8e35-5f34-b85e-2486e64d3099', 'word_count': 415, 'uri': 'nyt://article/846c31d9-8e35-5f34-b85e-2486e64d3099'}, {'abstract': 'Photographer Richard Barnes answers questions about what it was like to go deep underground to photograph the construction of the Second Avenue Subway.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/going-underground-behind-the-scenes-of-our-second-avenue-subway-cover-shoot/', 'snippet': 'Photographer Richard Barnes answers questions about what it was like to go deep underground to photograph the construction of the Second Avenue Subway.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For this week’s cover article, the photographer Richard Barnes went deep into the bowels of New York City to capture the otherworldly atmosphere of the Second Avenue Subway construction. I accompanied Barnes on one of his descents into the construction zone, and later conducted this Q. and A. with him.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Going Underground: Behind the Scenes of Our Second Avenue Subway Cover Shoot', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Second Avenue Subway (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T13:34:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathy Ryan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cda83e98-6002-5083-992b-d70cb7a15cd0', 'word_count': 598, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cda83e98-6002-5083-992b-d70cb7a15cd0'}, {'abstract': 'With Slovenia’s population of 2.06 million people, that works out to one medal per 514,385 residents.', 'web_url': 'https://london2012.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/slovenia-leads-games-in-medals-per-capita/', 'snippet': 'With Slovenia’s population of 2.06 million people, that works out to one medal per 514,385 residents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Once again, the United States, China and, to a lesser degree, Russia, are battling to become the nation with the biggest haul of Olympic medals. That is no surprise: their teams have the best resources – training facilities and so on – to produce athletes like Michael Phelps and a raft of Michael Jordan-like badminton and table tennis players.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Slovenia Leads Games in Medals Per Capita', 'kicker': '2012 London Olympics', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Slovenia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T13:37:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Belson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d77c7c5-85aa-5490-a280-da131f69ceb7', 'word_count': 249, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d77c7c5-85aa-5490-a280-da131f69ceb7'}, {'abstract': 'Anna Hazare announced dissolution of Team Anna on his blog, leaving his colleagues surprised.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/splitsville-for-team-anna/', 'snippet': 'Anna Hazare announced dissolution of Team Anna on his blog, leaving his colleagues surprised.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anna Hazare, the anti-corruption crusader, may be organizing a political party, but he has no plans to join it. Nor to be a candidate for office. Nor to continue the work of Team Anna.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Splitsville for Team Anna?', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hazare, Anna', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T13:47:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'W