edia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/f2bc256e-f92a-54cf-bdd8-b4027ad03992', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/f2bc256e-f92a-54cf-bdd8-b4027ad03992'}, {'abstract': 'The four major card networks vary greatly in the coverage that their credit cards provide. American Express does not offer rental car insurance on some popular sport utility vehicles, for example, while Visa covers the cost of towing.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/comparing-credit-cards-auto-rental-insurance/', 'snippet': 'The four major card networks vary greatly in the coverage that their credit cards provide. American Express does not offer rental car insurance on some popular sport utility vehicles, for example, while Visa covers the cost of towing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many credit card companies offer accident insurance when the cards are used to pay for a rental car. But details of coverage vary depending on the network behind the card, a report from CardHub.com finds.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Comparing Credit Cards’ Auto Rental Insurance', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Americans Abroad', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Insurance and Liability', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit Cards', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN EXPRESS COMPANY', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Discover Financial Services', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MasterCard Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Visa Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T17:51:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3408630a-19ba-5c9f-b209-fa768102eb78', 'word_count': 447, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3408630a-19ba-5c9f-b209-fa768102eb78'}, {'abstract': 'John C. McGinley, whose previous credits include the NBC comedy “Scrubs” and Oliver Stone’s Vietnam War drama “Platoon,” will play the embittered real estate salesman Dave Moss in a Broadway revival of “Glengarry Glen Ross.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/john-c-mcginley-joins-glengarry-glen-ross-revival-on-broadway/', 'snippet': 'John C. McGinley, whose previous credits include the NBC comedy “Scrubs” and Oliver Stone’s Vietnam War drama “Platoon,” will play the embittered real estate salesman Dave Moss in a Broadway revival of “Glengarry Glen Ross.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The high-testosterone crucible of David Mamet’s “Glengarry Glen Ross” continues to add to its roster of prominent possessors of Y chromosomes for a coming Broadway revival. John C. McGinley, who played the acid-tongued Dr. Perry Cox on the NBC comedy “Scrubs” and the tough-as-nails Sergeant O’Neill in Oliver Stone’s Vietnam War drama, “Platoon,” will play the embittered real estate salesman Dave Moss in the “Glengarry” revival, its producers said on Monday. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'John C. McGinley Joins ‘Glengarry Glen Ross’ Revival on Broadway', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cannavale, Bobby', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pacino, Al', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T17:59:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e28186d3-5645-53af-b2a1-9a8180681e31', 'word_count': 155, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e28186d3-5645-53af-b2a1-9a8180681e31'}, {'abstract': 'U.S. national team coach Jurgen Klinsmann has three distinct possibilities for constructing his roster for a coming match against Mexico at Estadio Azteca on Aug. 15.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/u-s-mexico-preview-part-1-the-a-team/', 'snippet': 'U.S. national team coach Jurgen Klinsmann has three distinct possibilities for constructing his roster for a coming match against Mexico at Estadio Azteca on Aug. 15.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One year ago this week, in his first game as United States national team coach, Jurgen Klinsmann’s squad eked out a 1-1 draw against Mexico at Lincoln Financial Field in Philadelphia. On that night the team started poorly but seemed to draw strength from its optimistic, charismatic leader. Trailing at the half, it clawed back and got a result. All things considered, it was a good showing for both coach and team.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S.-Mexico Preview, Part 1: The A Team', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Cup 2014 (Soccer)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bradley, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dempsey, Clint', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Howard, Tim', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Fabian', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Federation of Association Football (FIFA)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Major League Soccer', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Soccer Federation', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T18:04:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Godfrey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Godfrey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/358f6956-537d-5bc0-ad69-d85296b30f2b', 'word_count': 1010, 'uri': 'nyt://article/358f6956-537d-5bc0-ad69-d85296b30f2b'}, {'abstract': 'The company has announced the creation of a program for six dancers to work as apprentice members of Ballet Theater’s corps de ballet.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/american-ballet-theater-announces-apprentice-program/', 'snippet': 'The company has announced the creation of a program for six dancers to work as apprentice members of Ballet Theater’s corps de ballet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Developing young dancers will be a new focus at American Ballet Theater. The company has announced the creation of a program for six dancers to work as apprentice members of Ballet Theater’s corps de ballet, Kevin McKenzie, the artistic director, said on Monday. The selected dancers will train under Kate Lydon, ABT Studio Company’s artistic associate, and Clinton Luckett, ABT’s Ballet master, for 10 weeks beginning in September, and they will then advance to apprentice level with American Ballet Theater during the company’s performances of “The Nutcracker” at the Brooklyn Academy of Music. According to the announcement, training will provide full immersion into the ballets in ABT’s repertory, including tutorials in ballet story lines, history, style and mime. In addition, dancers will attend seminars in hair and makeup and company etiquette in preparation for entering the main company. The dancers will be selected first from among the students at the Jacqueline Kennedy Onassis School, second from among Summer Intensive students and by audition. Their apprenticeship will continue throughout ABT’s performance season ending July 2013. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'American Ballet Theater Announces Apprentice Program', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McKenzie, Kevin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN BALLET THEATER', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T18:06:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Bloom', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bloom', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb5ec178-0acf-5e2b-afa2-f29921652715', 'word_count': 176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb5ec178-0acf-5e2b-afa2-f29921652715'}, {'abstract': 'A new campaign for the Children’s Defense Fund uses social media to focus on the costs of trimming government-financed initiatives for children.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/business/media/ads-graphically-warn-about-cutting-initiatives-for-children-campaign-spotlight.html', 'snippet': 'A new campaign for the Children’s Defense Fund uses social media to focus on the costs of trimming government-financed initiatives for children.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A new campaign created by the Minneapolis office of Fallon Worldwide for the Children’s Defense Fund, the advocacy group, uses social media to explain the long-term costs of cutting government-financed initiatives for children.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/06/business/media/06adnewsletter-pic2/06adnewsletter-pic2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 784, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/06/business/media/06adnewsletter-pic2/06adnewsletter-pic2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1569, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/06/business/media/06adnewsletter-pic2/06adnewsletter-pic2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/06/business/media/06adnewsletter-pic2/06adnewsletter-pic2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/06/business/media/06adnewsletter-pic2/06adnewsletter-pic2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '3 Ads Warn About Budget Cuts for Children’s Programs', 'kicker': 'Campaign Spotlight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '3 Ads Warn About Budget Cuts for Children&#8217;s Programs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fallon Worldwide', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Children's Defense Fund", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BeCarefulWhatYouCut.com', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T18:07:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane L. Levere', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Levere', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df7a528c-7b7b-594b-9de5-5152068bfb89', 'word_count': 882, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df7a528c-7b7b-594b-9de5-5152068bfb89'}, {'abstract': 'A plant in Oregon aims to use restaurant wastewater to produce electricity and a fuel akin to natural gas.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/sparing-sewers-all-that-restaurant-grease/', 'snippet': 'A plant in Oregon aims to use restaurant wastewater to produce electricity and a fuel akin to natural gas.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every hour of every day, plants across the United States treat the waste that flows through the nation’s sewers. The process is energy-intensive. Wastewater treatment plants often consume more electricity than any other service a city provides — sometimes even as much as 30 to 40 percent of its overall energy consumption, according to the Environmental Protection Agency.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sparing Sewers All That Restaurant Grease', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Light and Power', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sewers and Sewage', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Waste Materials and Disposal', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnston, Alan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Environmental Protection Agency', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Portland (Ore)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southwestern States (US)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T18:08:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sophia Li', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sophia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Li', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7432bf42-1055-55ec-86bf-e88e52c29f20', 'word_count': 667, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7432bf42-1055-55ec-86bf-e88e52c29f20'}, {'abstract': 'After Harry Reid suggested Mitt Romney hadn’t paid taxes in ten years, Republicans took to the fainting couch.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/reckless-and-slanderous/', 'snippet': 'After Harry Reid suggested Mitt Romney hadn’t paid taxes in ten years, Republicans took to the fainting couch.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I won’t defend Senate Majority Leader Harry Reid, who—presumably because he’s not up for reelection—claimed last week that Mitt Romney won’t release his tax returns because he hasn’t paid taxes in ten years. His source: An unnamed Bain insider. That’s improbable and I agree with Senate Minority Leader Mitch McConnell that it’s “beneath the dignity” of Mr. Reid’s office to spread rumors. (If it turns out Mr. Reid is correct, I’ll be the first to apologize.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Reckless and Slanderous’', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_ki