e New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Ego in ‘Raising Successful Children’', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T18:27:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By KJ DELL'ANTONIA", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "DELL'ANTONIA", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a62c2c54-87c3-5781-8834-86fac8abd357', 'word_count': 681, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a62c2c54-87c3-5781-8834-86fac8abd357'}, {'abstract': 'A guitar maker pays a substantial cost for not insuring that its wood imports were legal.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/gibson-guitar-to-pay-350000-in-penalties-and-lose-seized-tropical-hardwood/', 'snippet': 'A guitar maker pays a substantial cost for not insuring that its wood imports were legal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Justice Department has closed its criminal investigation against the Gibson Guitar Company after the company agreed to a series of steps, including payment of $300,000 in penalties and forfeiture of seized rosewood and ebony shipments from India and ebony from Madagascar with an estimated value of more than $419,000.* [3:12 p.m. | Updated | The company may be able to get the Indian wood back, under the terms of the complicated agreement. ]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gibson Guitar To Pay $300,000 in Penalties and Lose Seized Tropical Hardwood', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Guitars', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smuggling', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Madagascar', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-06T18:32:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5cc50312-661f-5400-9699-c0c6604f9305', 'word_count': 644, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5cc50312-661f-5400-9699-c0c6604f9305'}, {'abstract': 'The interior secretary is on his way to Alaska, but Obama administration officials caution that this doesn’t mean that permits are imminent for Shell.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/the-arctic-drilling-countdown/', 'snippet': 'The interior secretary is on his way to Alaska, but Obama administration officials caution that this doesn’t mean that permits are imminent for Shell.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After several weeks of delays linked to mechanical problems and persistent sea ice, Shell is completing its final preparations for drilling in the Arctic Ocean, company officials said Monday. The company hopes over the next several days to demonstrate to regulators that its oil spill containment equipment is ready and to receive clearance from the Environmental Protection Agency to operate its drill ships without running afoul of air pollution laws.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Arctic Drilling Countdown', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Offshore Drilling and Exploration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'n