47d4e-b020-5d45-bd01-116faa118437'}, {'abstract': 'A new book and exhibit recount the proud history and sad fate of the S.S. United States, which for a time was the fastest and most advanced ocean liner ever built.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/06/between-the-devil-and-the-deep-blue-sea/', 'snippet': 'A new book and exhibit recount the proud history and sad fate of the S.S. United States, which for a time was the fastest and most advanced ocean liner ever built.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s some sadness in the story of the S.S. United States, which for a time in the 1950s and 1960s was the fastest and most advanced ocean liner ever built. It was the lifelong project of William Francis Gibbs, America’s preeminent naval architect, a self-taught engineer who, among his many other achievements, was instrumental in transforming a German liner captured during World War I into the Leviathan, the pride of America’s postwar passenger fleet. Then, during World War II, he pioneered a revolutionary process to construct the so-called Liberty Ships, which became symbols of America’s industrial might. All along, Gibbs nursed plans to build a revolutionary superliner. But his designs, hatched in his parents’ Philadelphia attic as early 1916, were not realized until 1952. Ten years after its christening, his transatlantic ship would prowl the icy North Atlantic as a kind of floating anachronism, jet planes having turned its record three-day, 10-hour crossing time into a sorry joke. Such is the sweep of “A Man and His Ship” (Simon & Schuster, $30) an absorbing, transporting new history by Steven Ujifusa, which coincides with a show about the S.S. United States now on view at the Forbes Gallery (through Oct. 20).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Between the Devil and the Deep Blue Sea', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_