ocument_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian X. Chen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': 'X.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3fb9321d-4cc2-5903-8ffd-f66e192c9e0b', 'word_count': 430, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3fb9321d-4cc2-5903-8ffd-f66e192c9e0b'}, {'abstract': 'The word “pain” is in Christophe Morin’s title because his company’s approach is to help marketers look at the frustrations and challenges consumers experience.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/07/the-secret-of-neuromarketing-go-for-the-pain/', 'snippet': 'The word “pain” is in Christophe Morin’s title because his company’s approach is to help marketers look at the frustrations and challenges consumers experience.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We all like to think we make buying decisions on a rational level, but neuroscientists tell us otherwise. While marketers have known this instinctively, brain mappers have shown that the smallest part of the brain, the amygdala, lights up like a Christmas tree when confronted with fight-or-flight or in this case buy-or-fly situations.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Secret of Neuromarketing: Go for the Pain', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fear (Emotion)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-07T11:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By MP Mueller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mp', 'middl