', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/3e8ab652-9de9-5fbc-ba52-c273c49e6188', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/3e8ab652-9de9-5fbc-ba52-c273c49e6188'}, {'abstract': 'Monsoon rains exacerbated by a typhoon have devastated much of the Philippine capital.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/07/on-our-radar-manila-as-waterworld/', 'snippet': 'Monsoon rains exacerbated by a typhoon have devastated much of the Philippine capital.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Deadly torrential rains worsened by a tropical storm submerge over half of the Philippine capital. The storm is now headed for Zhejiang Province in China, where more than 250,000 people have been evacuated ahead of its anticipated landfall late Wednesday. [Reuters]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Our Radar: Manila as Waterworld', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-07T17:57:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e0cfa38-7dc9-5ab8-bd46-54d5578c7946', 'word_count': 211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e0cfa38-7dc9-5ab8-bd46-54d5578c7946'}, {'abstract': 'Audiences come through, as expected, as three productions close.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/07/broadway-shows-closings-are-good-for-business/', 'snippet': 'Audiences come through, as expected, as three productions close.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Three Broadway productions saw expected upticks at the box office last week given that their runs were coming to an end, two newcomers showed mixed results and big-ticket shows that have been dominant for months mostly held steady, according to the weekly grosses and other data released by the productions.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Broadway Shows’ Closings Are Good for Business', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Closings Prove Good For Broadway Business', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martin, Ricky', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tyson, Mike', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-07T17:59:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a64bf11-617a-5947-8cea-238ab3705d16', 'word_count': 353, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a64bf11-617a-5947-8cea-238ab3705d16'}, {'abstract': 'In America, the reindeer is more akin to a mythical creature, like a unicorn. In Inari, the capital, of the Finnish Sami, it is a ubiquitous presence, sometimes found wandering even in the middle of town.', 'web_url': 'https://frugaltraveler.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/07/alongside-rudolphs-ilk-yoiking-in-lapland/', 'snippet': 'In America, the reindeer is more akin to a mythical creature, like a unicorn. In Inari, the capital, of the Finnish Sami, it is a ubiquitous presence, sometimes found wandering even in the middle of town.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nils-Heikki Paltto is far from the only reindeer herder who hates wolverines, loves the solitude of tracking his herd on snowmobile during the dim Arctic winter, and knows how to yoik, a form of singing that falls somewhere between a chant and a yodel. Yoiking is the unique tradition of his Sami people, the indigenous group that populates (minimally) the northern stretches of Norway, Sweden and Finland that we call Lapland.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Alongside Rudolph’s Ilk, Yoiking in Lapland', 'kicker': 'Frugal Traveler', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-07T18:01:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By SETH KUGEL', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Seth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'KUGEL', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3eea4243-c5ba-510b-b0eb-26bf65d7dc03', 'word_count': 1768, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3eea4243-c5ba-510b-b0eb-26bf65d7dc03'}, {'abstract': 'It often seemed as if Robert Hughes never wrote a bad sentence.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/07/the-quotable-robert-hughes/', 'snippet': 'It often seemed as if Robert Hughes never wrote a bad sentence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It often seemed as if Robert Hughes, the critic and historian who died Monday at 74, never wrote a bad sentence. And he wrote many hundreds of thousands of them in a wildly industrious career that stretched over almost a half-century, in books, in the pages of Time magazine and in almost any publication that asked him. Here is a highly subjective selection of some of his best:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Quotable Robert Hughes', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hughes, Robert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-07T18:03:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54d45386-a11b-561f-950e-6dfe80c417ba', 'word_count': 627, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54d45386-a11b-561f-950e-6dfe80c417ba'}, {'abstract': 'The federal agency released data indicating pedestrian fatalities increased 4 percent in 2010, the most recent period for which N.H.T.S.A. had releasable records, relative to 2009 levels.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/07/n-h-t-s-a-to-pedestrians-the-streets-are-getting-meaner/', 'snippet': 'The federal agency released data indicating pedestrian fatalities increased 4 percent in 2010, the most recent period for which N.H.T.S.A. had releasable records, relative to 2009 levels.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Monday, the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration issued an advisory to pedestrians after statistics showed pedestrian fatalities rose by 4 percent in 2010 relative to their levels in 2009. The statistics represented the most recent fatality data available to the federal government as reported by individual states. N.H.T.S.A. expects to release final numbers for 2011 later this year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'N.H.T.S.A. to Pedestrians: The Streets Are Getting Meaner', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Traffic Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Highway Traffic Safety Administration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-07T18:03:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cheryl Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cheryl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cad4a5fb-683b-5ba5-ba3d-e074514904bf', 'word_count': 157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cad4a5fb-683b-5ba5-ba3d-e074514904bf'}, {'abstract': 'A new pizzeria in the East Village, opened by the chef Michael White, offers pizzas as thick as the September issue of Vogue, with an abundance of toppings that would buckle an ordinary pie.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/08/dining/reviews/nicoletta-nyc-restaurant-review.html', 'snippet': 'A new pizzeria in the East Village, opened by the chef Michael White, offers pizzas as thick as the September issue of Vogue, with an abundance of toppings that would buckle an ordinary pie.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN an earlier, more primitive era, chefs with an entrepreneurial urge believed that success began with a delicious product. If you cooked something that enough people wanted to eat, you could build an empire that might one day span the country. First, though, you needed 11 secret herbs and spices. You needed special sauce. You needed Hot Doughnuts Now.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/08/dining/08RESTNICOLETTA_SPAN/08RESTNICOLETTA_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/08/dining/08RESTNICOLETTA_SPAN/08RESTNICOLETTA_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/08/dining/08RESTNICOLETTA_SPAN/08RESTNICOLETTA_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {