12/08/07/kremlin-critic-debugs-office-tweets-about-it/', 'snippet': 'Aleksei Navalny, a blogger and anticorruption crusader in Russia, arrived at work on Monday to find that his office had been bugged.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Monday, Aleksei Navalny, a blogger and anticorruption crusader who is perhaps Russia’s most influential Kremlin critic, arrived at work and with the help of a wiretap detector provided by a colleague discovered that his office was bugged. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kremlin Critic Debugs Office. Tweets About It.', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corruption (Institutional)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wiretapping and Other Eavesdropping Devices and Methods', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Navalny, Aleksei N', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-08T03:40:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Schwirtz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwirtz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f8c9ca2-d1d3-573e-abfa-add0e7628092', 'word_count': 345, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f8c9ca2-d1d3-573e-abfa-add0e7628092'}, {'abstract': 'German security officials are coming under fire for allowing a Tunisian man they believe may have served as a bodyguard to Osama bin Laden in 2000 to live in western Germany for years.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/08/world/europe/germany-criticism-over-man-suspected-as-bin-laden-guard.html', 'snippet': 'German security officials are coming under fire for allowing a Tunisian man they believe may have served as a bodyguard to Osama bin Laden in 2000 to live in western Germany for years.', 'lead_paragraph': ' German security officials are coming under fire for allowing a Tunisian man they believe may have served as a bodyguard to Osama bin Laden in 2000 to live in western Germany for years, despite being considered a “dangerous jihadist” who they say is spreading extremist views. German authorities insist they have done everything legally possible to curtail the activities of the man, identified only as Sami A., in accordance with privacy laws. He has been under observation since 2004 and is restricted to the city of Bochum, where he lives with his German wife and three children and must check in daily with the police, Birgit Axler, a spokeswoman for the Interior Ministry in North Rhine-Westphalia said Tuesday. Reuters quoted German authorities saying they have had trouble deporting him because of his marriage to a German. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Germany: Criticism Over Man Suspected as Bin Laden Guard', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Germany: Criticism Over Man Suspected as Bin Laden Guard', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tunisia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-08T03:42:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Eddy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastna