'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-08T19:25:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanna M. Foster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/96a75069-1849-5242-b9b5-e1b41ef1754a', 'word_count': 559, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96a75069-1849-5242-b9b5-e1b41ef1754a'}, {'abstract': 'In her new book, Breuer offers a thorough look at the journey to building a collection. The Moment gets tips from the fashion insider.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/08/asked-answered-susie-breuer/', 'snippet': 'In her new book, Breuer offers a thorough look at the journey to building a collection. The Moment gets tips from the fashion insider.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I love being behind the scenes,” Susie Breuer insists. “The spotlight isn’t a comfortable place for me.” But after 20 years of quietly working in fashion production for industry magnates like Tommy Hilfiger, Karl Lagerfeld and, currently, Calvin Klein, Breuer is finally getting a center-stage moment with the recent release of her first book, “Blue Is the New Black: the 10 Step Guide to Developing and Producing a Fashion Collection” (BIS Publishers, $45). Dispensing insider tips and personal anecdotes from years in the business, Breuer offers a thorough look at the somewhat arcane journey to building a collection, from conception to high street or catwalk, and the interplay among the many individuals who contribute along the way.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Asked & Answered | Susie Breuer', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-08T19:30:10+0000'