alifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/390d8a2a-290b-5ef5-9be2-fb0fb2f8f219', 'word_count': 374, 'uri': 'nyt://article/390d8a2a-290b-5ef5-9be2-fb0fb2f8f219'}, {'abstract': 'Bright tights, boots and those haunting balaclavas. The Russian punk collective Pussy Riot is known for its political performance art — and its flashy aesthetic.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/08/the-riot-girls-style/', 'snippet': 'Bright tights, boots and those haunting balaclavas. The Russian punk collective Pussy Riot is known for its political performance art — and its flashy aesthetic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It has been a shock to see the bravely smiling faces of three girls from the Russian punk collective Pussy Riot locked in a glass cage in a Moscow courtroom these past two weeks. Ordinarily they are dressed in shots of clashing colors, their faces hidden behind bright balaclavas. Stripped of their costumes and dressed in plain clothes (graphic tee, button-down shirt, day dress), they are unrecognizable. Hearing their names and seeing Nadezhda Tolokonnikova, 22, Maria Alyokhina, 24, and Yekaterina Samutsevich, 29, exposed and vulnerable on national television seems only to emphasize the band’s slogan: “We Are All Pussy Riot.” After all, if women who look like Tolokonnikova, Alyokhina and Samutsevich are Pussy Riot, why can’t I be, too?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Riot Girls’ Style', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Samutsevich, Yekaterina', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tolokonnikova, Nadezhda', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pussy Riot', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Moscow (Russia)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-08T21:30:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivien Goldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3cfa785-c27e-5e92-961d-62e9eb0bb06a', 'word_count': 800, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3cfa785-c27e-5e92-961d-62e9eb0bb06a'}, {'abstract': 'Residents of Nathkuva, next to the hulking coolant gas factory, complain of rashes and birth defects, which they say are the result of pollution from the plant.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/world/asia/indian-village-at-odds-with-gujarat-fluorochemicals-plant.html', 'snippet': 'Residents of Nathkuva, next to the hulking coolant gas factory, complain of rashes and birth defects, which they say are the result of pollution from the plant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NATHKUVA, India — By issuing carbon credits to companies for reducing emissions in the developing world, the United Nations aimed to inject cash into poor communities supporting the growth of sustainable industry. But in this remote village adjacent to Gujarat Fluorochemicals Limited’s coolant gas plant, environmental groups and residents have been at loggerheads with the company over pollution since it opened in 1989.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/09/world/09coolant2/COOLANT-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/09/world/09coolant2/COOLANT-2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In a Factory’s Shadow, Fears About Health', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In a Factory’s Shadow, Fears About Health', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Air Pollution', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birth Defects', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-08T21:31:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisabeth Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4074deaf-c9ef-5679-966c-e360c0cb4302', 'word_count': 631, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4074deaf-c9ef-5679-966c-e360c0cb4302'}, {'abstract': 'Ben Macintyre’s “Double Cross” and Stephen Talty’s “Agent Garbo” both tell the histories of the double agents who were able to trick their German contacts about the Allied invasion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/books/double-cross-and-agent-garbo.html', 'snippet': 'Ben Macintyre’s “Double Cross” and Stephen Talty’s “Agent Garbo” both tell the histories of the double agents who were able to trick their German contacts about the Allied invasion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ben Macintyre is the reigning champ when it comes to attention-grabbing World War II spy stories that are stranger than fiction. Each of his two most recent books, “Agent Zigzag” and “Operation Mincemeat,” has been suspensefully written and studded with exotic details. And each has been narrowly particular. “Agent Zigzag” focused on Eddie Chapman, the onetime thief who turned his duplicity toward deceiving Germany on behalf of the Allied war effort. The more amazing “Operation Mincemeat” outlined the elaborate use of a floating corpse to spread disinformation. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/09/arts/jpbook1/jpbook1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 922, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/09/arts/jpbook1/jpbook1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1843, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/09/arts/jpbook1/jpbook1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/09/arts/jpbook1/jpbook1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/09/arts/jpbook1/jpbook1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Agents Who Fooled the Nazis About D-Day', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Agents Who Fooled The Nazis About D-Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, '