tion': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/23d23229-1c77-5185-8d15-a444727930cd', 'word_count': 1180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23d23229-1c77-5185-8d15-a444727930cd'}, {'abstract': 'Alt-J played selections from its first album on Tuesday at Glasslands Gallery in Williamsburg, Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/arts/music/alt-j-from-england-makes-its-brooklyn-debut-at-glasslands.html', 'snippet': 'Alt-J played selections from its first album on Tuesday at Glasslands Gallery in Williamsburg, Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the stroke of midnight on Tuesday night Alt-J was at Glasslands Gallery in Williamsburg, Brooklyn, playing “Handmade,” the secret track from “An Awesome Wave,” its new full-length debut. A simmering hymn of one-way infatuation, it boiled down to a duologue between\xa0Joe Newman, the band’s frontman, and Gus Unger-Hamilton, its keyboardist. “Legions upon legions of craftsmen handmade my feelings,” they harmonized with studied imprecision, before reaching a moony refrain: “For yoooouuu.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/09/arts/altj/altj-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/09/arts/altj/altj-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/09/arts/altj/altj-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/09/arts/altj/altj-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1312, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/09/arts/altj/altj-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/09/arts/altj/altj-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/09/arts/altj/altj-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'They ‘Tessellate,’ and Dabble in Both the Sinister and the Naughty', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'They ‘Tessellate,’ and Dabble in Both the Sinister and the Naughty', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Alt-J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-08T22:14:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d008fb7f-94e4-569c-96f0-d4f3fa135679', 'word_count': 518, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d008fb7f-94e4-569c-96f0-d4f3fa135679'}, {'abstract': 'The Rebecca School in Manhattan, which serves developmentally disabled children, incorporated the London Olympics into its summer curriculum as a way to engage its older students.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/08/with-school-gym-as-olympic-stadium-autistic-students-chase-goals/', 'snippet': 'The Rebecca School in Manhattan, which serves developmentally disabled children, incorporated the London Olympics into its summer curriculum as a way to engage its older students.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In London, thousands gathered on Wednesday to watch as two Jamaican track stars, Usain Bolt and Johan Blake, limbered up for the 200-meter sprint. At the Rebecca School for developmentally disabled children in Manhattan, however, Sam Wilkinson had eyes for only one race: the one between his team, East Side Story, and its rival, the Subway Stars.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'With School Gym as Olympic Stadium, Autistic Students Chase Goals', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Autism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-08T22:16:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivian Yee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5001b80-67e7-5528-ad90-e9e6f7708048', 'word_count': 671, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5001b80-67e7-5528-ad90-e9e6f7708048'}, {'abstract': 'A modular wool carpet made from 14 pieces and patterns.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/garden/droogs-new-all-in-one-carpet.html', 'snippet': 'A modular wool carpet made from 14 pieces and patterns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For home dwellers who stress over finding the perfect size and shape for an area rug, the Dutch design company Droog has an answer: All in One, a reconfigurable wool carpet made from 14 different pieces covering about 388 square feet. “If you have a big room, you can use them all together as one piece,” said Renny Ramakers, director of Droog. Or you can split them up for different rooms.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'sou