ve Mr. Romney a five-point lead there, with 50 percent of the vote, to President Obama’s 45 percent.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Aug. 8: Colorado and Virginia Flip Places in Forecast', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-08T23:56:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60bae387-3a6b-5821-87b8-8a0ef73295b6', 'word_count': 927, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60bae387-3a6b-5821-87b8-8a0ef73295b6'}, {'abstract': 'A bandanna has the wherewithal to take on the most overheated head, neck and whatever else needs a mop. Loosely folded and tucked into your back pocket, the bandanna was born to be abused.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/fashion/in-city-heat-bandannas-to-the-rescuetrading-up.html', 'snippet': 'A bandanna has the wherewithal to take on the most overheated head, neck and whatever else needs a mop. Loosely folded and tucked into your back pocket, the bandanna was born to be abused.', 'lead_paragraph': 'YOU can’t help but wonder if the late Richard Carlson of San Francisco would have chosen “Don’t Sweat the Small Stuff” as the title of his motivational best seller if he had lived on the East Coast instead of the West. Surely the only self-help advice for this cruel summer in New York is “Oh, Just Go Ahead and Sweat Everything.” ', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/09/fashion/20120809_TRADINGUP-slide-M