s on Facebook.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the influx of outside spending on campaign ads this year in many states, it can seem as if TV is wall-to-wall political advertising, but in Hawaii, Adam Nagourney reports, the Republican candidate for United States Senate is turning that vision into reality. Linda Lingle, a former two-term governor, pays $2,500 a week for channel 110 on the digital cable dial for LL12, where she airs speeches, her own ads and endorsements and video issue papers in 10 of the languages spoke on the islands. The channel, while a relatively high number, is ensconced between CNN Headline News and Fox News, so presumably gets a few politically interested channel surfers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: At Last, Political Ads 24/7; and a Firing at Oxford American', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-09T13:06:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5be28c02-b34a-5410-a31b-48c60ba01085', 'word_count': 507, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5be28c02-b34a-5410-a31b-48c60ba01085'}, {'abstract': 'The Day: Many New Yorkers will recognize parts of the new documentary “You’ve Been Trumped,” which takes a dim view of Donald J. Trump for seeking to build golf courses on a delicate stretch of the Scottish coast.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/a-familiar-tale-of-trump-and-real-estate-this-time-in-scotland/', 'snippet': 'The Day: Many New Yorkers will recognize parts of the new documentary “You’ve Been Trumped,” which takes a dim view of Donald J. Trump for seeking to build golf courses on a delicate stretch of the Scottish coast.', 'lead_paragraph': ' With so many famous people calling New York home, it is not surprising that some of them catch the attention of documentary makers. Indeed, one of our better-known citizens is the main figure in a film that opened in Manhattan a few days ago. He happens not to be one of our more beloved citizens: Donald J. Trump. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Familiar Tale of Trump and Real Estate, This Time in Scotland', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baxter, Anthony', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Scotland', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-09T13:08:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1392085-81f2-561a-861e-622b7da2c097', 'word_count': 798, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1392085-81f2-561a-861e-622b7da2c097'}, {'abstract': 'Marvin Hamlisch, who won Emmy, Grammy, Tony and Academy Awards as well as the Pulitzer Prize, will be remembered at public visitations in New York on Sunday and Monday, and at a funeral service on Tuesday.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/funeral-services-planned-for-marvin-hamlisch/', 'snippet': 'Marvin Hamlisch, who won Emmy, Grammy, Tony and Academy Awards as well as the Pulitzer Prize, will be remembered at public visitations in New York on Sunday and Monday, and at a funeral service on Tuesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The life of Marvin Hamlisch, the highly decorated composer of “A Chorus Line” and “The Way We Were,” among many other works, will be commemorated at visitations and a funeral service, his press representatives said on Thursday. Public visitations will be held at the Frank E. Campbell Funeral Chapel at 1076 Madison Avenue, at 81st Street, in Manhattan, on Sunday between 2 p.m. and 5 p.m., and between 7 p.m. and 9 p.m., and on Monday during those same hours. The funeral, which is also open to the public, will be held on Tuesday 11 a.m. at Temple Emanu-El at 65th Street and 5th Avenue in Manhattan.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Funeral Services Planned for Marvin Hamlisch', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Funeral Services Set for Marvin Hamlisch', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Death and Dying', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emmy Awards', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Funerals and Memorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Grammy Awards', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hamlisch, Marvin', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Temple Emanu-el', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-09T13:15:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f33d874-4060-52e1-ab5c-085b97115ada', 'word_count': 140, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f33d874-4060-52e1-ab5c-085b97115ada'}, {'abstract': 'When oil was both dwindling and polluting, a convergence of economic and environmental common sense helped environmentalists win mainstream converts. Now, new energy sources threaten to pit the greens and the blue-collar against one another.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/when-the-choice-is-jobs-or-the-environment/', 'snippet': 'When oil was both dwindling and polluting, a convergence of economic and environmental common sense helped environmentalists win mainstream converts. Now, new energy sources threaten to pit the greens and the blue-collar against one another.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the past couple of decades, economic common sense and fears about climate change have coincided. Relying on fossil fuels was a mistake, both because we were running out of them and because of the impact of greenhouse gases on the environment.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When the Choice Is Jobs or the Environment', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-09T13:16:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chrystia Freeland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chrystia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Freeland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a70ea42-d62d-500d-aaa0-f6e052b6cf12', 'word_count': 302, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a70ea42-d62d-500d-aaa0-f6e052b6cf12'}, {'abstract': 'Seldom do plug-and-play devices live up to the name, but the xPrintServer, a $100 device that links iPhones and iPads wirelessly to a printer, may be the exception. Maybe.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/printing-from-your-iphone-at-a-price/', 'snippet': 'Seldom do plug-and-play devices live up to the name, but the xPrintServer, a $100 device that links iPhones and iPads wirelessly to a printer, may be the exception. Maybe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Seldom do plug-and-play devices live up to the name. It’s usually more like, plug, fail, reread instructions, curse, hold for tech assistance, fail, curse more, Google until you find an answer, play, try to soothe frazzled nerves.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Printing From Your iPhone, at a Price', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-09T13:21:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roy Furchgott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Furchgott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/497159ec-831e-5358-8e64-aba177fa3cf8', 'word_count': 366, 'uri': 'nyt://article/497159ec-831e-5358-8e64-aba177fa3cf8'}, {'abstract': 'These days it’s the government, not media conglomerates, that has the true monopoly on information in Argentina.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/argentinas-government-has-a-monopoly-on-information/', 'snippet': 'These days it’s the government, not media conglomerates, that has the true monopoly on information in Argentina.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BUENOS AIRES — Last week, Argentina’s small but active Twitter scene became transfixed with more than three dozen messages sent out by Juan Cruz Sanz, a young journalist at Clarín, the country’s largest circulation daily. Sanz’s short missives recounted how high-level officials pressured C5N — a cable TV news station recently bought by an executive close to the government — to renege on an offer to hire him as a panelist on a late-night program. The spokesman of President Cristina Fernández de Kirchner and her chief of cabinet allegedly made calls to C5N managers. (A manager at the channel later denied the allegations, also on Twitter.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'All the President’s Media', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kirchner, Cristina Fernandez de', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARGENTINA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-09T13:27:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Politi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Politi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/38fcf109-3189-556f-bb7a-f6d8b55d2522', 'word_count': 465, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38fcf109-3189-556f-bb7a-f6d8b55d2522'}, {'abstract': 'Pollutants from a coolant gas plant have damaging affect on families in a Gujarat village.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/in-a-gujarat-factorys-shadow-fears-about-health/', 'snippet': 'Pollutants from a coolant gas plant have damaging affect on families in a Gujarat village.', 'lead_paragraph': '“By issuing carbon credits to companies for reducing emissions in the developing world, the United Nations aimed to inject cash into poor communities supporting the growth of sustainable industry,” Elisabeth Rosenthal wrote in The New York Times. But in the remote village of Nathkuva adjacent to Gujarat Fluorochemicals Limited’s coolant gas plant, “environmental groups and residents have been at loggerheads with the company over pollution since it opened in 1989.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In a Factory’s Shadow, Fears About Health', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-09T13:27:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/57ccbf87-e686-52dc-9cc8-5a170f9c2f63', 'word_count': 234, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57ccbf87-e686-52dc-9cc8-5a170f9c2f63'}, {'abstract': 'Taxing Olympic medalists, an e-mail service with lots of smarts, a favorable clinical trial for new prenatal gene test and other consumer-focused news from Thursday’s Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/thursday-reading-the-financial-problems-of-parents-of-olympians/', 'snippet': 'Taxing Olympic medalists, an e-mail service with lots of smarts, a favorable clinical trial for new prenatal gene test and other consumer-focused news from Thursday’s Times.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Thursday Reading: The Financial Problems of Parents of Olympians', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-09T13:34:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Siegel Bernard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': 'Siegel', 'lastname': 'Bernard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d2af591-fe0a-52d1-9cec-1ef9ccfcf912', 'word_count': 188, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d2af591-fe0a-52d1-9cec-1ef9ccfcf912'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/what-were-reading-497/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jezebel: New Coke, Coke Zero and Diet Coke Plus have all been consigned to the recycling bin of history. But 30 years after its birth, regular old Diet Coke remains “the world’s liquid crack of choice.” Why? One theory holds that its mix of low-calorie cred and suspect chemicals allows conscientious people an acceptable way to be bad. — Patrick Farrell ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-09T13:36:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/36f16fe3-8626-5da3-a7b1-4b4153163c24', 'word_count': 504, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36f16fe3-8626-5da3-a7b1-4b4153163c24'}, {'abstract': 'A leading forum for conservative thought likes the Wisconsin congressman as a vice-presidential pick.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/wall-street-journal-editorial-praises-ryan-for-no-2-spot/', 'snippet': 'A leading forum for conservative thought likes the Wisconsin congressman as a vice-presidential pick.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Wall Street Journal editorial page, a leading forum of conservative thought, had high praise Thursday for Representative Paul D.Ryan as Mitt Romney’s possible running mate, citing his determination to curb entitlement spending. The editorial said that Mr. Ryan, the chairman of the House Budget Committee, had taken on entitlement spending “against the advice of every Beltway bed-wetter” concerned about the implications of such a cause.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wall Street Journal Editorial Praises Ryan for No. 2 Spot', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George W', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Portman, Rob', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Paul D Jr', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wall Street Journal', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-09T13:36:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gerry Mullany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gerry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mullany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/653b61c5-3d36-50a2-8fe8-89c3522641d4', 'word_count': 348, 'uri': 'nyt://article/653b61c5-3d36-50a2-8fe8-89c3522641d4'}, {'abstract': 'The long buildup to the selection of a running mate serves a great many ancillary agendas and interests.', 'web_url': 'https://bruni.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/the-veep-cometh/', 'snippet': 'The long buildup to the selection of a running mate serves a great many ancillary agendas and interests.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week we went from wading through Republican veep speculation to swimming in it. Mitt, I beg of you: announce your choice today or tomorrow, before we drown.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Veep Cometh!', 'kicker': 'Frank Bruni', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-09T13:44:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By FRANK BRUNI', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'BRUNI', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9eac14e-6271-5b24-8931-a130c6f2d3f4', 'word_count': 1231, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9eac14e-6271-5b24-8931-a130c6f2d3f4'}, {'abstract': 'Metropolitan Diary: A woman in a stretchy dress is saved from a major malfunction on the train, but heaven help the man who saves her.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/a-good-deed-goes-unthanked/', 'snippet': 'Metropolitan Diary: A woman in a stretchy dress is saved from a major malfunction on the train, but heaven help the man who saves her.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dear Diary:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Good Deed Goes Unthanked', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Metropolitan Ave', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customs, Etiquette and Manners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dresses', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Long Island Rail Road Co', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-09T13:45:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Russell T. Middleton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Russell', 'middlename': 'T.', 'lastname': 'Middleton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/757041cd-81a8-542e-8c0f-721474851f00', 'word_count': 249, 'uri': 'nyt://article/757041cd-81a8-542e-8c0f-721474851f00'}, {'abstract': 'California’s air regulators have announced a tool for showing which plants release the most greenhouse gases, suggesting how data visualization could one day greatly inform public policy.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/the-power-of-images-california-maps-greenhouse-gases/', 'snippet': 'California’s air regulators have announced a tool for showing which plants release the most greenhouse gases, suggesting how data visualization could one day greatly inform public policy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the most significant political problems facing campaigns against air pollution these days is this: by and large, you can’t see it. You can track its molecules, watch emergency room admissions go up and down as it waxes and wanes and estimate the number of lives shortened by it. But none of that provides the jolt of, say, a picture of a tornado’s path or a river on fire.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Power of Images: California Maps Greenhouse Gases', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gore, Al', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'California Air Resources Board', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-09T13:45:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicity Barringer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicity', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barringer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/40421ffa-9acf-5e7f-b76f-2432ec127d9b', 'word_count': 623, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40421ffa-9acf-5e7f-b76f-2432ec127d9b'}, {'abstract': 'The surviving members of the group say they will open the tour in California in November and conclude in New York in December.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/monkees-despite-death-of-davy-jones-say-they-will-go-on-tour/', 'snippet': 'The surviving members of the group say they will open the tour in California in November and conclude in New York in December.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The three surviving members of the Monkees say they will go on tour in November, minus the charismatic Davy Jones, who died in February.\xa0\xa0Michael Nesmith, Micky Dolenz and Peter Tork announced that they would start a 12-date American tour in Escondido, Calif., on Nov. 8, hitting several major markets before wrapping up with a concert in New York on Dec. 2.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monkees, Despite Death of Davy Jones, Say They Will Go on Tour', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Surviving Monkees To Go on Tour', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dolenz, Micky', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jones, Davy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nesmith, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tork, Peter', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MONKEES, THE', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-09T13:45:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2aba7e0-6d64-5256-8adc-ddfea0ee2781', 'word_count': 131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2aba7e0-6d64-5256-8adc-ddfea0ee2781'}, {'abstract': 'It’s probably fair to say that few governmental bodies have had a more troubled childhood than the Privacy and Civil Liberties Oversight Board.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/the-troubled-life-of-the-privacy-and-civil-liberties-oversight-board/', 'snippet': 'It’s probably fair to say that few governmental bodies have had a more troubled childhood than the Privacy and Civil Liberties Oversight Board.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Back in 2003, the national commission on the Sept. 11 attacks advised that as the country bulked up its defenses against terrorism, the watchers themselves would require watching. Congress heeded the warning and created the Privacy and Civil Liberties Oversight Board in 2004 to make certain more aggressive intelligence collection did not unduly infringe on Americans’ rights.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Troubled Life of the Privacy and Civil Liberties Oversight Board', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil Rights and Liberties', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Commission on Terrorist Attacks upon the United States', 'rank': 4,