, 'lead_paragraph': 'Alongside the presidential campaign we all know—centered at the moment on a debate between “Romney Hood” and “Obamaloney”—the candidates are waging a shadow war in Spanish, more closely focused on the issues politicians assume are particularly important to Latino voters. The newest foray is a dishonest\xa0Spanish-language\xa0ad running in Nevada, which attacks President Obama for breaking a campaign promise to pass immigration reform, and for deporting too many illegal immigrants.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Spanish Campaign', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dream Act (Development, Relief and Education for Minors Act)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-09T19:07:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lawrence Downes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lawrence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Downes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b4db3b5-6d5b-5aed-98a5-2a04ad2bee9f', 'word_count': 279, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b4db3b5-6d5b-5aed-98a5-2a04ad2bee9f'}, {'abstract': 'Advice from the Mail Lady of Beam Camp in New Hampshire: There are letters kids love to receive at camp, and then there are these.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/how-not-to-write-to-your-child-at-camp/', 'snippet': 'Advice from the Mail Lady of Beam Camp in New Hampshire: There are letters kids love to receive at camp, and then there are these.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is the season when rural mail carriers’ backs start breaking from the additional weight destined for the summer camps along their route. In my capacity as the Mail Lady of Beam Camp in Strafford, N.H., I see the impact these letters have on their intended recipients. Some bring joy. Others … well, some of the mail circumstances force me to deliver makes me feel fortunate that my job prevents me from subjecting the two campers I spawned to the same blunders, well meaning though they may be.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Not to Write to Your Child at Camp', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Camps and Camping', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-09T19:09:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ayun Halliday', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ayun', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Halliday', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/469ea4df-bfe7-5af5-8d59-33404b9ab3ad', 'word_count': 805, 'uri': 'nyt://article/469ea4df-bfe7-5af5-8d59-33404b9ab3ad'}, {'abstract': 'Taking 51 samples from soup sold in American cities, researchers identify eight different shark species. None of the soups were illegally served.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/in-shark-fin-soup-a-dash-of-biodiversity/', 'snippet': 'Taking 51 samples from soup sold in American cities, researchers identify eight different shark species. None of the soups were illegally served.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A warm, brothy bowl of fibrous shark fin cartilage may not appeal to many palates, but every culture has its delicacies. Think of crunchy fried tarantulas in Cambodia, or a plate of garlicky snails in France.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In the Soup, A Dash of Biodiversity', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-09T19:11:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kelly Slivka', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kelly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Slivka', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4851cbd6-8a63-5d7d-b168-7268c82a74c6', 'word_count': 556, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4851cbd6-8a63-5d7d-b168-7268c82a74c6'}, {'abstract': 'The author Cheryl Strayed traced a grief-stricken hike of the Pacific Crest Trail in “Wild.” Here, she talks about hiking the trail again with her family.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/12/travel/cheryl-strayed-author-of-wild-on-returning-to-her-trail-of-tears.html', 'snippet': 'The author Cheryl Strayed traced a grief-stricken hike of the Pacific Crest Trail in “Wild.” Here, she talks about hiking the trail again with her family.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN 1995, after her mother’s death and her own divorce, Cheryl Strayed decided to hike the mountainous Pacific Crest Trail, which runs from the Mojave Desert through California and Oregon to Washington State, alone. Oh, and she had no experience in distance hiking.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/travel/12QNA/12QNA-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'h