'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evelyn M. Rusli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evelyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/17e63625-5ae0-5866-a7af-3bbc54f7e391', 'word_count': 312, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17e63625-5ae0-5866-a7af-3bbc54f7e391'}, {'abstract': 'A college admission officer in the United States explains the types of extracurricular activities that American students often include on their college applications.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/extracurricular-activities/', 'snippet': 'A college admission officer in the United States explains the types of extracurricular activities that American students often include on their college applications.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Students interested in attending an American university should know that admissions officers will base their decisions on more than just good grades and high test scores. They want to know how a student might enhance the university just by being there. A good indicator is an examination of personal interests, often illustrated by extracurricular activities.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For U.S. College Applicants, Extra Activities Are Encouraged', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADMISSIONS STANDARDS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Students (in US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Internships', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organizations, Societies and Clubs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Incorporated', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Modern School', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Vassar College', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-09T23:30:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noor Brara', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brara', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e66ccd0-59e7-5b1a-9815-cbbd8a9cfc49', 'word_count': 885, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e66ccd0-59e7-5b1a-9815-cbbd8a9cfc49'}, {'abstract': 'After watching three hours of dressage, a dance critic concludes that while the ballet-for-horses description is apt, there are still questions perhaps only a horse can answer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/10/sports/olympics/dressage-is-not-quite-swan-lake-but-its-surprisingly-close.html', 'snippet': 'After watching three hours of dressage, a dance critic concludes that while the ballet-for-horses description is apt, there are still questions perhaps only a horse can answer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’re not a horse person, dressage is an odd event indeed. Horse and rider carry out carefully choreographed movements, often to music, in a formal manner, with their performance evaluated on tiny points of precision, line, apparent ease of execution and musicality. Then again, if you’re not a dance person, you could say almost exactly the same thing about ballet — a strange ritual involving carefully choreographed movements, often to music, in a formal manner, with performance evaluated on tiny points of precision, apparent ease of execution and musicality.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page'