': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Whaley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Whaley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0998d373-8502-5da5-9760-f57383be2d31', 'word_count': 885, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0998d373-8502-5da5-9760-f57383be2d31'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Teixeira and Eric Chavez hit solo home runs in the eighth inning as the Yankees rallied past Detroit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/10/sports/baseball/mlb-baseball-roundup.html', 'snippet': 'Mark Teixeira and Eric Chavez hit solo home runs in the eighth inning as the Yankees rallied past Detroit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DETROIT — Frustrations boiled over in dramatic fashion Thursday as Yankees Manager Joe Girardi — veins bulging in his neck, and arms gyrating over his head — screamed and stomped and threw his hat, gesturing emphatically to the crowd as he stormed off the field.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/10/sports/base/base-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 643, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/10/sports/base/base-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1942, 'width': 1220, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Girardi Loses His Cool, but Yankees Win the Game', 'kicker': 'Roundup', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Girardi Loses His Cool, but Yankees Win the Game', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Girardi, Joe', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Detroit Tigers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-09T23:55:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Waldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15983091-3510-58a5-9de2-a69629fb05e0', 'word_count': 796, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15983091-3510-58a5-9de2-a69629fb05e0'}, {'abstract': 'Peter Spens, a London artist, worked on impressionistic scenes of the beach volleyball action at Horse Guards Parade for eight to 10 hours a day.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/10/sports/olympics/painter-peter-spens-captures-beach-volleyball-at-the-olympics.html', 'snippet': 'Peter Spens, a London artist, worked on impressionistic scenes of the beach volleyball action at Horse Guards Parade for eight to 10 hours a day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Pop music blared as women in bikinis danced around in celebration and drunken fans continued chanting. But Peter Spens was focused on his selection of brush, color and perspective. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/10/sports/olympics/10painter-photo/10painter-photo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 754, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/10/sports/olympics/10painter-photo/10painter-photo-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1509, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A ‘Painting Marathon’ at Beach Volleyball', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A ‘Painting Marathon’ At Beach Volleyball', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beach Volleyball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spens, Peter', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'England', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-09T23:57:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mary Pilon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pilon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f187e38c-3371-5297-aa9d-95bdb5a9b812', 'word_count': 549, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f187e38c-3371-5297-aa9d-95bdb5a9b812'}, {'abstract': 'Released in France in 1971, “Max et les Ferrailleurs,” by Claude Sautet, is receiving a belated American opening in the Film Society of Lincoln Center’s retrospective of his work.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/10/movies/claude-sautets-max-et-les-ferrailleurs.html', 'snippet': 'Released in France in 1971, “Max et les Ferrailleurs,” by Claude Sautet, is receiving a belated American opening in the Film Society of Lincoln Center’s retrospective of his work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the late ’60s and early ’70s, on television and at the movies, the streets were full of complicated cops: big-city detectives whose confusion, frustration and unorthodox methods reflected a general crisis of authority in the societies they served. Popeye Doyle, Dirty Harry Callahan and Frank Serpico (at least as imagined by Al Pacino and Sidney Lumet) made common existential cause with small-screen counterparts like Kojak and Columbo. Max, the sad, obsessive Parisian flic at the heart of Claude Sautet’s “Max et les Ferrailleurs,” is an especially memorable addition to this international brotherhood of disaffected policemen, even if his contributions have, until now, been largely forgotten.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/10/arts/10MAX_SPAN/10MAX_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 366, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/10/arts/10MAX_SPAN/10MAX_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 366}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/10/arts/10MAX_SPAN/0810MAX-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 625, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/10/arts/10MAX_SPAN/0810MAX-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1250, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/10/arts/10MAX_SPAN/10MAX_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/10/arts/10MAX_SPAN/10MAX_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/10/arts/10MAX_SPAN/10MAX_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For a Zealous Cop and a Gang of Two-Bit Thieves, Crime Does Not Pay', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For a Zealous Cop and a Gang of Two-Bit Thieves, Crime Does Not Pay', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sautet, Claude', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Max et les ferrailleurs (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-09T23:59:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/403614f5-29d2-5429-9867-b116da931c39', 'word_count': 811, 'uri': 'nyt://article/403614f5-29d2-5429-9867-b116da931c39'}, {'abstract': 'Why don’t we give up our convictions when equally knowledgeable and rational people disagree with them? The author features responses from readers.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/political-disagreement-revisited/', 'snippet': 'Why don’t we give up our convictions when equally knowledgeable and rational people disagree with them? The author features responses from readers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was delighted by both the number and the quality of responses to my post last week about political disagreement.\xa0 Here I’ll try to rethink the issue in light of the many good ideas put forward by readers.\xa0 (And apologies to all those whose excellent comments I haven’t been able to mention here.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Political Disagreement, Revisited', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T00:00:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Gutting', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gutting', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/afde00e9-6780-585e-bc7a-ba0cef874def', 'word_count': 1082, 'uri': 'nyt://article/afde00e9-6780-585e-bc7a-ba0cef874def'}, {'abstract': 'Gary Woodland, one of the PGA Tour’s longest hitters, used his length to his advantage and posted 5-under 67 in the first round of the P.G.A. Championship, putting him in a four-way tie for second place.', 'web_url': 'https://onpar.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/09/big-hitting-woodland-looks-comfortable-on-ocean-course/', 'snippet': 'Gary Woodland, one of the PGA Tour’s longest hitters, used his length to his advantage and posted 5-under 67 in the first round of the P.G.A. Championship, putting him in a four-way tie for second place.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KIAWAH ISLAND, S.C. — Gary Woodland, one of the PGA Tour’s longest hitters, used his length to his advantage and posted 5-under 67 in the first round of the P.G.A. Championship. That score put him in a four-way tie for second place, one shot behind Carl Pettersson.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Big-Hitting Woodland Looks Comfortable on Ocean Course', 'kicker': 'On Par', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'PGA Championship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woodland, Gary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T00:03:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Schupak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schupak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/29662bb5-b141-5fe2-90b2-4505fe51f831', 'word_count': 504, 'uri': 'nyt://article/29662bb5-b141-5fe2-90b2-4505fe51f831'}, {'abstract': 'A Moscow court on Thursday ordered Eduard Limonov, a political opposition leader, to pay a $380 fine for organizing an unsanctioned demonstration.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/10/world/europe/russia-political-activist-fined-under-new-law.html', 'snippet': 'A Moscow court on Thursday ordered Eduard Limonov, a political opposition leader, to pay a $380 fine for organizing an unsanctioned demonstration.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A Moscow court on Thursday ordered a political opposition leader to pay a $380 fine for organizing an unsanctioned demonstration. It is one of the first punishments to be levied under a tough law adopted in June to clamp down on street protests. The activist, Eduard Limonov, who organized a protest without a permit in July to support the freedom of assembly, had faced the possibility of an even larger fine under the new guidelines. Mr. Limonov said he already owed nearly $17,000 from previous verdicts. “I earn less and less every day,” he said. “So I will not pay the $380 just like I did not pay the $16,880.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Russia: Activist Fined Under New Law', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Russia: Activist Fined Under New Law', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Limonov, Eduard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fines (Penalties)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Assembly', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T00:12:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ilya Mouzykantskii', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ilya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mouzykantskii', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a92f8f9-0dc9-52d6-8fe2-449595faa089', 'word_count': 111, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a92f8f9-0dc9-52d6-8fe2-449595faa089'}, {'abstract': 'Sandra Ávila Beltrán, the first woman to be charged as the leader of an international drug-smuggling operation, faces cocaine-smuggling charges filed in a United States District Court in Florida.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/10/world/americas/mexico-drug-suspect-extradited-to-us.html', 'snippet': 'Sandra Ávila Beltrán, the first woman to be charged as the leader of an international drug-smuggling operation, faces cocaine-smuggling charges filed in a United States District Court in Florida.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Sandra Ávila Beltrán, who is accused of being a drug trafficker and is known as the Queen of the Pacific, was extradited to the United States on Thursday to face cocaine-smuggling charges filed in a United States District Court in Florida. In 2007, Ms. Ávila’s arrest in Mexico City on charges stemming from the 2001 seizure of nine tons of cocaine from a boat in the Mexican port of Manzanillo caused a stir because she was the first woman to be charged as the leader of an international drug-smuggling operation. She was acquitted of the Mexican charges at the end of 2010 and had been fighting extradition. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mexico: Drug Suspect Extradited to U.S.', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | The Americas', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mexico: Drug Suspect Extradited to U.S.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Avila Beltran, Sandra', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smuggling', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Extradition', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T00:14:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisabeth Malkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Malkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc8a5fd0-f3fd-5ef5-8a60-c1820b4651bf', 'word_count': 109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc8a5fd0-f3fd-5ef5-8a60-c1820b4651bf'}, {'abstract': 'Representing the Olympics’ stab at a youth market more in tune with the X Games, the BMX event displayed an odd mix of bicycle pileups, D.J.’s and ruthlessness.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/10/sports/olympics/at-bmx-races-little-camaraderie-but-plenty-of-crashes.html', 'snippet': 'Representing the Olympics’ stab at a youth market more in tune with the X Games, the BMX event displayed an odd mix of bicycle pileups, D.J.’s and ruthlessness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Given the carnage on display Thursday, it is safe to assume that whoever designed the BMX racetrack really dislikes BMX racers. Or has no regard for their safety. Or kind of enjoys watching them crash.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': 