tion/columbia-program-merges-therapy-and-spirituality.html', 'snippet': 'A concentration at the university brings concepts like love and connection to the healing process for therapy clients.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The psychology graduate student ran a wooden stick across the edge of a Tibetan Buddhist singing bowl on Tuesday and asked the five homeless young men sitting in front of him to listen to the undulating sound, and to raise their hands when they could no longer hear it. One by one hands went up, until well after the sound had seemed to dissipate.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/10/nyregion/MINDFUL/MINDFUL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/10/nyregion/MINDFUL/MINDFUL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Merging Spirituality and Clinical Psychology at Columbia', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Merging Spirituality and Clinical Psychology at Columbia', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Therapy and Rehabilitation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Meditation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Columbia University', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T01:56:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sharon Otterman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sharon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Otterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/73b728b7-3803-5bb3-aae4-867471a0869b', 'word_count': 1008, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73b728b7-3803-5bb3-aae4-867471a0869b'}, {'abstract': 'Erin Leddy’s “My Mind Is Like an Open Meadow” is a meditation on the dignity of aging, the fragility of memory, and the inescapability of our own mortality.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/10/theater/reviews/my-mind-is-like-an-open-meadow-at-59e59.html', 'snippet': 'Erin Leddy’s “My Mind Is Like an Open Meadow” is a meditation on the dignity of aging, the fragility of memory, and the inescapability of our own mortality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2001 Erin Leddy lived with her grandmother Sarah Braveman and recorded some 20 hours of interviews about the older woman’s life, philosophy and physical deterioration. In “My Mind Is Like an Open Meadow,” her hourlong solo piece, Ms. Leddy replays and interacts with the remixed audio, which streams from speakers on and around the stage. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/10/arts/10MYMIND_SPAN/10MYMIND_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/10/arts/10MYMIND_SPAN/10MYMIND_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/10/arts/10MYMIND_SPAN/0810MYMINDsub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 650, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type'