dential pick, whoever that is.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — They say they have no idea whom they will be working for, but just as soon as Mitt Romney announces a running mate, 10 full-time staff members are ready to jump in to work for the No. 2. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Campaign Staff Awaits the Unnamed No. 2', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fehrnstrom, Eric', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stewart, David', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican National Committee', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Boston (Mass)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Carolina', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ohio', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Virginia', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T15:57:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Trip Gabriel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Trip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gabriel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/550b183e-d413-5acb-b5de-431748ff2031', 'word_count': 321, 'uri': 'nyt://article/550b183e-d413-5acb-b5de-431748ff2031'}, {'abstract': 'Michael J. Casey blames a “vast global financial system” for our economic malaise. Daniel Gross sees a brighter future ahead.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/12/books/review/books-by-michael-j-casey-and-daniel-gross.html', 'snippet': 'Michael J. Casey blames a “vast global financial system” for our economic malaise. Daniel Gross sees a brighter future ahead.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Each of these books illuminates one particular economic story very well, but fails to see the larger and more complex picture. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812COWEN/0812COWEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 354, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812COWEN/0812COWEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 354}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812COWEN/0812COWEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 607, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812COWEN/0812COWEN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1214, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812COWEN/0812COWEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812COWEN/0812COWEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812COWEN/0812COWEN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Reversal of Fortunes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reversal of Fortunes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gross, Daniel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Casey, Michael J', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T15:57:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tyler Cowen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tyler', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f8ba7b2-2afe-52b8-96b4-f2d8868851bf', 'word_count': 1067, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f8ba7b2-2afe-52b8-96b4-f2d8868851bf'}, {'abstract': 'Jill Lepore traces American ideas about life and death, from before the cradle to beyond the grave.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/12/books/review/the-mansion-of-happiness-by-jill-lepore.html', 'snippet': 'Jill Lepore traces American ideas about life and death, from before the cradle to beyond the grave.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Around 1806, Britain’s most popular board game was introduced to the United States. Called the Mansion of Happiness, it — like the New Game of Human Life, a somewhat less thrilling predecessor — was based on the idea that life is a voyage in which travelers are buffeted between vice and virtue. In 1843 an American edition was issued. It quickly caught on, selling 4,000 copies in 10 months. The “mansion” in question was a heavenly one, the final destination for the pious. (“Be virtuous then and forward press, / To gain the seat of happiness,” the rules read.)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812SHAPIRO/0812SHAPIRO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 789, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812SHAPIRO/0812SHAPIRO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1578, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812SHAPIRO/0812SHAPIRO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812SHAPIRO/0812SHAPIRO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812SHAPIRO/0812SHAPIRO-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Streams of Consciousness', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Streams of Consciousness', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lepore, Jill', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T15:57:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dani Shapiro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dani', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shapiro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2296ad2-0094-5e0f-8ede-367381aa62c2', 'word_count': 1176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2296ad2-0094-5e0f-8ede-367381aa62c2'}, {'abstract': 'Alan Ehrenhalt describes a demographic reversal, with the wealthy moving to cities and the working class moving to the suburbs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/12/books/review/the-great-inversion-and-the-future-of-the-american-city.html', 'snippet': 'Alan Ehrenhalt describes a demographic reversal, with the wealthy moving to cities and the working class moving to the suburbs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ever since the surge in crime that began in the 1960s, cosmopolitan Americans have been on a quest for urbanity, looking for a metropolitan culture that had been lost to social breakdown. They sought safety in the suburbs or in urban enclaves, but they were forced to give up the civic spaces, parks, boulevards and bustling street life that had made cities so stimulating in the past. Alan Ehrenhalt’s 1995 book, “The Lost City: Discovering the Forgotten Virtues of Community in the Chicago of the 1950s,” subtly evaluated all that had been forsaken.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812Siegel/0812Siegel-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 982, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812Siegel/0812Siegel-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1964, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812Siegel/0812Siegel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812Siegel/0812Siegel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812Siegel/0812Siegel-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Trading Places', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Trading Places', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ehrenhalt, Alan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T15:58:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fred Siegel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fred', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Siegel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5af07ce2-67e2-597f-bb93-fe293aed4433', 'word_count': 1232, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5af07ce2-67e2-597f-bb93-fe293aed4433'}, {'abstract': 'A sweeping cultural history of the ideal of sincerity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/12/books/review/sincerity-by-r-jay-magill-jr.html', 'snippet': 'A sweeping cultural history of the ideal of sincerity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Things weren’t going well for sincerity even before Hallmark strip-mined it down to muck. Lionel Trilling pronounced it dead some 40 years ago. Snark and irony have long had more cultural cachet. Among its many pitfalls is that the more you seek or proclaim it, the less sincere you seem. (Only politicians have yet to get the message.) Another small problem: if people truly did say what was in their hearts on a regular basis, marriages would rupture, friendships would founder and no one would ever sit through a faculty meeting again. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812Kipnis/0812Kipnis-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 955, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812Kipnis/0812Kipnis-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1910, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812Kipnis/0812Kipnis-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812Kipnis/0812Kipnis-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812Kipnis/0812Kipnis-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'I Mean It', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'I Mean It', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Magill, R Jay Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T15:58:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laura Kipnis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kipnis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/881aa82a-6aa0-5833-8ea1-5221e8fb454c', 'word_count': 1229, 'uri': 'nyt://article/881aa82a-6aa0-5833-8ea1-5221e8fb454c'}, {'abstract': 'New books by Sadie Jones, Natasa Dragnic, Jane Rogers, Jennifer Miller and Simon Mawer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/12/books/review/new-books-by-sadie-jones-simon-mawer-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'New books by Sadie Jones, Natasa Dragnic, Jane Rogers, Jennifer Miller and Simon Mawer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE UNINVITED GUESTS  By Sadie Jones.  Harper/HarperCollins, $24.99. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/12stuart/12stuart-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 865, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/12stuart/12stuart-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1081, 'width': 913, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/12stuart/12stuart-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/12stuart/12stuart-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/12stuart/12stuart-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fiction Chronicle', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fiction Chronicle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mawer, Simon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jones, Sadie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dragnic, Natasa', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rogers, Jane', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miller, Jennifer', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schillinger, Liesl', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T15:58:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jan Stuart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stuart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a7a2073-d3f5-5db6-8c12-e44fbb53480c', 'word_count': 1025, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a7a2073-d3f5-5db6-8c12-e44fbb53480c'}, {'abstract': 'Vaddey Ratner’s first novel, which parallels her own life, tells of a little girl’s struggle under the Khmer Rouge.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/12/books/review/in-the-shadow-of-the-banyan-by-vaddey-ratner.html', 'snippet': 'Vaddey Ratner’s first novel, which parallels her own life, tells of a little girl’s struggle under the Khmer Rouge.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When a 7-year-old Cambodian girl named Raami first sets eyes on a member of the Khmer Rouge, clad in black pajamas and sandals made from rubber tires, she pretends he is a tevoda, an angel. “You must be Dark One!” she greets him, and pouts when he won’t join her game. She is shocked to learn that this shabby apparition is a revolutionary soldier, come to order the immediate evacuation of her family to the countryside, along with all the other residents of Phnom Penh. “This is a Khmer Rouge?” she thinks. “Where was the many-named larger-than-life deity I’d expected?”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812MISHAN/0812MISHAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 755, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812MISHAN/0812MISHAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1511, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812MISHAN/0812MISHAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812MISHAN/0812MISHAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812MISHAN/0812MISHAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lost in the Killing Fields', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lost in the Killing Fields', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ratner, Vaddey', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Khmer Rouge', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cambodia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T15:59:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ligaya Mishan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ligaya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mishan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0f5db7b-5150-50d2-b925-f25ea73ff0d1', 'word_count': 642, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0f5db7b-5150-50d2-b925-f25ea73ff0d1'}, {'abstract': 'A young hacker on the run in the Middle East finds himself in a secret world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/12/books/review/alif-the-unseen-by-g-willow-wilson.html', 'snippet': 'A young hacker on the run in the Middle East finds himself in a secret world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In her 2010 memoir, “The Butterfly Mosque,” G. Willow Wilson told the story of her conversion to Islam, charting her transformation from child of atheist parents to Boston University-\xadeducated undergraduate to faithful Muslim with an Egyptian husband and an apartment in Cairo. Wilson wrote of the contrast between East and West, and of feeling compelled to keep her religious beliefs secret. “In the West,” she observed, “anything that must be hidden is suspect; availability and honesty are interlinked. This clashes irreconcilably with Islam, . . . where the things that are most precious, most perfect and most holy are always hidden: the Kaaba, the faces of prophets and angels, a woman’s body, Heaven.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'App for the Ancients', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'App for the Ancients', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, G Willow', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T16:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pauls Toutonghi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pauls', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toutonghi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/35ee30ea-4a38-5970-a448-fda6fd64c515', 'word_count': 705, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35ee30ea-4a38-5970-a448-fda6fd64c515'}, {'abstract': 'The French journalist Claude Lanzmann is best known for his epic film, “Shoah.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/12/books/review/the-patagonian-hare-by-claude-lanzmann.html', 'snippet': 'The French journalist Claude Lanzmann is best known for his epic film, “Shoah.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Israeli Ministry of Foreign Affairs approached Claude Lanzmann in 1973 and suggested that, with Israel’s backing, he make a documentary film about the murder of the European Jews. Lanzmann was and is a French journalist, and his qualifications for undertaking such a project were obvious at a glance. He had spent many years producing copy for the glossy French magazine Elle and, then again, for mass-\xadreadership newspapers. He sat on the editorial committee of Jean-Paul Sartre’s magazine Les Temps Modernes. He was handy with a film camera. Also, he had displayed an acute sympathy for the plight of the Israelis — a less-than-\xaduniversal trait even in those days.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812BERMAN/0812BERMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 764, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812BERMAN/0812BERMAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1528, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812BERMAN/0812BERMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812BERMAN/0812BERMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812BERMAN/0812BERMAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 149, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Witness', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Witness', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wynne, Frank', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lanzmann, Claude', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T16:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Berman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/95c2c8c9-b1e4-5629-8e12-450c098ede37', 'word_count': 2122, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95c2c8c9-b1e4-5629-8e12-450c098ede37'}, {'abstract': 'Sadakat Kadri’s history of Shariah explores how Islamic law has evolved.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/12/books/review/heaven-on-earth-by-sadakat-kadri.html', 'snippet': 'Sadakat Kadri’s history of Shariah explores how Islamic law has evolved.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In recent years, America has succumbed to a peculiar form of Shariah-phobia. According to this narrative, covert jihadis are working to usurp the law of the land and replace it with Islamic rule. A caliphate will rise on the ashes of the Constitution, Americans will be forced to pray in mosques and judges will mete out stonings and amputations. “Stealth jihadis use political, cultural, societal, religious, intellectual tools; violent jihadis use violence,” Newt Gingrich told the American Enterprise Institute in July 2010. “But in fact they’re both engaged in jihad, and they’re both seeking to impose the same end state, which is to replace Western civilization with a radical imposition of Shariah.” During the Republican presidential primaries, every candidate weighed in on the Shariah threat. In November 2010, Oklahoma voters approved a constitutional amendment banning the use of Islamic law in court. A federal appeals court blocked the amendment, but more than two dozen other states have considered legislation to restrict judges from consulting foreign and religious laws.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812BAZZI/0812BAZZI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 312, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812BAZZI/0812BAZZI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 312}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812BAZZI/0812BAZZI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 533, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812BAZZI/0812BAZZI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1065, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812BAZZI/0812BAZZI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812BAZZI/0812BAZZI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812BAZZI/0812BAZZI-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Crime and Punishment', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Crime and Punishment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kadri, Sadakat', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shariah (Islamic Law)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T16:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mohamad Bazzi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mohamad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bazzi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f5cda71-91c5-57ec-8f4c-c2ec0ee11a2a', 'word_count': 1198, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f5cda71-91c5-57ec-8f4c-c2ec0ee11a2a'}, {'abstract': 'Although the N.B.A.’s off-season market may appear unchanged, the effects of the new collective bargaining agreement have already begun to alter the league’s salary distribution.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/10/n-b-a-s-new-market-begins-to-take-shape/', 'snippet': 'Although the N.B.A.’s off-season market may appear unchanged, the effects of the new collective bargaining agreement have already begun to alter the league’s salary distribution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The N.B.A.’s collective bargaining agreement is a fascinating case study in subtlety and a masterwork of delayed repercussion. On the surface, the agreement has had little effect on the N.B.A. since its introduction last year. Players are still receiving lucrative multiyear contracts and owners have shown little restraint in offering similar deals to even the lesser tier of players. Yet the entire market for players has rotated, quietly altering its dynamic. The more things change, after all, the more they superficially appear to stay the same.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'N.B.A.’s New Market Begins to Take Shape', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T16:00:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Mahoney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mahoney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ca52ba4-a645-5f24-bfa8-c9d3f60f8a3f', 'word_count': 655, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ca52ba4-a645-5f24-bfa8-c9d3f60f8a3f'}, {'abstract': 'In Chicago, 1919 brought bloodshed, riots and municipal crises.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/12/books/review/city-of-scoundrels-by-gary-krist.html', 'snippet': 'In Chicago, 1919 brought bloodshed, riots and municipal crises.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Out of a clear blue sky, amid a wave of terrorist bombings, a commercial airship crashes among the skyscrapers of the financial district, burning victims alive at their desks. But this catastrophe was not the work of Al Qaeda. It happened on July 21, 1919, when a stray spark ignited the 10,000 cubic feet of hydrogen in the Goodyear blimp Wingfoot Express as it floated above Chicago’s crowded Loop. With Twitteresque speed, sportswriters at Comiskey Park watching the Yankees play the soon-to-be-Black Sox began to telegraph the news across the country, even before the Wingfoot exploded through the skylight of the Illinois Trust and Savings Bank, killing more than a dozen people.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812MCMANUS/0812MCMANUS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812MCMANUS/0812MCMANUS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812MCMANUS/0812MCMANUS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 752, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812MCMANUS/0812MCMANUS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1504, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Very Bad Year', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Very Bad Year', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Krist, Gary', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T16:01:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By James McManus', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McManus', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3a98bc4-2184-57dd-a208-98793959680f', 'word_count': 889, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3a98bc4-2184-57dd-a208-98793959680f'}, {'abstract': 'Every generation rewrites the book’s epitaph.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/12/books/review/the-death-of-the-book-through-the-ages.html', 'snippet': 'Every generation rewrites the book’s epitaph.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two decades ago, the Book Review ran an essay, “The End of Books,” in which the novelist Robert Coover questioned whether print could survive the age of “video transmissions, cellular phones, fax machines, computer networks, and in particular out in the humming digitalized precincts of avant-garde computer hackers, cyberpunks and hyperspace freaks.” Was the book as “dead as God”?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812-bks-PRICE/0812-bks-PRICE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 938, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812-bks-PRICE/0812-bks-PRICE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1875, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812-bks-PRICE/0812-bks-PRICE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812-bks-PRICE/0812-bks-PRICE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812-bks-PRICE/0812-bks-PRICE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dead Again', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dead Again', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Books and Readers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T16:04:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leah Price', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Price', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d19ec5e-7dff-5488-ab3f-22f4d3b7babf', 'word_count': 1274, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d19ec5e-7dff-5488-ab3f-22f4d3b7babf'}, {'abstract': 'Despite predictions of the death of the book, it’s as lively as ever.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/12/books/review/the-tradition-of-the-book-continues.html', 'snippet': 'Despite predictions of the death of the book, it’s as lively as ever.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By now, we’ve all heard the warnings: Humanity is under siege. Ever-encroaching communications technologies are replacing intimate connections. We prefer the companionship of Siri — the iPhone 4S’s temperamental personal assistant — to real-life interaction, and walk around with buds lodged in our ears, touchpads attached to our fingers and (soon, some say) neural chips embedded in our brains. In this cyborgian age, inanimate things take on a life of their own, endowed with properties that once upon a time distinguished people from things. They pulse with vitality, their beeps echo our touch and they feel melded to our bodies. The very nature of what it means to be human may be changing before our eyes. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812-SILVERMAN/0812-SILVERMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 686, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812-SILVERMAN/0812-SILVERMAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1372, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812-SILVERMAN/0812-SILVERMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812-SILVERMAN/0812-SILVERMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812-SILVERMAN/0812-SILVERMAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'It’s Alive!', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'It’s Alive!', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Books and Readers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T16:04:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gillian Silverman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gillian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silverman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50f3b7b4-a4a6-5302-a35b-9dd26829186a', 'word_count': 1249, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50f3b7b4-a4a6-5302-a35b-9dd26829186a'}, {'abstract': 'Ron Powers on being “the ultimate journeyman.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/12/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'Ron Powers on being “the ultimate journeyman.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1973, when Ron Powers was a 31-year-old columnist for The Chicago Sun-Times, he became the first TV critic to win a Pulitzer Prize. (Television had been pervasive for 25 years or so, which isn’t bad by Pulitzer standards; no car critic landed a prize until 2004.) Powers soon moved out of the newspaper world into broadcasting, where he won two Emmys (one local and one national), and then into books, where he has twice topped the New York Times best-seller list (for “True Compass,” written with Ted Kennedy, and “Flags of Our Fathers,” with James Bradley).', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812-bks-Upfront/0812-bks-Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 647, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/books/review/0812-bks-Upfront/0812-bks-Upfront-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1295, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Powers, Ron', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T16:05:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d30de5a-9173-5685-a684-e9517ee3cdf2', 'word_count': 283, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d30de5a-9173-5685-a684-e9517ee3cdf2'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/12/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHY DOES THE WORLD EXIST? AN EXISTENTIAL DETECTIVE STORY,  by Jim Holt. (Liveright/Norton, $27.95.) An elegant and witty writer converses with philosophers and cosmologists who ponder the question of why there is something rather than nothing.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T16:05:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4599bbf-64aa-5475-8e65-71b8e89fc4bd', 'word_count': 262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4599bbf-64aa-5475-8e65-71b8e89fc4bd'}, {'abstract': 'A new study shows the prime role of the bullfrog trade in spreading a deadly amphibian disease.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/10/genetic-study-finds-bullfrog-trade-is-prime-pathway-for-devastating-amphibian-fungus/', 'snippet': 'A new study shows the prime role of the bullfrog trade in spreading a deadly amphibian disease.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A University of Michigan genetic analysis has found that the global trade in bullfrogs is a prime conduit for the chytrid fungus, which has been devastating amphibian populations around the world. The paper is being published in the journal Molecular Ecology [link is subscription only].', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Genetic Study Finds Bullfrog Trade is Prime Pathway For Devastating Amphibian Fungus', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AMPHIBIANS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biology and Biochemistry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frogs', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fungi', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ecology (Journal)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Michigan', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ecuador', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Taiwan', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T16:05:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '