on.html', 'snippet': 'The match between Brazil and Mexico is expected to bring crowds and fanfare usually reserved for the World Cup.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — The men’s soccer tournament rarely garners significant attention at the Olympics, and in many ways this is by design. Unlike the women’s tournament, which is contested by teams using their best players, the men’s tournament has an age restriction: players must be under 23, with three exceptions allowed per team. This restriction, the thinking goes, was designed by FIFA to keep the Olympic tournament from being competition for the globally popular World Cup.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Usually a Sideshow, Men’s Soccer Final Is Drawing Attention', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "A Meaningful Men's Soccer Final", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T17:31:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Borden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Borden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/80f8719f-9605-5c1c-8d74-d04731ff429a', 'word_count': 488, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80f8719f-9605-5c1c-8d74-d04731ff429a'}, {'abstract': '“Aftermath” details Ms. Cusk’s separation and divorce from her husband.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/10/book-review-podcast-rachel-cusks-divorce-memoir/', 'snippet': '“Aftermath” details Ms. Cusk’s separation and divorce from her husband.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In The New York Times Book Review, Emma Gilbey Keller reviews Rachel Cusk’s “Aftermath,” a memoir about Ms. Cusk’s separation and divorce from her husband. Ms. Cusk is also an acclaimed novelist, but is perhaps best known for an earlier memoir, “A Life’s Work,” which dealt very candidly with her conflicted feelings on becoming a mother. Ms. Keller writes:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Book Review Podcast: Rachel Cusk’s Divorce Memoir', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Podcasts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bosman, Julie', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cowen, Tyler', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cowles, Gregory', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cusk, Rachel', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gilbey, Emma', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stiglitz, Joseph E', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tanenhaus, Sam', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T17:40:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae5ea085-cd41-5bed-8f21-41f56eeac482', 'word_count': 205, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae5ea085-cd41-5bed-8f21-41f56eeac482'}, {'abstract': 'Deep into its eastbound charge across Russia, the Trans-Siberian Railway sprouts an artery known as the BAM — a remote railroad with its own curious, complex story to tell.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/12/travel/the-other-siberian-railroad.html', 'snippet': 'Deep into its eastbound charge across Russia, the Trans-Siberian Railway sprouts an artery known as the BAM — a remote railroad with its own curious, complex story to tell.', 'lead_paragraph': '“FOR me, a young girl in Belarus, listening to the late-night radio broadcasts of them building this rail line in the Siberian frontier had a romance that made me want to be part of it,” said my newest train friend, Mila Kozlova, 57, as we rocked back and forth in our coupe across the Siberian wilderness. She gazed contentedly at the ocean of birch trees rolling by our window, and pulled out two jars of home-pickled vegetables from her canvas bag, adding them to the smorgasbord crowding the windowsill table. A day into our trip on the Baikal-Amur Mainline railroad — Russians call it BAM — had become a 2,500-mile all-you-can-eat buffet.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/travel/12SIBERIA_SPAN/12SIBERIA_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 494, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/12/travel/12SIBERIA_SPAN/12SIBERIA_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 494}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/travel/12SIBERIA_SPAN/12SIBERIA_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 494, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/travel/12SIBERIA_SPAN/12SIBERIA_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 494, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption'