 the Orlando Magic are looking at a return package much less promising than anticipated.', 'lead_paragraph': '3:08 p.m. | Updated Dwight Howard’s rumor-fueled stranglehold on the basketball world has come to a close, just in time for his limelit tenure in Los Angeles to begin. The Orlando Magic finally completed a four-team deal Friday that includes the Denver Nuggets and Philadelphia 76ers, but ultimately makes Dwight Howard a Laker.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'With Dwight Howard Trade, Orlando Aims Low', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T18:25:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Mahoney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mahoney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ed0b82f-ed05-54c4-9d5d-57354f51d203', 'word_count': 809, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ed0b82f-ed05-54c4-9d5d-57354f51d203'}, {'abstract': 'Coach Les Miles said that Tyrann Mathieu, the Tigers’ most dynamic player, violated team rules, but he was not more specific.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/11/sports/ncaafootball/tyrann-mathieu-is-off-louisiana-states-football-team.html', 'snippet': 'Coach Les Miles said that Tyrann Mathieu, the Tigers’ most dynamic player, violated team rules, but he was not more specific.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tyrann Mathieu, the dynamic Louisiana State defensive back and a Heisman Trophy finalist last season, was dismissed from the football team Friday for violating an unspecified athletic department rule.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/11/sports/11badger-1/11badger-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/11/sports/11badger-1/11badger-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/11/sports/11badger-1/11badger-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/11/sports/11badger-1/11badger-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/11/sports/11badger-1/11badger-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Heisman Finalist From L.S.U. Is Dismissed', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Heisman Finalist From L.S.U. Is Dismissed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football (College)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mathieu, Tyrann', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miles, Les', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Louisiana State University', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T18:35:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'College Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Rohan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4604587-233b-551e-b5ec-1a61e2c7dacb', 'word_count': 519, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4604587-233b-551e-b5ec-1a61e2c7dacb'}, {'abstract': 'Bucks readers tell of their experiences in trying to sell their own homes.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/10/tales-of-frustration-in-selling-a-house/', 'snippet': 'Bucks readers tell of their experiences in trying to sell their own homes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The subject of Paul Sullivan’s Wealth Matters column this week is a man, Bob Fisher, who is trying to sell his house himself after two brokers were unable to sell two other properties he owns. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tales of Frustration in Selling a House', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fisher, Bob', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Carolina', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T18:38:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bucks Editors', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bucks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Editors', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/00786237-c8e0-58b4-9153-b547b75929c8', 'word_count': 173, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00786237-c8e0-58b4-9153-b547b75929c8'}, {'abstract': 'The series is about South Dakota’s fictional Prairie Rose Indian Reservation and Dashiell Bad Horse.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/10/graphic-books-best-sellers-sex-violence-and-intrigue-in-scalped/', 'snippet': 'The series is about South Dakota’s fictional Prairie Rose Indian Reservation and Dashiell Bad Horse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At No. 4 on the softcover list this week is Volume Nine of “Scalped,” written by Jason Aaron and illustrated by R.M. Guera. The series is about South Dakota’s fictional Prairie Rose Indian Reservation and Dashiell Bad Horse, who left the reservation as a teenager and returns as an undercover agent for the F.B.I. He hopes to get enough evidence to bring down Lincoln Red Crow, a corrupt casino owner. This volume reprints issues No. 50-55. The series will end with issue No. 60, which goes on sale on Aug. 22. The final collected edition is scheduled for Oct. 31. It’s difficult to discuss the book without giving away plot points, but a couple of characters have major changes of heart that result in a dynamic shift in the power struggles. And, as always, there is a bounty of sex, violence and intrigue. “Scalped” has been a compelling read and the creators have developed rich characters, but it can be difficult to find an audience in the comic-book market place. Issue No. 59, which went on sale in June, had estimated sales of slightly more than 6,000 copies. The collected editions, at least, have done a little better: Volume Seven hit the Graphic Books Best Sellers list in March. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Graphic Books Best Sellers: Sex, Violence and Intrigue in ‘Scalped’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T18:39:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Gene Gustines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': 'Gene', 'lastname': 'Gustines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15430c5b-c5df-50b4-861f-85d878105abb', 'word_count': 228, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15430c5b-c5df-50b4-861f-85d878105abb'}, {'abstract': 'Mitt Romney has no choice but to vacillate. The G.O.P. won’t let him tout his own record.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/10/forcing-flip-flops/', 'snippet': 'Mitt Romney has no choice but to vacillate. The G.O.P. won’t let him tout his own record.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2004, Mitt Romney had this to say about John Kerry’s supposed tendency to change his mind:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Forcing Flip-Flops', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coulter, Ann', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kerry, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Limbaugh, Rush', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T18:57:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Juliet Lapidos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Juliet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lapidos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8808537-1b9e-502f-810b-4dda8d363586', 'word_count': 474, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8808537-1b9e-502f-810b-4dda8d363586'}, {'abstract': 'Lower East Side storefronts along the Bowery are providing new homes for a series of after-hours murals.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/10/out-there-signing-on/', 'snippet': 'Lower East Side storefronts along the Bowery are providing new homes for a series of after-hours murals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New Museum’s dynamic silhouette and jazzy accouterments — Ugo Rondinone’s rainbow “HELL, YES!” that branded the building’s third story when it first opened in 2007 and the spindly steel rose by Isa Genzken that now occupies that perch — have become familiar sights on the Bowery. And while it’s no secret that an influx of art galleries has transformed the entire Lower East Side into Chelsea’s junior rival, whatever culture business takes place on this historically gritty thoroughfare in particular is still shrouded by industrial facades.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Out There | Signing On', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T19:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin McGarry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGarry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/852bd5c5-815d-53cc-94fe-aaa46f697cdf', 'word_count': 480, 'uri': 'nyt://article/852bd5c5-815d-53cc-94fe-aaa46f697cdf'}, {'abstract': 'A pair of brick buildings are destined to be gut-renovated and reimagined as a 12,000-plus-square-foot grand residence.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/10/big-ticket-sold-for-32-million/', 'snippet': 'A pair of brick buildings are destined to be gut-renovated and reimagined as a 12,000-plus-square-foot grand residence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two adjoining brick buildings in the Greenwich Village Historic District that in a former incarnation served as an ice cream factory, a garage and a Steinway piano showroom, sold for $32 million, and combined are the biggest sale of the week, according to city records.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Big Ticket | Sold for $32 Million', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T19:04:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Finn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/77b80738-5ef9-5db1-be88-5ccae84d95bb', 'word_count': 487, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77b80738-5ef9-5db1-be88-5ccae84d95bb'}, {'abstract': 'From the pool to the pitch and many arenas in between, Olympic champions face moral questions on the way to the podium.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/11/sports/olympics/separating-the-person-from-the-olympic-performance.html', 'snippet': 'From the pool to the pitch and many arenas in between, Olympic champions face moral questions on the way to the podium.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — There are people who believe greatness and goodness are like synchronized divers or horse and equestrian rider — in perfect harmony. Among those spreading this misconception is the four-time Olympic track champion Michael Johnson, who had this to say about the Olympic heptathlon champion, Jessica Ennis of Britain, in an interview with The Daily Mail: “The mental preparation and the ability to focus makes her a great person.”', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/11/sports/11crouse-1/11crouse-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/11/sports/11crouse-1/11crouse-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/11/sports/11crouse-1/11crouse-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/11/sports/11crouse-1/11crouse-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/11/sports/11crouse-1/11crouse-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Shades of Gray on Way to Podium', 'kicker': 'Inside the Rings', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Shades Of Gray On Way To Podium', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Officiating (Sports)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cheating', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T19:12:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Crouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26ba4675-07a9-5494-9fea-b3ac11e506b6', 'word_count': 1134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26ba4675-07a9-5494-9fea-b3ac11e506b6'}, {'abstract': 'The Tokyo Electric Power Company released video this week that was recorded during teleconferences last year in the first days of the crisis at the utility’s nuclear plant at Fukushima.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/10/video-shows-fukushima-crisis-talks/', 'snippet': 'The Tokyo Electric Power Company released video this week that was recorded during teleconferences last year in the first days of the crisis at the utility’s nuclear plant at Fukushima.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Hiroko Tabuchi reports in Friday’s New York Times, the Tokyo Electric Power Company released 150 hours of video this week that was recorded during teleconferences last year in the first days of the crisis at the utility’s nuclear plant at Fukushima.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Video Shows Fukushima Crisis Talks', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant (Japan)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fukushima Daini Nuclear Power Plant (Japan)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Japan Earthquake and Tsunami (2011)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Energy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kan, Naoto', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tokyo Electric Power Co', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T19:21:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f4b391a-32f7-5787-8c88-94190bba9fdc', 'word_count': 471, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f4b391a-32f7-5787-8c88-94190bba9fdc'}, {'abstract': 'Playing host to the Games rarely turns out to be an economic benefit for a country or city, and sports economists have shown how silly the expectations sometimes are.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/10/the-elusive-economic-lift-of-the-olympics/', 'snippet': 'Playing host to the Games rarely turns out to be an economic benefit for a country or city, and sports economists have shown how silly the expectations sometimes are.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you love athletics, rampant nationalism or large economies bullying small ones, you’ve probably enjoyed the Olympic Games in London immensely. If you bet that the British government’s predictions of economic benefits would come true, you’re probably less happy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Elusive Economic Lift of the Olympics', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T19:23:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Mills', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mills', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/43e1ef76-33ce-5a5d-92e6-b181aa073e4f', 'word_count': 723, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43e1ef76-33ce-5a5d-92e6-b181aa073e4f'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Zakaria’s recent column on gun control had passages that closely tracked those in a New Yorker piece by the historian Jill Lepore.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/10/time-magazine-to-examine-plagiarism-accusation-against-zakaria/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Zakaria’s recent column on gun control had passages that closely tracked those in a New Yorker piece by the historian Jill Lepore.', 'lead_paragraph': '8:21 p.m. | Updated Time magazine and CNN suspended Fareed Zakaria, the writer and television host, on Friday after he apologized for plagiarizing a New Yorker article in his column on gun control in the Aug. 20 issue of Time.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'CNN and Time Suspend Journalist After Admission of Plagiarism', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'CNN and Time Suspend Journalist After Admission of Plagiarism', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Plagiarism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lepore, Jill', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zakaria, Fareed', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Time', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T19:26:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Haughney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haughney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a838315-7f2d-50eb-8bf4-65965edf5520', 'word_count': 712, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a838315-7f2d-50eb-8bf4-65965edf5520'}, {'abstract': 'Long Island City, which has shed much of its grittiness and is now home to almost 30,000 people, has six subway lines just one stop from Midtown Manhattan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2012/08/12/realestate/20120812-LIVING.html', 'snippet': 'Long Island City, which has shed much of its grittiness and is now home to almost 30,000 people, has six subway lines just one stop from Midtown Manhattan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Long Island City, 