': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'APRICOTS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Desserts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T21:00:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Clark', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clark', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/278d618f-92a9-55bb-b539-795edec95341', 'word_count': 378, 'uri': 'nyt://article/278d618f-92a9-55bb-b539-795edec95341'}, {'abstract': 'There is a big question facing a project being readied off the Maine coast: how well will it withstand the force of the rushing water?', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/10/the-physics-of-tidal-energy/', 'snippet': 'There is a big question facing a project being readied off the Maine coast: how well will it withstand the force of the rushing water?', 'lead_paragraph': 'As my colleague Jess Bidgood reported in Friday’s paper, a tidal energy project is moving ahead in Maine, with high costs but high hopes too. But the 180-kilowatt unit that the Ocean Renewable Power Company hopes to put under water next week is really just a first step. The big question is, how well will it withstand the force of the rushing water?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Physics of Tidal Energy', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric Light and Power', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Energy and Power', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oceans and Seas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ocean Renewable Power Co', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Verdant Power', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bay of Fundy (Canada)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Maine', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T21:02:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew L. Wald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1dc3d96d-3db3-541a-aac2-48d92f2bec4d', 'word_count': 448, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1dc3d96d-3db3-541a-aac2-48d92f2bec4d'}, {'abstract': 'There’s some moment, for parents, when they realize that the boy in a dress isn’t just playing dress-up, but exploring something that might be more complicated.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/10/when-does-a-boy-in-a-dress-become-something-more/', 'snippet': 'There’s some moment, for parents, when they realize that the boy in a dress isn’t just playing dress-up, but exploring something that might be more complicated.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What’s So Bad About a Boy Who Wants to Wear a Dress? asks the headline of The New York Times magazine. What follows is a complex study of an even more complex topic: the world of boys who don’t conform to their gender expectations. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Does a Boy in a Dress Become Something More?', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-10T21:03:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None,