'lead_paragraph': 'The Glasspar G2 Mameco-Ardun sports car featured in Sunday’s Automobile section has a story to tell. That tale, however, might not have ended particularly well were it not for Mark Brinker, who purchased the long-lost car and financed an exacting restoration.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mark Brinker, Automotive Surgeon', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collectors and Collections', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-11T00:21:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Stenquist', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stenquist', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3314202-1fdf-5b3f-a836-871cbc721257', 'word_count': 553, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3314202-1fdf-5b3f-a836-871cbc721257'}, {'abstract': 'The Federal Trade Commission said it would review a rule after one commissioner wrote a blistering dissent to a settlement with Facebook.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/11/business/facebook-settlement-on-privacy-is-finalized-by-ftc.html', 'snippet': 'The Federal Trade Commission said it would review a rule after one commissioner wrote a blistering dissent to a settlement with Facebook.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The Federal Trade Commission finished a settlement with Facebook on Friday over allegations that the company had violated its privacy policy, and in the process said it would re-examine its own practice of allowing companies to settle charges of wrongdoing while denying that they had done anything wrong.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/11/business/FTC/FTC-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 709, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/11/business/FTC/FTC-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1418, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Letting Companies Settle While Denying Guilt Reconsidered by F.T.C.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Letting Companies Settle While Denying Guilt Reconsidered by F.T.C.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rosch, J Thomas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Trade Commission', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-11T00:26:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wyatt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wyatt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/98e7a91a-c324-5b72-bae2-bb51e0f26e2a', 'word_count': 763, 'uri': 'nyt://article/98e7a91a-c324-5b72-bae2-bb51e0f26e2a'}, {'abstract': 'The corporation that operates the oasis behind the New York Public Library’s main branch hired four artists in residence this summer to paint scenes of park life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/11/nyregion/bryant-park-hosts-summer-artists-in-residence.html', 'snippet': 'The corporation that operates the oasis behind the New York Public Library’s main branch hired four artists in residence this summer to paint scenes of park life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bryant Park’s pristine lawn, lush hydrangeas and movable bistro tables have been well documented in photographs and videos, but not so much in paint. This summer, to correct that situation, the Bryant Park Management Corporation, which operates the park, chose four artists to be painters in residence: each is being paid $2,000, in addition to the cost of materials, to spend two weeks depicting life in the park.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/11/nyregion/BRYANT1/BRYANT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/11/nyregion/BRYANT1/BRYANT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/11/nyregion/BRYANT1/BRYANT1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 696, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/11/nyregion/BRYANT1/BRYANT1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1391, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/11/nyregion/BRYANT1/BRYANT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/11/nyregion/BRYANT1/BRYANT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/11/nyregion/BRYANT1/BRYANT1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Painting Bryant Park, While Adding to Its Charm', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Painting Bryant Park, While Adding To Its Charm', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bryant Park (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mollica, Patti', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Imata, Yuka', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bryant Park Management Corp', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Biederman, Daniel A', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mosley, Walter Lynn', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Arroyo, Andrea', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-11T00:29:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa W. Foderaro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Foderaro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8480fe1-09c7-516b-bb40-3f0505db48e4', 'word_count': 792, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8480fe1-09c7-516b-bb40-3f0505db48e4'}, {'abstract': 'In James Nares’s “STREET,” at the Wadsworth Atheneum Museum of Art in Hartford, silent Manhattan vignettes unfold to the sound of a jangling, droning acoustic guitar soundtrack.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/12/nyregion/in-james-naress-street-taming-the-galloping-city.html', 'snippet': 'In James Nares’s “STREET,” at the Wadsworth Atheneum Museum of Art in Hartford, silent Manhattan vignettes unfold to the sound of a jangling, droning acoustic guitar soundtrack.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THERE are moments in history when everything interesting seems to take place indoors: clandestine meetings, documents being signed, objects and discoveries being made in private studios and ateliers. The past year, however, saw a different moment, in which plazas were occupied and protests and revolutions unfolded in the streets. Public space was activated — which might partially explain why James Nares’s “STREET,” at the Wadsworth Atheneum Museum of Art in Hartford, feels particularly relevant at this moment. ', 'print_section': 'CT', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/nyregion/12ARTSCT1_SPAN/12ARTSCT1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 298, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/12/nyregion/12ARTSCT1_SPAN/12ARTSCT1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 298}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/nyregion/12ARTSCT1_SPAN/12ARTSCT1_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 508, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/nyregion/12ARTSCT1_SPAN/12ARTSCT1_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1016, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/12/nyregion/12ARTSCT1_SPAN/12ARTSCT1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'ima