aries and Caucuses', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wisconsin', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Neumann, Mark W', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-11T01:53:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Monica Davey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Monica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Davey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9092be8a-03f2-561d-9297-514647454c19', 'word_count': 1193, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9092be8a-03f2-561d-9297-514647454c19'}, {'abstract': 'The Japanese Parliament approved Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda’s plan to double the sales tax and address national debt, but only after he made a promise to hold elections “soon.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/11/world/asia/japan-parliament-passes-sales-tax-increase.html', 'snippet': 'The Japanese Parliament approved Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda’s plan to double the sales tax and address national debt, but only after he made a promise to hold elections “soon.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda’s plan to double Japan’s sales tax was approved by Parliament on Friday after months of haggling, but only after Mr. Noda promised opposition lawmakers that he would call early elections — a move that is likely to end his term in office and his party’s hold on power.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Japan Sales Tax Increase Passed, on Pledge of Early Election', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Japan Sales Tax Increase Passed, on Pledge of Early Election', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sales and Excise Taxes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Noda, Yoshihiko', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-11T01:54:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hiroko Tabuchi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hiroko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tabuchi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ef060df4-ccf5-579c-a81a-0d300ebb06d1', 'word_count': 693, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ef060df4-ccf5-579c-a81a-0d300ebb06d1'}, {'abstract': 'The attack in the Sinai Peninsula has forced an inexperienced leader to confront competing interests to bring the situation under control.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/11/opinion/president-morsis-first-crisis.html', 'snippet': 'The attack in the Sinai Peninsula has forced an inexperienced leader to confront competing interests to bring the situation under control.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mohamed Morsi was forced to respond quickly to his first security crisis as Egypt’s first freely elected president. After 16 Egyptian soldiers were killed by gunmen in the Sinai Peninsula last Sunday, he dispatched troops to secure the border, moved to assert control of his security leadership team and avoided conflict with Israel. It was a challenging beginning for an inexperienced leader who had been in office less than two months. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'President Morsi’s First Crisis', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'President Morsi&#8217;s First Crisis', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': Non