name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Paul D Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Norfolk (Va)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vice Presidents and Vice Presidency (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-11T14:37:49+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/582606f3-ba7f-53e2-8dd6-905c01e03f4f', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/582606f3-ba7f-53e2-8dd6-905c01e03f4f'}, {'abstract': 'The U.S. women’s soccer team won its third straight Olympic gold medal, beating Japan last Thursday. But the elite players will come home to landscape that is barren in terms of a top league for female players.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/11/for-womens-leagues-strike-1-strike-2-strike-3/', 'snippet': 'The U.S. women’s soccer team won its third straight Olympic gold medal, beating Japan last Thursday. But the elite players will come home to landscape that is barren in terms of a top league for female players.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One league started after the United States won the 1999 Women’s World Cup. Another began play months after the American team won the Olympic gold medal at the 2008 Beijing Games. Neither league — Women’s United Soccer Association or Women’s Professional Soccer — survived past three seasons. Now, in the afterglow of another United States triumph, in London on Thursday, the focus is back on the prospects for the long-term survival of top-level women’s league.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Women’s Leagues, Strike 1. Strike 2. Strike 3?', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subj