there is bootstrapping, and ne’er the twain shall meet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Meet Liz, an aspiring pop socialite in the Olivia Palermo mold, haughty enough to disdain the commoners around her but vain enough to crave their attention. Her father is an art collector, and at least partly because of that, she’s been able to secure an internship at Eli Klein Fine Art, a SoHo gallery. That’s where she’ll work — but not very hard — on “Gallery Girls,” the Bravo reality series that has its premiere on Monday and that is rooted in the belief that there is privilege, and there is bootstrapping, and ne’er the twain shall meet.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/13/arts/gallery/gallery-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/13/arts/gallery/gallery-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/13/arts/gallery/gallery-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/13/arts/gallery/gallery-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/13/arts/gallery/gallery-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fighting Their Way Up Art World Ladder', 'kicker': 'Television Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fighting Their Way Up Art World Ladder', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Gallery Girls (TV Program)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bravo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reality Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-12T21:15:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cde9dd83-5f6f-53ee-b77e-2265ddf13340', 'word_count': 647, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cde9dd83-5f6f-53ee-b77e-2265ddf13340'}, {'abstract': 'Carolina Performing Arts at the University of North Carolina, Chapel Hill, is set to announce a new concluding program for its performance season, “The Rite of Spring at 100.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/arts/dance/footnote.html', 'snippet': 'Carolina Performing Arts at the University of North Carolina, Chapel Hill, is set to announce a new concluding program for its performance season, “The Rite of Spring at 100.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Carolina Performing Arts at the University of North Carolina, Chapel Hill, is set to announce a new concluding program for its performance season, “The Rite of Spring at 100,” to be presented by the Martha Graham Dance Company. Titled “Myth & Transformation,” the two-night program will feature Graham’s interpretation of Stravinsky’s “Rite of Spring”; the world premiere of a work choreographed by Nacho Duato with music by Arvo Pärt; and performances by the guest artist Wendy Whelan, a principal dancer for the New York City Ballet. The program is scheduled for April 26 and 27.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Footnote', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Footnote', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Graham, Martha, Dance Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of North Carolina', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-12T21:22:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f12345d-b86a-5c32-a2b5-133ac5fe54ab', 'word_count': 102, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f12345d-b86a-5c32-a2b5-133ac5fe54ab'}, {'abstract': 'The Downtown Dance Festival began on Saturday with six troupes from the city, upstate and abroad performing amid the shade in Battery Park.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/arts/dance/downtown-dance-festival-and-battery-dance-company.html', 'snippet': 'The Downtown Dance Festival began on Saturday with six troupes from the city, upstate and abroad performing amid the shade in Battery Park.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In perhaps the most famous aria in all of Handel’s operas, “Ombra mai fu,” King Xerxes of Persia states that no shade was ever so dear as that of his beloved plane tree. Though I’ve observed plane trees all my life and have known that aria for decades, only on Saturday did Handel’s sublime melody come to mind — when I spent over two hours in the shade of plane trees in Battery Park, watching the opening event of the 31st annual Downtown Dance Festival. The charm of those trees’ shade on a warm afternoon, the ravishing way they filtered the brightness of the light, and the dappled look of the leaves from beneath: all brought back Xerxes’ words as set to music by Handel.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/13/arts/battery/battery-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 316, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/13/arts/battery/battery-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 316}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/13/arts/battery/battery-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 539, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/13/arts/battery/battery-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1078, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/13/arts/battery/battery-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/13/arts/battery/battery-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/13/arts/battery/battery-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Beneath the Dappled Light, There’s Safety and Charm in Numbers', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beneath the Dappled Light, There’s Safety and Charm in Numbers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keyw