ic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Central Bank', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T13:21:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Jolly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jolly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa56dac7-c569-5c0b-aad6-3ddbf5c2efb3', 'word_count': 571, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa56dac7-c569-5c0b-aad6-3ddbf5c2efb3'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/what-were-reading-499/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Decanter: The pendulum has swung away from overtly oaky wines as winemakers adopt a more refined approach to using barrels, Andrew Jefford reports. — Eric Asimov ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T13:35:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee080ccc-0147-5cb7-b7d3-f3d2e6f36136', 'word_count': 418, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee080ccc-0147-5cb7-b7d3-f3d2e6f36136'}, {'abstract': 'There’s a depressing continuum between Stalin’s last show trial against 13 intellectuals and Putin’s latest against the Pussy Riot.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/the-stalin-in-putin/', 'snippet': 'There’s a depressing continuum between Stalin’s last show trial against 13 intellectuals and Putin’s latest against the Pussy Riot.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thirteen members of the Jewish Anti-Fascist Committee were killed in Moscow 60 years ago this past Sunday. This tragedy is sometimes referred to as “Stalin’s last execution.” Surely other people were put to death before the tyrant died in March 1953, but it was the last of the show trials to have been completed. The date has also often been referred to in the West as The Night of the Murdered Poets, based on the erroneous assumption that all of those killed wrote verse. In fact, the men were intellectuals from different fields.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Stalin in Putin', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Khodorkovsky, Mikhail B', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stalin, Joseph', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tolokonnikova, Nadezhda', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pussy Riot', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Moscow (Russia)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'USSR (Former Soviet Union)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T13:44:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Masha Gessen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Masha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gessen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5fd9366-2910-593b-8dc4-50a096cafca2', 'word_count': 737, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5fd9366-2910-593b-8dc4-50a096cafca2'}, {'abstract': 'Also, a new Spanish-language TV channel tries to create an identity, and newsweekly magazines struggle to reverse their slide in sales and advertising.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/the-breakfast-meeting-lobbying-romney-to-pick-ryan-and-seeking-an-olympic-afterglow/', 'snippet': 'Also, a new Spanish-language TV channel tries to create an identity, and newsweekly magazines struggle to reverse their slide in sales and advertising.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mitt Romney’s selection of Representative Paul D. Ryan of Wisconsin over the weekend to be his running mate is being credited with shoring up Mr. Romney’s conservative bona fides — something his campaign would have known because conservative publications had been telling him through editorials that he should pick Mr. Ryan to shore up his conservative bona fides. The Weekly Standard and The Wall Street Journal both wrote editorials promoting Mr. Ryan for the Republican ticket: The Journal’s editorial on Thursday (Why Not Paul Ryan?) and The Weekly Standard’s editorial dated Monday but online earlier (Go for the Gold, Mitt!). Perhaps this is the reason, Michael Barbaro writes, that Mr. Romney’s camp was eager to announce the day he made the decision, Aug. 1: “Mr. Romney is sensitive to the perception that he acts at the behest of the party’s right wing. Now, he is making the case that he had settled on Mr. Ryan well before a chorus of conservatives told him to.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Lobbying Romney to Pick Ryan, and Seeking an Olympic Afterglow', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T13:48:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/06f5bd0f-feb5-5389-8ab2-af4404c7ea41', 'word_count': 684, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06f5bd0f-feb5-5389-8ab2-af4404c7ea41'}, {'abstract': 'The 81-member strong Indian contingent won only four bronze and two silver medals at the London Olympics.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/indias-olympic-program-under-spotlight-again/', 'snippet': 'The 81-member strong Indian contingent won only four bronze and two silver medals at the London Olympics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Six medals. That is all that India – the world’s second most populous country – managed to win at the London Olympics. While six is a record, it is still a pitiful showing for a country with ambitions to become a great power.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'India’s Olympic Program Under Spotlight Again', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T14:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nikhilesh Bhattacharya', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nikhilesh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bhattacharya', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/09780d32-9ce4-5728-9382-0e7acd56c0a8', 'word_count': 1326, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09780d32-9ce4-5728-9382-0e7acd56c0a8'}, {'abstract': 'The Wisconsin killings have sparked efforts to recognize Sikhs for who they are. But this is a classic case of stigmatizing the victim instead of the perpetrator.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/being-sikh-in-america/', 'snippet': 'The Wisconsin killings have sparked efforts to recognize Sikhs for who they are. But this is a classic case of stigmatizing the victim instead of the perpetrator.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DELHI — From 1988 to 1993 I was a graduate student at New York University. Like many nonobservant Sikhs, I did not wear a turban, but I did keep a beard. When I would travel to small-town America, my appearance sometimes gave rise to a barely concealed hostility, occasionally even a comment or two.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Being Sikh in America', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oak Creek, Wis, Shooting (2012)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sikhs and Sikhism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Page, Wade Michael', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wisconsin', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T14:10:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hartosh Singh Bal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hartosh', 'middlename': 'Singh', 'lastname': 'Bal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/900e1665-1c64-530f-9477-4210734ddf3e', 'word_count': 613, 'uri': 'nyt://article/900e1665-1c64-530f-9477-4210734ddf3e'}, {'abstract': 'Big sport can sometimes get tangled up with big politics, and the just-ended Olympic Games in London was no exception.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/olympic-politics-what-does-it-mean-for-boris/', 'snippet': 'Big sport can sometimes get tangled up with big politics, and the just-ended Olympic Games in London was no exception.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Big sport can sometimes get tangled up with big politics, and the just-ended Olympic Games in London — the subject of my latest column — was no exception. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Olympic Politics: What Does It Mean for Boris?', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Boris', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T14:18:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Cowell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54d558fb-0e36-51c9-90e3-fbec6d3f95cc', 'word_count': 334, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54d558fb-0e36-51c9-90e3-fbec6d3f95cc'}, {'abstract': 'The surviving Woolworth houses in Glen Cove and Manhattan exist today in different states of grace; some are single-family homes, and one has been chopped into smaller co-ops.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/14/nyregion/the-stately-homes-of-a-five-and-dime-store-founder.html', 'snippet': 'The surviving Woolworth houses in Glen Cove and Manhattan exist today in different states of grace; some are single-family homes, and one has been chopped into smaller co-ops.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Crescent Beach Road in Glen Cove, on Long Island, is a pleasant suburban stretch, all pitched roofs and tidy green lawns. On a leisurely drive, it might look like a million other serene spots around the country, until suddenly, something very different emerged from around the bend: a towering marble archway about 15 feet high, which appears at the side of the road like a gateway to another era, fallen from the sky.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/14/nyregion/APPRAISAL1/APPRAISAL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/14/nyregion/APPRAISAL1/APPRAISAL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/14/nyregion/APPRAISAL1/APPRAISAL1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 448, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/14/nyregion/APPRAISAL1/APPRAISAL1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 896, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/14/nyregion/APPRAISAL1/APPRAISAL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/14/nyregion/APPRAISAL1/APPRAISAL1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/14/nyregion/APPRAISAL1/APPRAISAL1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Stately Homes of a Five-and-Dime Store Founder', 'kicker': 'The Appraisal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'His Discount Stores Are Gone, but Not His Lavish Homes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restoration and Renovation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Long Island (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mansions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Woolworth Building (NYC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T14:23:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth A. Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f91b6d2-7f30-539d-a860-bdc1cff3bac7', 'word_count': 1168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f91b6d2-7f30-539d-a860-bdc1cff3bac7'}, {'abstract': 'Entrants for the project, called Gesamt, are asked to create short films or recordings inspired by one of six existing works, including James Joyce’s “Ulysses,” August Strindberg’s “The Family” and the Zeppelinfield in Nuremberg, created by Hitler’s main architect, Albert Speer.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/lars-von-trier-seeks-film-submissions-for-user-generated-project/', 'snippet': 'Entrants for the project, called Gesamt, are asked to create short films or recordings inspired by one of six existing works, including James Joyce’s “Ulysses,” August Strindberg’s “The Family” and the Zeppelinfield in Nuremberg, created by Hitler...', 'lead_paragraph': 'While he prepares for his next act of cinematic provocation, the Danish director Lars von Trier is inviting audiences to engage in their own acts of button-pushing filmmaking as part of a user-generated project that seeks reinterpretations of well-known works by Paul Gauguin, August Strindberg and Albert Speer, among others.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lars von Trier Seeks Film Submissions for User-Generated Project', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Von Trier Seeks Works From the People', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Sammy Jr', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gauguin, Paul', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Joyce, James', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strindberg, August', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'von Trier, Lars', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Copenhagen (Denmark)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Denmark', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T14:38:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7757bf2-6bf3-5e7e-be1b-89e1ea2dd1f1', 'word_count': 349, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7757bf2-6bf3-5e7e-be1b-89e1ea2dd1f1'}, {'abstract': 'The best of readers’ looks for the w