eep-their-pouches-clean.html', 'snippet': 'Kangaroo mothers use their tongues to clean out their pouches, and they can hold embryos in suspended development, waiting for their pouches to empty.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q. How does a female kangaroo rid itself of the dirt that its joey brings into its pouch as it jumps in and out? ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/14/science/14QA/14QA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1019, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/14/science/14QA/14QA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1493, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/14/science/14QA/14QA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/14/science/14QA/14QA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/14/science/14QA/14QA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Marsupial Maintenance', 'kicker': 'Q & A', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Marsupial Maintenance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kangaroos', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T17:36:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By C. Claiborne Ray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'C.', 'middlename': 'Claiborne', 'lastname': 'Ray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f6d6d8b-8f75-512f-b205-0214a6c7719a', 'word_count': 243, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f6d6d8b-8f75-512f-b205-0214a6c7719a'}, {'abstract': 'It’s the gullible news media.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/romneyryan-the-real-target/', 'snippet': 'It’s the gullible news media.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So, let me clarify what I believe is really going on in the choice of Paul Ryan as VP nominee. It is not about satisfying the conservative base, which was motivated anyway by Obama-hatred; it is not about refocusing on the issues, because R&R are both determined to avoid providing any of the crucial specifics about their plans. It is — as Jonathan Chait also seems to understand — about exploiting the gullibility and vanity of the news media, in much the same way that George W. Bush did in 2000.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney/Ryan: The Real Target', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T17:54:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1285bbd-fbcd-5918-a879-bcd474ec5b65', 'word_count': 455, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1285bbd-fbcd-5918-a879-bcd474ec5b65'}, {'abstract': 'It was Ambrose’s second consecutive victory at the road circuit, one of two on the Nascar circuit.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/monday-motorsports-marcos-ambrose-surges-late-to-win-sprint-cup-race-at-watkins-glen/', 'snippet': 'It was Ambrose’s second consecutive victory at the road circuit, one of two on the Nascar circuit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In what some race analysts called the most thrilling race finish of the 2012 Nascar Sprint Cup season, Marcos Ambrose secured a victory at Watkins Glen on Sunday, seizing the lead on the final lap and aggressively defending his position against Brad Keselowski for his second consecutive win at the road course.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monday Motorsports: Marcos Ambrose Surges Late to Win Sprint Cup Race at Watkins Glen', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T17:54:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b5fd43f-8175-5f4d-a7b6-6e1f9d3d857c', 'word_count': 480, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b5fd43f-8175-5f4d-a7b6-6e1f9d3d857c'}, {'abstract': 'As it seeks to reinvent Motorola, Google cuts jobs. | Britain cleans up and tallies its tab after the Olympics.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/business-day-live-olympics-clean-up/', 'snippet': 'As it seeks to reinvent Motorola, Google cuts jobs. | Britain cleans up and tallies its tab after the Olympics.', 'lead_paragraph': ' As it seeks to reinvent Motorola, Google cuts jobs. | Britain cleans up and tallies its tab after the Olympics.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Business Day Live: Olympics Clean-Up', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T17:55:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f3d114e-5479-5768-a5d7-2d984c36b01a', 'word_count': 21, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f3d114e-5479-5768-a5d7-2d984c36b01a'}, {'abstract': 'The crush at Heathrow and T.S.A. complaints and approval in our weekly capsule of travel news.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/monday-walkabout-813-heathrow-crowds-and-t-s-a-plaudits-and-complaints/', 'snippet': 'The crush at Heathrow and T.S.A. complaints and approval in our weekly capsule of travel news.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One Last Olympic Record After last night’s closing ceremony of the London 2012 Games, Heathrow Airport expects that today’s passenger volume will break the record of 236,955 passengers set on July 24. To accommodate the influx of travelers, Heathrow has built a temporary Games Terminal to assist the 10,100 athletes and Games officials and their 37,900 bags. (BBC)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monday Walkabout – 8/13: Heathrow Crowds and T.S.A. Plaudits and Complaints', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRPORTS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Racial Profiling', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Heathrow Airport (London)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Transportation Security Administration', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T17:57:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9035fc84-3696-556b-996c-24872f2ce6b3', 'word_count': 231, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9035fc84-3696-556b-996c-24872f2ce6b3'}, {'abstract': 'Mitt Romney naming Representative Paul D. Ryan as his vice-presidential choice could make Mr. Ryan’s home state of Wisconsin a much closer contest.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/could-ryan-tip-wisconsin-toward-romney/', 'snippet': 'Mitt Romney naming Representative Paul D. Ryan as his vice-presidential choice could make Mr. Ryan’s home state of Wisconsin a much closer contest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today we continue our Presidential Geography series, a one-by-one examination of the peculiarities that drive the politics in all 50 states and the District of Columbia. Today’s stop: Wisconsin, the Badger State. FiveThirtyEight spoke with Charles Franklin, the director of the Marquette University Law School Poll and a professor in the political science department at the University of Wisconsin-Madison.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Could Ryan Tip Wisconsin Toward Romney?', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T18:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Micah Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Micah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ebf4272-c8eb-51dc-8652-e4ab264dd48d', 'word_count': 1208, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ebf4272-c8eb-51dc-8652-e4ab264dd48d'}, {'abstract': 'Mitt Romney’s running mate lacks experience in foreign policy, and in the private sector.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/what-paul-ryan-doesnt-offer/', 'snippet': 'Mitt Romney’s running mate lacks experience in foreign policy, and in the private sector.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anyone who’s picked up a newspaper or turned on a TV news program in the last 48 hours knows by now that Paul Ryan, Mitt Romney’s running mate, is best known as the very conservative author of a very conservative budget plan, which would cut $6 trillion from federal spending over the next 10 years. Anyone who’s ventured below the fold probably also knows that he’s a social conservative: He opposes same-sex marriage and has said that he’s “as pro-life as a person gets,” which seems accurate since he’s the co-sponsor of a bill that would grant fetuses “all the legal and constitutional attributes and privileges of personhood.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Paul Ryan Doesn’t Offer', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vice Presidents and Vice Presidency (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Paul D Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T18:01:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Juliet Lapidos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Juliet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lapidos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e283ade-4e65-5c92-a35c-90880c0a0133', 'word_count': 473, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e283ade-4e65-5c92-a35c-90880c0a0133'}, {'abstract': 'The self-invented, devil-may-care attitude that’s characterized the irreverent sound of so many New York bands has had a big influence on Skaters.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/the-sound-of-new-yorks-skaters/', 'snippet': 'The self-invented, devil-may-care attitude that’s characterized the irreverent sound of so many New York bands has had a big influence on Skaters.', 'lead_paragraph': '“None of us can skateboard. We just don’t have the coordination, I guess,” says Michael Ian Cummings, the lead singer for a band called, of all things, Skaters. “The name just reminds me of my youth and the way I felt listening to music and hanging out with my friends running around the city being a hooligan.” The self-invented, devil-may-care attitude that’s characterized both skate culture and the irreverent sound of so many New York bands (the Ramones and Suicide come to mind) has had a big influence on Skaters, who released their debut EP, “Schemers,” for free on the Web earlier this year. A rose-colored, wistful affection for the city is shared by the group’s three members — Cummings, Noah Rubin (drums) and Joshua Hubbard (guitar) — and can be heard loud and clear in songs like “Good Weird Woman,” with its subway-station-sounding saxophone, and “Are We Just Doomed?” a bittersweet account of leaving home and hardly sleeping, something any New York band worth its salt knows intimately. “I couldn’t imagine making or listening to this music in any other environment,” Cummings says. And even without the skateboards or skills, Skaters do look the part. Dressed in the British men’s-wear label Folk, as they were for their T photo shoot, there’s no covering up their romantically ragged edges.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Sound of New York’s Skaters', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T18:03:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alex Frank', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alex', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Frank', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5f7bc65-5100-5750-b990-8be8f275a43e', 'word_count': 231, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5f7bc65-5100-5750-b990-8be8f275a43e'}, {'abstract': 'Responding to a report in The New York Post, CNN said it would be adding weekend programs that are similar to a documentary-style travel show hosted by Anthony Bourdain that it will begin showing next year.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/cnn-to-promote-nonfiction-tv-not-reality-tv-network-says/', 'snippet': 'Responding to a report in The New York Post, CNN said it would be adding weekend programs that are similar to a documentary-style travel show hosted by Anthony Bourdain that it will begin showing next year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CNN says it is not getting into the reality television business, though it is considering adding weekend programs that are similar to a documentary-style travel show hosted by Anthony Bourdain that it will begin showing next year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'CNN to Promote Nonfiction TV, not Reality TV, Network Says', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bourdain, Anthony', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CNN', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T18:07:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill 