'_id': 'nyt://article/a4ccd7ad-6e45-535d-83d3-ea2ef18e37f4', 'word_count': 221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4ccd7ad-6e45-535d-83d3-ea2ef18e37f4'}, {'abstract': 'My gratitude as an ex-pat to my live-in nanny pushed me to recognize the agonizing realities of life for many domestic workers abroad.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/the-heartache-of-the-migrant-nanny/', 'snippet': 'My gratitude as an ex-pat to my live-in nanny pushed me to recognize the agonizing realities of life for many domestic workers abroad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Domestic workers: the nannies, cooks and cleaners that make many families’ lives so much better, are at a high risk of being abused and exploited. Because the work they do takes place in private homes, they are invisible and often can’t get government protection.On Aug. 6, the International Labor Organization’s Convention on Domestic Work was ratified – setting international standards to protect the world’s 50 million to 100 million domestic workers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Heartache of the Migrant Nanny', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Domestic Service', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Singapore', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T18:13:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Francesca Segrè', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Francesca', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Segrè', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'ra