ract': 'Beacon High School is getting a brand new facility on West 44th Street in Manhattan. When construction is done, the screened school will have a cafeteria, gym, and technology and arts labs. There also will be space for the popular school to accept about 300 more students.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/schoolbook/2012/08/13/city-breaks-ground-on-new-beacon-hs-facility/', 'snippet': 'Beacon High School is getting a brand new facility on West 44th Street in Manhattan. When construction is done, the screened school will have a cafeteria, gym, and technology and arts labs. There also will be space for the popular school to accept...', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'City Breaks Ground on New Beacon High School Building', 'kicker': 'SchoolBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T20:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By HITEN SAMTANI', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hiten', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'SAMTANI', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/99d73700-2677-5e47-8cc1-16b459f7aa63', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99d73700-2677-5e47-8cc1-16b459f7aa63'}, {'abstract': 'Gallant & Jones produce a North American version of a British chair, sold through Lin Morris.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/garden/gallant-jones-deck-chairs.html', 'snippet': 'Gallant & Jones produce a North American version of a British chair, sold through Lin Morris.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2009, Tamra Gallant, a Vancouver-based entrepreneur with a background in woodworking, visited Great Britain and saw deck chairs in parks and on beaches that she wished could be readily found in North America. She founded Gallant & Jones with Gwyneth Jones, a textile designer, and began producing similar chairs in white oak and organic cotton fabrics. “We made them ourselves at first because we’re perfectionists,” Ms. Jones said. Naturally protected from water damage and rot, the oak frame has a coating of ultraviolet-resistant oil for further safekeeping. The sling and pillow fabrics are easily removable for cleaning and storage. Available through Lin Morris starting at $280, lin-morris.com.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/13/garden/13CURRENTS1/13CURRENTS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 899, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/13/garden/13CURRENTS1/13CURRENTS1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1798, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/13/garden/13CURRENTS1/13CURRENTS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/13/garden/13CURRENTS1/13CURRENTS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/13/garden/13CURRENTS1/13CURRENTS1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Deck Chairs That Crossed the Atlantic', 'kicker': 'Currents | Furniture', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Deck Chairs That Crossed the Atlantic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jones, Gwyneth', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chairs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Furniture', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T20:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6787d0d-9f47-512f-9bc1-6d4ec01867fd', 'word_count': 112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6787d0d-9f47-512f-9bc1-6d4ec01867fd'}, {'abstract': 'Alexandra Mayzler recently asked the group for advice on one of her latest ideas for her company, Thinking Caps Tutoring. Settling on a tag line that sounds good and differentiates the company has been difficult.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/a-tutoring-company-in-search-of-a-tagline/', 'snippet': 'Alexandra Mayzler recently asked the group for advice on one of her latest ideas for her company, Thinking Caps Tutoring. Settling on a tag line that sounds good and differentiates the company has been difficult.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the last couple of months, Alexandra Mayzler, a member of the She Owns It business group, has struggled to come up with a new tag line for her company, Thinking Caps Tutoring. Thinking Caps, which she started in her college dorm room, has outgrown its current tag, “Students teaching students.” Settling on something new that both sounds good and differentiates the company has been so difficult that she wonders how she ever came up with the first one.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Tutoring Company in Search of a Tag Line', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slogans and Mottoes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tutors and Tutoring', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T20:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adriana Gardella', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adriana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gardella', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/416d74ff-8e74-58ee-9afe-916acf1cfa46', 'word_count': 263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/416d74ff-8e74-58ee-9afe-916acf1cfa46'}, {'abstract': 'On a visit to the United Nations, the diva made a standout statement in a white, businesslike ensemble.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/look-of-the-moment-beyonce-knowles-2/', 'snippet': 'On a visit to the United Nations, the diva made a standout statement in a white, businesslike ensemble.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Look: White Flag. A serious-business dress comes with a number of curves — peplum, bell sleeve, exaggerated shoulders — making a standout statement, with just a hint of décolletage.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Look of The Moment | Beyoncé Knowles', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T20:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Barsamian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barsamian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a8bf448-4d75-592e-8a4f-88d14fb700c9', 'word_count': 60, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a8bf448-4d75-592e-8a4f-88d14fb700c9'}, {