'_id': 'nyt://article/c8603beb-ed1a-569a-92c2-9a82c2eb5af0', 'word_count': 642, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8603beb-ed1a-569a-92c2-9a82c2eb5af0'}, {'abstract': 'The Bard Music Festival devoted the first of two straight weekends to Camille Saint-Saëns.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/14/arts/music/a-bard-music-festival-weekend-of-saint-saens.html', 'snippet': 'The Bard Music Festival devoted the first of two straight weekends to Camille Saint-Saëns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ANNANDALE-ON-HUDSON, N.Y. — Memorializing the French composer Camille Saint-Saëns nine days after his death, in an article published on Christmas Day in 1921, the New York Times music critic Richard Aldrich wrote of Saint-Saëns’s stature and accomplishments in the most glowing terms. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/14/arts/BARD-jp/BARD-jp-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 699, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/14/arts/BARD-jp/BARD-jp-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1398, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/14/arts/BARD-jp/BARD-jp-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/14/arts/BARD-jp/BARD-jp-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/14/arts/BARD-jp/BARD-jp-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Celebrating a Once-Mighty Tree, Pruned but Hardly Forgotten', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Celebrating a Once-Mighty Tree, Pruned but Hardly Forgotten', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bard Music Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Saint-Saens, Camille', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T21:41:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a885616-b730-5c2c-8474-907a53821709', 'word_count': 1248, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a885616-b730-5c2c-8474-907a53821709'}, {'abstract': 'U.S. Coach Jurgen Klinsmann has put together a curious lineup for Wednesday’s match at Mexico.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/against-mexico-is-the-u-s-in-it-to-win-it/', 'snippet': 'U.S. Coach Jurgen Klinsmann has put together a curious lineup for Wednesday’s match at Mexico.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On June 8, after the United States defeated Antigua and Barbuda, 3-1, in a World Cup qualifier in Tampa, Fla., Coach Jurgen Klinsmann fielded a question about the difference between friendly matches and World Cup qualifiers. To hear him tell it, little, if anything, should distinguish the two.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Against Mexico, Is the U.S. in It to Win It?', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Cup (Soccer)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Altidore, Jozy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dempsey, Clint', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Howard, Tim', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klinsmann, Jurgen', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T21:51:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Godfrey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Godfrey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d0057d5-fe2c-5433-8ae5-33c093c6acb3', 'word_count': 780, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d0057d5-fe2c-5433-8ae5-33c093c6acb3'}, {'abstract': 'The cheerleading musical’s ticket sales were promising enough for the producers to announce an extension of performances.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/bring-it-on-starts-modestly-at-sis-boom-box-office/', 'snippet': 'The cheerleading musical’s ticket sales were promising enough for the producers to announce an extension of performances.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The only new Broadway musical of the summer, “Bring It On,” grossed $472,810 in its first full week after opening on Aug. 1, a modest showing at the box office that was still promising enough for the producers to announce a three-month extension of performances. “Bring It On,” inspired by the 2000 movie about a high school cheerleading rivalry, took in only 39 percent of its maximum possible gross last week – in part because many choice seats were not on sale at three performances. Those tickets instead went complimentary to late-coming critics and other members of the media.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Bring It On’ Starts Modestly at Sis-Boom-Box Office', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martin, Ricky', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T21:52:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a0cfc4c-f6c2-57ff-9f2d-9db483fc45cd', 'word_count': 276, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a0cfc4c-f6c2-57ff-9f2d-9db483fc45cd'}, {'abstract': 'That total over 17 days made the Games the most watched entertainment or sports event ever on American television.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/nbc-says-nearly-220-million-watched-the-olympics/', 'snippet': 'That total over 17 days made the Games the most watched entertainment or sports event ever on American television.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NBC announced on Monday that its coverage of the London Olympics was the most watched entertainment or sporting event ever on American television.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'NBC Says Nearly 220 Million Watched the Olympics', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T22:03:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19bc27c6-9061-549c-bcae-cfa633cdddc7', 'word_count': 342, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19bc27c6-9061-549c-bcae-cfa633cdddc7'}, {'abstract': 'Birdsong, more anxious than chipper, permeated four concerts by the International Contemporary Ensemble that were part of the Mostly Mozart Festival.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/14/arts/music/international-contemporary-ensemble-at-mostly-mozart.html', 'snippet': 'Birdsong, more anxious than chipper, permeated four concerts by the International Contemporary Ensemble that were part of the Mostly Mozart Festival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The most famous bird in Mozart lore is the starling he bought with some silver coins in 1784 and buried, with much part-serious solemnity, three years later. He transcribed its song in his notebook along with the price he paid for it — 34 kreutzer — and the melody, which he may well have taught it, closely resembles the carefree opening theme of his Piano Concerto No. 17.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/14/arts/ICE1/ICE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 697, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/14/arts/ICE1/ICE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1394, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Finding Their Way to Concert Halls: Blackbirds, Sea Gulls and Chickens', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Finding Their Way to Concert Halls: Blackbirds, Sea Gulls and Chickens', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Contemporary Ensemble', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ebene Quartet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mostly Mozart Festival', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T22:05:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/336f9272-f3c7-555b-bc1e-3dbeaebda096', 'word_count': 831, 'uri': 'nyt://article/336f9272-f3c7-555b-bc1e-3dbeaebda096'}, {'abstract': 'The New Yorker on Romney’s faith and the Mormon relationship to money.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/romney-mormonism-and-money/', 'snippet': 'The New Yorker on Romney’s faith and the Mormon relationship to money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My Sunday column\xa0— written before the Paul Ryan pick, but still relevant in its aftermath\xa0— argues that Mitt Romney’s understandable reluctance to talk about his Mormon faith has cut him off from what would otherwise be a very natural part of his campaign narrative, both in personal and philosophical terms. My argument runs counter to some of the arguments in Adam Gopnik’s tour d’horizon of Mormonism in a recent issue of the New Yorker, and particularly this passage:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney, Mormonism and Money', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-13T22:07:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person'