+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bloomberg News', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bloomberg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'News', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/14c8a4a5-9483-5d6e-bc29-dd26a080f001', 'word_count': 315, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14c8a4a5-9483-5d6e-bc29-dd26a080f001'}, {'abstract': 'A reader says we should not censor musical expression, no matter how offensive.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/14/opinion/free-speech-even-if-hateful.html', 'snippet': 'A reader says we should not censor musical expression, no matter how offensive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Free Speech, Even if Hateful', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Free Speech, Even if Hateful', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Censorship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Neo Nazi Groups', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-14T01:44:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/390dd38e-c902-56a3-8f0c-0ed807c92da5', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/390dd38e-c902-56a3-8f0c-0ed807c92da5'}, {'abstract': 'How to find good writing in that dark, cavernous place called the mind.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/13/where-do-sentences-come-from/', 'snippet': 'How to find good writing in that dark, cavernous place called the mind.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sift the debris of a young writer’s education, and you find dreadful things — strictures, prohibitions, dos, don’ts, an unnatural and nearly neurotic obsession with style, argument and transition. Yet in that debris you find no traces of a fundamental question: where do sentences come from? This is a philosophical question, as valuable in the asking as in the answering. But it’s a practical question, too. Think about it long enough, and you begin to realize that many, if not most, of the things we believe about writing are false. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Do Sentences Come From?', 'kicker': 'Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reading and Writing Skills', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-14T01:45:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Verlyn Klinkenborg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Verlyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klinkenborg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/312c2739-1463-5184-b9ba-95e047c47fbe', 'word_count': 1013, 'uri': 'nyt://article/312c2739-1463-5184-b9ba-95e047c47fbe'}, {'abstract': 'Stolen e-mails released by WikiLeaks set off a flurry of concern about a counterterrorist software program called TrapWire, but the fears appear to be wildly exaggerated.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/14/us/trapwire-antiterrorist-software-leaks-set-off-web-furor.html', 'snippet': 'Stolen e-mails released by WikiLeaks set off a flurry of concern about a counterterrorist software program called TrapWire, but the fears appear to be wildly exaggerated.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — A new release of stolen corporate e-mails by WikiLeaks has set off a flurry of concern and speculation around the world about a counterterrorist software program called TrapWire, which analyzes images from surveillance cameras and other data to try to identify terrorists planning attacks.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'WikiLeaks Stirs Global Fears on Antiterrorist Software', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'WikiLeaks Stirs Global Fears on Antiterrorist Software', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'TrapWire Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'WikiLeaks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Strategic Forecasting Inc (Stratfor)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Central Intelligence Agency', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Anonymous (Internet Group)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberattacks and Hackers', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-14T01:45:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Shane', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/62546349-8953-59c5-aa4a-74ef70404e3b', 'word_count': 523, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62546349-8953-59c5-aa4a-74ef70404e3b'}, {'abstract': 'Israeli leaders are again talking about possible military action to stop Iran’s nuclear ambitions. But diplomacy, not an attack, is still the best solution.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/14/opinion/israel-and-iran.html', 'snippet': 'Israeli leaders are again talking about possible military action to stop Iran’s nuclear ambitions. But diplomacy, not an attack, is still the best solution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Israeli leaders are again talking about possible military action against Iran. This is, at best, mischievous and, at worst, irresponsible, especially when diplomacy has time to run. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Israel and Iran', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Israel and Iran', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-14T01:45:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/b28ea3cc-b3d6-5009-9a72-3f98ebde8fba', 'word_count': 471, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b28ea3cc-b3d6-5009-9a72-3f98ebde8fba'}, {'abstract': 'Dokes used his punishing fists to become the world heavyweight boxing champion in 1982 and, 15 years later, to beat his girlfriend so savagely that he was convicted of attempted murder.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/14/sports/michael-dokes-larger-than-life-heavyweight-boxer-dies-at-54.html', 'snippet': 'Dokes used his punishing fists to become the world heavyweight boxing champion in 1982 and, 15 years later, to beat his girlfriend so savagely that he was convicted of attempted murder.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michael Dokes, who used his swift, punishing fis