ommiting-photography-in-public/', 'snippet': 'Remembering Helen Gurley Brown, and moderators are announced for the presidential and vice-presidential debates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Helen Gurley Brown, who died on Monday at 90, was remembered for her transformation of Cosmopolitan magazine, and ultimately the field of women’s magazines. She was hired to run the stodgy magazine in 1965 after her book “Sex and the Single Girl” sold millions copies and was turned into a movie starring Natalie Wood. As Margolit Fox writes in her obituary: “Her influence on Cosmopolitan was swift and certain: she did not so much revamp the magazine as vamp it.” As for her influence on society, Ms. Fox concludes:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: How H.G.B. (Re)vamped Magazines, and Commiting Photography in Public', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-14T12:58:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0df55387-2e53-5c2e-99f2-c9426442de3d', 'word_count': 438, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0df55387-2e53-5c2e-99f2-c9426442de3d'}, {'abstract': 'Five days after computer security experts announced their discovery of Gauss, a computer virus targeting computers in Lebanon, they asked mathematicians and cryptographers to help them decrypt its mysterious “warhead.”', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/14/unable-to-crack-computer-virus-security-researchers-issue-cry-for-help/', 'snippet': 'Five days after computer security experts announced their discovery of Gauss, a computer virus targeting computers in Lebanon, they asked mathematicians and cryptographers to help them decrypt its mysterious “warhead.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Five days after a computer security firm disclosed the discovery of Gauss, a computer virus targeting computers in Lebanon, they issued a cry for help.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Unable to Crack Computer Virus, Security Firm Seeks Help', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-14T13:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicole Perlroth', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicole', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perlroth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41ffa6f5-3477-5020-933a-e4d06a794830', 'word_count': 479, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41ffa6f5-3477-5020-933a-e4d06a794830'}, {'abstract': 'The London-born, New York-based Georgia Louise uses a combination of cutting-edge machines, meditation, massage and holistic nutritional advice to tend to the skin — and spirits — of A-listers.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/14/glow-biz/', 'snippet': 'The London-born, New York-based Georgia Louise uses a combination of cutting-edge machines, meditation, massage and holistic nutritional advice to tend to the skin — and spirits — of A-listers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'She isn’t called the ‘‘cosmic facialist’’ for nothing. The London-born, New York-based Georgia Louise uses a combination of cutting-edge machines, meditation, massage and holistic nutritional advice to tend to the skin — and spirits — of A-listers like Emma Stone, Cameron Diaz and Linda Evangelista. Go to georgialouise.co.uk; (212) 388-0800.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '128', 'source': 'The New York Tim