early 70’s congregated in a dilapidated plaza in central Madrid. Those wizened by past experience brought folding chairs; others tore off cardboard slabs from a nearby recycling heap to fashion makeshift cushions. Newcomers stood or crouched.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Democracy in Spain', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economics (Theory and Philosophy)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreclosures', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Madrid (Spain)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Spain', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-15T13:14:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Blitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Blitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d195bbe2-ce27-5548-9eb5-09c353f6cd48', 'word_count': 637, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d195bbe2-ce27-5548-9eb5-09c353f6cd48'}, {'abstract': 'Iran chatter – whether to attack; what to squirrel away in home shelters – is the only game in town this summer in Israel.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/15/the-things-they-stored/', 'snippet': 'Iran chatter – whether to attack; what to squirrel away in home shelters – is the only game in town this summer in Israel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TEL AVIV — I got home Monday evening to find in my mailbox an official reminder: Did we have enough gas masks for the whole family? Getting one, or replacing an old one, is simple: the distribution center isn’t far; it’s at the building where the Boy Scouts’ meet around the corner, right near where my youngest boy goes to school.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Things They Stored', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Text Messaging', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABRAMS, ELLIOTT', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Netanyahu, Benjamin', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Council on Foreign Relations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tel Aviv (Israel)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-15T13:32:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shmuel Rosner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shmuel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/22f299bf-71a9-549d-bae8-2a6d5822b3a3', 'word_count': 664, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22f299bf-71a9-549d-bae8-2a6d5822b3a3'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/15/what-were-reading-501/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Lo-Down: As the demographics of Manhattan’s Chinatown change, why shouldn’t its restaurants? Wilson Tang, the second-generation proprietor of Nam Wah Tea Parlor, discusses his own evolving ideas in an age that prizes “authenticity.” — Eric Asimov', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-15T13:36:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6eed33a5-914e-564c-83d8-a131c8e1c59a', 'word_count': 393, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6eed33a5-914e-564c-83d8-a131c8e1c59a'}, {'abstract': 'Also, NBC’s ratings fall back to earth but “Major Crimes” surges on TNT.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/15/the-breakfast-meeting-a-new-chief-at-the-times-company-and-farewells-to-h-g-b/', 'snippet': 'Also, NBC’s ratings fall back to earth but “Major Crimes” surges on TNT.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reaching well outside the newspaper business, The New York Times Company announced that it had hired Mark Thompson, the departing director general of the BBC, as its next president and chief executive. Mr. Thompson has worked almost his entire career at the public broadcaster, making him an unusual choice. Robert Andrews of paidContent gives a British perspective on the hire, and Ken Doctor, the newspaper analyst, offers his take.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: A New Chief at the Times Company and Farewells to H.G.B.', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-15T13:39:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Edito