om events that make up our lives.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Adoption, Destiny and Magical Thinking', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADOPTIONS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-15T14:21:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew Hutson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hutson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4052175-82d0-50fc-a833-e6dbc7a68ae0', 'word_count': 876, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f4052175-82d0-50fc-a833-e6dbc7a68ae0'}, {'abstract': 'The New York police have come under scrutiny over racial disparities in their stop-and-frisk practice. But the frequency of officers’ use of force in those encounters is often overlooked.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/16/nyregion/in-police-stop-data-pockets-where-force-is-used-more-often.html', 'snippet': 'The New York police have come under scrutiny over racial disparities in their stop-and-frisk practice. But the frequency of officers’ use of force in those encounters is often overlooked.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The crowded neighborhoods of the West Bronx come alive at night. Residents, young and old, cluster around door stoops. Teenagers fill playground basketball courts. Police officers from the nearby 44th and 46th Precincts patrol the streets, from time to time stopping and frisking young men, mostly black and Latino. And when they do, statistics show, they use physical force far more often than the police do anywhere else in the city.', 'print_secti