g', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/16/garden/16FURNITURE_SPAN/16FURNITURE_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No House Training Needed', 'kicker': 'Currents | Furniture', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No House Training Needed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANIMALS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Furniture', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chairs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T00:01:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rima Suqi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rima', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Suqi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0b22705-4619-512d-a024-5c0d3bee14f8', 'word_count': 167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0b22705-4619-512d-a024-5c0d3bee14f8'}, {'abstract': 'Sponsored by Hollister House Garden and the Garden Conservancy, the event will bring together master landscape designers of the Northeast.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/16/garden/in-late-august-a-chance-to-learn-from-master-gardeners.html', 'snippet': 'Sponsored by Hollister House Garden and the Garden Conservancy, the event will bring together master landscape designers of the Northeast.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From Aug. 24 to 26, gardeners may be inspired by earth-friendly techniques while exploring the art historian George Schoellkopf’s Hollister House Garden in Washington, Conn., at the symposium “Gardening Anew: Fresh Perspectives on the Garden.” Sponsored by Hollister House Garden and the Garden Conservancy, it will convene master landscape designers and plant gurus of the Northeast. Speakers will include Edwina von Gal, a designer whose clients include Richard Serra and Calvin Klein; William Cullina, executive director of Coastal Maine Botanical Gardens, a 250-acre expanse of organically grown native plants; and Eric T. Fleisher, director of horticulture for Battery Park City Parks Conservancy, who will discuss composting, soil biology and water conservation. Late-season plants from New England’s best nurseries will be sold, and there will be tours of seven area gardens, including Mr. Schoellkopf’s 25-acre masterpiece, developed over three decades.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/16/garden/16EVENTS_SPAN/16EVENTS_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/16/garden/16EVENTS_SPAN/16EVENTS_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/16/garden/16EVENTS_SPAN/16EVENTS_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 426, 'width': 640, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/16/garden/16EVENTS_SPAN/16EVENTS_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 426, 'width': 640, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/16/garden/16EVENTS_SPAN/16EVENTS_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/16/garden/16EVENTS_SPAN/16EVENTS_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Connecticut, a Convergence of Green Thumbs', 'kicker': 'Currents | Events', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Connecticut, a Convergence of Green Thumbs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Garden Conservancy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T00:01:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anne Raver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Raver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d7dd39a-0947-50c2-bcca-194a836f26be', 'word_count': 161, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d7dd39a-0947-50c2-bcca-194a836f26be'}, {'abstract': 'At Comma Workshop in Colorado, quilts are embroidered with poetry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/16/garden/quilts-stitched-together-with-poetry.html', 'snippet': 'At Comma Workshop in Colorado, quilts are embroidered with poetry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s not easy to read Kerry Larkin’s poetry without feeling the tug of the natural world. Truth be told, it’s not easy to read Ms. Larkin’s poetry, period. Sometimes the words are embroidered in white on white, and the messages are more like texture. Or in A Perfect Moon, above left, which mentions “pines that resemble construction paper cut outs against the almost black sky,” the lines are alternating bands of autumnal color.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/16/garden/16GOODS1_SPAN/16GOODS_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2