ed members of a criminal cell had confessed to the killing of five journalists in the state of Veracruz this year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/16/world/americas/confessions-are-reported-in-journalist-killings-in-mexico.html', 'snippet': 'The Mexican authorities announced Wednesday that captured members of a criminal cell had confessed to the killing of five journalists in the state of Veracruz this year.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The Mexican authorities announced Wednesday that captured members of a criminal cell had confessed to the killing of five journalists in the state of Veracruz this year. According to the state attorney, the criminals, one of whom was detained last week carrying identification cards belonging to Irasema Becerra, a recently slain newspaper employee, admitted to a total of 36 crimes. Press freedom activists expressed dissatisfaction with the announcement, saying that it was unacceptable for the authorities to announce the resolution of these cases based on confessions rather than conducting full investigations. A 2012 report from Human Rights Watch attributed the country’s failing justice system in part to the “acceptance by some judges of confessions obtained through torture and other ill treatment.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mexico: Confessions Are Reported in the Killing of 5 Journalists', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | The Americas', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mexico: Confessions Are Reported In the Killing of 5 Journalists', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Confessions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Veracruz (Mexico)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of the Press', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T00:28:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karla Zabludovsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zabludovsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/9050a590-3750-5e62-ad6c-504de0c5e4f5', 'word_count': 122, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9050a590-3750-5e62-ad6c-504de0c5e4f5'}, {'abstract': 'The Mujahedeen Khalq, also known as M.E.K., has been lobbying to be removed from the State Department’s terrorist list, but its refusal to leave a camp in Iraq has caused concern.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/16/world/middleeast/iranian-group-mujahedeen-khalq-may-remain-on-us-terror-list.html', 'snippet': 'The Mujahedeen Khalq, also known as M.E.K., has been lobbying to be removed from the State Department’s terrorist list, but its refusal to leave a camp in Iraq has caused concern.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — State Department officials are preparing for a possible decision by Secretary of State Hillary Rodham Clinton to redesignate an Iranian opposition group as a terrorist organization, in part because of the group’s resistance to abandoning its camp in Iraq, two American officials said Wednesday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iran Group May Remain on U.S. Terror List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iraq: Iranian Group May Return To U.S. Terrorist List Over Camp', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mujahedeen Khalq', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank':