rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T03:44:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cieply', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cieply', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/efb9d6e8-b955-5a14-bae7-f361b83e3ed6', 'word_count': 405, 'uri': 'nyt://article/efb9d6e8-b955-5a14-bae7-f361b83e3ed6'}, {'abstract': 'Although I have learned a lot since my diagnosis — and I am trying to be hopeful for the future — living with cancer is also just really, really hard. We don’t always talk about those times. We self-censor many parts of the journey.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/16/life-interrupted-putting-a-positive-spin-on-cancer/', 'snippet': 'Although I have learned a lot since my diagnosis — and I am trying to be hopeful for the future — living with cancer is also just really, really hard. We don’t always talk about those times. We self-censor many parts of the journey.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a cancer patient, I think a lot about how we talk about illness — or more often, how we talk around it. Even the word “cancer” is ugly, scary, burdensome — a roadblock for a conversation before it even starts. Who wants to go there? Much of the time, I’d rather not bring it up if I don’t have to — and I’m the one with the disease!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/16/health/16Well_suleika/16Well_suleika-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/16/health/16Well_suleika/16Well_suleika-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subT