sed by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sex Life of Teenagers Is Subject of Study', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sex Life Of Teenagers Is Subject Of Study', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teenagers and Adolescence', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Centers for Disease Control and Prevention', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T04:47:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Douglas Quenqua', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Douglas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Quenqua', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46e27a19-0559-541c-8744-0bd1d66e7751', 'word_count': 469, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46e27a19-0559-541c-8744-0bd1d66e7751'}, {'abstract': 'Overt and covert representations of the Indian-Pakistani relations in Indian cinema over the decades.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/16/how-bollywoods-views-on-pakistan-evolved/', 'snippet': 'Overt and covert representations of the Indian-Pakistani relations in Indian cinema over the decades.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If one looks at India’s national trajectory through the lens of the Hindi film industry, in hundred years of its existence, there will be one major gap: India’s troubled relationship with Pakistan was conveniently ignored by the industry for decades.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Bollywood’s Views on Pakistan Evolved', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T04:49:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Raksha Kumar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Raksha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kumar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aff998b4-38b0-5715-b49a-0da137e02e28', 'word_count': 1104, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aff998b4-38b0-5715-b49a-0da137e02e28'}, {'abstract': 'Did the Obama campaign maneuver Mitt Romney into choosing Paul Ryan as his running mate?', 'web_url': 'https://campaignstops.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/16/blow-the-electoral-art-of-war/', 'snippet': 'Did the Obama campaign maneuver Mitt Romney into choosing Paul Ryan as his running mate?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Attack your opponent’s strength: that has become the fundamental rule of modern politics.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Electoral Art of War', 'kicker': 'Campaign Stops', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Biden, Joseph R Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Paul D Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T05:10:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles M. Blow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization