:56:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc3f5c74-67ea-54c8-91bc-73c5a46b8e88', 'word_count': 235, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc3f5c74-67ea-54c8-91bc-73c5a46b8e88'}, {'abstract': 'Can you correctly spell the adjective that means “like a mountain” or “huge”?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/16/test-yourself-english-aug-16-2012/', 'snippet': 'Can you correctly spell the adjective that means “like a mountain” or “huge”?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The question was written by Shannon Doyne. It comes from the Q.&A. article “Returning to Her Trail of Tears.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | English, Aug. 16, 2012', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T08:31:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/63416a5e-d700-5b69-9ec1-13f51b085677', 'word_count': 47, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63416a5e-d700-5b69-9ec1-13f51b085677'}, {'abstract': 'The anti-graft movement has helped rather than hurt the Congress Party, writes columnist Manu Joseph.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/16/floundering-foes-help-indian-lawmakers/', 'snippet': 'The anti-graft movement has helped rather than hurt the Congress Party, writes columnist Manu Joseph.', 'lead_paragraph': '“There are times when the quality of the people who hate you is the most generous compliment you can receive,” Manu Joseph wrote in The International Herald Tribune. “The Indian National Congress, which heads the government in Delhi, is a beneficiary of its foes.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Floundering Foes Help Indian Lawmakers', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T08:54:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aeeefc50-353b-5e22-b466-1ce5c0660275', 'word_count': 348, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aeeefc50-353b-5e22-b466-1ce5c0660275'}, {'abstract': 'A utility post.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/16/whats-in-the-ryan-plan/', 'snippet': 'A utility post.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A number of commenters have asked for a summary of what’s actually in the Ryan plan. So this is a utility post.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s In The Ryan Plan?', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T08:58:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a3a0417-a297-5b66-8f12-14d0cc857132', 'word_count': 570, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a3a0417-a297-5b66-8f12-14d0cc857132'}, {'abstract': 'In the 1920s, The New York Times made a convention of photographing artists with their works and their subjects. The resulting images show art imitating art imitating life.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/16/artists-caught-on-a-cameras-canvas/', 'snippet': 'In the 1920s, The New York Times made a convention of photographing artists with their works and their subjects. The resulting images show art imitating art imitating life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sometimes art imitates life, sometimes life imitates art. But something special happens when art imitates art imitating life. In the 1920s, with some spillover before and after, The New York Times made a convention of photographing artists with both their works of art and the people (or pets) depicted in those works.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Artists Caught on a Camera’s Canvas', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sculpture', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Benjamin, Walter', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chandler, Howard', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hockney, David', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keller, Helen', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T09:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Leland and Darcy Eveleigh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Darcy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eveleigh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d66943a-4947-51ee-bf5