, 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/16342c6a-d7cc-5cb0-b8b6-ea7507c2c3ec', 'word_count': 674, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16342c6a-d7cc-5cb0-b8b6-ea7507c2c3ec'}, {'abstract': 'The deal to pay $3.6 billion for wireless airwaves owned by Time Warner Cable, Comcast, Cox Communications and Bright House Networks still needs F.C.C. approval.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/16/justice-dept-approves-with-changes-verizon-wireless-spectrum-purchase/', 'snippet': 'The deal to pay $3.6 billion for wireless airwaves owned by Time Warner Cable, Comcast, Cox Communications and Bright House Networks still needs F.C.C. approval.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The United States Justice Department approved a deal struck by Verizon Wireless to purchase spectrum from the country’s largest cable operators, but officials also required that the agreement be altered to protect against higher prices for consumers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Justice Dept. Approves, With Changes, Verizon Wireless Spectrum Purchase', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cellular Telephones', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Communications Commission', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Verizon Communications Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T15:59:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Chozick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chozick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fdef6482-63fc-59af-96a4-17d694cd344c', 'word_count': 230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fdef6482-63fc-59af-96a4-17d694cd344c'}, {'abstract': 'A 1966 classic, updated in time for Fashion Week.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/16/the-get-smythsons-currency-case/', 'snippet': 'A 1966 classic, updated in time for Fashion Week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Back in the 1950s, Katherine Hepburn wouldn’t board a plane without her Smythson address book. In its pages, Hepburn logged meetings with friends like Laurence Olivier, Frank Sinatra and Vivien Leigh. On its cover, she’d stamped her initials K.H. and three places she frequented — London, California and New York — initiating a new way to personalize paper goods. (For memorabilia buffs, Hepburn’s book is currently on display in Smythson’s in-store museum at 4 West 57th Street.) Now, in honor of Hepburn’s brilliant idea, the London stationer is offering four-zipper currency cases embossed with Fashion Week’s four biggest cities — New York, London, Milan and Paris — and available with the option of monogramming, too. An update on the company’s original 1966 currency case, these new ones come in violet, olive and classic brown crocodile.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Get | Smythson’s Currency Case', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T16:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c670861-da71-5a50-8ebf-1821345a7afe', 'word_count': 142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c670861-da71-5a50-8ebf-1821345a7afe'}, {'abstract': 'With the release of the bike messenger chase film “Premium Rush,” a look back at movie chase scenes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2012/08/16/movies/chase-scenes.html', 'snippet': 'With the release of the bike messenger chase film “Premium Rush,” a look back at movie chase scenes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the release of the bike messenger chase film “Premium Rush,” a look back at movie chase scenes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Cinematic Chase', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Premium Rush (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Bullitt (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The French Connection (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Seven-Ups (Movie)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Ben-Hur (Movie)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T16:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Movies', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/b08ba10b-d1d6-5612-80af-534fa02bfb31', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/b08ba10b-d1d6-5612-80af-534fa02bfb31'}, {'abstract': 'Instagram, the popular photo-sharing application, released a new version of its service that will let users plot their archive of photographs on an interactive map of the world.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/16/instagram-refreshes-app-include-photo-maps/', 'snippet': 'Instagram, the popular photo-sharing application, released a new version of its service that will let users plot their archive of photographs on an interactive map of the world.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Instagram may be waiting for its billion-dollar sale to Facebook to be finalized, but the company is not resting on its laurels. The company, working out of offices in San Francisco, continues to crank out updates to its popular photo-sharing application. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/16/blogs/16bits/16bits-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 480, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/16/blogs/16bits/16bits-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 480, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/16/blogs/16bits/16bits-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/16/blogs/16bits/16bits-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/16/blogs/16bits/16bits-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Instagram Refreshes App by Including Photo Maps', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Maps', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Systrom, Kevin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Instagram Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T16:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenna Wortham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wortham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/59412760-b515-5aac-b07b-cdca6f39482e', 'word_count': 409, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59412760-b515-5aac-b07b-cdca6f39482e'}, {'abstract': 'A team of engineers has developed fuel cells that harness a mix of microbes to clean wastewater while producing their own power', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/16/in-fuel-cells-some-hope-for-urban-sanitation/', 'snippet': 'A team of engineers has developed fuel cells that harness a mix of microbes to clean wastewater while producing their own power', 'lead_paragraph': 'As more and more people around the world flock to cities, urban areas in developing nations are struggling to keep up with the human influx and the waste that people produce. In 2010 roughly 2.5 billion people lacked basic sanitation, according to the World Health Organization.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Fuel Cells, Some Hope for Urban Sanitation', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chemicals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Engineering and Engineers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Microbiology', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Third World and Developing Countries', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Urban Areas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Waste Materials and Disposal', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oregon State University', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T16:00:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sophia Li', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sophia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Li', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7cf8c457-3793-5c82-9f49-b7e826cbdc17', 'word_count': 523, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7cf8c457-3793-5c82-9f49-b7e826cbdc17'}, {'abstract': 'There’s money to be made in Precita Park, but there are also neighbors to be lost.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/16/who-owns-that-house/', 'snippet': 'There’s money to be made in Precita Park, but there are also neighbors to be lost.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Owns That House?', 'kicker': 'Townies', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Francisco (Calif)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T16:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Orner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Orner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/91fbda35-5cbc-5eb4-9376-b97ad8923cec', 'word_count': 1083, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91fbda35-5cbc-5eb4-9376-b97ad8923cec'}, {'abstract': 'David Koepp’s “Premium Rush,” starring Joseph Gordon-Levitt as a bike messenger in New York City, largely avoids computerized effects for its stunts in heavy traffic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/19/movies/joseph-gordon-levitt-dodges-traffic-in-premium-rush.html', 'snippet': 'David Koepp’s “Premium Rush,” starring Joseph Gordon-Levitt as a bike messenger in New York City, largely avoids computerized effects for its stunts in heavy traffic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT took a lot of nerve for the director David Koepp to include a chase under an elevated New York City train in “Premium Rush,” about a bike messenger’s frantic race to deliver a package from one end of Manhattan to the other.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/arts/19CHASE1_SPAN/CHASE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/19/arts/19CHASE1_SPAN/CHASE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/arts/19CHASE1_SPAN/CHASE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/arts/19CHASE1_SPAN/CHASE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/arts/19CHASE1_SPAN/CHASE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/19/arts/19CHASE1_SPAN/CHASE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/arts/19CHASE1_SPAN/CHASE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Feverishly Pursuing a Nondigital Chase', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Feverishly Pursuing A Nondigital Chase', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gordon-Levitt, Joseph', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Premium Rush (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stunts and Stuntmen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koepp, David', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bicycles and Bicycling', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T16:00:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tom Roston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/92af5bb3-e3f3-50c9-aaa3-c356a261a2f5', 'word_count': 1265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92af5bb3-e3f3-50c9-aaa3-c356a261a2f5'}, {'abstract': 'A few weeks ago, we presented a minute mystery, hoping to identify the photographer who put together a small yet fascinating Christmas gift for an art teacher in 1915. How close did we come?', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/16/update-whither-eleanors-album/', 'snippet': 'A few weeks ago, we presented a minute mystery, hoping to identify the photographer who put together a small yet fascinating Christmas gift for an art teacher in 1915. How close did we come?', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few weeks ago, we published a piece about a modest yet fascinating photo album that had found its way to the displays of the University of New Mexico Art Museum. “Random Shots Compiled for the Edification of Eleanor T. Wragg” read its first page — it was a Christmas gift from 1915, made up of 25 photos. Its pictures varied from quiet silhouettes and commonplace scenes to divers, airplanes and Gov. Jim Ferguson at the Cotton Palace Parade in Waco, Tex.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Update: Whither Eleanor’s Album?', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'FERGUSON, JIM', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Baylor University', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of New Mexico', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T16:00:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt McCann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McCann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/da463c61-defa-579d-8143-55082c697f42', 'word_count': 497, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da463c61-defa-579d-8143-55082c697f42'}, {'abstract': 'That Brooklyn lacks an Alfred Kazin statue is almost enough to make a bookish type want to move to Oxford, England — or at least to Oxford, Miss.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/16/5-critics-who-deserve-a-statue/', 'snippet': 'That Brooklyn lacks an Alfred Kazin statue is almost enough to make a bookish type want to move to Oxford, England — or at least to Oxford, Miss.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Sunday’s Riff, Dwight Garner, a literary critic for The New York Times, makes the case for critics who are actually critical. “Critics take a beating, especially in popular culture,” Garner writes. “Jean Sibelius’s observation — ‘No statue has ever been erected to a critic’ — seems to be cited somewhere weekly. As well-known quotations go, this one strikes me as especially banal. It implies something disheartening about our culture. . . . That Brooklyn lacks an Alfred Kazin statue is almost enough to make a bookish type want to move to Oxford, England — or at least to Oxford, Miss.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '5 Critics Who Deserve a Statue', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T16:03:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Staff', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Staff'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1067821d-39c1-57b2-8438-066ae0d760c5', 'word_count': 275, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1067821d-39c1-57b2-8438-066ae0d760c5'}, {'abstract': 'We asked for your favorite Julia Child recipes — the recipes that, unlike her storied boeuf bourguignon, are part of everyday kitchen life — and you delivered.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/16/those-julia-child-treasures/', 'snippet': 'We asked for your favorite Julia Child recipes — the recipes that, unlike her storied boeuf bourguignon, are part of everyday kitchen life — and you delivered.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We asked for your favorite Julia Child recipes — the recipes that, unlike her storied boeuf bourguignon, are part of everyday kitchen life — and lo and behold, you more than delivered. The following is a list, selected from over 200 comments, that highlight your most treasured Child standbys. They also demonstrate that the lessons she taught us about food and cooking continue to resonate. (See all the suggested recipes, and add to the list, here.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Those Julia Child Treasures', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Child, Julia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T16:11:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9470d66-14c6-5f66-87fa-7f89b6959ac5', 'word_count': 1077, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9470d66-14c6-5f66-87fa-7f89b6959ac5'}, {'abstract': 'The host of “This American Life” and co-writer of the coming film “Sleepwalk With Me” would like to meet Edgar Allan Poe. “I don’t have a question, but dude just seems like he could use a hug.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/19/books/review/ira-glass-by-the-book.html', 'snippet': 'The host of “This American Life” and co-writer of the coming film “Sleepwalk With Me” would like to meet Edgar Allan Poe. “I don’t have a question, but dude just seems like he could use a hug.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The host of “This American Life” and co-writer of the coming film “Sleepwalk With Me” would like to meet Edgar Allan Poe. “I don’t have a question, but dude just seems like he could use a hug.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/0819-bks-ByTheBook/0819-bks-ByTheBook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 630, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, '