name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assassinations and Attempted Assassinations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interrogations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Arbabsiar, Mansour J', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T21:36:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Colin Moynihan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Colin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moynihan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/491e1b33-9a98-594d-a399-79d64a89d85d', 'word_count': 542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/491e1b33-9a98-594d-a399-79d64a89d85d'}, {'abstract': 'Several groups make bicycling around the park a social experience as well as a workout.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/nyregion/cycling-and-socializing-in-central-park.html', 'snippet': 'Several groups make bicycling around the park a social experience as well as a workout.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IF you’re looking for a cycling workout in New York City without having to navigate much traffic, the six-mile loop in Central Park offers one of the world’s best urban cycling courses. But for a less solitary ride, a variety of groups do the loop at paces from high octane to moderate to downright leisurely. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/17/arts/17URBAN_SPAN/17URBAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/17/arts/17URBAN_SPAN/17URBAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/17/arts/17URBAN_SPAN/17URBAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/17/arts/17URBAN_SPAN/17URBAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/17/arts/17URBAN_SPAN/17URBAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/17/arts/17URBAN_SPAN/17URBAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cycling and Socializing in Central Park', 'kicker': 'Urban Athlete', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cycling and Socializing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'CENTRAL PARK (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exercise', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bicycles and Bicycling', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T21:36:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Krieger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krieger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/58f1e4b8-cd99-54bf-a9b1-847f504135e2', 'word_count': 672, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58f1e4b8-cd99-54bf-a9b1-847f504135e2'}, {'abstract': 'Kathryn Harrison on why she’s drawn to Joan of Arc, the subject of her next book.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/19/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'Kathryn Harrison on why she’s drawn to Joan of Arc, the subject of her next book.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reviewing Martin Amis’s new novel, “Lionel Asbo,” this week, Kathryn Harrison observes that it harks back to Dickens in its themes and characters. But Harrison’s own work has been casting back farther than that lately, as she writes her biography of Joan of Arc. Joan’s story resonates on so many levels — the religious, the military, the political, the feminist — that we wondered which speaks loudest to Harrison.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/0819-Upfront/0819-Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 763, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/0819-Upfront/0819-Upfront-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1661, 'width': 1237, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T21:39:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4577763f-c83e-5b04-bdb2-1f4062bb8c07', 'word_count': 290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4577763f-c83e-5b04-bdb2-1f4062bb8c07'}, {'abstract': 'Norman, the focus of this beautiful-looking 3-D stop-motion animation, is an 11-year-old who cannot only see dead people, but is happy to hang out with them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/movies/movie-review-paranorman-about-a-boy-and-zombies-with-elaine-stritch.html', 'snippet': 'Norman, the focus of this beautiful-looking 3-D stop-motion animation, is an 11-year-old who cannot only see dead people, but is happy to hang out with them.', 'lead_paragraph': '“ParaNorman,” a beautiful-looking, charmingly heartfelt 3-D stop-motion animation about a boy and his ghouls, comes with an assortment of hair-raising frights. Some of these are just the flesh-and-blood people in Norman’s life, the bullies and teachers and even relatives who, with degrees of impatience and love, chastise the 11-year-old for his unusually morbid habits. Norman, a sprout whose black hair stands at attention as if he were in a state of perpetual alarm (he is voiced with vivid emotion by Kodi Smit-McPhee), can’t help but be a little creepy. After all, he doesn’t just see dead people, he also hangs out with them, sometimes while watching creature-features on the family TV.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/17/arts/17PARANORMAN_SPAN/17PARANORMAN_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/17/arts/17PARANORMAN_SPAN/17PARANORMAN_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/17/arts/17PARANORMAN_SPAN/0817PARANORMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/17/arts/17PARANORMAN_SPAN/0817PARANORMAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 844, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'ur