s', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad0bfc4d-f943-5d5c-b375-054b1285b170', 'word_count': 3068, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad0bfc4d-f943-5d5c-b375-054b1285b170'}, {'abstract': 'Districts stymied by residents’ opposition to new taxes are finding creative ways to meet their responsibilities — often at extremely high costs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/business/schools-pass-debt-to-the-next-generation.html', 'snippet': 'Districts stymied by residents’ opposition to new taxes are finding creative ways to meet their responsibilities — often at extremely high costs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The deleveraging of America is well under way, as individuals and companies recover from the excess borrowing that helped to produce the boom and left many people vulnerable when the bust arrived. Household debt is down nearly $900 billion over the last four years, partly from repayments and partly from defaults.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/04/19/business/norris-75-standardinline/norris-75-standardinline-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2010/04/19/business/norris-75-standardinline/norris-75-standardinline-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Schools Pass Debt to the Next Generation', 'kicker': 'High & Low Finance', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Schools Pass Debt To the Next Generation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Municipal Bonds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T21:51:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Norris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Norris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f43cf1d2-a924-59f6-af52-f6d8db580ef4', 'word_count': 1052, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f43cf1d2-a924-59f6-af52-f6d8db580ef4'}, {'abstract': 'In “Chicken With Plums,” from the artist behind “Persepolis,” a tormented violinist takes to his bed to await the end of his life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/movies/movie-review-chicken-with-plums-about-contemporary-iran.html', 'snippet': 'In “Chicken With Plums,” from the artist behind “Persepolis,” a tormented violinist takes to his bed to await the end of his life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Graphic novels, at their best, do whatever they want; movies, all too often, do what they think they are supposed to do. No artist is freer than one with a good story and a sufficient supply of paper and ink, and the graphic-novel form, as it has evolved over the last two decades — to encompass memoir, history and eyewitness journalism — is bracingly unconstrained by visual or narrative convention. It is not surprising that some of the most interesting films in recent years have tried to capture that freedom. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/17/arts/17chicken_SPAN/17chicken_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/17/arts/17chicken_SPAN/17chicken_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargehe