nk': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-16T22:40:53+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Arts', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/0db41d19-3f5c-5fde-b02e-2debd6d12572', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/0db41d19-3f5c-5fde-b02e-2debd6d12572'}, {'abstract': 'The French director Christophe Honoré laces “Beloved,” a musical film starring Catherine Deneuve and Chiara Mastroianni, with whimsy and loss.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/movies/catherine-deneuve-in-beloved.html', 'snippet': 'The French director Christophe Honoré laces “Beloved,” a musical film starring Catherine Deneuve and Chiara Mastroianni, with whimsy and loss.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The characters in Christophe Honoré’s musical films — the new one, “Beloved” (“Les Bien-Aimés”), and its predecessor, “Love Songs” (2007) — do not burst into song so much as wander into it, giving voice to complicated thoughts about their feelings rather than to the feelings themselves. The musical numbers, which occur with minimal choreography on ordinary streets (and are written, for both movies, by Alex Beaupain), are less expressive than analytical, as if singing were above all a kind of philosophical reflection.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/17/arts/17BELOVED_SPAN/17BELOVED_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/17/arts/17BELOVED_SPAN/17BELOVED_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'r