1cc5a5e8c68'}, {'abstract': 'As societal changes transform Bangalore, many young Indians are struggling to cope, and they take their problems to the only symbol of authority that is accessible around the clock: the police.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/world/asia/relationships-troubled-young-indians-seek-help-from-police.html', 'snippet': 'As societal changes transform Bangalore, many young Indians are struggling to cope, and they take their problems to the only symbol of authority that is accessible around the clock: the police.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BANGALORE, India — The three stars and the red-and-blue ribbon on his shoulder strap indicate that M. L. Purushotham is a police inspector. But his unofficial job title might well be this: redeemer of relationships and savior of marriages.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Society’s Dizzying Changes Cast the Police in the Role of Relationship Counselor', 'kicker': 'Bangalore Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Society’s Dizzying Changes Cast the Police in the Role of Relationship Counselor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bangalore (India)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marriages', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T00:23:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Saritha Rai', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Saritha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rai', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15f15242-2bbc-5226-b94b-cc8bd593cd19', 'word_count': 1067, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15f15242-2bbc-5226-b94b-cc8bd593cd19'}, {'abstract': 'After decades of neglect, 20 acres of grassy hills and crumbling mansions in a corner of the Brooklyn Navy Yard that few New Yorkers have ever seen may get a new life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/nyregion/brooklyn-navy-yard-is-site-of-proposed-media-campus.html', 'snippet': 'After decades of neglect, 20 acres of grassy hills and crumbling mansions in a corner of the Brooklyn Navy Yard that few New Yorkers have ever seen may get a new life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hidden behind weeds and broken bricks, amid the hum of traffic from the nearby Brooklyn-Queens Expressway, lie 20 acres of abandoned grassy hills, crumbling Greek Revival mansions and Second Empire structures that few New Yorkers have ever seen.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/17/nyregion/YARD/YARD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1010, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/17/nyregion/YARD/YARD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1930, 'width': 1904, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/17/nyregion/YARD/YARD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/17/nyregion/YARD/YARD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/17/nyregion/YARD/YARD-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From Weeds and Bricks to Media Hub in Brooklyn', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Film and Technology Hub Planned in Brooklyn Navy Yard', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Steiner Studios', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Navy Yard (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate (Commercial)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Steiner, Douglas C', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T00:28:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Satow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Satow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/71945a84-4930-5972-b45b-df32918d326c', 'word_count': 998, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71945a84-4930-5972-b45b-df32918d326c'}, {'abstract': 'Park Avenue in Midtown was a very different place in the early 20th century, before the switching yards of Grand Central were covered and the railroad electrified.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/19/realestate/streetscapes-park-avenue-the-tween-years.html', 'snippet': 'Park Avenue in Midtown was a very different place in the early 20th century, before the switching yards of Grand Central were covered and the railroad electrified.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE reconstruction of Park Avenue in Midtown is just getting under way, first with Harry Macklowe’s quarter-mile-high apartment house at 56th Street, then with similar projects waiting in the wings. But even at flood tide it will be nothing like the transformation of the same stretch in the early 20th century. That was when, over the course of a decade, a string of apartment houses like McKim, Mead & White’s giant 277 Park Avenue, rose up, in some cases out of thin air. ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/realestate/19STREETS2/19STREETS2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/19/realestate/19STREETS2/19STREETS2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/realestate/19STREETS2/19STREETS2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 825, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/realestate/19STREETS2/19STREETS2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1649, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/realestate/19STREETS2/19STREETS2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/19/realestate/19STREETS2/19STREETS2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/realestate/19STREETS2/19STREETS2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Park Avenue, the ’Tween Years', 'kicker': 'Streetscapes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Park Avenue, The ’Tween Years', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Park Avenue (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Grand Central Terminal (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T00:28:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Gray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a5cc6cb-1797-53e9-a0fc-7653a9e791ea', 'word_count': 885, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a5cc6cb-1797-53e9-a0fc-7653a9e791ea'}, {'abstract': 'Adopting the reductionism that equates humans with other animals or computers has a serious downside: it wipes out the meaning of your own life.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/16/reality-is-flat-or-is-it/', 'snippet': 'Adopting the reductionism that equates humans with other animals or computers has a serious downside: it wipes out the meaning of your own life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a recent essay for The Stone, I claimed that humans are “something more than other animals, and essentially more than any computer.” Some readers found the claim importantly or trivially true; others found it partially or totally false; still others reacted as if I’d said that we’re not animals at all, or that there are no resemblances between our brains and computers. Some pointed out, rightly, that plenty of people do fine research in biology or computer science without reducing the human to the subhuman.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reality Is Flat. (Or Is It?)', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T00:30:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Polt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Polt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea91918c-d3d7-5bea-83b7-28d632b2f3f7', 'word_count': 1621, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea91918c-d3d7-5bea-83b7-28d632b2f3f7'}, {'abstract': 'The documentary “Speak” chronicles the 2008 Toastmasters World Championship of Public Speaking and the emotional investments of its contestants.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/movies/speak-a-documentary-by-paul-galichia-and-brian-weidling.html', 'snippet': 'The documentary “Speak” chronicles the 2008 Toastmasters World Championship of Public Speaking and the emotional investments of its contestants.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Documentaries about competitions of various sorts — spelling bees, kiddie beauty pageants, even muskrat skinning — have become common, and many are somewhat formulaic: they focus on a few contestants to let viewers develop a rooting interest, then culminate in the crowning of the winner. “Speak,” about the Toastmasters World Championship of Public Speaking, follows that formula most of the way through, but in its final minutes, after the contest is over, it delivers an unexpected jolt.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/17/arts/17RDP_SPEAK_SPAN/17RDP_SPEAK_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy':