ument_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/822afaf5-0b55-5748-939c-6b89fc971ea4', 'word_count': 221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/822afaf5-0b55-5748-939c-6b89fc971ea4'}, {'abstract': 'The suspects in the killings of three photojournalists and a publicist were picked up on drug and weapons charges, and confessed to the murders.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/world/americas/skepticism-greets-arrests-in-murders-of-mexican-journalists.html', 'snippet': 'The suspects in the killings of three photojournalists and a publicist were picked up on drug and weapons charges, and confessed to the murders.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MEXICO CITY — The local authorities in Veracruz State declared the case closed. Members of a drug gang had confessed to one of the state’s most notorious crimes in recent months: the murders of three photojournalists and a newspaper publicist. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Murder Arrests in Mexico Met With Skepticism', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Murder Arrests In Mexico Met With Skepticism', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Veracruz (Mexico)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of the Press', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Confessions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Crime', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T00:32:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisabeth Malkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Malkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/24de41c4-5b47-5209-9c45-b7bfb2eb00c6', 'word_count': 487, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24de41c4-5b47-5209-9c45-b7bfb2eb00c6'}, {'abstract': 'With rents rising, Carlo Dellaverson bade his roommates goodbye and started looking for an apartment he could afford in Manhattan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/19/realestate/the-hunt-career-and-apartment-finally-in-sync.html', 'snippet': 'With rents rising, Carlo Dellaverson bade his roommates goodbye and started looking for an apartment he could afford in Manhattan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR a few years, Carlo Dellaverson was more than happy living with friends in shared apartments. After his graduation from the University of Richmond, he lived in a series of “three frat-pad apartments with three guys,” he said. “We loved living in the city. Everyone was classic rent-poor but having a great time.” ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/realestate/19HUNT4_SPAN/hunt-4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/19/realestate/19HUNT4_SPAN/hunt-4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'c