012-08-17T01:54:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Waldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/72050ac8-ad28-5a5a-9763-e07b372fc902', 'word_count': 1509, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72050ac8-ad28-5a5a-9763-e07b372fc902'}, {'abstract': 'Instead of repudiating a casino mogul’s billions, Mitt Romney and Paul Ryan get even closer and jeopardize the integrity of their campaign.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/opinion/in-thrall-to-sheldon-adelson.html', 'snippet': 'Instead of repudiating a casino mogul’s billions, Mitt Romney and Paul Ryan get even closer and jeopardize the integrity of their campaign.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Three days after Paul Ryan became the presumptive Republican vice presidential candidate, he made a pilgrimage on Tuesday to the Las Vegas gambling palace of Sheldon Adelson, the casino tycoon who is spending more than any other donor to try to send Mr. Ryan and Mitt Romney to the White House. No reporters were allowed, perhaps because the campaign didn’t want them asking uncomfortable questions about the multiple federal investigations into the company behind Mr. Adelson’s wealth.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Thrall to Sheldon Adelson', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Thrall to Sheldon Adelson', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adelson, Sheldon G', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gambling', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Casinos', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Action Committees', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Las Vegas Sands Corporation', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Paul D Jr', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T01:54:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f317bba-6802-55e7-b7eb-8b2ed975e9d4', 'word_count': 614, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f317bba-6802-55e7-b7eb-8b2ed975e9d4'}, {'abstract': 'The decision was made by the Food and Drug Administration after a wire that connects a defibrillator to a patient’s heart caused unintended shocks. St. Jude Medical stopped selling the wire in 2010.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/business/fda-asks-st-jude-for-more-studies-on-flawed-heart-device.html', 'snippet': 'The decision was made by the Food and Drug Administration after a wire that connects a defibrillator to a patient’s heart caused unintended shocks. St. Jude Medical stopped selling the wire in 2010.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Food and Drug Administration ordered St. Jude Medical on Thursday to conduct additional studies on patients implanted with heart-device components that were recalled last year and that have been linked to as many as 20 deaths. It also recommended that patients receive X-rays or other imaging to check for problems with their devices.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'St. Jude Is Told to Study a Defect in He