s_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tiffany Frasier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tiffany', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Frasier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4feee127-6c61-501a-8dee-69f621c3d6e4', 'word_count': 22, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4feee127-6c61-501a-8dee-69f621c3d6e4'}, {'abstract': 'A samizdat kind of crowd turned out in New York in support of a Russian punk group whose three young members have been jailed in Moscow since March.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/18/arts/music/in-new-york-a-show-of-solidarity-for-russian-punk-band.html', 'snippet': 'A samizdat kind of crowd turned out in New York in support of a Russian punk group whose three young members have been jailed in Moscow since March.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Scant Russian was heard, but on Thursday night, a samizdat kind of crowd turned out in support of the Russian punk art group Pussy Riot. At 7:30 p.m., hundreds of people — mostly women, but some men, a few in homemade “Free Pussy Riot” T-shirts — lined up around the boutique Ace Hotel, stretching along 29th Street and down Broadway. The hotel’s subterranean Liberty Hall is normally the province of exclusive rock shows and fashion afterparties. But on this evening, a handful of performers stood before a bank of speakers there, reading heady statements related to the trial of Maria Alyokhina, Yekaterina Samutsevich and Nadezhda Tolokonnikova, the three young members of Pussy Riot who have been jailed in Moscow since March, and whose case has become a global cause célèbre.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/17/arts/17riot_span/17riot_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/17/arts/17riot_span/17riot_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/17/arts/17riot_span/17riot_span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/17/arts/17riot_span/17riot_span-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On Eve of Sentencing, an Artistic Show of Solidarity for Russian Punk Band', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On Eve of Sentencing, an Artistic Show of Solidarity for Pussy Riot', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pussy Riot', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Putin, Vladimir V', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sevigny, Chloe', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T06:13:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/654c23dc-5620-5887-ae32-5b913caf651f', 'word_count': 951, 'uri': 'nyt://article/654c23dc-5620-5887-ae32-5b913caf651f'}, {'abstract': 'A review of the Bollywood fi