its rival Zillow into the public market with a $75 million stock offering.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/trulia-files-to-go-public/', 'snippet': 'The real estate search site plans to follow its rival Zillow into the public market with a $75 million stock offering.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Trulia, the real estate search site, has filed to go public. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Trulia Files to Go Public', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Trulia.com', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T12:37:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1003ff24-5430-5954-9c80-0782957ac1f4', 'word_count': 262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1003ff24-5430-5954-9c80-0782957ac1f4'}, {'abstract': 'West Nile cases on the rise, what can you learn in a 15-minute doctor visit, help for an ailing router and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/friday-reading-west-nile-cases-on-the-rise/', 'snippet': 'West Nile cases on the rise, what can you learn in a 15-minute doctor visit, help for an ailing router and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and on our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday Reading: West Nile Cases on the Rise', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T12:41:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b258fec-48b7-564d-b973-70218acc4bbd', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b258fec-48b7-564d-b973-70218acc4bbd'}, {'abstract': 'A Chinese court is expected to issue a verdict on Monday in the murder trial of Gu Kailai, the wife of a prominent politician, who is reported to have all but confessed to killing a British businessman.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/18/world/asia/verdict-due-monday-in-chinese-murder-trial.html', 'snippet': 'A Chinese court is expected to issue a verdict on Monday in the murder trial of Gu Kailai, the wife of a prominent politician, who is reported to have all but confessed to killing a British businessman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — A Chinese court is expected to issue a verdict on Monday in the murder trial of Gu Kailai, the wife of a prominent politician, who is reported to have all but confessed to killing a British businessman during what prosecutors say was a dispute over money. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Verdict Due Monday in Chinese Murder Trial', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China: Verdict Soon in Major Trial', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gu Kailai', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Heywood, Neil', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bo Xilai', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T12:50:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1368d0e-0f13-53da-b847-537c365c7cdf', 'word_count': 601, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1368d0e-0f13-53da-b847-537c365c7cdf'}, {'abstract': 'To cap off an extended stay in New Zealand with her family, Miranda Kerr gives Wellington a whirl in fall’s white shirts and strappy flats. And this time her movie-star husband is behind the camera.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/t-magazine/miranda-kerr-goes-kiwi.html', 'snippet': 'To cap off an extended stay in New Zealand with her family, Miranda Kerr gives Wellington a whirl in fall’s white shirts and strappy flats. And this time her movie-star husband is behind the camera.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Miranda Kerr was 18, just out of high school, a girl from the Australian countryside curious about what the rest of the world had to offer, when she first visited New Zealand. Along with a close friend from Gunnedah, the pastoral town where she grew up, Kerr set out to experience what for many is the draw of New Zealand: to escape and explore, to get a little lost and, in the process, get a little found. They slept in a tent. Swam in natural hot springs. Hiked terrain so raw and depopulated and expansive it felt as if they were the first humans to discover it. Kerr didn’t know it at the time, but her life would change significantly in the years that followed, thanks largely to her enviable genetic gifts: the long pale legs, the shimmering doe eyes and, most famously, the pert, dimpled cheeks that have made her one of the highest paid, most recognizable models in the world. Modeling, for Kerr, has been its own sort of adventure, one she will be the first to acknowledge as a stroke of good fortune not to be taken for granted. “Still, that backpacking trip, just having that freedom, was one of the best times in my life,” Kerr recently reflected. “Ever since, I’ve wanted to get back to New Zealand.”', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '212', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/t-magazine/19well-kerr/19well-kerr-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/t-magazine/19well-kerr/19well-kerr-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/t-magazine/19well-kerr/19well-kerr-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/19/t-magazine/19well-kerr/19well-kerr-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/t-magazine/19well-kerr/19well-kerr-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Kerr Goes Kiwi', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kerr Goes Kiwi', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kerr, Miranda', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Models (Professional)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Zealand', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T13:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Amsden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amsden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5196debf-df92-568a-88e5-0f6dc9f353c8', 'word_count': 1968, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5196debf-df92-568a-88e5-0f6dc9f353c8'}, {'abstract': 'An artist renovates mud homes in Bali Island in West Bengal using clay, bamboo, dried hogla leaves, stones and cow dung.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/in-the-sundarbans-folk-homes-for-modern-living/', 'snippet': 'An artist renovates mud homes in Bali Island in West Bengal using clay, bamboo, dried hogla leaves, stones and cow dung.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most of the residents in the Sundarbans in West Bengal live in mud homes, but only until they get the money needed to build concrete houses. Bidyut Roy, an artist and architect, is on a mission to persuade locals that mud and other natural materials can make for a modern home.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In the Sundarbans, Folk Homes for Modern Living', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T13:08:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anuradha Sharma', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anuradha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sharma', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51218273-dbd9-5b82-9ed2-30677cc002e1', 'word_count': 1121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51218273-dbd9-5b82-9ed2-30677cc002e1'}, {'abstract': 'Would an Istanbul capable of staging the Games be a city in control of its future?', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/confessions-of-an-olympic-bid-writer/', 'snippet': 'Would an Istanbul capable of staging the Games be a city in control of its future?', 'lead_paragraph': 'ISTANBUL — “Bah! Humbug!” When the subject of the Olympic Games comes up, I feel like Ebenezer Scrooge in his unreformed middle period, ready to douse the first signs of enthusiasm with a cold bucket of cynicism. The reason why my guard is still up a week after the London torch moved on to Rio de Janeiro is that I live in a city that’s trying to bring home yet more gold.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Confessions of an Olympic Bid Writer', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infrastructure (Public Works)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Istanbul (Turkey)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T13:09:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Finkel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finkel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba3868af-0069-50f6-a2c3-3cbbe9fd647f', 'word_count': 616, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba3868af-0069-50f6-a2c3-3cbbe9fd647f'}, {'abstract': 'AMC said it was picking up two as-yet untitled reality shows, one that will chronicle the field of taxidermy and the other set at a freak show at Venice Beach in California.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/amc-adds-new-reality-shows-and-renews-the-pitch/', 'snippet': 'AMC said it was picking up two as-yet untitled reality shows, one that will chronicle the field of taxidermy and the other set at a freak show at Venice Beach in California.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While AMC is perhaps best known for scripted dramas like “Mad Men” and “Breaking Bad,” that cable channel is continuing to add to its roster of unscripted series — you might also call them reality shows — for those hours when Don Draper and Walter White are taking it easy. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'AMC Adds New Reality Shows and Renews ‘The Pitch’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'AMC Adds Reality Shows', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reality Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMC (TV Network)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Venice Beach (Calif)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T13:10:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/739916d6-6a94-5566-8427-90a61eba8761', 'word_count': 227, 'uri': 'nyt://article/739916d6-6a94-5566-8427-90a61eba8761'}, {'abstract': 'Can’t British employers do more to accommodate Muslims during Ramadan?', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/the-fast-and-the-furious/', 'snippet': 'Can’t British employers do more to accommodate Muslims during Ramadan?', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — I stopped by my favorite fruit stall in Brixton earlier this week, only to find the seller fast asleep, with head propped against cart and face cupped in hand. I began sorting through various cartons of peaches, making just enough noise to wake Pervez Khan, a fellow Pakistani who has been based in south London for more than a decade. As I tossed the fruit into a basket, Khan awoke and began apologizing. “It’s terrible of me to sleep on the job,” he said, “but it’s so hard to stay awake when you’re fasting.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fast and the Furious', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fasting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2012)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ramadan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Working Hours', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T13:16:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Huma Yusuf', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Huma', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yusuf', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50476f43-60bf-53df-a3ec-4609279b7281', 'word_count': 515, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50476f43-60bf-53df-a3ec-4609279b7281'}, {'abstract': 'Three members of the Russian feminist punk band Pussy Riot were found guilty on Friday over a stunt they staged in February, and China is following a Western template with the hope to bolster its image and influence around the world.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/the-breakfast-meeting-guity-verdicts-in-pussy-riot-case-and-chinas-media-strategy/', 'snippet': 'Three members of the Russian feminist punk band Pussy Riot were found guilty on Friday over a stunt they staged in February, and China is following a Western template with the hope to bolster its image and influence around the world.', 'lead_paragraph': '10:59 a.m. | Updated Three members of the Russian feminist punk band Pussy Riot were found guilty on Friday over a stunt they staged in February against Vladimir V. Putin in the main Moscow Russian Orthodox cathedral, David Herszenhorn reports. The scene in hot, overcrowded courtroom with the three women behind bulletproof glass was being streamed live by Russia Today, fueling worldwide interest in the prosecution of the band members, who have received support from musicians around the world including Paul McCartney and Madonna. The judge sentenced each to a two-year prison sentence. The YouTube video of the incident is at nearly 450,000 views.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Guilty Verdicts in Pussy Riot Case, and China’s Media Strategy', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T13:21:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': 