ut 7) began doing an interpretive dance in the aisle, and didn’t stop. We could not look at the stage without seeing her. After a while, my husband politely asked the parents to restrain the girl. The mother carried her away, kicking and screaming. She wagged her finger at us, saying: “See what you’ve done.” Another man told us we had broken the girl’s heart and owed her an apology. Were we out of line?', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 599}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 923, 'width': 925, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 923, 'width': 925, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Making a Child Stop Her Distracting Dance', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "You're No Dancer, Kid", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customs, Etiquette and Manners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T14:09:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip Galanes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Galanes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9b18db4-3174-55d2-847e-1727b138012e', 'word_count': 802, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9b18db4-3174-55d2-847e-1727b138012e'}, {'abstract': 'Wines from the Finger Lakes can be a bargain.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/from-the-finger-lakes-a-concentration-of-good-wine-value/', 'snippet': 'Wines from the Finger Lakes can be a bargain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In my column this week, partly in response to a reader’s challenge, I suggest a dozen great values in American wines, all $20 and under. Some of you may be surprised at the selection, which includes four bottles from New York State.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From the Finger Lakes, a Concentration of Good Wine Value', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T14:12:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Asimov', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Asimov', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/80b4a057-6602-55d9-bd37-9bd55a7d7720', 'word_count': 201, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80b4a057-6602-55d9-bd37-9bd55a7d7720'}, {'abstract': 'Are dinners with divorced parents helpful for a 4-year-old, or confusing?', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/helping-a-child-adjust-to-divorce/', 'snippet': 'Are dinners with divorced parents helpful for a 4-year-old, or confusing?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week, reader M. asked other readers, divorced parents and children of divorce, to weigh in on the monthly dinners she and her ex-husband have been having with their 4-year-old. M. and her spouse divorced last fall, when their daughter was 3, and instituted the dinners a few months later.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Helping a Child Adjust to Divorce', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T14:15:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e64aa6b-a43b-5a7e-8863-2e455150d6ae', 'word_count': 464, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e64aa6b-a43b-5a7e-8863-2e455150d6ae'}, {'abstract': 'The chef Andy Ricker is closing Pok Pok Wing on the Lower East Side, and reopening it as Pok Pok Phat Thai.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/pok-pok-wing-to-get-a-new-name-and-dish-phat-thai/', 'snippet': 'The chef Andy Ricker is closing Pok Pok Wing on the Lower East Side, and reopening it as Pok Pok Phat Thai.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The elephant on the plate, as far as Andy Ricker, the owner of the Pok Pok restaurants, is concerned, has always been the noodle dish called phat Thai, or pad Thai. It is about the most popular Thai dish among Americans, but Mr. Ricker has long made a point of not serving it. Until now.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pok Pok Wing to Get a New Name and Dish: Phat Thai', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T14:23:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/96312d95-dac0-54ac-887d-bf779cf5878f', 'word_count': 395, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96312d95-dac0-54ac-887d-bf779cf5878f'}, {'abstract': 'The New York Times critics on “Cosmopolis,” “Compliance” and “Chicken With Plums.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/this-weeks-movies-august-17/', 'snippet': 'The New York Times critics on “Cosmopolis,” “Compliance” and “Chicken With Plums.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In today’s episode, our critics offer their takes on three independent films: David Cronenberg’s “Cosmopolis,” Craig Zobel’s “Compliance” and the first live-action film from the French animators Marjane Satrapi and Vincent Paronnaud, “Chicken With Plums.” Full reviews of all of this week’s films can be found here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Week’s Movies: August 17', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cronenberg, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Satrapi, Marjane', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zobel, R Craig', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T14:30:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3e92a99-48ba-5702-ab82-01099ea831a7', 'word_count': 47, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3e92a99-48ba-5702-ab82-01099ea831a7'}, {'abstract': 'As cousins come of age, a killer stalks young girls in this first novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/19/books/review/what-dies-in-summer-by-tom-wright.html', 'snippet': 'As cousins come of age, a killer stalks young girls in this first novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Why do teenagers make such ideal protagonists? Maybe it’s because they’re doing just what novels do: struggling to make sense of a troubling and imperfect world. And at first, Jim, called Biscuit, and L. A. (Lee Ann), the teenage cousins at the heart of Tom Wright’s feisty first novel, are \xadexactly what you hope they’ll be: funny, frank, mouthy and more than a touch off kilter. Both are forced to live with their grandmother because their mothers aren’t up to the task of child rearing. Their homes are haunted by alcoholism and violence, but Gram takes a simple, affectionate, responsibility for them — though it’s almost inevitable that certain questions, if not the answers, will push their ugly way to the surface sooner or later.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19MEYERSON/19MEYERSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 950, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19MEYERSON/19MEYERSON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1855, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop