e', 'url': 'images/2012/08/17/business/video-bdl-120817/video-bdl-120817-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/17/business/video-bdl-120817/video-bdl-120817-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Business Day Live | August 17, 2012', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T15:26:14+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'Mac William Bishop, Channon Hodge and Vijai Singh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mac', 'middlename': 'William', 'lastname': 'Bishop', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Channon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodge', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Vijai', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Singh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/93b99262-7187-5906-bb46-39c8fc149017', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/93b99262-7187-5906-bb46-39c8fc149017'}, {'abstract': 'The first volume of a biography of the British artist covers the years 1937 to 1975.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/19/books/review/david-hockney-by-christopher-simon-sykes.html', 'snippet': 'The first volume of a biography of the British artist covers the years 1937 to 1975.', 'lead_paragraph': 'David Hockney is often described as England’s most popular artist, but in his youth he dreamed of Los Angeles. He moved there in 1963, at age 26, unable to drive and believing he would get around the city by bus. This did not prevent him from traveling in his imagination to another California, one that bore little resemblance to the surfer-girl paradise enshrined by the Beach Boys. Instead of sand and the frothy ocean, Hockney painted quiet, slightly vacant-looking scenes of turquoise swimming pools in private backyards. They’re landscapes waiting for people to arrive, even when the people are already there.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19HOCKNEY/19HOCKNEY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 820, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19HOCKNEY/19HOCKNEY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1639, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19HOCKNEY/19HOCKNEY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19HOCKNEY/19HOCKNEY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19HOCKNEY/19HOCKNEY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'California Dreams', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'California Dreams', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sykes, Christopher Simon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hockney, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T15:26:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deborah Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deborah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c32af11f-a2e3-5265-a367-b36111fc8f0f', 'word_count': 1162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c32af11f-a2e3-5265-a367-b36111fc8f0f'}, {'abstract': 'In Karin Fossum’s novel “The Caller,” children are both victims and villains and far more dangerous than any adult.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/19/books/review/the-caller-by-karin-fossum-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'In Karin Fossum’s novel “The Caller,” children are both victims and villains and far more dangerous than any adult.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thrillers not so thrilling lately? Serial killers too silly? Police detectives too sensitive? Wondering where all the tough guys went? I give you the Norwegian author Karin Fossum, whose new novel, THE CALLER (Houghton Mifflin Harcourt, $25), is one of the darkest, most disturbing crime stories you’re likely to read this year.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19crime/19crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 563, 'width': 487, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19crime/19crime-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 563, 'width': 487, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19crime/19crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19crime/19crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Disturbing the Peace', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Disturbing the Peace', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Banville, John', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fossum, Karin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cain, Chelsea', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Black, Benjamin', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koryta, Michael', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T15:27:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce6d4492-bf24-5666-ae98-b1ec8d871577', 'word_count': 886, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce6d4492-bf24-5666-ae98-b1ec8d871577'}, {'abstract': 'A visit to the centers of this country’s poverty in the 21st century.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/19/books/review/days-of-destruction-days-of-revolt.html', 'snippet': 'A visit to the centers of this country’s poverty in the 21st century.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This book is a collaboration between Chris Hedges and Joe Sacco, showing us daily life in four centers of 21st-century American poverty. Hedges’ contribution — a combination of reportage and commentary — is in a long tradition of literary journalism. Sacco’s is the sort of graphic art popularized by Art Spiegelman in “Maus.” Both writers have decades of experience as correspondents in war zones, but in “Days of Destruction, Days of Revolt” they turn their attention to the bombed-out and collapsed areas of their own country.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19meyer/19meyer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 657, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19meyer/19meyer-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1597, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19meyer/19meyer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19meyer/19meyer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19meyer/19meyer-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 102, 'width': 102, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Other America', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Other America', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sacco, Joe', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poverty', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hedges, Chris', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T15:27:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philipp Meyer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philipp', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Meyer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c80ad0ae-bc41-5240-ae5d-00bb49a1d388', 'word_count': 779, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c80ad0ae-bc41-5240-ae5d-00bb49a1d388'}, {'abstract': 'Part memoir and part criticism, Maureen N. McLane’s book includes essays about poets, along with lineated poem-games.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/19/books/review/my-poets-by-maureen-n-mclane.html', 'snippet': 'Part memoir and part criticism, Maureen N. McLane’s book includes essays about poets, along with lineated poem-games.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Poetry changes nothing, W. H. Auden suggested. For once in his life he was wrong. Poetry changes \xadeverything, starting with the good behavior of anyone who reads a lot of it, as the poet-critic Maureen N. McLane discovers in her beguiling new book, “My \xadPoets.” “I was good at school,” she writes. “The very things that made me good at school — a talent for aligning with authority, or for knowing what it wanted; a capacity for self-\xadestranging self-discipline; an ability to use anxiety as a fuel; an \xadoveridentification with established codes — were precisely the things that might render me not a writer, not a poet.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19FRIED/19FRIED-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 628, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19FRIED/19FRIED-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1255, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19FRIED/19FRIED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19FRIED/19FRIED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19FRIED/19FRIED-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Under the Influence', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Under the Influence', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McLane, Maureen N', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T15:27:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daisy Fried', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daisy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fried', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b25c128-17f1-5664-a495-a750012b1144', 'word_count': 1235, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b25c128-17f1-5664-a495-a750012b1144'}, {'abstract': 'Peter Hoffman’s downtown restaurants will be celebrating crabs with pop-ups in the Rockaways on Saturday, Aug. 18, and again on Aug. 25.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/back-forty-to-serve-crabs-on-the-rockaway-boardwalk/', 'snippet': 'Peter Hoffman’s downtown restaurants will be celebrating crabs with pop-ups in the Rockaways on Saturday, Aug. 18, and again on Aug. 25.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Back Forty and Back Forty West, Peter Hoffman’s downtown restaurants, will be celebrating crabs with pop-ups in the Rockaways, in Queens, on Saturday, Aug. 18, and again on Aug. 25, on the boardwalk at 96th Street. The crabs will be hot and spicy starting around 2 p.m. and will be served as long as the supply lasts, perhaps until early evening. For $25 you will get a bucket of six steamed crabs, some potatoes and two ears of corn. There is a bar nearby. No reservations, or even shoes, for this!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Back Forty to Serve Crabs on the Rockaway Boardwalk', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T15:29:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7905fb3c-2fd3-59bf-88cb-febdad9d7c6c', 'word_count': 91, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7905fb3c-2fd3-59bf-88cb-febdad9d7c6c'}, {'abstract': 'A festival dedicated to Hindu snake goddess “Manasa” celebrated in Kolkata, West Bengal.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/image-of-the-day-august-17/', 'snippet': 'A festival dedicated to Hindu snake goddess “Manasa” celebrated in Kolkata, West Bengal.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Image of the Day: August 17', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hinduism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kolkata (India)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T15:30:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4218218f-4d70-50cd-ad83-7b7785ee2cf4', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4218218f-4d70-50cd-ad83-7b7785ee2cf4'}, {'abstract': 'Alix Ohlin’s characters struggle to make personal connections.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/19/books/review/inside-and-signs-and-wonders-by-alix-ohlin.html', 'snippet': 'Alix Ohlin’s characters struggle to make personal connections.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are two species of novelist: one writes as if the world is a known locale that requires dutiful reporting, the other as if the world has yet to be made. The former enjoys the complacency of the au courant and the lassitude of at-hand language, while the latter believes with Thoreau that “this world is but canvas to our imaginations,” that the only worthy assertion of imagination occurs by way of linguistic originality wed to intellect and emotional verity. You close “Don Quixote” and “Tristram Shandy,” “Middlemarch” and “Augie March,” and the cosmos takes on a coruscated import it rather lacked before, an “eternal and irrepressible freshness,” in Pound’s apt phrase. His definition of literature is among the best we have: “Language charged with meaning.” How charged was the last novel you read?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/0819-bks-GIRALDI/0819-bks-GIRALDI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 253, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/0819-bks-GIRALDI/0819-bks-GIRALDI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 507, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/0819-bks-GIRALDI/0819-bks-GIRALDI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/0819-bks-GIRALDI/0819-bks-GIRALDI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/0819-bks-GIRALDI/0819-bks-GIRALDI-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Here if You Need Me', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Here if You Need Me', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ohlin, Alix', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T15:32:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Giraldi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Giraldi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ccb699a3-8e69-505c-81a4-f3771d4359d5', 'word_count': 1390, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ccb699a3-8e69-505c-81a4-f3771d4359d5'}, {'abstract': 'New books by Elizabeth Haynes, Suzanne Ruta, Karen Thompson Walker, Patrick Lapeyre and Benjamin Wood.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/19/books/review/into-the-darkest-corner-by-elizabeth-haynes-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'New books by Elizabeth Haynes, Suzanne Ruta, Karen Thompson Walker, Patrick Lapeyre and Benjamin Wood.', 'lead_paragraph': ' INTO THE DARKEST CORNER  By Elizabeth Haynes.  Harper/HarperCollins, $25.99.  ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19MCCOLLOCH/19MCCOLLOCH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 718, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19MCCOLLOCH/19MCCOLLOCH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1436, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19MCCOLLOCH/19MCCOLLOCH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19MCCOLLOCH/19MCCOLLOCH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/19MCCOLLOCH/19MCCOLLOCH-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fiction Chronicle', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fiction Chronicle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ruta, Suzanne', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walker, Karen Thompson', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wood, Benjamin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lapeyre, Patrick', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Haynes, Elizabeth', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T15:32:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison McCulloch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McCulloch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7817e8d-aa3f-5517-a7d0-44b0d2f06596', 'word_count': 1130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7817e8d-aa3f-5517-a7d0-44b0d2f06596'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/19/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THREE STRONG WOMEN,  by Marie NDiaye. Translated by John Fletcher. (Knopf, $25.95.) In loosely linked narratives, three women from Senegal struggle with fathers and husbands in France. This subtle, hypnotic novel won the Prix Goncourt in 2009.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "Editors' Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T15:32:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/a749e4fc-023f-5faa-ade2-da6eeab78e90', 'word_count': 304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a749e4fc-023f-5faa-ade2-da6eeab78e90'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/19/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEGIN AGAIN: A Biography of John Cage,  by Kenneth Silverman. (Northwestern University, $24.95.) Cage (1912-92), the avant-garde composer whose most famous piece called for the performer not to make a sound, upended long-held conventions about experiencing art. Silverman presents a nuanced view of Cage, from his childhood in interwar Los Angeles and his creative awakening in Paris to his revolutionary work and his lifelong partnership with the choreographer Merce Cunningham.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T15:33:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/8b84fd46-7215-5162-82ea-1d25e441cdcf', 'word_count': 527, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8b84fd46-7215-5162-82ea-1d25e441cdcf'}, {'abstract': 'Bob Spitz’s biography of Julia Child, “Dearie” — one of a flurry of books timed to the centennial of the beloved chef’s birth — hits the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 6.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/26/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Bob Spitz’s biography of Julia Child, “Dearie” — one of a flurry of books timed to the centennial of the beloved chef’s birth — hits the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 6.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THERE’S SOMETHING ABOUT JULIA: Aug. 15 marked the centennial of Julia Child’s birth, and publishers are serving up biographies to satisfy every reading level and taste. There’s Julia Child for ailurophiles — “Julia’s Cats.” Julia Child for children — the picture book “Bon Appétit!” Julia Child for ailurophiles and children — “Minette’s Feast: The Delicious Story of Julia Child and Her Cat.” For everyone else, there’s “Dearie,” the biography by Bob Spitz, new on the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 6.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/26insidelist/26insidelist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 221, 'width': 216, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/26insidelist/26insidelist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 221, 'width': 216, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/26insidelist/26insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/26insidelist/26insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/26insidelist/26insidelist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': 'TBR', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T15:34:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Parul Sehgal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Parul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sehgal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/97e9680e-575d-5c2d-8984-2e622f044bde', 'word_count': 624, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97e9680e-575d-5c2d-8984-2e622f044bde'}, {'abstract': 'The best sequels enrich and extend the original story, but they do not treat it as an unfinished work that needs completion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/19/books/review/the-art-of-the-sequel.html', 'snippet': 'The best sequels enrich and extend the original story, but they do not treat it as an unfinished work that needs completion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A lot of people curl their lips at the very idea of literary prequels and sequels, and it’s easy to see why. The road to damnation is paved with examples that suggest the form is practically unworkable. Some disappoint simply by having a distinctly second-best prose style — and so insult the author to whom they mean to show respect. Some by creating a pallid version of the original, strong characters. Some by unraveling the tension of the received plot. Some by the sheer silliness of their reinvention: “I, Sherlock Holmes.” “Little Women and Werewolves.” “Pride and Promiscuity.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/0819-bks-MOTION/0819-bks-MOTION-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 257, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/0819-bks-MOTION/0819-bks-MOTION-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 257}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/0819-bks-MOTION/0819-bks-MOTION-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 439, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/0819-bks-MOTION/0819-bks-MOTION-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 878, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/0819-bks-MOTION/0819-bks-MOTION-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/19/books/review/0819-bks-MOTION/0819-bks-MOTION-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'To Be Continued', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Be Continued', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T15:34:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Motion', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Motion', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0453cd6-edc1-5a21-a768-4a3209d5a0c5', 'word_count': 1211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0453cd6-edc1-5a21-a768-4a3209d5a0c5'}, {'abstract': 'The National Highway Traffic Safety Administration said data collected by the devices in collisions would consequently be easier to analyze by automakers and parties in lawsuits.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/n-h-t-s-a-sets-standards-for-data-collected-from-black-boxes-in-cars/', 'snippet': 'The National Highway Traffic Safety Administration said data collected by the devices in collisions would consequently be easier to analyze by automakers and parties in lawsuits.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Beginning next month, new cars equipped with so-called black boxes — instruments that record crash information — must meet certain criteria for categories of data captured, accuracy and crash survivability. But the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration is still working on a rule that would require all new cars sold to contain the devices in the first place.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'N.H.T.S.A. Sets Standards for Data Collected From Black Boxes in Cars', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defects', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Center for Auto Safety', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Media General Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Highway Traffic Safety Administration', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T15:35:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew L. Wald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/18c46c99-fef6-5342-91ac-c56829cda133', 'word_count': 682, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18c46c99-fef6-5342-91ac-c56829cda133'}, {'abstract': 'More new hedge funds started in the first quarter of this year than in any quarter since 2007, according to data from Hedge Fund Research. Some newcomers as well as industry veterans are branching out on their own.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/new-hedge-funds-abound-despite-tepid-industry-performance/', 'snippet': 'More new hedge funds started in the first quarter of this year than in any quarter since 2007, according to data from Hedge Fund Research. Some newcomers as well as industry veterans are branching out on their own.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s been a rough couple of years to be a hedge fund. Weak performance, enhanced regulation and an aggressive crackdown on insider trading have cast a harsh spotlight on the industry. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Hedge Funds Abound, Despite Tepid Industry Performance', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hedge Funds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'APPALOOSA MANAGEMENT', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Citadel Investment Group', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'JAT', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Renaissance Technologies', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'SAC Capital Advisors', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T15:40:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Azam Ahmed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Azam', 'middlename