 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Frosch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Frosch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de85e5b6-ad1f-526b-b960-c4a56f139a26', 'word_count': 818, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de85e5b6-ad1f-526b-b960-c4a56f139a26'}, {'abstract': 'Ministers are working throughout the year, and congregational buildings offer services year-round.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/18/us/unitarians-shift-away-from-extended-time-off-in-summers.html', 'snippet': 'Ministers are working throughout the year, and congregational buildings offer services year-round.', 'lead_paragraph': '“If you phone a Unitarian Church between the middle of June and Labor Day in September, the most you are apt to get is a recorded message,” Charles S. Slap said in his sermon at the First Unitarian Society of Schenectady, N.Y., on Sept. 8, 1985. “Our more orthodox friends never cease to be astounded by the contents of the message: ‘This church is closed for the summer. If you are one of those people who actually need a church during the summer, try the Presbyterians.’ ”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Unitarians Break With Tradition of Extended Time Off in Summer', 'kicker': 'Beliefs', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unitarians Break With Tradition of Extended Time Off in Summer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ministers (Protestant)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Unitarian Universalist Assn', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-17T23:39:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', '