Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Thailand: 4 Die in Nightclub Fire', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fires and Firefighters', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Phuket (Thailand)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'NIGHTCLUBS AND CABARETS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-18T01:26:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Fuller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fuller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/632a848f-be4f-57f4-ad55-0a672f03bdcc', 'word_count': 131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/632a848f-be4f-57f4-ad55-0a672f03bdcc'}, {'abstract': 'Smallpox killed thousands of Civil War soldiers, North and South alike.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/the-minister-of-death/', 'snippet': 'Smallpox killed thousands of Civil War soldiers, North and South alike.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From April 29 to May 30, 1862, some 300,000 men — including my great-great grandfather, Pvt. Edward Willis — converged at the tiny railroad depot of Corinth, in northern Mississippi, where they laid siege to the Confederate forces under Gen. P.G.T. Beauregard. The Union soldiers scarred the landscape with miles of earthen fortifications. But the trenches could not protect Edward and his fellow soldiers from what Thomas Macaulay called “the most terrible of all the ministers of death,” a predator that craved those close, confined spaces, where men’s blood, breath and spit mingled freely. Although they fought on opposite sides of the trenches, the Union and Confederate forces shared a common enemy: smallpox.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Minister of Death', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smallpox', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-18T01:30:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carole Emberton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carole', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Emberton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/baa006df-10a4-528d-8947-0cdd43e959ec', 'word_count': 1615, 'uri': 'nyt://article/baa006df-10a4-528d-8947-0cdd43e959ec'}, {'abstract': 'Melky Cabrera is no baseball icon. But his suspension for failing a drug test brings us one step closer to shaking the faith we invest in the game.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/17/baseball-faith-and-doubt/', 'snippet': 'Melky Cabrera is no baseball icon. But his suspension for failing a drug test brings us one step closer to shaking the faith we invest in the game.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was impossible this week not to think about the incredible staying power of baseball.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Baseball, Faith and Doubt', 'kicker': 'Doug Glanville', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Steroids', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests (Drug Use)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Braun, Ryan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cabrera, Melky', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-18T01:30:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Doug Glanville', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Doug', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glanville', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/99031cca-a34d-5a85-b279-0b07abf678af', 'word_count': 1017, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99031cca-a34d-5a85-b279-0b07abf678af'}, {'abstract': 'Pressure to adopt a cholera vaccine as part of the routine response to outbreaks mounted as two expert panels advised the World Health Organization to use it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/18/health/push-for-more-cholera-inoculations.html', 'snippet': 'Pressure to adopt a cholera vaccine as part of the routine response to outbreaks mounted as two expert panels advised the World Health Organization to use it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pressure to adopt a cholera vaccine as part of the routine response to outbreaks mounted as two expert panels advised the World Health Organization to use it. On Thursday, experts meeting in Washington endorsed the use of the vaccine to control the continuing outbreak in Haiti and the Dominican Republic. Studies conducted by medical charities there showed that up to 90 percent of people who had received one dose of the vaccine returned for a second dose and were therefore protected. Separately, an expert panel advised the World Health Organization this year to seek enough money to create a global stockpile of two million doses for emergencies. On Friday, Sierra Leone declared its cholera outbreak a national humanitarian emergency; it has had almost 11,000 cases and 176 deaths since January. Cholera kills through rapid dehydration. The vaccine is for healthy people, but it could be used to surround outbreaks and keep them from spreading.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Push for More Cholera Inoculations', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Science', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Push for More Cholera Inoculations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cholera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Health Organization', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vaccination and Immunization', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Epidemics', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-18T01:30:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Donald G. McNeil Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Donald', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'McNeil', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/3525718f-de29-5aa6-84ca-00d0196ab4e7', 'word_count': 157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3525718f-de29-5aa6-84ca-00d0196ab4e7'}, {'abstract': 'Senator Charles E. Schumer keeps close track of office romances and is known to cajole, nag and outright pester his staff toward connubial bliss.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/18/nyregion/senator-senator-make-me-a-match-for-staff-schumer-is-cupid.html', 'snippet': 'Senator Charles E. Schumer keeps close track of office romances and is known to cajole, nag and outright pester his staff toward connubial bliss.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even by the standards of Capitol Hill, where ambitious and awkward young people are thrust together in cramped workplaces and crowded bars, the nuptial output of Senator Charles E. Schumer’s office stands out.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'ty