k': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/819c9c16-978c-5012-a636-80f0ec39b3cb', 'word_count': 332, 'uri': 'nyt://article/819c9c16-978c-5012-a636-80f0ec39b3cb'}, {'abstract': 'With Pier 40’s roof falling in and the pilings underneath it deteriorating, the pier has turned into a drain on Hudson River Park’s finances.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/18/nyregion/repair-costs-could-bring-down-a-popular-pier.html', 'snippet': 'With Pier 40’s roof falling in and the pilings underneath it deteriorating, the pier has turned into a drain on Hudson River Park’s finances.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When it was created in the late 1990s, Hudson River Park seemed like a new genre of public space, a state-city hybrid built on and over the water, with continuing maintenance to be paid for by commercial development on a handful of piers. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/18/nyregion/PIER1/PIER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/18/nyregion/PIER1/PIER1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/18/nyregion/PIER1/PIER1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 703, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/18/nyregion/PIER1/PIER1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1406, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/18/nyregion/PIER1/PIER1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/18/nyregion/PIER1/PIER1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/18/nyregion/PIER1/PIER1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Repair Costs Could Bring Down a Popular Pier', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Repair Costs Could Bring Down a Popular Pier', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hudson River Park (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hudson River', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hudson River Park Trust', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-18T02:10:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa W. Foderaro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Foderaro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1207d40-3ce2-570a-9b3b-86c0a21e0194', 'word_count': 1036, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1207d40-3ce2-570a-9b3b-86c0a21e0194'}, {'abstract': 'The new policy that gives young illegal immigrants a reprieve from deportation and a chance to work is an application of common sense.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/18/opinion/long-lines-and-big-dreams-for-illegal-immigrants.html', 'snippet': 'The new policy that gives young illegal immigrants a reprieve from deportation and a chance to work is an application of common sense.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The lines on Wednesday were huge, like the ribbon of humanity at Navy Pier in Chicago that snaked through halls and stairwells and along the pier and then stretched, amazingly, out to and under Lake Shore Drive. Young illegal immigrants by the tens of thousands formed similar lines in other cities across the country. They lined up outside churches and nonprofit agencies, holding paperwork and folders, to learn more about a new Obama administration policy that would protect them from deportation and give them permission to work.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Long Lines and Big Dreams', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Long Lines and Big Dreams', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigrants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dream Act (Development, Relief and Education for Minors Act)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deportation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-18T02:11:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee8d85ea-254e-59fc-a1a7-0348f121630a', 'word_count': 536, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee8d85ea-254e-59fc-a1a7-0348f121630a'}, {'abstract': 'On a recent night the crowd at Rosemary’s, a two-month old trattoria in the West Village, included a Norwegian-Italian couple, who discussed wedding traditions with the husband’s boss.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/19/nyregion/at-rosemarys-elegant-newlyweds-out-with-the-boss.html', 'snippet': 'On a recent night the crowd at Rosemary’s, a two-month old trattoria in the West Village, included a Norwegian-Italian couple, who discussed wedding traditions with the husband’s boss.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is there a local restaurant rule — Farmer’s Law? — that holds that the simpler and earthier the cuisine, the more sophisticated the clientele? That, at least, would seem to be the case at Rosemary’s, a two-month-old trattoria at the corner of Greenwich Avenue and 10th Street in the West Village. Rosemary’s not only has a rough-hewed Tuscan farmhouse look, with distressed wood and exposed beams; it also has a rooftop garden of herbs and vegetables (and soon, perhaps, a beehive and a chicken coop). The daily produce is transported from garden to kitchen in a retro-old-world manner: dropped in a basket and lowered with a block and tackle. In the dining room, one finds a discerning and affluent crowd, which one night last week included a newly wed Norwegian-Italian couple and the husband’s boss.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/nyregion/19TABLE_SPAN/19TABLE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/19/nyregion/19TABLE_SPAN/19TABLE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/nyregion/19TABLE_SPAN/19TABLE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/nyregion/19TABLE_SPAN/19TABLE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, 