'Continuing with my Ryan series, let’s look at what his budget (pdf) actually proposes (as opposed to vaguely promises) in its first decade.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ryan: The First Decade', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-18T06:40:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7fa53cd8-2691-5c6c-bb9f-2f4988fe9619', 'word_count': 260, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7fa53cd8-2691-5c6c-bb9f-2f4988fe9619'}, {'abstract': 'Summary of the findings released by India’s comptroller and auditor general on Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/18/reports-on-government-contracts-deal-another-blow-to-coalition/', 'snippet': 'Summary of the findings released by India’s comptroller and auditor general on Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reports on Government Contracts Deal Another Blow to Coalition', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coal', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Reliance Power', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-18T06:51:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hari Kumar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hari', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kumar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8b1325e2-5ba4-524c-bb13-33aeb58445e4', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8b1325e2-5ba4-524c-bb13-33aeb58445e4'}, {'abstract': 'What began as an isolated communal conflict has set off a frenzied exodus by migrants from northeast living and working outside their native states.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/18/echoes-of-northeastern-violence-ripple-across-indian-cities/', 'snippet': 'What began as an isolated communal conflict has set off a frenzied exodus by migrants from northeast living and working outside their native states.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Like a fever, fear has spread across India this week, from big cities like Bangalore to smaller places like Mysore, a contagion fueling a message: Run. Head home. Flee,” Jim Yardley wrote in The New York Times. “And that is what thousands of migrants from the country’s distant northeastern states are doing, jamming into train stations in an exodus challenging the Indian ideals of tolerance and diversity.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Echoes of Northeastern Violence Ripple Across Indian Cities', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-18T08:06:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b854ec68-f772-5582-b5be-f61d40d2e1ce', 'word_count': 238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b854ec68-f772-5582-b5be-f61d40d2e1ce'}, {'abstract': 'Political news from today’s Times and around the Web, plus a summary of the weekly party addresses.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/18/the-weekend-word-empty-benches/', 'snippet': 'Political news from today’s Times and around the Web, plus a summary of the weekly party addresses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today’s Times', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Weekend Word: Empty Benches', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-18T10:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jada F. Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jada', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/749034ad-4e94-5061-92e2-52f13961f487', 'word_count': 610, 'uri': 'nyt://article/749034ad-4e94-5061-92e2-52f13961f487'}, {'abstract': 'If Jamaal Charles stays healthy, this offense can be good enough to get by, and the Chiefs have a chance to be contenders in the A.F.C. West.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/18/kansas-city-chiefs-2012-n-f-l-season-preview/', 'snippet': 'If Jamaal Charles stays healthy, this offense can be good enough to get by, and the Chiefs have a chance to be contenders in the A.F.C. West.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two years ago, everything was going great for the Kansas City Chiefs. They were a bright young team under a bright young coach, Todd Haley. They rode the league’s best rushing attack to a surprising A.F.C. West title. They had invested $28 million guaranteed in quarterback Matt Cassel, whom they felt was a good fit in Haley’s shotgun-based passing game. General Manager Scott Pioli was building this team the Patriot Way, stockpiling depth on both sides of the ball and retooling the classic 3-4 defense that former Patriots defensive coordinator Romeo Crennel was running. This was a team on the rise.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kansas City Chiefs, 2012 N.F.L. Season Preview', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kansas City Chiefs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-18T10:00:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Benoit', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benoit', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1bf42df-c134-58cb-853f-04bf7c276273', 'word_count': 2804, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1bf42df-c134-58cb-853f-04bf7c276273'}, {'abstract': 'A round-up of news from across India.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/18/newswallah-bharat-edition-52/', 'snippet': 'A round-up of news from across India.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jammu and Kashmir: In the Shopian district of southern Kashmir, people using the names Lashkar Al Qaida and Al Qaida Mujahideen\xa0 have been putting up posters threatening acid attacks on women who do not live in purdah or who carry cellphones, Kashmir Live  reported. The police said the perpetrators were not affiliated with any actual militant outfit and that two school dropouts were suspected, according to the newspaper.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Newswallah: Bharat Edition', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-18T11:40:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3fb6d833-46ec-579c-86cc-eea16981f9bd', 'word_count': 377, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3fb6d833-46ec-579c-86cc-eea16981f9bd'}, {'abstract': 'The report of Kim Jong-un’s visit comes a few days before the United States and South Korea begin joint military exercises.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/19/world/asia/north-korean-leader-visits-troops-near-disputed-sea-border.html', 'snippet': 'The report of Kim Jong-un’s visit comes a few days before the United States and South Korea begin joint military exercises.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEOUL, South Korea — The North Korean leader, Kim Jong-un, has visited soldiers stationed near the two Koreas’ disputed sea border and bestowed official accolades on the artillery unit that shelled a South Korean island in 2010, killing four people, the North’s state-run news agency reported Saturday. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/world/19korea/19korea-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/19/world/19korea/19korea-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/world/19korea/19korea-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 658, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/world/19korea/19korea-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1316, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'image