'byline': {'original': 'Edward Chun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/9962b7dc-839c-54d1-a17f-a1e494aa07d4', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/9962b7dc-839c-54d1-a17f-a1e494aa07d4'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama took on the budget proposals of Mitt Romney and Representative Paul D. Ryan at a campaign stop in New Hampshire, accusing the Republican ticket of seeking to cut trillions of dollars in taxes for the wealthy while raising taxes for the middle class.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/18/to-obama-republican-budget-plans-are-snake-oil/', 'snippet': 'President Obama took on the budget proposals of Mitt Romney and Representative Paul D. Ryan at a campaign stop in New Hampshire, accusing the Republican ticket of seeking to cut trillions of dollars in taxes for the wealthy while raising taxes for...', 'lead_paragraph': 'WINDHAM, N.H. — President Obama took on the budget proposals of Mitt Romney and Representative Paul D. Ryan on Saturday, accusing the Republican ticket of seeking to cut trillions of dollars in taxes for the wealthy while raising taxes for the middle class and making deep cuts to programs that would benefit working Americans.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To Obama, Republican Budget Plans Are ‘Snake Oil’', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Paul D Jr', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Hampshire', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-18T18:22:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Helene Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Helene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c9e7594-4fb3-50cd-93cd-9cbc7d72cb32', 'word_count': 485, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c9e7594-4fb3-50cd-93cd-9cbc7d72cb32'}, {'abstract': 'Writing fiction draws on many of the same skills that, as an actor, I have been practicing my entire life.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/18/act-like-a-writer/', 'snippet': 'Writing fiction draws on many of the same skills that, as an actor, I have been practicing my entire life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I think there is a natural curiosity that many people have when they hear about an actor writing fiction. While it’s not exactly comparable to proving a mathematical theorem (Danica McKellar, the girl from “The Wonder Years,” can boast about that feat), it does seem an unusual endeavor to some.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Act Like a Writer', 'kicker': 'Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-18T18:26:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Molly Ringwald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Molly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ringwald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/76d9298a-e277-597c-8348-96ccc7710c4a', 'word_count': 1220, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76d9298a-e277-597c-8348-96ccc7710c4a'}, {'abstract': 'Why do we continue to insist on our independence and self-determination — stubbornly, irrationally and often recklessly?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/18/deluded-individualism/', 'snippet': 'Why do we continue to insist on our independence and self-determination — stubbornly, irrationally and often recklessly?', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is a curious passage early in Freud’s “Ego and the Id” where he remarks that the id behaves “as if” it were unconscious. The phrase is puzzling, but the meaning is clear: the id is the secret driver of our desires, the desires that animate our conscious life, but the ego does not recognize it as such. The ego — what we take to be our conscious, autonomous self — is ignorant to the agency of the id, and sees itself in the driver seat instead. Freud offers the following metaphor: the ego is like a man on horseback, struggling to contain the powerful beast beneath; to the extent that the ego succeeds in guiding this beast, it’s only by “transforming the id’s will into action as if it were its own.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Deluded Individualism', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tea Party Movement', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Freud, Sigmund', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spinoza, Baruch', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-18T18:30:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Firmin DeBrabander', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Firmin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'DeBrabander', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4659f8fa-dcae-535f-98d6-417d9bd5323d', 'word_count': 1395, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4659f8fa-dcae-535f-98d6-417d9bd5323d'}, {'abstract': 'Jonathan Hargett was once thought to be the first point guard to jump from high school to the N.B.A., but that was before a tumultuous college career and a prison sentence. He blames himself for his past decisions, which include years of abusing marijuana.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/19/sports/basketball/basketball-star-jonathan-hargetts-story-is-a-cautionary-tale.html', 'snippet': 'Jonathan Hargett was once thought to be the first point guard to jump from high school to the N.B.A., but that was before a tumultuous college career and a prison sentence. He blames himself for his past decisions, which include years of abusing m...', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHESAPEAKE, Va. — From the playgrounds of Richmond, Va., to the highest levels of international basketball, the exploits of Jonathan Hargett still resonate.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/sports/hargettjp1/hargettjp1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/19/sports/hargettjp1/hargettjp1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/19/sports/hargettjp1/hargettjp1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'ran