lic fast against graft and the governing Congress party. “Ramdev’s appeal is a strange mix of religiosity, television fame, rustic braggadocio and OBC aspiration,” she wrote, referring to the category of castes and communities known as “Other Backward Class.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Newswallah: Long Reads Edition', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-19T10:37:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Niharika Mandhana', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Niharika', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mandhana', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9eb92507-9be1-588e-af0c-88acc0013a18', 'word_count': 428, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9eb92507-9be1-588e-af0c-88acc0013a18'}, {'abstract': 'For a second straight preseason game, the Jets did not produce an offensive touchdown. But if you were in the right mood, they provided a chuckle.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/19/jets-offense-supplies-the-comedy/', 'snippet': 'For a second straight preseason game, the Jets did not produce an offensive touchdown. But if you were in the right mood, they provided a chuckle.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Thank God it’s a preseason game.”— Jets right tackle Wayne Hunter, with perhaps the quotation of the preseason', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jets’ Offense Supplies the Comedy', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hunter, Wayne', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tebow, Tim', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-19T10:38:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6963dbd9-4d7a-5930-bf64-0a4cf9abaaf6', 'word_count': 405, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6963dbd9-4d7a-5930-bf64-0a4cf9abaaf6'}, {'abstract': 'My objections to my son’s musical choices aren’t about taste, but about lyrics that objectify women and legitimize violence against them.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/19/remixing-parental-objections-into-demeaning-rap-lyrics/', 'snippet': 'My objections to my son’s musical choices aren’t about taste, but about lyrics that objectify women and legitimize violence against them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few weeks ago, I was cruising down the freeway with my 14-year-old son, Nathaniel, and as always, he asked if he could listen to music. I said yes, and he slipped in a CD. Many times, when I am driving and he is listening to music, I tune out. But on this occasion, I was very tuned in — and horrified by what I heard. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Remixing Parental Objections Into Rap Lyrics', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rap and Hip-Hop', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teenagers and Adolescence', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tyga', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-19T11:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randye Hoder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randye', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4635c4c9-93cc-5d85-bbd7-51d728470821', 'word_count': 726, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4635c4c9-93cc-5d85-bbd7-51d728470821'}, {'abstract': 'The new coach at Swansea, Michael Laudrup, was respected as one of the most creative players ever. Now the question is whether he can pass his gift on to lesser players.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/20/sports/soccer/20iht-soccer20.html', 'snippet': 'The new coach at Swansea, Michael Laudrup, was respected as one of the most creative players ever. Now the question is whether he can pass his gift on to lesser players.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SHEFFIELD, ENGLAND — He played like an angel. He was a passing player in every sense of the term, moving through the ’80s and ’90s to help clubs win in Denmark, Italy, Spain and the Netherlands.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nuggets of Value at Swansea City', 'kicker': 'Global Soccer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Role, but a Glimpse of the Old', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'English Premier League', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Queens Park Rangers (Soccer Team)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-19T12:17:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Hughes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'midd