his week’s contest were varied and unpredictable, and as usual, we had a hard time choosing our favorites. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Student Contest Winner | A Staged Drowning', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-20T00:54:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/17039fb1-6855-5cca-8a6a-685a1bb1cdf9', 'word_count': 570, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17039fb1-6855-5cca-8a6a-685a1bb1cdf9'}, {'abstract': 'We’re taking a weeklong break on the blog, but here are five ways to use our offerings to plan ahead for the school year.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/19/five-ways-to-plan-for-the-school-year-while-were-on-vacation-this-week/', 'snippet': 'We’re taking a weeklong break on the blog, but here are five ways to use our offerings to plan ahead for the school year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We’re taking a break this week, both to get ready for the new school year and to indulge in summer pleasures while we still can. (Deep fried butter on a stick, anyone?) We hope you’re doing the same.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Five Ways to Plan for the School Year (While We’re on Vacation This Week)', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-20T00:55:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2a21f51-80ff-58d7-a023-5d15a56a0100', 'word_count': 860, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2a21f51-80ff-58d7-a023-5d15a56a0100'}, {'abstract': 'The past five Republican vice-presidential candidates have all been quite conservative. But the four who preceded Ryan also had other strengths — and they were usually chosen in the hope of balancing out some perceived deficiency at the top of the ticket.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/19/in-picking-ryan-romney-makes-an-all-in-move/', 'snippet': 'The past five Republican vice-presidential candidates have all been quite conservative. But the four who preceded Ryan also had other strengths — and they were usually chosen in the hope of balancing out some perceived deficiency at the top of the...', 'lead_paragraph': 'The past five Republican vice-presidential candidates have all been quite conservative. But the four who preceded Representative Paul D. Ryan also had other strengths — and they were usually chosen in the hope of balancing out some perceived deficiency at the top of the ticket.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Picking Ryan, Romney Makes an All-In Move', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Picking Ryan, Romney Makes an All-In Move', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vice Presidents and Vice Presidency (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Paul D Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-20T00:56:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b41633b-41a2-5e4e-b73a-624e33d590f0', 'word_count': 1224, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b41633b-41a2-5e4e-b73a-624e33d590f0'}, {'abstract': 'So much for the real debate we were supposed to have once Paul Ryan joined the ticket. What we have instead is the triumph of style over substance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/20/opinion/krugman-an-unserious-man.html', 'snippet': 'So much for the real debate we were supposed to have once Paul Ryan joined the ticket. What we have instead is the triumph of style over substance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mitt Romney’s choice of Paul Ryan as his running mate led to a wave of pundit accolades. Now, declared writer after writer, we’re going to have a real debate about the nation’s fiscal future. This was predictable: never mind the Tea Party, Mr. Ryan’s true constituency is the commentariat, which years ago decided that he was the Honest, Serious Conservative, whose proposals deserve respect even if you don’t like him.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Unserious Man', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Unserious Man', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Paul D Jr', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vice Presidents and Vice Presidency (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-20T00:57:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1952fe2-9afe-5723-b81a-3b3dd6f57dea', 'word_count': 828, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1952fe2-9afe-5723-b81a-3b3dd6f57dea'}, {'abstract': 'Catholic schools have been bleeding enrollment for years, but some have attracted a new clientele by offering services normally found in expensive private schools.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/20/nyregion/to-survive-some-catholic-schools-rebrand-to-lure-wealthier-families.html', 'snippet': 'Catholic schools have been bleeding enrollment for years, but some have attracted a new clientele by offering services normally found in expensive private schools.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Catholic schools have been bleeding enrollment and money for years, and many have been forced to close. But some, like St. Stephen of Hungary, on the Upper East Side of Manhattan, have found a way to thrive — attracting a more affluent clientele by offering services and classes more commonly found in expensive private schools. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/20/nyregion/JP-CATHOLIC1/JP-CATHOLIC1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/20/nyregion/JP-CATHOLIC1/JP-CATHOLIC1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/20/nyregion/JP-CATHOLIC1/JP-CATHOLIC1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/20/nyregion/JP-CATHOLIC1/JP-CATHOLIC1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': '