beginning to benefit from a Supreme Court law that mandates 25 percent reservation in admissions for those whose families make less than $1,800 annually.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Like many 6-year-olds, Pankaj Disht clams up when speaking to a stranger,” Gayatri Rangachari Shah wrote in The International Herald Tribune. “But since switching to a private school, he has become more open and says he enjoys school and has many friends.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Private Schools Open Doors to Kids From Low-Income Families', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-20T06:17:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ddf9f97-97b1-5ba0-bbef-a1a6a2e5d0ba', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ddf9f97-97b1-5ba0-bbef-a1a6a2e5d0ba'}, {'abstract': 'Amitava Kumar, writer and professor, asks natives of Patna, the capital of Bihar State to share experiences about their city.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/20/what-happens-in-patna-stays-in-patna/', 'snippet': 'Amitava Kumar, writer and professor, asks natives of Patna, the capital of Bihar State to share experiences about their city.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the travel writer Trevor Fishlock went to my hometown of Patna, a journalist greeted him by saying, “Welcome to hell.” A few days later, that particular journalist, who had been zealous in his defense of the freedom of the press, was beaten unconscious.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Happens in Patna, Stays in Patna?', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bihar (India)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-20T07:21:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amitava Kumar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amitava', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kumar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/63780806-65ba-5e59-bf2f-a33392286b31', 'word_count': 1116, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63780806-65ba-5e59-bf2f-a33392286b31'}, {'abstract': 'After 10 American service members were killed by Afghan security forces over a two-week period, senior generals met in Kabul to discuss a wave of such green-on-blue attacks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/21/world/asia/us-generals-meet-to-study-afghan-green-on-blue-attacks.html', 'snippet': 'After 10 American service members were killed by Afghan security forces over a two-week period, senior generals met in Kabul to discuss a wave of such green-on-blue attacks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KABUL, Afghanistan — Gen. Martin E. Dempsey, chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, arrived in Afghanistan on Monday for discussions on the progress of the war, including an intensified wave of insider attacks by Afghan forces on NATO service members, even as New Zealand became the latest coalition partner to announce an accelerated troop withdrawal. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/21/world/21afghanistan/21afghanistan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 1024, 'subT