ualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/036f8135-e163-52c1-a42d-a87d7f0335c1', 'word_count': 253, 'uri': 'nyt://article/036f8135-e163-52c1-a42d-a87d7f0335c1'}, {'abstract': 'August is hardly as slow as it seems. Public hearings are scheduled almost every weeknight for the remainder of the month on proposals to open charter schools in 2013, and the Panel for Educational Policy is meeting this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/schoolbook/2012/08/20/calendar-for-this-months-department-of-education-hearings/', 'snippet': 'August is hardly as slow as it seems. Public hearings are scheduled almost every weeknight for the remainder of the month on proposals to open charter schools in 2013, and the Panel for Educational Policy is meeting this week.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': "Calendar for this Month's Department of Education Hearings", 'kicker': 'SchoolBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-20T16:20:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By BETH FERTIG', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Beth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'FERTIG', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2a9d354-2625-51ab-8ea5-4f0780fd456b', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2a9d354-2625-51ab-8ea5-4f0780fd456b'}, {'abstract': 'The Russian punk band Pussy Riot did something artists always aim to do but rarely achieve: It has made people question their opinions and beliefs.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/20/back-from-the-u-s-s-r/', 'snippet': 'The Russian punk band Pussy Riot did something artists always aim to do but rarely achieve: It has made people question their opinions and beliefs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Twenty-one years ago, Russia’s future looked bleak. The country was still the U.S.S.R, and for a few years it seemed to be splitting apart at the seams even as it continued to resemble an unmovable monolith. The Soviet president, Mikhail Gorbachev, had relaxed censorship and lifted some restraints on private enterprise, unleashing a liberating force. But on Aug. 19, 1991, he was placed under house arrest, and a K.G.B.-led junta declared itself in charge. It seemed the Soviet flirtation with democracy was ending.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Back from the U.S.S.R.', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cold War Era', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Coups D'Etat and Attempted Coups D'Etat", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of the Press', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monuments and Memorials (Structures)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gorbachev, Mikhail S', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Putin, Vladimir V', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yeltsin, Boris N', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'KGB', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pussy Riot', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Moscow (Russia)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'USSR (Former Soviet Union)', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-20T16:28:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Masha Gessen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Masha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gessen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/83273d69-7098-5fe2-b057-80d04259651a', 'word_count': 700, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83273d69-7098-5fe2-b057-80d04259651a'}, {'abstract': 'In 1862 the president oversaw the suppression of a violent American Indian uprising – and dealt with it with surprising clemency.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/20/lincoln-and-the-sioux/', 'snippet': 'In 1862 the president oversaw the suppression of a violent American Indian uprising – and dealt with it with surprising clemency.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During his term as president, Abraham Lincoln was responsible for the largest mass execution – and the greatest act of clemency – in our nation’s history. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lincoln and the Sioux', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Native Americans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Minnesota', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-20T16:30:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ron Soodalter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Soodalter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/33853936-730d-5b73-9246-05ea6e2165b4', 'word_count': 1317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33853936-730d-5b73-9246-05ea6e2165b4'}, {'abstract': 'Focusing on paying off a higher number of accounts quickly may lead to more success than paying off the higher-interest debt first, according to new research.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/20/to-get-out-of-debt-it-may-help-to-think-small/', 'snippet': 'Focusing on paying off a higher number of accounts quickly may lead to more success than paying off the higher-interest debt first, according to new research.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Score one for Dave Ramsey, it seems.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To Get Out of Debt, It May Help to Think Small', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-20T16:42:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95ed3a3d-0394-5a25-92dc-f161d24e3b95', 'word_count': 621, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95ed3a3d-0394-5a25-92dc-f161d24e3b95'}, {'abstract': 'House Speaker John A. Boehner said Monday that Representative Eric Cantor of Virginia, the majority leader, did the right thing by scolding Republican House members who jumped into the Sea Of Galilee – one of them without any clothes on – during a trip to Israel.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/20/boehner-says-cantor-reprimanded-members-over-galilee-swim/', 'snippet': 'House Speaker John A. Boehner said Monday that Representative Eric Cantor of Virginia, the majority leader, did the right thing by scolding Republican House members who jumped into the Sea Of Galilee – one of them without any clothes on – during a...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Speaker John A. Boehner said on Monday that Representative Eric Cantor of Virginia, the House majority leader, had done the right thing by scolding Republican House members who jumped into the Sea Of Galilee – one of them without any clothes on – during a trip to Israel.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Boehner Says Cantor Reprimanded Members Over Galilee Swim', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cantor, Eric', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-20T16:49:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Steinhauer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steinhauer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4133ef02-89ee-550e-bfb2-dc539150a389', 'word_count': 281, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4133ef02-89ee-550e-bfb2-dc539150a389'}, {'abstract': 'Most Mormons tilt to the Republican Party, but some are promoting their ties to the Democrats.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/20/mormon-democrats-to-gather-at-charlotte-convention/', 'snippet': 'Most Mormons tilt to the Republican Party, but some are promoting their ties to the Democrats.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mormons who are Democrats are always a lonely bunch, but never more so than in this election year when one of their fellow church members is about to make history and be crowned the Republican nominee for president. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mormon Democrats to Gather at Charlotte Convention', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mormon Democrats To Gather in Charlotte', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Democratic National Convention', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mormons (Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-Day Saints)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eisenhower, Dwight David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reid, Harry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nevada', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Utah', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-20T16:59:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laurie Goodstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laurie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2fcf52d-caa8-5170-9bde-2c7d0d36e773', 'word_count': 594, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2fcf52d-caa8-5170-9bde-2c7d0d36e773'}, {'abstract': 'A new high-style hostel may be Miami Beach’s most unlikely scenester hideaway.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/20/first-look-the-freehand-miami-beach/', 'snippet': 'A new high-style hostel may be Miami Beach’s most unlikely scenester hideaway.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When it opens in time for Art Basel Miami, the high-style hostel Freehand may be Miami Beach’s most unlikely scenester hideaway. And its developer, Andrew Zobler, the C.E.O. of Sydell Group (which owns the NoMad Hotel and brought the Ace Hotels to New York and Palm Springs), is betting that it’s a game changer in the hospitality business. He hired Roman & Williams, which designed the Ace New York, to create the interiors — colorful spaces, some with bunk beds, that look like a styled-out room at sleepaway camp. “Think seaside boathouse, lifeguard school, a homemade summer camp near the ocean,” says the designer Robin Standefer. Zobler, who traveled to hostels in the States and abroad and says he found that they “offered something lacking in many hotels — a culture where the guests were open to and often seeking out meaningful interactions with each other and where new friendships were being formed.” To that end, Freehand will have a vegetable garden, swimming pool, table tennis, bicycles and even art classes, as well as a restaurant and bar run by the folks behind local favorite Broken Shaker. And there will be towels, sheets, pillows and an amenity-stocked bathroom for every room — not always a given at hostels. As Standefer puts it, “it’s not a place with cheap beds to just crash in.” About 15 of the 60 rooms will be private (for one or two guests), while shared rooms will accommodate up to eight beds, with rates starting at about $50 for a bed in a shared room. Booking begins today.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'First Look | The Freehand Miami Beach', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'HOTELS AND MOTELS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Miami Beach (Fla)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-20T17:00:35+0000'