e': 'subject', 'value': 'ABORTION', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-20T18:49:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear and Trip Gabriel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0bf802af-151d-55dd-93cf-4bc8b3cf4955', 'word_count': 943, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0bf802af-151d-55dd-93cf-4bc8b3cf4955'}, {'abstract': 'Researchers tracking whales off Cape Cod are hoping to use their findings to push for changes in fishing and shipping rules.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/21/science/earth/close-to-cape-cod-shore-humpback-whales-are-far-from-safe.html', 'snippet': 'Researchers tracking whales off Cape Cod are hoping to use their findings to push for changes in fishing and shipping rules.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WOODS HOLE, Mass. — When the whale known as Touché is hungry for a school of fatty fish, he circles below them, fashioning a net of air by streaming bubbles from his blowhole. Then he corkscrews toward the surface of the Gulf of Maine, herding the fish into an ever tighter packet with the bubbles and his 30-ton body. Finally he opens his jaw wide, takes a monstrous gulp and relaxes, breathing deeply at the water’s surface.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/21/science/21WHAL_SPAN/21WHAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 241, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/21/science/21WHAL_SPAN/21WHAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 241}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'typ