e in the ratings, is leaving the network to become PepsiCo’s senior vice president of global strategy and planning.', 'lead_paragraph': '9:57 a.m. | Updated ABC News will cover this presidential campaign without one of its veteran journalists. Jon Banner, executive producer of George Stephanopoulos’s Sunday morning public affairs show “This Week,” will leave ABC News for a job at PepsiCo.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Head of ABC’s ‘This Week’ Is Leaving to Join PepsiCo', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suspensions, Dismissals and Resignations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Banner, Jon', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ABC News', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pepsico Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-21T13:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Chozick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chozick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d59e70e-984a-526f-8343-41b3bcdfe750', 'word_count': 339, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d59e70e-984a-526f-8343-41b3bcdfe750'}, {'abstract': 'Russian dancers performed bhangra and danced to popular Bollywood tunes at the Downtown Dance Festival, which ended Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/21/indian-dance-via-bollywood-by-way-of-russia-near-wall-street/', 'snippet': 'Russian dancers performed bhangra and danced to popular Bollywood tunes at the Downtown Dance Festival, which ended Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The weeklong Downtown Dance Festival closed Friday in downtown New York with its annual “Erasing Borders” program of Indian dance, Alastair Macaulay wrote in The New York Times. Despite the Indo-American label, the organizers included countries other than India and the United States, he wrote. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Indian Dance via Bollywood, by Way of Russia, Near Wall Street', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-21T13:00:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/972dc43d-a81d-57da-8c8c-45404610ad33', 'word_count': 239, 'uri': 'nyt://article/972dc43d-a81d-57da-8c8c-45404610ad33'}, {'abstract': 'Mitt Romney emphasizes the commonalities between the Mormon faith and mainstream Christianity in talking about his religion.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/21/obama-and-romney-discuss-role-of-faith-in-their-lives/', 'snippet': 'Mitt Romney emphasizes the commonalities between the Mormon faith and mainstream Christianity in talking about his religion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Religion is enough of a political minefield that President Obama and Mitt Romney have declined interviews with reporters about their faith. But they agreed to write answers to nine questions about their personal faith and the role of faith in public life posed to them by Cathedral Age, the magazine of the Washington National Cathedral.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama and Romney Discuss Role of Faith in Their Lives', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mormons (Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-Day Saints)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Washington National Cathedral', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-21T13:01:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laurie Goodstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laurie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/04937073-a960-512a-8e8e-2bb0728a7974', 'word_count': 416, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04937073-a960-512a-8e8e-2bb0728a7974'}, {'abstract': 'Todd Akin of Missouri, a Republican candidate for Senate, said he used “the wrong words in the wrong way” when he suggested last weekend that rape rarely leads to pregnancy and asked voters for their forgiveness.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/21/akin-asks-for-forgiveness-in-new-ad/', 'snippet': 'Todd Akin of Missouri, a Republican candidate for Senate, said he used “the wrong words in the wrong way” when he suggested last weekend that rape rarely leads to pregnancy and asked voters for their forgiveness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As part of his effort to keep his place on the November ballot, Representative Todd Akin, Republican of Missouri, released an ad Tuesday in which he asks for viewers’ forgiveness for his comments on Sunday about rape.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Akin Asks for ‘Forgiveness’ in New Ad', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Akin, Todd', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Missouri', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-21T13:29:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katharine Q. Seelye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katharine', 'middlename': 'Q.', 'lastname': 'Seelye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd5ba32a-2d02-5300-b91f-44d0ca1f0397', 'word_count': 222, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd5ba32a-2d02-5300-b91f-44d0ca1f0397'}, {'abstract': 'Also, the outcry over the jailing of the feminist punk band Pussy Riot hasn’t stopped Russian prosecutors from pressing ahead in the case, and a Japanese journalist is killed covering the fighting in Syria.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/21/the-breakfast-meeting-akins-evolving-media-strategy-and-student-autonomy/', 'snippet': 'Also, the outcry over the jailing of the feminist punk band Pussy Riot hasn’t stopped Russian prosecutors from pressing ahead in the case, and a Japanese journalist is killed covering the fighting in Syria.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In trying to calm the anger swirling over his comments about “legitimate rape” and whether rape can lead to pregnancy, Representative Todd Akin, the Republican candidate for Senate from Missouri, scheduled a prime-time appearance on CNN on Monday, but ultimately didn’t show up. The host who was stood up by Mr. Akin, Piers Morgan, made the most of the nonappearance, Brian Stelter writes, telling the audience: “You’re looking live at the empty chair that Todd Akin was supposed to be sitting in for a live, prime-time exclusive interview.” The camera was fixed on the empty chair during the show, which appeared in an inset box.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Akin’s Evolving Media Strategy, and Student Autonomy', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-21T13:47:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc969d48-4571-5e94-8d61-2306a3b951eb', 'word_count': 602, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc969d48-4571-5e94-8d61-2306a3b951eb'}, {'abstract': 'Sameer Bhalotra, President Obama’s former senior director of cybersecurity, is joining Impermium, a security start-up that wants to remove malware and spam from the social Web using “big data” analytical techniques. He is the latest cybersecurity expert to leave the public sector for a security start-up.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/21/former-white-house-cybersecurity-official-joins-start-up/', 'snippet': 'Sameer Bhalotra, President Obama’s former senior director of cybersecurity, is joining Impermium, a security start-up that wants to remove malware and spam from the social Web using “big data” analytical techniques. He is the latest cybersecurity ...', 'lead_paragraph': ' In January, Sameer Bhalotra left his position as President Obama’s senior director for cybersecurity without discussing his next move.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Former White House Cybersecurity Official Joins Start-Up', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name'