 starting the season opener for the Dolphins?” It was not exactly Nobel Prize territory.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Leading Off: Augusta National Inches Forward', 'kicker': 'On Par', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bayless, Skip', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Payne, Billy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Augusta National Golf Club', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-21T16:12:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Reddicliffe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Reddicliffe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f837fa02-7e21-5ec0-a524-03b7a8a566ca', 'word_count': 576, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f837fa02-7e21-5ec0-a524-03b7a8a566ca'}, {'abstract': 'Bill Gates, Liberty Global and Lux Capital are investing in Kymeta, a start-up uses a lightweight material to make antennas intended to improve satellite connections used for broadband Internet.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/08/21/antenna-company-raises-12-million-from-bill-gates-and-lux-capital/', 'snippet': 'Bill Gates, Liberty Global and Lux Capital are investing in Kymeta, a start-up uses a lightweight material to make antennas intended to improve satellite connections used for broadband Internet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Intellectual Ventures may be best known for its huge archive of patents and its willingness to pursue lawsuits in defense of those patents, but it’s also a part-time incubator to promising start-ups.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Antenna Company Raises $12 Million From Bill Gates and Lux Capital', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inventions and Patents', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gates, Bill', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cisco Systems Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Intellectual Ventures', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lux Capital', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corporation', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'TerraPower', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-21T16:12:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evelyn M. Rusli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evelyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa2c0705-7fc5-5401-9433-ef31824a4dd4', 'word_count': 230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa2c0705-7fc5-5401-9433-ef31824a4dd4'}, {'abstract': 'A cheese from Beecher’s in Seattle and one from Morris County, N.J., win top honors from the American Cheese Society.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/22/dining/american-to-the-rind.html', 'snippet': 'A cheese from Beecher’s in Seattle and one from Morris County, N.J., win top honors from the American Cheese Society.', 'lead_paragraph': 'They’ve been doing back flips at Beecher’s, the Seattle-based cheese company that has a shop, restaurant and cheesemaking room in New York: one of their entries won the gold at the annual meeting of the American Cheese Society earlier this month. Flagsheep, an aged mix of sheep’s and cow’s milk that comes from Seattle in tall 16-pound clothbound cylinders, beat out 1,710 other cheeses at the event in Raleigh, N.C. It has a richly grassy aroma and flavor, and a firm texture that suggests Cheddar. And what has the folks at Beecher’s especially proud is that it was made with pasteurized milks. (Pasteurization kills some beneficial bacteria that could enhance aging and the quality of the cheese.) “It’s a testament to the cheesemaking that a pasteurized cheese could win,” said Elena Santogade, the company’s lead cheesemonger. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/22/dining/22STUFF_CHEESE/22CHEESE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/22/dining/22STUFF_CHEESE/22CHEESE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1363, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/22/dining/22STUFF_CHEESE/22STUFF_CHEESE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/22/dining/22STUFF_CHEESE/22STUFF_CHEESE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'American to the Rind', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'American to the Rind', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'American Cheese Society', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cheese', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Beecher's Handmade Cheese", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-21T16:15:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/c118e0af-4c71-5820-9e46-7eaa159fcb69', 'word_count': 209, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c118e0af-4c71-5820-9e46-7eaa159fcb69'}, {'abstract': 'As Chinese Valentine’s Day nears, preparations for matchmaking events are picking up. Never mind chemistry, though, what counts is money and connections.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/21/romance-with-chinese-characteristics/', 'snippet': 'As Chinese Valentine’s Day nears, preparations for matchmaking events are picking up. Never mind chemistry, though, what counts is money and connections.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — Dating is hard at the best of times. In China the stakes are high from the outset: the expectation is that it should lead to marriage; never mind love for love’s sake.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romance With Chinese Characteristics', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dating and Courtship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marriages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reality Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Single Persons', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Valentine's Day", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Confucius', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beijing (China)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chengdu (China)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-21T16:16:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clarissa Sebag-Montefiore', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clarissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sebag-Montefiore', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/78e62914-42be-54a3-b8a2-29e0f6854e6d', 'word_count': 697, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78e62914-42be-54a3-b8a2-29e0f6854e6d'}, {'abstract': 'Du Jour, a new bakery specializing in desserts, has opened in Park Slope, Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/22/dining/more-dessert-for-park-slope.html', 'snippet': 'Du Jour, a new bakery specializing in desserts, has opened in Park Slope, Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Du Jour, a new Brooklyn bakery, offers a collection of well-made cookies, muffins, scones, cupcakes, cakes, brioches, croissants and desserts like butterscotch pudding. The simple but inviting brick-walled space is a largely do-it-yourself project by T. J. Obias; his wife, Vera Tong Obias; and their partner, Tae Kyung, who did most of the design and construction. The couple (shown) are both pastry chefs who have worked in a number of New York restaurants, including Dovetail, Morimoto, Café Gray and Buddakan. “But we never worked together,” Mr. Obias said. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/22/dining/22STUFF_BAKERY/22BAKERY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumb