ing (Real Estate)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate (Commercial)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-21T21:18:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'subsection_name': 'Commercial Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Satow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Satow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca40d4fb-4a92-5919-a1d6-f91bc929a772', 'word_count': 1244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca40d4fb-4a92-5919-a1d6-f91bc929a772'}, {'abstract': 'The writer and director Gurvinder Singh’s film “Alms for a Blind Horse” shows situations during a day and a night in the Punjab, in northwestern India.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/22/movies/alms-for-a-blind-horse-gurvinder-singhs-look-at-life.html', 'snippet': 'The writer and director Gurvinder Singh’s film “Alms for a Blind Horse” shows situations during a day and a night in the Punjab, in northwestern India.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “Alms for a Blind Horse” the writer and director Gurvinder Singh cares more about the weathered faces of villagers and the morning fog that settles over their town and fields than he does about storytelling. His method is a slow, careful formalism — so slow and so careful that his film can feel airless and overly aestheticized. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/22/arts/22alms/22alms-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/22/arts/22alms/22alms-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth'