Germany', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-22T15:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dfc857c3-1785-500f-9d8a-3dbf4826f08c', 'word_count': 376, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dfc857c3-1785-500f-9d8a-3dbf4826f08c'}, {'abstract': 'Floor plans for the Élysée Palace, the Interior Ministry, the Paris police headquarters and several large companies were stolen from a Parisian train station on Sunday, officials said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/22/world/europe/france-floor-plans-for-government-offices-stolen.html', 'snippet': 'Floor plans for the Élysée Palace, the Interior Ministry, the Paris police headquarters and several large companies were stolen from a Parisian train station on Sunday, officials said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A USB memory stick and discs containing the floor plans for the Élysée Palace, the Interior Ministry, the Paris police headquarters and several large companies were in a bag stolen from a car near a Parisian train station on Sunday, the Interior Ministry confirmed Tuesday. The plans, which were not encrypted, did not contain information about who occupied various rooms and were not classified, the ministry said in a statement. “The information they contain does not imperil the security of the sites concerned,” the statement said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'France: Floor Plans for Government Offices Stolen', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robberies and Thefts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-22T15:05:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Erlanger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Erlanger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/536e6983-96b5-57b8-aecf-115b8374905e', 'word_count': 152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/536e6983-96b5-57b8-aecf-115b8374905e'}, {'abstract': '“Ready Player One,” written by Ernest Cline, is a science fiction novel set in 2044 on a grimy version of Earth where people prefer to live in a video game over real life.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/22/one-on-one-ernest-cline-author-of-ready-player-one/', 'snippet': '“Ready Player One,” written by Ernest Cline, is a science fiction novel set in 2044 on a grimy version of Earth where people prefer to live in a video game over real life.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Ready Player One,” written by the 40-year-old Ernest Cline, is a science fiction novel set in 2044. It takes place on a grimy version of Earth where people prefer to live in a\xa0virtual\xa0reality video game rather than in real life. The book follows the story\xa0of\xa0Wade Watts, who is on an epic quest to solve a multilevel puzzle that involves playing 1980s video games and trivia. Here is an edited interview with Mr. Cline.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'One on One: Ernest Cline, Author of ‘Ready Player One’', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value':