'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Crime', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Doe, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roe, Richard', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-22T18:52:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Colin Moynihan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Colin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moynihan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42cc381b-f7c8-505b-b39a-aea468bc23ca', 'word_count': 659, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42cc381b-f7c8-505b-b39a-aea468bc23ca'}, {'abstract': 'The U.S. Postal Service, which printed 1 billion stamps featuring characters from “The Simpsons” in 2009 to commemorate the program’s 20-year-run on Fox, finds itself left with 682 million.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/22/postal-service-stuck-with-glut-of-simpsons-stamps/', 'snippet': 'The U.S. Postal Service, which printed 1 billion stamps featuring characters from “The Simpsons” in 2009 to commemorate the program’s 20-year-run on Fox, finds itself left with 682 million.', 'lead_paragraph': '4:42 p.m. | Updated Perhaps recalling the heady days when Bart Simpson T-shirts sold in the millions and a song like “Do the Bartman” had the power to become a cultural touchstone, the UnitedStates Postal Service produced 1 billion stamps featuring characters from “The Simpsons” in 2009 to commemorate the show’s 20th year on Fox. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Postal Service Stuck With Glut of ‘Simpsons’ Stamps', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Glut of ‘Simpsons’ Stamps', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Postal Service (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-22T18:55:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2cb23204-6474-51cc-bc16-dda226932fef', 'word_count': 282, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2cb23204-6474-51cc-bc16-dda226932fef'}, {'abstract': 'Having Cerebral Ballzy play at Santos Party House is like putting a tiger in a paper cage.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/22/notes-from-the-underground-cerebral-ballzy/', 'snippet': 'Having Cerebral Ballzy play at Santos Party House is like putting a tiger in a paper cage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Having Cerebral Ballzy play at Santos Party House is like putting a tiger in a paper cage. Ballzy, one of New York’s loudest, most raucous fire starters, comes with a thick crowd — one of elbow-throwing, neck-snapping, mosh-pit-shoving ruffians that doesn’t exactly jibe with Santos’s thorough, and intimidating, fleet of security guards. At a recent performance, a prominently displayed sign forbade moshing, leaving the audience somewhat castrated in its enthusiasm, but it was clear that on any other night, the scene would be pure chaos. The singer Honor Titus — channeling a mixture of Jimi Hendrix, Tony Hawk and a punk prince — was apparently singing about getting black-out drunk, refusing to be told what to do and being a native New Yorker, but you’d have to be reading a karaoke screen to know that. For the raucous among you, Cerebral Ballzy will perform at the Afro Punk festival in Brooklyn this weekend.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Notes From the Underground | Cerebral Ballzy', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-22T18:59:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By IO Tillett Wright', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Io', 'middlename': 'Tillett', 'lastname': 'Wright', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/00a3531d-8662-55f6-950d-081c52c291e4', 'word_count': 153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00a3531d-8662-55f6-950d-081c52c291e4'}, {'abstract': 'MyBizHomepage, a provider of business accounting software, was shut down by a wave of cyber attacks that apparently came from a disgruntled former employee.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/22/can-this-company-recover-from-a-cyberattack/', 'snippet': 'MyBizHomepage, a provider of business accounting software, was shut down by a wave of cyber attacks that apparently came from a disgruntled former employee.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A case study we’ve just published explains the challenges faced by Peter Justen, chief executive of MyBizHomepage, a Middleburg, Va., provider of business accounting software. The business was shut down by a wave of cyber attacks that apparently came from a disgruntled former employee.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Can This Company Recover From a Cyberattack?', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Starting Over After Cyberattack', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberattacks and Hackers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-22T19:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Darren Dahl', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Darren', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dahl', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b11e11e-6ed7-5736-b200-665370414755', 'word_count': 642, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b11e11e-6ed7-5736-b200-665370414755'}, {'abstract': 'A look at the blog Amass, whose creator is devoted to collecting examples of typography.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/22/a-manic-collection-of-orderly-designs/', 'snippet': 'A look at the blog Amass, whose creator is devoted to collecting examples of typography.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a design student with an inexplicable desire for accumulating all kinds of\xa0objects, I was filled with joy upon discovering Amass, the blog of James Phillips Williams, a former art director at Bergdorf Goodman. A successful graphic designer, Williams lives and works in New York with his wife, Allison Muench, where they run their own design studio, MW, whose clients have included the department store Takashimaya. I recently e-mailed Williams to find out more about his acquisitive nature and the blog.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Manic Collection of Orderly Designs', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Design', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-22T19:01:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sophie Mascatello', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sophie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mascatello', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/73ebff32-0437-5d4e-a43d-682ec47ad7d7', 'word_count': 236, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73ebff32-0437-5d4e-a43d-682ec47ad7d7'}, {'abstract': 'Her latest single, “We Are Never Ever Getting Back Together,” was released last week and shot to the top of radio playlists and iTunes’ song ranking, selling 623,000 downloads.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/22/taylor-swift-song-sets-a-record-for-digital-sales/', 'snippet': 'Her latest single, “We Are Never Ever Getting Back Together,” was released last week and shot to the top of radio playlists and iTunes’ song ranking, selling 623,000 downloads.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week on the music charts, Taylor Swift’s new song set a digital sales record, and the debut album by the rapper 2 Chainz opens at No. 1.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Taylor Swift Song Sets a Record for Digital Sales', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Lot of Downloads', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': '2 Chainz', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adele (Singer)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'BILLBOARD', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nielsen SoundScan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-22T19:08:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d591463b-4b44-57f3-846f-e3bf201c1e8d', 'word_count': 346, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d591463b-4b44-57f3-846f-e3bf201c1e8d'}, {'abstract': 'The bondholders have no direct way of making Belize pay up, but the country probably has to worry more about long-term access to the financial markets than a country like Argentina or even Greece.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/08/22/the-dance-between-belize-and-its-bondholders/', 'snippet': 'The bondholders have no direct way of making Belize pay up, but the country probably has to worry more about long-term access to the financial markets than a country like Argentina or even Greece.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is the dynamic at work in the negotiations between Belize and its bondholders. Really it’s the dynamic in most sovereign debt restructurings.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Dance Between Belize and Its Bondholders', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Belize', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-22T19:14:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen J. Lubben', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Lubben', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b163deb1-6177-570d-b59b-8e2b5eb5a77a', 'word_count': 454, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b163deb1-6177-570d-b59b-8e2b5eb5a77a'}, {'abstract': 'Loanlook.com aims to help students better manage their financial aid and student loans.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/22/a-web-site-that-aims-to-help-manage-student-loans/', 'snippet': 'Loanlook.com aims to help students better manage their financial aid and student loans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I don’t have student loans anymore (th