T21:47:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/faf2dc62-6ebe-54ba-8d73-2bcf80d33eeb', 'word_count': 1442, 'uri': 'nyt://article/faf2dc62-6ebe-54ba-8d73-2bcf80d33eeb'}, {'abstract': 'Planning for and adapting to climate change gained more formal status in the New York City Council when two new panels were added to the city’s government structure.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/22/new-yorks-city-council-adds-climate-change-panels/', 'snippet': 'Planning for and adapting to climate change gained more formal status in the New York City Council when two new panels were added to the city’s government structure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The exodus of City Hall aides has already begun as Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg’s term approaches its end next year, but climate change isn’t going anywhere. So in an attempt to continue Mr. Bloomberg’s environmental focus long after he’s gone, the City Council passed legislation\xa0on Wednesday that would make two panels advising the city on the threats of global warming a permanent fixture of government.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York’s City Council Adds Climate Change Panels', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'City Council (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-22T22:04