e': {'original': 'By Tim McKeough', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McKeough', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba24ab17-d408-5ef0-a63d-371e09c809b6', 'word_count': 156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba24ab17-d408-5ef0-a63d-371e09c809b6'}, {'abstract': 'David Weeks’s Lonely City USB hub mimics the New York skyline.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/23/garden/david-weekss-lonely-city-usb-hub.html', 'snippet': 'David Weeks’s Lonely City USB hub mimics the New York skyline.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like so many other New Yorkers, David Weeks is a bit of a voyeur. His latest product, the Lonely City USB hub, is the result of “walking down the street late at night, looking up at a building and seeing that one window lit up,” he said. “Maybe it’s a lamp, maybe it’s the glow of a TV.” The hub illuminates when plugged into an outlet, regardless of whether a device is charging (there’s room for four to charge at any given time). The buildings are based on a Google map view of 16th Street between Fifth and Sixth Avenues, a block Mr. Weeks said he has frequented over the years, whether on the way to teaching at Parsons or shopping at Paul Smith. “Google maps shows the ghosted buildings at a 45-degree angle — we looked at that block, broke it into several parts and then elaborated on it,” he said. The Windows- and Mac-compatible hub is $25 on Mr. Weeks’s retail site, tweekstudio.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/23/garden/23TOOLS_SPAN/23CURR3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/23/garden/23TOOLS_SPAN/23CURR3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight