e': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Data-Mining and Database Marketing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Poindexter, John M', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Security Agency', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T00:58:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shane Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/0be4b7f8-f583-5546-a1b6-db6b3b6ad34d', 'word_count': 827, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0be4b7f8-f583-5546-a1b6-db6b3b6ad34d'}, {'abstract': 'The Supreme Court’s solicitude for the free speech rights of corporations makes it likely that a Food and Drug Administration rule requiring graphic anti-smoking images on cigarette packages is doomed.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/22/the-free-speech-puzzle/', 'snippet': 'The Supreme Court’s solicitude for the free speech rights of corporations makes it likely that a Food and Drug Administration rule requiring graphic anti-smoking images on cigarette packages is doomed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thanks to a ruling last week by Australia’s High Court, smokers in that country soon won’t be able to pick up a pack of cigarettes without seeing what Advertising Age magazine calls “nauseatingly graphic pictures” of what their habit is doing to their bodies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Free Speech Puzzle', 'kicker': 'Linda Greenhouse', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leon, Richard 