le/c68ca188-17c2-5ea9-927a-589aab64cf0b', 'word_count': 216, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c68ca188-17c2-5ea9-927a-589aab64cf0b'}, {'abstract': 'The judgment was the latest victory in a battle that the city and state have waged to collect taxes on cigarettes sold by Indian-owned businesses to non-Indians.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/23/nyregion/tobacco-companies-broke-law-by-selling-untaxed-cigarettes-judge-rules.html', 'snippet': 'The judgment was the latest victory in a battle that the city and state have waged to collect taxes on cigarettes sold by Indian-owned businesses to non-Indians.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A federal judge ruled last week that two tobacco wholesalers illegally skirted paying millions of dollars in taxes by selling truckloads of untaxed cigarettes to retailers on an American Indian reservation on Long Island, which then resold them in the city at cut-rate prices. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tobacco Companies Broke Law by Selling Untaxed Cigarettes, Judge Rules', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tobacco Companies Broke Law by Selling Untaxed Cigarettes, Judge Rules', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smoking and Tobacco', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Native Americans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sales and Excise Taxes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Long Island (NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Amon, Carol Bagley', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T01:53:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mosi Secret', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mosi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Secret', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/23af3d49-0b1b-5af4-aaea-7497b93bfd86', 'word_count': 449, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23af3d49-0b1b-5af4-aaea-7497b93bfd86'}, {'abstract': 'The Environmental Protection Agency can’t win for losing. Once again, its carefully vetted rules aimed at reducing air pollution that crosses state lines have been shot down in court.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/23/opinion/another-rebuff-to-the-epa-for-cleaner-air.html', 'snippet': 'The Environmental Protection Agency can’t win for losing. Once again, its carefully vetted rules aimed at reducing air pollution that crosses state lines have been shot down in court.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What’s a federal agency to do? Twice in the last eight years, the Environmental Protection Agency has issued carefully vetted rules aimed at reducing air pollution that crosses state lines — the smog-forming nitrogen oxide pollutants and health-threatening sulfur dioxide that drift eastward from Midwestern power plants. And, in both cases, the agency has been shot down by the United States Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Another Rebuff for Cleaner Air', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Another Rebuff for Cleaner Air', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Environmental Protection Agency', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Air Pollution', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Clean Air Act', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T01:53:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1a48ad0-68d0-5682-91ea-ef72aabca401', 'word_count': 306, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1a48ad0-68d0-5682-91ea-ef72aabca401'}, {'abstract': 'A cavernous California warehouse that serves as an informal office building for the tech-enamored doesn’t comply with city regulations. But no one, including the city, wants to close it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/23/technology/techies-fight-to-save-hacker-dojo-a-popular-silicon-valley-work-space.html', 'snippet': 'A cavernous California warehouse that serves as an informal office building for the tech-enamored doesn’t comply with city regulations. But no one, including the city, wants to close it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOUNTAIN VIEW, Calif. — Hacker Dojo is equal parts shared office, lecture hall and after-hours salon for a variety of tinkerers, software coders and entrepreneurs who intend to reinvent the future. The idea for Pinterest was cooked up here. The makers of Pebble watches used the space as their West Coast headquarters. Today, however, it is threatened with extinction. City officials in Mountain View have ordered Hacker Dojo to comply with city regulations for offices or move out. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/23/business/HACKER/HACKER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/23/business/HACKER/HACKER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0,