unt': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/e44d23bc-cf20-5a57-a1a3-7b4a0119f540'}, {'abstract': 'It’s been 16 months since my diagnosis and four months since my bone marrow transplant, and now I want to share what I’ve learned along the way.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/23/life-interrupted-six-ways-to-cope-with-cancer/', 'snippet': 'It’s been 16 months since my diagnosis and four months since my bone marrow transplant, and now I want to share what I’ve learned along the way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'People are always giving advice to cancer patients. Whether it is nutritional advice or doctor recommendations or tips on how to quell the nausea that accompanies chemotherapy, just as soon as people hear you are sick they usually want to find a way to help. Most of the advice is welcome and encouraging, though in some cases you’re just not in the mood to hear what someone has learned because you’re too busy treading water.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/23/health/23suleika/23suleika-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 612, 'width': 612, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/23/health/23suleika/23suleika-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 612, 'width': 612, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/23/health/23suleika/23suleika-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/23/health/23suleika/23suleika-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/23/health/23suleika/23suleika-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 149, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Life, Interrupted: Six Ways to Cope With Cancer', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cancer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T04:01:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Suleika Jaouad', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Suleika', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jaouad', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/25bebaef-6185-5aa6-99c0-278729c2d4ef', 'word_count': 1325, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25bebaef-6185-5aa6-99c0-278729c2d4ef'}, {'abstract': 'Is play the key to academic success? A growing body of research suggests that playing certain kinds of childhood games may be the best way to increase a child’s ability to do well in school.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/23/simon-says-dont-use-flashcards/', 'snippet': 'Is play the key to academic success? A growing body of research suggests that playing certain kinds of childhood games may be the best way to increase a child’s ability to do well in school.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Parents who want to stimulate their children’s brain development often focus on things like early reading, flashcards and language tapes. But a growing body of research suggests that playing certain kinds of childhood games may be the best way to increase a child’s ability to do well in school. Variations on games like Freeze Tag and Simon Says require relatively high levels of executive function, testing a child’s ability to pay attention, remember rules and exhibit self-control — qualities that also predict academic success.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Simon Says Don’t Use Flashcards', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T04:02:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tara Parker-Pope', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker-Pope', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5115d49-dbe6-51fa-ac3b-58b5d01942b1', 'word_count': 454, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5115d49-dbe6-51fa-ac3b-58b5d01942b1'}, {'abstract': 'Campaigning in swing states on Wednesday, Mitt Romney spoke about the economy while President Obama focused on education.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2012/08/22/us/20120822_campaign.html', 'snippet': 'Campaigning in swing states on Wednesday, Mitt Romney spoke about the economy while President Obama focused on education.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Campaigning in swing states on Wednesday, Mitt Romney spoke about the economy while President Obama focused on education.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/22/us/20120822_campaign-slide-1828/20120822_campaign-slide-1828-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/22/us/20120822_campaign-slide-1828/20120822_campaign-slide-1828-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama and Romney Rally Swing State Support', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Paul D Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T04:11:23+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/0b5245ff-14a9-521e-9f65-3bae14815f94', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/0b5245ff-14a9-521e-9f65-3bae14815f94'}, {'abstract': 'Illegal immigration, porous borders are root causes of the current problems in Assam, adviser says.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/23/a-conversation-with-assam-students-union-adviser-bhattacharya/', 'snippet': 'Illegal immigration, porous borders are root causes of the current problems in Assam, adviser says.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the last week, the conflict between the indigenous Bodo tribe and Muslims in Assam has led to panic in various Indian cities. On Aug.15, rumors of possible violence led several thousand people from India’s northeastern region to flee Bangalore. The exodus continued over the next few days as thousands flocked to train stations in Bangalore, Chennai and Pune, while Indian leaders attempted to restore calm.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Conversation With: Assam Students’ Union Adviser Bhattacharya', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bodos (Ethnic Group)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subj