-08-23T10:00:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Southall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Southall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8101ff2d-02c6-585c-a1bb-3d59c2b081a9', 'word_count': 487, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8101ff2d-02c6-585c-a1bb-3d59c2b081a9'}, {'abstract': 'The Saints clearly have great pieces on offense, but it’s a matter of how well they can be organized in Sean Payton’s absence.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/23/new-orleans-saints-2012-n-f-l-season-preview/', 'snippet': 'The Saints clearly have great pieces on offense, but it’s a matter of how well they can be organized in Sean Payton’s absence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'No one really knows whether the New Orleans Saints can overcome the distractions and fallout of the bounties scandal. Their situation – Coach Sean Payton suspended for the year, the interim head coach Joe Vitt suspended for the first six games, General Manager Mickey Loomis suspended for the first eight – is unprecedented in the N.F.L. Until Wednesday, it was still unknown who, exactly, would be in charge for the first month and a half of the season while Vitt was away. \xa0(The Saints chose offensive line coach Aaron Kromer.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Orleans Saints, 2012 N.F.L. Season Preview', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Orleans Saints', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T10:00:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Benoit', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benoit', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df26efef-21b6-55d9-a5c2-98a657db65df', 'word_count': 2778, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df26efef-21b6-55d9-a5c2-98a657db65df'}, {'abstract': 'Worries about the use of chemical weapons have reinforced a concern that the time for diplomacy may be gone.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/24/world/middleeast/syrian-forces-renew-raids-on-damascus-suburbs.html', 'snippet': 'Worries about the use of chemical weapons have reinforced a concern that the time for diplomacy may be gone.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Britain and France raised the possibility of military intervention in the Syria conflict on Thursday, with the British prime minister joining President Obama in warning that he would not tolerate the transport or deployment of Syria’s chemical weapons stockpile, and France’s defense minister saying that a partial no-fly zone should be considered.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/world/middleeast/24syria/24syria-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/24/world/middleeast/24syria/24syria-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/world/middleeast/24syria/24syria-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 690, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/world/middleeast/24syria/24syria-s