take of asylum seekers to 20,000, from 13,700, but also plans to reopen offshore detention centers to deter illegal immigrants.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/24/world/asia/australia-increases-refugee-quota-and-also-deterrents.html', 'snippet': 'The Labor government increased the annual intake of asylum seekers to 20,000, from 13,700, but also plans to reopen offshore detention centers to deter illegal immigrants.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SYDNEY, Australia — Prime Minister Julia Gillard on Thursday ordered an immediate increase in the number of refugees that her country accepts yearly, part of an immigration reform package aimed at encouraging migrants to use official channels for asylum rather than long and dangerous boat journeys.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Australia Increases Refugee Quota in Broad Immigration Reform', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Australia Increases Refugee Quota in Broad Immigration Reform', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Australia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigrants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Asylum, Right of', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gillard, Julia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T13:50:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Siegel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Siegel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b9704b7-acbf-5018-a289-fff40d8aadfa', 'word_count': 517, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b9704b7-acbf-5018-a289-fff40d8aadfa'}, {'abstract': 'A striking California-raised beauty, and former champion fighter, has a knack for making elegant dresses.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/23/new-label-alert-nellie-partow/', 'snippet': 'A striking California-raised beauty, and former champion fighter, has a knack for making elegant dresses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'All week, T is introducing the lines to look out for this fall.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Label Alert | Nellie Partow', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T13:50:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsea Zalopany', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zalopany', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5cd937d1-89b5-56dc-a2ee-165bc6ab0c32', 'word_count': 321, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5cd937d1-89b5-56dc-a2ee-165bc6ab0c32'}, {'abstract': 'From Park Avenue to Brooklyn, the city is awash with outdoor art, some beautiful, some not.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/24/arts/design/work-by-niki-de-saint-phalle-and-bruce-high-quality-foundation.html', 'snippet': 'From Park Avenue to Brooklyn, the city is awash with outdoor art, some beautiful, some not.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Public art makes me think of what the Conceptualist Douglas Huebler once said: “The world is full of objects, more or less interesting; I do not wish to add any more.” That may be a funny thing for an artist to say, but if you live in a place as densely packed with objects of all kinds as New York City, you may appreciate its wisdom. Nevertheless, for better or worse, temporary public art displays pop up all over the city during the warm seasons in hopes of adding beauty and zest to the urban fabric.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24SCULPTURE_SPAN/24SCULPTURE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24SCULPTURE_SPAN/24SCULPTURE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24SCULPTURE_SPAN/24SCULPTURE_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24SCULPTURE_SPAN/24SCULPTURE_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1000, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24SCULPTURE_SPAN/24SCULPTURE_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24SCULPTURE_SPAN/24SCULPTURE_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24SCULPTURE_SPAN/24SCULPTURE_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Eye Candy or Eyesore?', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Eye Candy or Eyesore?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sculpture', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Long, Charles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Saint Phalle, Niki de', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tuazon, Oscar', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chamberlain, John (1927-2011)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T14:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b47ac8b9-3643-523f-9ced-381ddffb91c6', 'word_count': 1649, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b47ac8b9-3643-523f-9ced-381ddffb91c6'}, {'abstract': 'Whether Americans call themselves middle class doesn’t necessarily have a lot to do with whether they earn close to the median income, a survey finds.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/23/who-counts-as-middle-class/', 'snippet': 'Whether Americans call themselves middle class doesn’t necessarily have a lot to do with whether they earn close to the median income, a survey finds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As I’ve written before, Americans don’t have a great sense of where they fall in the income distribution, or even what the income distribution actually looks like. Now, in a new report, the Pew Research Center has given us an update on the types of Americans most likely to call themselves middle class, and the answer doesn’t necessarily have a lot to do whether respondents earn close to the median income.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Counts as Middle Class?', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pew Research Center', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T14:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/668bb34f-ec39-5693-90ab-5aae21f07268', 'word_count': 444, 'uri': 'nyt://article/668bb34f-ec39-5693-90ab-5aae21f07268'}, {'abstract': 'The focus of this year’s event is jazz drumming.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/23/monk-competition-announces-semifinalists/', 'snippet': 'The focus of this year’s event is jazz drumming.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Twelve semifinalists have been selected for the 25th Annual Thelonious Monk International Jazz Competition, to be held on Sept. 22 and 23 in Washington. Presented by the Thelonious Monk Institute of Jazz, the competition is widely regarded as the most prestigious of its kind, with the winner receiving a $25,000 music scholarship and a recording contract with the Concord Music Group.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Monk Competition Announces Semifinalists', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Monk Competition Announces Semifinalists', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALBRIGHT, MADELEINE K', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blade, Brian', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Haynes, Roy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Riley, Ben', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kennedy, John F, Center for the Performing Arts', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Monk, Thelonious, Institute of Jazz', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T14:06:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15318412-69f6-57a7-b5c0-af6aeccb5897', 'word_count': 314, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15318412-69f6-57a7-b5c0-af6aeccb5897'}, {'abstract': 'After being home-schooled and marrying at a young age, Nathan Harden was deeply shocked by the sexual culture at Yale.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/26/books/review/sex-and-god-at-yale-by-nathan-harden.html', 'snippet': 'After being home-schooled and marrying at a young age, Nathan Harden was deeply shocked by the sexual culture at Yale.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The conservative movement loves an innocent. Better yet if he has attended an Ivy League college and witnessed the debauchery of the elites firsthand. For this particular position, Nathan Harden, the author of “Sex and God at Yale,” possesses impeccable credentials. He was home-schooled, was already married when he got to college and had worshiped the institution so blindly that he was bound to be disappointed.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'captio