{'firstname': 'Hanna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b65d9e6c-9d50-5e35-a6b8-7e2218d00cc2', 'word_count': 1186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b65d9e6c-9d50-5e35-a6b8-7e2218d00cc2'}, {'abstract': 'Bruce Bawer argues that the contemporary American university is a place of hypocrisy and fear.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/26/books/review/the-victims-revolution-by-bruce-bawer.html', 'snippet': 'Bruce Bawer argues that the contemporary American university is a place of hypocrisy and fear.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When generals prepare to “fight the last war,” as the saying goes, they’re bound to be surprised by new enemies deploying new tactics over new terrain. On the evidence of Bruce Bawer’s fighting-mad book, “The Victims’ Revolution: The Rise of Identity Studies and the Closing of the Liberal Mind,” this rule holds true for the academic culture wars as well.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/0826-Delbanco/0826-Delbanco-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/0826-Delbanco/0826-Delbanco-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/0826-Delbanco/0826-Delbanco-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 629, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/0826-Delbanco/0826-Delbanco-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1258, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJum