ditional books to life — through animation, music, photos, videos and more. That they do so while weighing less than a cinder block is a nice perk, too.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/0826-Biersdorfer-02/0826-Biersdorfer-02-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/0826-Biersdorfer-02/0826-Biersdorfer-02-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/0826-Biersdorfer-02/0826-Biersdorfer-02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/0826-Biersdorfer-02/0826-Biersdorfer-02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/0826-Biersdorfer-02/0826-Biersdorfer-02-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A World at Your Fingertips', 'kicker': 'Applied Reading', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A World at Your Fingertips', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'British Library', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crane, Nick', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Maps', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schultz, Patricia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T14:16:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. D. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5826f01-79a7-512b-af9a-3a8fe5f2f4a6', 'word_count': 903, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5826f01-79a7-512b-af9a-3a8fe5f2f4a6'}, {'abstract': 'For success, character trumps cognitive skills, Paul Tough says.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/26/books/review/how-children-succeed-by-paul-tough.html', 'snippet': 'For success, character trumps cognitive skills, Paul Tough says.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most readers of The New York Times probably subscribe to what Paul Tough calls “the cognitive hypothesis”: the belief “that success today depends primarily on cognitive skills — the kind of intelligence that gets measured on I.Q. tests, including the abilities to recognize letters and words, to calculate, to detect patterns — and that the best way to develop these skills is to practice them as much as possible, beginning as early as possible.” In his new book, “How Children Succeed,” Tough sets out to replace this assumption with what might be called the character hypothesis: the notion that noncognitive skills, like persistence, self-control, curiosity, conscientiousness, grit and self-confidence, are more crucial than sheer brainpower to achieving success.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/26paul/26paul-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 292, 'width': 387, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/26paul/26paul-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 292, 'width': 387, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/26paul/26paul-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/26paul/26paul-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/26paul/26paul-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'School of Hard Knocks', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'School of Hard Knocks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tough, Paul', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'How Children Succeed: Grit, Curiosity, and the Hidden Power of Character (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T14:17:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Annie Murphy Paul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Annie', 'middlename': 'Murphy', 'lastname': 'Paul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/da498343-3af1-5cb3-be67-a7b5231a944d', 'word_count': 1281, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da498343-3af1-5cb3-be67-a7b5231a944d'}, {'abstract': 'Molly Ringwald’s fiction debut centers on a couple in a troubled marriage.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/26/books/review/when-it-happens-to-you-by-molly-ringwald.html', 'snippet': 'Molly Ringwald’s fiction debut centers on a couple in a troubled marriage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even when she was MOLLY RINGWALD, Molly Ringwald wasn’t content just being Molly Ringwald. In 1988 the teen idol reteamed with her “Pretty in Pink” co-star Andrew McCarthy for “Fresh Horses,” a talky drama about a tough, sullen Kentucky country girl who catches the eye of an engaged engineering student. For moviegoers who went in expecting the plucky high schooler she played in the earlier movie, or “Sixteen Candles,” “Fresh Horses” was a real downer. The group of much more popular friends to whom I recommended it abandoned me halfway through for “Ernest Saves Christmas” and never hung out with me again. I loved it, though — in part because you could see how hard Ringwald was working to be not just a teen-movie star but an actress. That she couldn’t yet pull it off made “Fresh Horses” poignant, and no less enjoyable — and as fans of the ABC Family series “The Secret Life of the American Teenager” know, in the end she has become an actress, and not a bad one at all.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/26KOIS/26KOIS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 694, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/26KOIS/26KOIS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1387, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/26KOIS/26KOIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/26KOIS/26KOIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/26KOIS/26KOIS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Grown-Up Angst', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Grown-Up Angst', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ringwald, Molly', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T14:19:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Kois', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kois', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/57870ec5-598d-5725-b771-5f84e8c966ae', 'word_count': 952, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57870ec5-598d-5725-b771-5f84e8c966ae'}, {'abstract': 'Andrew Motion’s sequel to Robert Louis Stevenson’s 19th-century adventure classic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/26/books/review/silver-return-to-treasure-island-by-andrew-motion.html', 'snippet': 'Andrew Motion’s sequel to Robert Louis Stevenson’s 19th-century adventure classic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Alas for the unintended cruelty of a book-reviewing aunt! On the long ride to catch the sunset from the dunes above Lake Michigan, I handed my restless 7-year-old nephew a book from my beach bag: Robert Louis Stevenson’s “Treasure Island.” Instantly and intently, he began reading aloud, propelled by the excitement of discovery. Later, back at the cottage, he asked, “Can I have my book now?” Guiltily, I told him he’d have to wait: I needed it for a story I was writing about “Treasure Island” and its new sequel, “Silver,” by the British poet Andrew Motion. I felt like a blackhearted mutineer.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/0826-Schillinger/0826-Schillinger-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/0826-Schillinger/0826-Schillinger-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/0826-Schillinger/0826-Schillinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/0826-Schillinger/0826-Schillinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/0826-Schillinger/0826-Schillinger-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Buccaneers and Bullion', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Buccaneers and Bullion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Motion, Andrew', 