: 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Beth Kephart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Beth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kephart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1587c730-5abe-506e-a2c6-bed7434277cc', 'word_count': 860, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1587c730-5abe-506e-a2c6-bed7434277cc'}, {'abstract': 'Variations on Goldilocks, the Three Little Pigs and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/26/books/review/goldilocks-and-just-one-bear-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Variations on Goldilocks, the Three Little Pigs and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GOLDILOCKS AND THE THREE DINOSAURS  Written and illustrated by Mo Willems.  40 pp. Balzer & Bray/HarperCollins Publishers. $17.99. (Picture book; ages 3 to 7) ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/books/review/26folktales-ss-slide-ZNZT/26folktales-ss-slide-ZNZT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 348, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/24/books/review/26folktales-ss-slide-ZNZT/26folktales-ss-slide-ZNZT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 348}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/books/review/26folktales-ss-slide-ZNZT/26folktales-ss-slide-ZNZT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 594, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/books/review/26folktales-ss-slide-ZNZT/26folktales-ss-slide-ZNZT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1188, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/books/review/26folktales-ss-slide-ZNZT/26folktales-ss-slide-ZNZT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/24/books/review/26folktales-ss-slide-ZNZT/26folktales-ss-slide-ZNZT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/books/review/26folktales-ss-slide-ZNZT/26folktales-ss-slide-ZNZT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bookshelf: Funny Folk Tales', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Willems, Mo', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hodgkinson, Leigh', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schwartz, Corey Rosen', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Levinthal, David', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stimpson, Colin', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nickle, John', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santat, Dan', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T14:34:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pamela Paul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pamela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Paul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba8563dd-c566-5f22-b8de-0c4504f484dd', 'word_count': 642, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba8563dd-c566-5f22-b8de-0c4504f484dd'}, {'abstract': 'School, relationships, popularity. Navigating life’s uncertainties, the teenage girls in these novels are pushed to the brink.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/26/books/review/choke-by-diana-lopez-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'School, relationships, popularity. Navigating life’s uncertainties, the teenage girls in these novels are pushed to the brink.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you’re a teenager, especially if you’re a girl, it’s deceptively easy to wreak havoc, if unintentionally, on your body. The pressure to appear flawless and to fit in without ever breaking a sweat requires putting your best poker face forward while shoving the messiness of adolescence under the bed. Skipping meals? Why not? Sticking two fingers down your throat after too many carbs? Easy as pie. Becoming a little fast and loose with alcohol and drugs, or negotiating a quick slice of the knife across your inner thigh? It happens all the time.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fear and Self-Loathing', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sheinmel, Alyssa B', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lopez, Diana', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Price, Nora', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T14:35:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexis Burling', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burling', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e7697a9-95c6-5bb8-96eb-c1360aac4f5f', 'word_count': 1039, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e7697a9-95c6-5bb8-96eb-c1360aac4f5f'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/26/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LIONEL ASBO: State of England,  by Martin Amis. (Knopf, $25.95.) The Dickensian theme of Amis’s novel is the corrupting influence of money, as his antihero, a career criminal with a vestigial conscience, wins the lottery and is baffled by legitimate wealth.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T14:36:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1eb72c5-1e4e-5807-aca4-d1684fdcdf7d', 'word_count': 282, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1eb72c5-1e4e-5807-aca4-d1684fdcdf7d'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/26/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ROCK THE CASBAH: Rage and Rebellion Across the Islamic World,  by Robin Wright. (Simon & Schuster, $17.) Wright, a veteran foreign correspondent and the author of “Dreams and Shadows: The Future of the Middle East” (2008), examines the causes and repercussions of revolts against autocratic rule in countries like Tunisia, Egypt, Libya and Yemen. Traveling across the region, she profiles poets, playwrights, feminists, TV imams and others working toward a “counter-jihad” to reclaim Islam from militants who crave perpetual holy war.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T14:36:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/197167b3-f3fd-500e-8d7b-17477340a1c0', 'word_count': 508, 'uri': 'nyt://article/197167b3-f3fd-500e-8d7b-17477340a1c0'}, {'abstract': 'Young girls learn about the limits of their parents in two new books about the strains produced by the Iraq war.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/26/books/review/new-books-by-frances-oroark-dowell-and-mary-sullivan.html', 'snippet': 'Young girls learn about the limits of their parents in two new books about the strains produced by the Iraq war.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A convention of novels aimed at children in the middle-grade years holds that whatever problem a girl confronts, her parents will not be the ones to help. This often makes sense both psychologically and narratively. These are the years when children begin to differentiate themselves from their parents and learn to fight their own battles. And really, how exciting would a story be in which our heroine, say, is ganged up on by other girls, but then tells her mother, who steps in, does all the right things, and every\xadthing’s fine?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In the Shadow of War', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "Dowell, Frances O'Roark", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sullivan, Mary', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T14:39:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Russo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Russo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e09892f-f3f0-5852-a8ca-16ec909fc3f5', 'word_count': 884, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e09892f-f3f0-5852-a8ca-16ec909fc3f5'}, {'abstract': 'Three new middle-grade novels feature girls from very different backgrounds dealing with the challenges of school.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/26/books/review/the-year-of-the-book-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Three new middle-grade novels feature girls from very different backgrounds dealing with the challenges of school.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Businesses are quick to lump together tweens and teenagers into one big marketing opportunity, but when it comes to books, younger readers are very different from their young adult counterparts. How do you define that shifting period of transitions between the ages of 8 and 10, or 7 and 10, when children read at very different rates, with different aptitudes and for different reasons?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Universal Struggles', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Halpin, Abigail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ross, Chudney', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cheng, Andrea', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Watts, Jeri', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T14:40:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Veronica Chambers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Veronica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chambers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3aa781cb-2086-5d6b-9881-be3067c859ef', 'word_count': 840, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3aa781cb-2086-5d6b-9881-be3067c859ef'}, {'abstract': 'Hoping to bridge the gap between physical and digital toys, Mattel has created a line of enhanced iPad games called Apptivity. One of the first offerings in the line is Batman, inspired by the movie “The Dark Knight Rises.”', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/23/batmans-new-perch-above-gotham-your-ipad/', 'snippet': 'Hoping to bridge the gap between physical and digital toys, Mattel has created a line of enhanced iPad games called Apptivity. One of the first offerings in the line is Batman, inspired by the movie “The Dark Knight Rises.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'From the shadows, Batman swoops down and lands on your iPad to fight a gang of thugs. But fear not! He’ll throw a lot of punches, but he won’t scratch your screen.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Batman’s New Perch Above Gotham: Your iPad', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mattel Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T14:41:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Schmidt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schmidt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f60a360-95b7-5d4a-9387-e8cc53de1db4', 'word_count': 347, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f60a360-95b7-5d4a-9387-e8cc53de1db4'}, {'abstract': 'Hanna Rosin on women’s success in college.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/26/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'Hanna Rosin on women’s success in college.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, Hanna Rosin reviews Nathan Harden’s “Sex and God at Yale,” an investigation into oversexualized college life — and its dangers to young women. A senior editor at The Atlantic and Slate, Rosin conducted her own campus research for her forthcoming book, “The End of Men” — although she came to markedly different conclusions. She maintains that the “hookup culture” Harden decries might be “integral” to women’s success in college. Why? Casual relationships “won’t derail future life plans.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/0826-bks-Upfront/0826-bks-Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 670, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/books/review/0826-bks-Upfront/0826-bks-Upfront-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1898, 'width': 1242, 'subType': 'superJumbo',