rs of the recent Off Broadway musical “Soul Doctor,” about the life and singing career of Rabbi Shlomo Carlebach, are in talks to move the show either to Broadway’s Circle in the Square Theater or to Off Broadway’s New World Stages, according to the lead producer of the show, Jeremy Chess. He said he is now seeking to expand his pool of investors to remount the show as he awaits word from Paul Libin of Circle in the Square while pursuing separate negotiations with executives at New World Stages.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Soul Doctor,’ Musical About Singing Rabbi, Eyes Next Stage', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Soul Doctor’ Eyes Next Stage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ANDERSON, ERIC', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carlebach, Shlomo', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wise, Daniel S', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Circle in the Square Theater', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New World Stages', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T20:40:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5f42a7e-cd00-5d83-b846-338cabd3f49c', 'word_count': 503, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5f42a7e-cd00-5d83-b846-338cabd3f49c'}, {'abstract': 'Atsushi Numata improvises with what surrounds him at a stall within the Essex Street Market.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/29/dining/reviews/ni-japanese-delicacies-hungry-city.html', 'snippet': 'Atsushi Numata improvises with what surrounds him at a stall within the Essex Street Market.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FIRST you must find it, somewhere in the depths of the Essex Street Market on the Lower East Side, past the cheesemonger, the Latin grocer and the baklava shop. Ni Japanese Delicacies, which opened last September, is not a restaurant, just a cluttered stall (a nook, really) without tables or chairs. At a short counter along the back wall, the owner and chef, Atsushi Numata, labors alone over a cutting board wedged against an induction burner. Pots and scissors dangle. Unlidded Tupperware and jars of condiments inch toward the edge.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/29/dining/29HUNGRY1_SPAN/29HUNGRY1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/29/dining/29HUNGRY1_SPAN/29HUNGRY1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/29/dining/29HUNGRY1_SPAN/29HUNGRY1_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/29/dining/29HUNGRY1_SPAN/29HUNGRY1_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/29/dining/29HUNGRY1_SPAN/29HUNGRY1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/29/dining/29HUNGRY1_SPAN/29HUNGRY1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In a Tiny Nook, an Array of Treats', 'kicker': 'Hungry City', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In a Tiny Nook, An Array of Treats', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Essex Street Market', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ni Japanese Delicacies (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lower East Side (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T20:41:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'subsection_name': 'Dining & Wine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ligaya Mishan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ligaya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mishan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8b6eeed-f4b5-5933-bc95-b661a87bdbb4', 'word_count': 657, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8b6eeed-f4b5-5933-bc95-b661a87bdbb4'}, {'abstract': 'For several years, the Dutch photographer Sarah Wong has been documenting children in her country who said they felt they were born in the wrong body.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/23/sarah-wongs-portraits-of-dutch-transgendered-children/', 'snippet': 'For several years, the Dutch photographer Sarah Wong has been documenting children in her country who said they felt they were born in the wrong body.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Several years before Lindsay Morris photographed the gender-fluid children featured in our\xa0recent cover story, the Dutch photographer Sarah Wong was documenting children in her country who said they felt they were born in the wrong body.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sarah Wong’s Portraits of Dutch Transgendered Children', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gender', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Netherlands', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T20:52:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Kellner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kellner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d4ca457-e95c-5cd3-9be5-a547ee91e5f3', 'word_count': 238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d4ca457-e95c-5cd3-9be5-a547ee91e5f3'}, {'abstract': 'The next generation of the compact model will continue to be produced at the factory, where more than 500,000 Cruzes have been built.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/23/g-m-readies-a-retooling-of-lordstown-plant-for-next-chevy-cruze/', 'snippet': 'The next generation of the compact model will continue to be produced at the factory, where more than 500,000 Cruzes have been built.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Thursday, General Motors announced that it would spend $220 million equipping plants in Lordstown and Parma, Ohio, to build the next generation of the Chevrolet Cruze. The automaker said the redesigned compact car would offer revised exterior and interior styling, along with improved fuel economy, but provided no further details.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'G.M. Readies a Retooling of Lordstown Plant for Next Chevy Cruze', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Motors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ohio', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T21:02:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Stenquist', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stenquist', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4d2001f-236e-5e15-b1e2-c90a31cdc369', 'word_count': 488, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4d2001f-236e-5e15-b1e2-c90a31cdc369'}, {'abstract': '“Neighboring Sounds” illuminates the present state of Brazilian society by exposing the complex relationships and simmering contradictions in its enclaves of privilege.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/24/movies/neighboring-sounds-directed-by-kleber-mendonca-filho.html', 'snippet': '“Neighboring Sounds” illuminates the present state of Brazilian society by exposing the complex relationships and simmering contradictions in its enclaves of privilege.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Neighboring Sounds,” Kleber Mendonça Filho’s revelatory debut feature, takes place mostly on a wealthy block in the northeastern Brazilian city of Recife. Identical, gleaming white high-rises — some with fancy names like Vivaldi, Westminster and Camille Claudel — dominate the skyline, obscuring the nearby ocean and also the urban poverty that has been central to Brazil’s social and cinematic identity for decades. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24NEIGHBOR_SPAN/24NEIGHBOR_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24NEIGHBOR_SPAN/24NEIGHBOR_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24NEIGHBOR_SPAN/24NEIGHBOR_SPAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 545, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24NEIGHBOR_SPAN/24NEIGHBOR_SPAN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1091, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24NEIGHBOR_SPAN/24NEIGHBOR_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24NEIGHBOR_SPAN/24NEIGHBOR_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24NEIGHBOR_SPAN/24NEIGHBOR_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Leisure Class Bears Its Burden', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Leisure Class Bears Its Burden', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mendoca Filho, Kleber', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T21:06:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a92adee-9e36-5bb4-bf62-8b6153f454d4', 'word_count': 774, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a92adee-9e36-5bb4-bf62-8b6153f454d4'}, {'abstract': 'With Suzanne Donnegan’s new line, affordable bespoke jewelry is within reach.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/23/worth-its-weight-mannins-new-insignia-collection/', 'snippet': 'With Suzanne Donnegan’s new line, affordable bespoke jewelry is within reach.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With gold prices continuing to rise, jewelers are looking for new ways to execute their designs without compromising quality. Suzanne Donnegan is among them. “There was really a void in the market for affordable bespoke jewelry,” says Donnegan, who, four years ago, introduced the minimalistic fine jewelry line Mannin. It features 18-karat solid gold monogrammable pendants, rings and cuff links — exquisite but very expensive expressions of quiet luxury. (Jennifer Aniston, Jessica Biel and Halle Berry are customers.) Enter Donnegan’s new Insignia collection: necklaces, rings, bracelets, earrings, cuff links and money clips, in all-silver and silver with 22-karat gold overlay for a sheen and weight that mimics, uncannily, their 18-karat solid gold counterparts. “New luxury should be about both high quality and accessibility,” Donnegan says. In other words, same dash, less cash. “The Insignia shopper is interested in collecting jewelry. She appreciates understated design details and loves owning pieces that reflect her personal identity.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Worth Its Weight | Mannin’s New Insignia Collection', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T21:18:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Barsamian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barsamian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b380e350-e3b1-5e63-8d00-73f340c19bcf', 'word_count': 159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b380e350-e3b1-5e63-8d00-73f340c19bcf'}, {'abstract': 'An retrospective at the Rubin Museum of Art lays out the respectful, mostly neutral, approach of Homai Vyarawalla, a leading Indian photographer during the creation and early decades of her country’s independence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/24/arts/design/candid-photos-by-homai-vyarawalla-at-rubin-museum.html', 'snippet': 'An retrospective at the Rubin Museum of Art lays out the respectful, mostly neutral, approach of Homai Vyarawalla, a leading Indian photographer during the creation and early decades of her country’s independence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Indian photographer Homai Vyarawalla, who died in January at 98, spoke many times, with undiminished regret, of two opportunities missed.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24RUBIN/24RUBIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1004, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24RUBIN/24RUBIN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1849, 'width': 1885, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24RUBIN/24RUBIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24RUBIN/24RUBIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24RUBIN/24RUBIN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Restrained Chronicler of Tumultuous Times', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Restrained Chronicler Of Tumultuous Times', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rubin Museum of Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vyarawalla, Homai', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T21:21:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f1bace6-e9fd-5276-8817-d292e8d98d49', 'word_count': 1329, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f1bace6-e9fd-5276-8817-d292e8d98d49'}, {'abstract': 'A new tool from Nerdwallet helps compare student checking accounts at big banks and campus credit unions.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/23/university-credit-unions-can-be-good-choice-for-student-banking/', 'snippet': 'A new tool from Nerdwallet helps compare student checking accounts at big banks and campus credit unions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Students heading to college generally prefer a bank that’s convenient to campus and doesn’t charge a lot of fees. A new report from the Web site Nerdwallet.com suggests that university credit unions, when available, are often a better deal for students than big banks.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'University Credit Unions Can Be Good Choice for Student Banking', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Massachusetts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AMHERST (MASS)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T21:28:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5ab20f4-d32a-5bb4-8d9b-8f1eb63614aa', 'word_count': 362, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5ab20f4-d32a-5bb4-8d9b-8f1eb63614aa'}, {'abstract': 'Anonymous hackers laid claim to a cyberattack on Saudi Aramco, the world’s largest oil company, last week. They say they unleashed a malicious computer virus that destroyed three-quarters of the company’s computers. Saudi Aramco confirmed that its network experienced a disruption but said its oil production systems were not affected.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/23/hackers-lay-claim-to-saudi-aramco-cyberattack/', 'snippet': 'Anonymous hackers laid claim to a cyberattack on Saudi Aramco, the world’s largest oil company, last week. They say they unleashed a malicious computer virus that destroyed three-quarters of the company’s computers. Saudi Aramco confirmed that its...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Unknown computer hackers claim they forced the world’s largest oil company, Saudi Aramco, to quarantine its oil production systems from infected PCs inside the company last week. They threatened to attack the company again this Saturday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hackers Lay Claim to Saudi Aramco Cyberattack', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberattacks and Hackers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pastebin.com', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Saudi Aramco', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T21:30:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicole Perlroth', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicole', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perlroth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/715b72bb-4c4b-5c9b-b776-fe28deb99c16', 'word_count': 441, 'uri': 'nyt://article/715b72bb-4c4b-5c9b-b776-fe28deb99c16'}, {'abstract': 'What are the symptoms of West Nile infection? When should you see a doctor? Expert answers to questions about the current outbreak.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/23/q-and-a-on-west-nile-virus/', 'snippet': 'What are the symptoms of West Nile infection? When should you see a doctor? Expert answers to questions about the current outbreak.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The outbreak of West Nile virus this year has spread to nearly every state and is shaping up to be the largest one on record since the first human cases were reported in the United States in 1999.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/health/24well_nile/24well_nile-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 705, 'width': 1024, 'subType': '