value': 'Museum of Fine Arts (Boston)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T22:02:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hilarie M. Sheets', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hilarie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/079a3023-17a0-5574-b355-e49c9c4f053d', 'word_count': 1254, 'uri': 'nyt://article/079a3023-17a0-5574-b355-e49c9c4f053d'}, {'abstract': 'A single woman dances both Dido and the Sorceress in the Mostly Mozart revival of Mark Morris’s version of “Dido and Aeneas.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/24/arts/dance/dido-and-aeneas-from-mark-morris-at-mostly-mozart.html', 'snippet': 'A single woman dances both Dido and the Sorceress in the Mostly Mozart revival of Mark Morris’s version of “Dido and Aeneas.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The choreographer Mark Morris has staged nothing more tragic than his 1989 version of Henry Purcell’s hourlong opera “Dido and Aeneas,” and nothing more fixated on the nexus of love, sex, destruction and death. For many people it is the greatest work Mr. Morris has ever made. What matters more, though, is that Mr. Morris has created a dazzling interpretation of Purcell’s superlative work. The more closely you follow it, the more packed its movement is with meanings; even though I have seen the work a number of times over the years, it keeps surprising me. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24MORRIS_SPAN/24MORRIS_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge'