content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T22:17:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0368ac70-f3ca-50c5-8176-eab210a737f0', 'word_count': 133, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0368ac70-f3ca-50c5-8176-eab210a737f0'}, {'abstract': 'In “12.21” a doctor and a Maya scholar join forces to save the world from a sudden collapse, one foreshadowed by the puzzling demise of ancient Maya civilizations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/24/books/12-21-a-novel-by-dustin-thomason.html', 'snippet': 'In “12.21” a doctor and a Maya scholar join forces to save the world from a sudden collapse, one foreshadowed by the puzzling demise of ancient Maya civilizations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dustin Thomason begins “12.21” with a prologue in which a mysterious ancient figure pricks his arm, draws blood and then begins writing what may become “the most valuable artifact in the history of Mesoamerican studies.” In the genre to which this book belongs — thrillers Dan Brown could write with a reanimated Michael Crichton, which, given the emphasis on weird science, is not out of the question — this is just the author’s way of saying hello.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24BOOK2/24BOOK1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24BOOK2/24BOOK1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Guano and Maya Glyphs Just Might Save the World', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Guano and Maya Glyphs Just Might Save the World', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thomason, Dustin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T22:20:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Janet Maslin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Janet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maslin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/595d44fe-33f6-52c6-94e6-8f6384db90ac', 'word_count': 848, 'uri': 'nyt://article/595d44fe-33f6-52c6-94e6-8f6384db90ac'}, {'abstract': 'Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg responded to a question about a sharp rise in the number of homeless people in the city’s shelter system.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/23/mayor-cites-better-shelters-and-slow-economy-for-need-for-more-beds/', 'snippet': 'Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg responded to a question about a sharp rise in the number of homeless people in the city’s shelter system.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Asked about a sharp rise in the number of homeless people in New York City’s shelter system, Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg suggested Thursday that people were staying in shelters longer in part because the shelters were “much more pleasurable” than they used to be, making people less eager to leave.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mayor Offers Ideas for Why Homeless Numbers Are Up', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homeless Persons', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Blasio, Bill', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Advantage Program (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T22:21:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eb7af202-7a6c-5445-acb2-fcd25513d271', 'word_count': 434, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eb7af202-7a6c-5445-acb2-fcd25513d271'}, {'abstract': 'Raissa Katona Bennett brings new songs and standards to Feinstein’s at Loews Regency for her show there, “Another Kind of Light.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/24/arts/music/raissa-katona-bennett-at-feinsteins-at-loews-regency.html', 'snippet': 'Raissa Katona Bennett brings new songs and standards to Feinstein’s at Loews Regency for her show there, “Another Kind of Light.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the singer Raissa Katona Bennett peeled away the layer of perky, ingratiating sweetness that so many Broadway performers feel obliged to affect in nightclubs, a tart-tongued commentator on modern life with a dark sense of humor revealed itself on Wednesday evening at Feinstein’s at Loews Regency. Her show, “Another Kind of Light,” directed by Eric Michael Gillett, had its sticky inspirational moments. But in its best numbers, Ms. Katona Bennett, who played Christine in “The Phantom of the Opera,” dropped the official facade of graciousness and sentimentality to voice a cheerful sense of the absurd.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24RAISSA_SPAN/24RAISSA_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24RAISSA_SPAN/24RAISSA_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24RAISSA_SPAN/24RAISSA_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24RAISSA_SPAN/24RAISSA_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24RAISSA_SPAN/24RAISSA_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24RAISSA_SPAN/24RAISSA_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24RAISSA_SPAN/24RAISSA_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Singing for Siri, and Others, With Humor', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Singing for Siri, and Others, With Humor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Katona Bennett, Raissa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T22:26:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/afabf3c6-f50e-53c2-813d-56b4073b9cde', 'word_count': 375, 'uri': 'nyt://article/afabf3c6-f50e-53c2-813d-56b4073b9cde'}, {'abstract': 'Filmed in 25 countries and using striking images but no narration or dialogue, “Samsara” examines the state of the modern world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/24/movies/samsara-a-documentary-directed-by-ron-fricke.html', 'snippet': 'Filmed in 25 countries and using striking images but no narration or dialogue, “Samsara” examines the state of the modern world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Susan Sontag concluded her book “On Photography” with a plea for “an ecology of images,” an approach to making and reproducing photographs that would protect both the meaning of particular pictures and the integrity of the reality they depict. Since the 1970s, when the essays in Sontag’s book were written, the global glut of images has grown almost beyond measure. In the age of Instagram and Google Earth it is easy to believe — it is sometimes hard not to believe — that every inch of the planet, every human face and patch of wilderness, has been snapped up and uploaded. We have seen it all.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24SAMSARA_SPAN/24SAMSARA_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 275, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24SAMSARA_SPAN/24SAMSARA_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 275}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype