pe': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joyce Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joyce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26080363-8917-5af9-ab1e-02f737b42944', 'word_count': 1045, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26080363-8917-5af9-ab1e-02f737b42944'}, {'abstract': 'The author writes that universities are not the only ones responsible for falsifying their admissions data.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/23/the-true-culprits-of-college-data-fudging/', 'snippet': 'The author writes that universities are not the only ones responsible for falsifying their admissions data.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For this week’s installment of The Choice on India Ink, we have an essay about the malpractice of United States colleges and universities that misreport data to climb the college rankings charts. The author, Ben Murray, is an intern for The Choice and a student at New York University. — Tanya Abrams', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The True Culprits of College Data Fudging', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACT (Examination)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADMISSIONS STANDARDS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'SAT (College Admission Test)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ACT, Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Baylor University', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Claremont McKenna College', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Emory University', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Iona College', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Illinois', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Villanova University', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T23:30:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Murray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42d5413c-b10d-54fe-9e1f-2f069ea9c599', 'word_count': 628, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42d5413c-b10d-54fe-9e1f-2f069ea9c599'}, {'abstract': 'Socrates pays a visit to the author to argues that presidential elections are no better than a coin toss.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/23/should-we-have-elections/', 'snippet': 'Socrates pays a visit to the author to argues that presidential elections are no better than a coin toss.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sometimes I dream that I’m talking to Socrates. I’ve tried to talk philosophy with him, but he’s not interested. My guess is that he’s either figured everything out or decided there are no answers. But he does, oddly, have quite an interest in current events in the United States. So I asked him about the presidential election coming up in November.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Should We Cancel the Election? (A Socratic Dialog)', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Democracy (Theory and Philosophy)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Socrates', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T23:30:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Gutting', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gutting', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dfe91c45-7a7a-50e7-9be4-67af77e0df7e', 'word_count': 975, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dfe91c45-7a7a-50e7-9be4-67af77e0df7e'}, {'abstract': 'The seizure of a multimillion-dollar property owned by the son of the president of Equatorial Guinea underscores how France is changing its relationship with Africa.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/24/world/europe/for-obiangs-son-high-life-in-paris-is-over.html', 'snippet': 'The seizure of a multimillion-dollar property owned by the son of the president of Equatorial Guinea underscores how France is changing its relationship with Africa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — For years, Teodoro Nguema Obiang Mangue seemed to lead a charmed life, visiting his five-story pied-à-terre here on the chic Avenue Foch two or three times a year, choosing among 11 supercars, ordering bottles of Romanée-Conti Burgundies and watching movies in his home cinema.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/world/paris1/paris1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/world/paris1/paris1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/world/paris1/paris1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/24/world/paris1/paris1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/world/paris1/paris1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A French Shift on Africa Strips a Dictator’s Son of His Treasures', 'kicker': 'Paris Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A French Shift on Africa Strips a Dictator’s Son of His Abundant Treasures', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obiang Mangue, Teodoro Nguema', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Equatorial Guinea', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obiang Nguema Mbasogo, Teodoro', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T23:32:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maïa de la Baume', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maïa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'de la Baume', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a709680f-f5bf-5ef3-944f-4f3b42c61954', 'word_count': 1138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a709680f-f5bf-5ef3-944f-4f3b42c61954'}, {'abstract': 'A resort in Lyndonville on Thursday settled a lawsuit accusing it of discriminating against a lesbian couple when its owners declined to host their wedding reception.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/24/us/vermont-inn-settles-bias-suit-involving-lesbian-pair.html', 'snippet': 'A resort in Lyndonville on Thursday settled a lawsuit accusing it of discriminating against a lesbian couple when its owners declined to host their wedding reception.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A resort in Lyndonville on Thursday settled a lawsuit accusing it of discriminating against a lesbian couple when its owners declined to host their wedding reception. The lawsuit, filed by the American Civil Liberties Union, claimed that the Wildflower Inn violated that state’s Fair Housing and Public Accommodations Act when a representative said the inn would not host the wedding reception of Kate and Ming Linsley because of the owners’ “personal feelings.” The resort will pay $10,000 to the Vermont Human Rights Commission, a co-plaintiff in the case, and $20,000 to a charitable trust that the couple will disburse. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vermont: Inn Settles Bias Suit Involving Lesbian Pair ', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | New England', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Vermont: Inn Settles Bias Suit Involving Lesbian Pair', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wildflower Inn', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vermont', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T23:38:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jess Bidgood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jess', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bidgood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/dbf59971-7b3d-5fbb-ba86-a4df4d1a3bed', 'word_count': 101, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dbf59971-7b3d-5fbb-ba86-a4df4d1a3bed'}, {'abstract': 'James E. Holmes, who has been charged with killing 12 people in a July 20 shooting spree, had been barred from the campus of the University of Colorado Denver after making threats.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/24/us/colorado-campus-had-barred-massacre-suspect.html', 'snippet': 'James E. Holmes, who has been charged with killing 12 people in a July 20 shooting spree, had been barred from the campus of the University of Colorado Denver after making threats.', 'lead_paragraph': ' James E. Holmes, the former graduate student charged with killing 12 people in a July 20 shooting spree inside a movie theater, had been barred from the campus of the University of Colorado Denver after making threats in June, prosecutors said Thursday in a court hearing. They did not give details about the threats during the hearing, in which they sparred with the defense over whether the prosecution should have access to Mr. Holmes’s school records. They confirmed published reports that university officials had reported concerns about Mr. Holmes to the police, and said that he had also performed poorly on oral exams that capped his first year at the university. Mr. Holmes was dropping out of the neuroscience program in the month before the shooting. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Colorado: Campus Had Barred Massacre Suspect', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Rockies', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Colorado: Campus Had Barred Massacre Suspect ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Aurora, Colo, Shooting (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holmes, James Eagan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Colorado', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Denver (Colo)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T23:41:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/b6b6c849-8f5f-59da-b4f2-6182a1f217aa', 'word_count': 128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b6b6c849-8f5f-59da-b4f2-6182a1f217aa'}, {'abstract': 'A fresh visual explainer reveals the weak spot in assertions that global warming has stopped.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/23/a-fresh-look-at-the-difference-between-climate-trends-and-variations/', 'snippet': 'A fresh visual explainer reveals the weak spot in assertions that global warming has stopped.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For awhile I’ve been wanting to draw attention to this visual deconstruction of the “global warming has stopped” meme often used by people whose profession or worldview makes them apt to reject science pointing to a greenhouse-heated climate:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Fresh Look at the Difference Between Climate Trends and Variations', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANIMATED FILMS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Statistics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Norway', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-23T23:42:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1b04a5b-234c-5a4e-b018-4c65e3c37988', 'word_count': 116, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1b04a5b-234c-5a4e-b018-4c65e3c37988'}, {'abstract': 'In “The Revenant” a good-hearted soldier dies, becomes a zombie, and realizes he needs infusions of human blood to remain undead.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/24/movies/the-revenant-horror-comedy-directed-by-kerry-prior.html', 'snippet': 'In “The Revenant” a good-hearted soldier dies, becomes a zombie, and realizes he needs infusions of human blood to remain undead.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A middling zombie movie elevated by clever writing and gooeylicious special effects, Kerry Prior’s “Revenant” toys with big themes but settles for uneasy laughs. Even so, when you consider that most American horror-comedies are about as funny as rotting flesh, this small sleeper delivers a surprising number of pleasingly putrid punch lines.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24RDP_REVENANT_SPAN/0824REVENANT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/24/arts/24RDP_REVENANT_SPAN/0824REVENANT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'imag