 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/602f3e97-8b15-5738-8cfe-30b80b79a068', 'word_count': 133, 'uri': 'nyt://article/602f3e97-8b15-5738-8cfe-30b80b79a068'}, {'abstract': 'I may see my son as struggling socially, but he doesn’t, and he went to summer camp anxiety-free.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/24/sending-a-child-with-autism-to-sleep-away-camp/', 'snippet': 'I may see my son as struggling socially, but he doesn’t, and he went to summer camp anxiety-free.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My 13-year-old son Jonah and his new best friend, Sebastian, were in the pool the other day, armed with Styrofoam water guns, when things escalated. I wasn’t there, but my wife, Cynthia, told me the story later. It seems Jonah was getting the worst of the shootout and after a while Sebastian’s mother suggested her son go easy. He didn’t. I’m guessing the fact that Jonah found the whole thing hilarious played a part in his friend persisting. But Sebastian also had another, more big-hearted reason for drenching my son.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sending a Child With Autism to Sleep-Away Camp', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Autism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Camps and Camping', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Friendship', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-24T14:34:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joel Yanofsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yanofsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9723bdcf-7365-53cb-b126-8ea4a0e098bf', 'word_count': 660, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9723bdcf-7365-53cb-b126-8ea4a0e098bf'}, {'abstract': 'A team of scientists hopes to discover whether the Chinle Formation of southern Utah was drier and more seasonal than that of Arizona and, if so, how that affected the types of plants and animals living there.', 'web_url': 'https://scientistatwork.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/24/traveling-to-tropical-utah/', 'snippet': 'A team of scientists hopes to discover whether the Chinle Formation of southern Utah was drier and more seasonal than that of Arizona and, if so, how that affected the types of plants and animals living there.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Randall Irmis, curator of paleontology at the Natural History Museum of Utah and an assistant professor at the University of Utah, is investigating the rise of dinosaurs in southeastern Utah.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Traveling to Tropical Utah', 'kicker': 'Scientist at Work', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paleontology', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Natural History Museum of Utah', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Utah', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-24T14:37:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randall Irmis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randall', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Irmis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c0320eef-645b-5d66-b22d-e3764b74a1df', 'word_count': 528, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0320eef-645b-5d66-b22d-e3764b74a1df'}, {'abstract': 'Remaining loyal to a terrible sports team can feel like a self-imposed prison. But it can also be an insight into your soul.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/26/magazine/why-does-anyone-root-for-incompetent-failing-teams.html', 'snippet': 'Remaining loyal to a terrible sports team can feel like a self-imposed prison. But it can also be an insight into your soul.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In January 2002, the Oakland Raiders, a football team I have worshiped since I was 5, flew east to face the New England Patriots in a playoff game. I was living 30 miles north of Foxboro Stadium, and I can still remember the color of the sky that morning, the dense gunmetal of a looming storm. By late afternoon, snow was falling at an almost comical rate. It blotted out the yard markers and hampered traction, which lent the game a surreal, slapstick air.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '56', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/magazine/26riff/26riff-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 89, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/26/magazine/26riff/26riff-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 89}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/magazine/26riff/mag-26Riff-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 162, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/magazine/26riff/mag-26Riff-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 323, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'capt