edia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sisterhood', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-24T15:27:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stacey Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stacey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90a34f8e-109a-5df9-b62d-20a472378439', 'word_count': 89, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90a34f8e-109a-5df9-b62d-20a472378439'}, {'abstract': 'Gold prices crossed 31, 000 rupees (about $560) per 10 grams in the Indian market.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/24/image-of-the-day-august-24/', 'snippet': 'Gold prices crossed 31, 000 rupees (about $560) per 10 grams in the Indian market.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Image of the Day: August 24', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-24T15:36:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2984a2a0-3789-576b-acb3-81bf3553a52d', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2984a2a0-3789-576b-acb3-81bf3553a52d'}, {'abstract': 'A gunman opened fire outside the Empire State Building. And Remington doesn’t want Albany to pass new gun control laws.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/24/another-shooting/', 'snippet': 'A gunman opened fire outside the Empire State Building. And Remington doesn’t want Albany to pass new gun control laws.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Consider this juxtaposition, courtesy of The Times home page. At the top of the site we’re reporting that a gunman opened fire outside the Empire State Building, shooting and killing a former co-worker before he was killed by the police. A bit lower down on the page, Thomas Kaplan reports from Ilion, New York, that the Remington Arms company, which has been making guns there for more than 200 years, is threatening to leave the state and close its factory if lawmakers have the temerity to pass any new gun laws.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Another Shooting', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Remington Arms Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-24T15:51:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f9ab59c-1b2c-5905-926c-8fb613affb14', 'word_count': 427, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f9ab59c-1b2c-5905-926c-8fb613affb14'}, {'abstract': 'For tennis fans and spectators, a fresh and local menu will be served at the United States Open, which starts on Monday and runs until Sept. 9 in Flushing Meadows, Queens.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/24/at-the-u-s-open-serving-a-fresh-and-local-menu/', 'snippet': 'For tennis fans and spectators, a fresh and local menu will be served at the United States Open, which starts on Monday and runs until Sept. 9 in Flushing Meadows, Queens.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Novak Djokovic, the reigning United States Open men’s singles champion, partly credits his success to the gluten-free diet he adopted two years ago, which he said has made him quicker, faster and stronger. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At the U.S. Open, Serving a Fresh and Local Menu', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-24T16:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sophie Brickman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sophie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brickman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5576a160-f3ef-5993-a447-d8641ac69c88', 'word_count': 356, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5576a160-f3ef-5993-a447-d8641ac69c88'}, {'abstract': 'With no major city, few minorities and few union members, South Dakota is rough terrain for Democratic presidential candidates. The last Democrat to carry the state was Lyndon Johnson in 1964.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/24/in-south-dakota-only-the-farm-trumps-conservatism/', 'snippet': 'With no major city, few minorities and few union members, South Dakota is rough terrain for Democratic presidential candidates. The last Democrat to carry the state was Lyndon Johnson in 1964.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We continue our Presidential Geography series, a one-by-one examination of the peculiarities that drive the politics in all 50 states and the District of Columbia. Here, a look at South Dakota, the Mount Rushmore State. FiveThirtyEight spoke with Michael Card, an associate professor of political science at the University of South Dakota; John Miller, a former professor of history at South Dakota State University; and Erin Fouberg, a professor of geography at Northern State University in Aberdeen, S.D.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In South Dakota, Only the Farm Trumps Conservatism', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-24T16:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Micah Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Micah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/34e9b7e6-3e23-56ef-ba2e-4f9152c0bc77', 'word_count': 983, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34e9b7e6-3e23-56ef-ba2e-4f9152c0bc77'}, {'abstract': 'The European private equity firm Permira is buying Akindo Sushiro, an Osaka-based operator of sushi bars in Japan, for roughly $1 billion including debt.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/08/24/permira-to-buy-japanese-sushi-chain-for-about-1-billion/', 'snippet': 'The European private equity firm Permira is buying Akindo Sushiro, an Osaka-based operator of sushi bars in Japan, for roughly $1 billion including debt.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The European private equity firm Permira has struck a deal to acquire a Japanese sushi restaurant chain, with plans to help it expand overseas.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Permira to Buy Japanese Sushi Chain for About $1 Billion', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mergers: Equity Firm In Europe Acquires Stake In a Sushi Chain', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Permira Advisers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-24T16:12:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': '