ization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/602f03ed-de47-5180-b67f-b2855da2dd88', 'word_count': 1489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/602f03ed-de47-5180-b67f-b2855da2dd88'}, {'abstract': 'Two pianists have different ways of stepping out: H J Lim audaciously recorded nearly all the Beethoven piano sonatas for her debut release, while David Greilsammer is focusing on a conceptual approach to boldly varied programming.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/26/arts/music/david-greilsammer-and-h-j-lim-pianists-stepping-out.html', 'snippet': 'Two pianists have different ways of stepping out: H J Lim audaciously recorded nearly all the Beethoven piano sonatas for her debut release, while David Greilsammer is focusing on a conceptual approach to boldly varied programming.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN the spring of 1970 Rudolf Serkin announced that he would play the 32 Beethoven piano sonatas in a series of eight recitals at Carnegie Hall during the 1970-71 season to commemorate the 200th anniversary of Beethoven’s birth. Then nearly 67, Serkin was a pre-eminent Beethoven interpreter. Yet he had never played a complete Beethoven sonata cycle. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/arts/26INTROS1_SPAN/26INTROS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/26/arts/26INTROS1_SPAN/26INTROS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/arts/26INTROS1_SPAN/26INTROS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/arts/26INTROS1_SPAN/