Leyden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liz', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leyden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c378e60-730a-5ed2-a51e-4234c691dc8f', 'word_count': 910, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c378e60-730a-5ed2-a51e-4234c691dc8f'}, {'abstract': 'The works in “Men of Fire: José Clemente Orozco and Jackson Pollock” are filled with the ideas and concerns of the 1930s and the various mythologies that fascinated artists of that era.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/26/nyregion/a-review-of-men-of-fire-jose-clemente-orozco-and-jackson-pollock-in-east-hampton.html', 'snippet': 'The works in “Men of Fire: José Clemente Orozco and Jackson Pollock” are filled with the ideas and concerns of the 1930s and the various mythologies that fascinated artists of that era.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jackson Pollock is best known for the paintings he made from 1947 to 1951 in which he poured and dripped enamel paint onto large, unprimed canvases tacked to the floor of his studio, which at that time happened to be in Springs, N.Y., a hamlet in the town of East Hampton. These are the works that broke the ice for other painters of his generation, as Willem de Kooning put it, and that turned Pollock into a legend: “Jack the Dripper,” according to a 1956 Life magazine article, whose approach signaled the end of easel painting for many younger artists. ', 'print_section': 'LI', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/nyregion/26ARTSLI4_SPAN/26ARTSLI4_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/26/nyregion/26ARTSLI4_SPAN/26ARTSLI4_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'ca