at purports to provide true measures of many economic variables.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Another Alternative Inflation Measure', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-25T16:12:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5cf5a6bf-b813-5e53-b868-324d3821944d', 'word_count': 60, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5cf5a6bf-b813-5e53-b868-324d3821944d'}, {'abstract': 'The financial crisis is receding, without prosecutions to deter wrongdoing even as a fuller picture is emerging of the range of banks’ reckless and lawless activities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/26/opinion/sunday/no-crime-no-punishment.html', 'snippet': 'The financial crisis is receding, without prosecutions to deter wrongdoing even as a fuller picture is emerging of the range of banks’ reckless and lawless activities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the Justice Department recently closed its criminal investigation of Goldman Sachs, it became all but certain that no major American banks or their top executives would ever face criminal charges for their role in the financial crisis. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No Crime, No Punishment', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Crime, No Punishment', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subprime Mortgage Crisis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'},