a Confrontational Tilt Brings the Crowd Along for the Ride', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Band With a Confrontational Tilt Brings the Crowd Along for the Ride', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Duchess Says (Music Group)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-26T21:11:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc0e47a7-4c12-5279-9e00-2375ef95e0f9', 'word_count': 739, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc0e47a7-4c12-5279-9e00-2375ef95e0f9'}, {'abstract': 'The following tax-exempt fixed-income issues are scheduled for pricing this week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/business/treasury-auctions-set-for-this-week.html', 'snippet': 'The following tax-exempt fixed-income issues are scheduled for pricing this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Treasury’s schedule of financing this week includes Monday’s regular weekly auction of new three- and six-month bills and an auction of four-week bills on Tuesday.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Treasury Auctions Set for This Week', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Treasury Auctions Set for This Week', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Government Bonds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-26T21:12:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c57cdce-f1fd-5403-b5d6-63a1a9fcf5f0', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c57cdce-f1fd-5403-b5d6-63a1a9fcf5f0'}, {'abstract': '“The Matchmaker,” Thornton Wilder’s 1954 farce, has been given a rare and loving revival at the Stratford Shakespeare Festival this year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/theater/reviews/the-matchmaker-at-stratford-shakespeare-festival.html', 'snippet': '“The Matchmaker,” Thornton Wilder’s 1954 farce, has been given a rare and loving revival at the Stratford Shakespeare Festival this year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'STRATFORD, Ontario — It may take a little while for audiences to stop waiting for the band to strike up during “The Matchmaker,” the 1954 farce by Thornton Wilder that has been given a rare and loving revival at the Stratford Shakespeare Festival. Wilder’s confectionary comedy was the most popular of his plays on Broadway, where the original production ran for more than a year. But the fame of “The Matchmaker” has since been eclipsed both by the enduring greatness of Wilder’s two masterworks, “Our Town” and “The Skin of Our Teeth” — each a Pulitzer Prize winner — and by the huge commercial success and undeniable charm of the musical version of the play, “Hello, Dolly!” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/27/arts/27MATCH/27MATCH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 737, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/27/arts/27MATCH/27MATCH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1474, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/27/arts/27MATCH/27MATCH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/27/arts/27MATCH/27MATCH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/27/arts/27MATCH/27MATCH-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Adventure as a Cure for Ailing Love Lives', 'kicker': 'Theater Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Adventure As a Cure For Ailing Love Lives', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilder, Thornton', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McKenna, Seana', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Abraham, Chris', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stratford Shakespeare Festival', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-26T21:20:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/42c3e35c-dfcd-5f34-89a0-d33764992c45', 'word_count': 1125, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42c3e35c-dfcd-5f34-89a0-d33764992c45'}, {'abstract': 'Nicole Polizzi, the star of MTV’s “Jersey Shore” who is better known as Snooki, gave birth to her first child.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/arts/television/snooki-is-a-mom.html', 'snippet': 'Nicole Polizzi, the star of MTV’s “Jersey Shore” who is better known as Snooki, gave birth to her first child.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nicole Polizzi, below, the star of MTV’s “Jersey Shore” who is better known as Snooki, gave birth to her first child, Lorenzo Dominic, on Sunday at a hospital in Livingston, N.J., according to MTV. Ms. Polizzi and her fiancé, Jionni LaValle, announced their engagement and the pregnancy last March, and the development became a focal point in the first season of the “Jersey Shore” spinoff “Snooki & JWoww.” Cameras followed the couple and Jennifer Farley, known as Jwow, to an ultrasound, and Ms. Polizzi appeared to give up the hard-partying ways that made her one of the most popular personalities in reality programming. MTV said that the last few months of Ms. Polizzi’s pregnancy would be featured in the sixth season of “Jersey Shore,” which has its premiere in October.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Snooki Is a Mom', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Snooki Is a Mom', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Polizzi, Nicole', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Jersey Shore (TV Program)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-26T21:23:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/53f192dc-f4fb-52f3-948b-caef1b81a110', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/53f192dc-f4fb-52f3-948b-caef1b81a110'}, {'abstract': 'Relievers threw four and one-third scoreless innings after the starter Freddy Garcia struggled as the Yankees increased their lead over the idle Tampa Bay Rays to four games.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/sports/baseball/bullpen-rescues-garcia-to-seal-yankees-victory.html', 'snippet': 'Relievers threw four and one-third scoreless innings after the starter Freddy Garcia struggled as the Yankees increased their lead over the idle Tampa Bay Rays to four games.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CLEVELAND — Danger lurked everywhere during the Yankees’ six-game Midwestern trip.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/27/sports/YYANKEES/YYANKEES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 713, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/27/sports/YYANKEES/YYANKEES-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1427, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/27/sports/YYANKEES/YYANKEES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/27/sports/YYANKEES/YYANKEES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/27/sports/YYANKEES/YYANKEES-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bullpen Rescues Garcia to Seal Yankees’ Win', 'kicker': 'Yankees 4, Indians 2', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bullpen Rescues Garcia to Seal Yankees’ Win', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cleveland Indians', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cabrera, Asdrubal', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-26T21:27:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Waldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bdaf1398-681e-57d3-a4a3-9264891ce6be', 'word_count': 867, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bdaf1398-681e-57d3-a4a3-9264891ce6be'}, {'abstract': 'Tana French puts her career into perspective as her latest murder mystery, “ Broken Harbor,” sits on the best-seller list.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/books/tana-french-finds-her-niche-in-dark-themes.html', 'snippet': 'Tana French puts her career into perspective as her latest murder mystery, “ Broken Harbor,” sits on the best-seller list.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DUBLIN — The identity of the murderer in Tana French’s thriller “Broken Harbor” is concealed, of course, until the end. But in some ways the killer is there all along, in the form not of a person but of a house — a malevolent family house rotting in a half-empty development begun in the Irish boom in the 2000s and all but abandoned in the subsequent crash.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/27/arts/subtana/subtana-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/27/arts/subtana/subtana-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/27/arts/subtana/subtana-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 659, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/27/arts/subtana/subtana-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1317, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/27/arts/subtana/subtana-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/27/arts/subtana/subtana-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/27/arts/subtana/subtana-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Writer Finds Her Niche in Dark Themes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Writer Finds Her Niche in Dark Themes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'French, Tana', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ireland', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-26T21:28:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Lyall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lyall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/93141b53-beaa-5349-9e2c-e14d84589782', 'word_count': 1038, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93141b53-beaa-5349-9e2c-e14d84589782'}, {'abstract': 'For the first time in its 50-year history the New York Film Festival has created a Midnight Movies sidebar that will celebrate genre films.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/movies/midnight-movies-at-new-york-festival.html', 'snippet': 'For the first time in its 50-year history the New York Film Festival has created a Midnight Movies sidebar that will celebrate genre films.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the first time in its 50-year history the New York Film Festival has created a Midnight Movies sidebar that will celebrate genre films, according to The Hollywood Reporter. The three chosen films to be screened during the festival, which starts on Sept. 28 and runs through Oct. 14 at Lincoln Center, are “The Bay” by Barry Levinson, about a deadly parasite outbreak; Takeshi Kitano’s yakuza action film “Outrage Beyond” (with Beat Takeshi, above); and Peter Strickland’s film within a film, “Berberian Sound Studio.” The center also said Friday that it would host a two-day conference, “NYFF Convergence,” with panels and workshops devoted to transmedia, a form of storytelling that uses multiple digital platforms. It is to be held Sept. 29 and 30 at the Elinor Bunin Munroe Film Center.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Midnight Movies at New York Festival', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Midnight Movies At New York Festival', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Levinson, Barry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New York Film Festival', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kitano, Takeshi', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Bay (Movie)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-26T21:29:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea332043-b0c8-581c-b3a2-ff01a14ccf69', 'word_count': 136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea332043-b0c8-581c-b3a2-ff01a14ccf69'}, {'abstract': 'The Mostly Mozart Festival came to a close with Louis Langrée conducting Beethoven’s Mass in C and with Martin Fröst as soloist in Mozart’s Clarinet Concerto.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/arts/music/mostly-mozart-festival-with-martin-frost-and-louis-langree.html', 'snippet': 'The Mostly Mozart Festival came to a close with Louis Langrée conducting Beethoven’s Mass in C and with Martin Fröst as soloist in Mozart’s Clarinet Concerto.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Summers seem to fly by faster than they used to, but if you are in denial about whether this one is about to end, you need only have stopped at Lincoln Center early on Saturday evening. The plaza was packed with listeners facing the Metropolitan Opera House, where “The Enchanted Island” — the opening installment of the Met’s summer-closing HD Festival — was about to be shown on a large, flat-screen monitor. And inside Avery Fisher Hall the musicians of the Mostly Mozart Festival Orchestra were filing onstage to play the festival’s closing concert — as sure a sign as any that the season’s days are numbered. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/27/arts/submozart/submozart-jumbo.jp