ome to golf’s great summer revival, a traveling exhibition of born-again careers. On Sunday, Nick Watney became the latest prodigal putter when he closed with a two-under-par 69 at the Black Course at Bethpage State Park for a 72-hole total of 10-under 274 for a three-stroke victory over Brandt Snedeker at the Barclays, the opening leg of the PGA Tour’s FedEx Cup playoffs. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/27/sports/golf/golf-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 671, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/27/sports/golf/golf-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1343, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/27/sports/golf/golf-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/27/sports/golf/golf-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/27/sports/golf/golf-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rejuvenation at the Barclays, and Maybe a Ryder Cup Spot', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rejuvenation at the Barclays, And Maybe a Ryder Cup Spot', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Snedeker, Brandt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Watney, Nick', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garcia, Sergio', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woods, Tiger', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T01:02:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Crouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c809be7e-37ed-58a6-b1ff-575148f409b4', 'word_count': 929, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c809be7e-37ed-58a6-b1ff-575148f409b4'}, {'abstract': 'The grass is heavy with dew in the mornings, and when the low sun shines through the seed heads of the grasses, it looks like a ground mist hanging over the pasture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/opinion/these-cool-mornings.html', 'snippet': 'The grass is heavy with dew in the mornings, and when the low sun shines through the seed heads of the grasses, it looks like a ground mist hanging over the pasture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The temperature now drops into the 50s every night. The grass is heavy with dew in the mornings, and when the low sun shines through the seed heads of the grasses, it looks like a ground mist hanging over the pasture. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'These Cool Mornings', 'kicker': 'Editorial | The Rural Life', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'These Cool Mornings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flowers and Plants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trees and Shrubs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rural Areas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T01:20:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Verlyn Klinkenborg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Verlyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klinkenborg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd363670-705c-5702-998c-ee2a90ae760a', 'word_count': 310, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd363670-705c-5702-998c-ee2a90ae760a'}, {'abstract': 'A compassionate Senate bill would address reproductive-health needs for disabled veterans, as well as their spouses and surrogates.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/opinion/fertility-services-for-veterans.html', 'snippet': 'A compassionate Senate bill would address reproductive-health needs for disabled veterans, as well as their spouses and surrogates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the unexpected and little-noted consequences of the wars in Iraq and Afghanistan has been an increase in the number of service members whose battlefield wounds leave them unable to have children. One apparent reason is the type of combat: when troops on foot patrols are struck by improvised bombs, they are particularly vulnerable to pelvic injuries that damage their reproductive systems. Military records show that since 2003, more than 1,800 service members in those wars — the majority of them Army troops — have suffered such wounds. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fertility Services for Veterans', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fertility Services for Veterans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infertility', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Veterans Affairs Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murray, Patty', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'In Vitro Fertilization', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T01:20:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/ffbf867b-1b1b-5573-94f7-805f3960548e', 'word_count': 380, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ffbf867b-1b1b-5573-94f7-805f3960548e'}, {'abstract': 'As more travelers choose rail, Mitt Romney, Chris Christie and Republicans in Washington should be doing everything in their power to improve train service, not stand in the way.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/opinion/the-amtrak-option.html', 'snippet': 'As more travelers choose rail, Mitt Romney, Chris Christie and Republicans in Washington should be doing everything in their power to improve train service, not stand in the way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Helped by the high cost of gasoline and airline tickets, Amtrak is doing very well. The railroad recently announced that when given a choice between air or rail, three-quarters of travelers between New York and Washington take the train. Between New York and Boston, more than half prefer the rails. One of the biggest impediments to further growth is the pair of 100-year-old tunnels under the Hudson River — one going in each direction between New Jersey and New York City. The tunnels operate at capacity, creating a bottleneck that will only grow worse as the metropolitan area’s population grows. It’s obviously time for a new tunnel complex. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Amtrak Option', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Amtrak Option', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMTRAK', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Railroads', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Christie, Christopher J', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T01:26:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/8dc784c6-8d2c-584e-baab-646a5876063f', 'word_count': 322, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8dc784c6-8d2c-584e-baab-646a5876063f'}, {'abstract': 'Lottery numbers for Aug. 26, 2012, for New York, New Jersey and Connecticut.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/nyregion/lottery-numbers-for-new-york-new-jersey-and-connecticut.html', 'snippet': 'Lottery numbers for Aug. 26, 2012, for New York, New Jersey and Connecticut.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Aug. 26, 2012', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lottery Numbers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lottery Numbers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lotteries', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T01:33:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'new