 the youngest population, and the oldest leaders. A startling four-decade gap between the median age of India’s people and that of its government officials most recently reared its head with a heavy-handed and widely-maligned crackdown on free speech on the Internet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is a Youth Revolution Brewing in India?', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gandhi, Rahul', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T07:25:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sambuddha Mitra Mustafi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sambuddha', 'middlename': 'Mitra', 'lastname': 'Mustafi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a831bad-1b5a-5d04-9133-fa079c3daefd', 'word_count': 1360, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a831bad-1b5a-5d04-9133-fa079c3daefd'}, {'abstract': 'When the Republican Party gathered for its convention in 1976, the outcome was anything but certain. Political favors dispensed by the White House and a fight over the picking of the vice president — specifically, Rule 16-C — gave President Gerald Ford the nomination over his challenger, Ronald Reagan.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/the-forensics-of-a-contested-convention/', 'snippet': 'When the Republican Party gathered for its convention in 1976, the outcome was anything but certain. Political favors dispensed by the White House and a fight over the picking of the vice president — specifically, Rule 16-C — gave President Gerald...', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — There was a contemporary political convention where the outcome was uncertain — the Republican gathering in Kansas City in 1976, when it was all about Rule 16-C.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Forensics of a Contested Convention', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Republican National Convention', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ford, Gerald Rudolph Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reagan, Ronald Wilson', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T07:35:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Albert R. Hunt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Albert', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Hunt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5886875c-507d-5b36-b50c-cef135e4adb0', 'word_count': 463, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5886875c-507d-5b36-b50c-cef135e4adb0'}, {'abstract': 'Government officials in Afghanistan have scrambled to explain how the beheaded bodies of 15 men and 2 women lay in the desert in the northeast Helmand Province on Sunday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/28/world/asia/attacks-on-soldiers-and-civilians-leave-dozens-dead-in-afghanistan.html', 'snippet': 'Government officials in Afghanistan have scrambled to explain how the beheaded bodies of 15 men and 2 women lay in the desert in the northeast Helmand Province on Sunday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KABUL, Afghanistan — When the killing ended in northeast Helmand Province on Sunday, the only thing Afghan officials knew for certain was that the beheaded bodies of 15 men and 2 women lay in the desert, in Taliban territory, after a startling spasm of brutality. The question now dogging them is why.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Differing Theories in Killing of 17 in Taliban Stronghold', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Differing Theories in Killing of 17 in Taliban Stronghold', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civilian Casualties', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Helmand Province (Afghanistan)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T08:17:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard A. Oppel Jr. and Taimoor Shah', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Oppel', 'qualifier': 'Jr.', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Taimoor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shah', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4734863-40ce-5a2d-924c-17e58d81c772', 'word_count': 738, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4734863-40ce-5a2d-924c-17e58d81c772'}, {'abstract': 'Today’s question is about the percentage of India’s gross domestic product that is spent on education.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/test-yourself-math-aug-27-2012/', 'snippet': 'Today’s question is about the percentage of India’s gross domestic product that is spent on education.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Test your math skills with this question created by Pete Diamantis at Math for America from the article “India Opens a Door to Private Education.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | Math, Aug. 27, 2012', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T08:28:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/417dd695-bc95-589a-be9c-db14b84e639f', 'word_count': 53, 'uri': 'nyt://article/417dd695-bc95-589a-be9c-db14b84e639f'}, {'abstract': 'Manmohan Singh presented 32 different points Monday to refute accusations the government squandered potential revenues.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/from-the-prime-minister-a-lengthy-defense-on-coal/', 'snippet': 'Manmohan Singh presented 32 different points Monday to refute accusations the government squandered potential revenues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Prime Minister Manmohan Singh spoke to India’s Parliament Monday about a recent report from India’s Comptroller and Auditor General concerning the allocation of coal assets in the country. The report alleges that the government lost out on nearly $34 billion in revenues by selling coal assets in private negotiations, rather than auctioning them. The prime minister and other central government officials dispute this figure. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From the Prime Minister, a Lengthy Defense on Coal', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coal', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mines and Mining', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Singh, Manmohan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T08:52:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c93a7f24-2dc5-53b8-addc-636713955f1b', 'word_count': 2250, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c93a7f24-2dc5-53b8-addc-636713955f1b'}, {'abstract': 'Coming out is hard enough. But bucking stereotypes can be even harder. The photographer Alan Charlesworth explores “bears,” or larger men, in gay culture.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/the-brotherhood-of-the-bears/', 'snippet': 'Coming out is hard enough. But bucking stereotypes can be even harder. The photographer Alan Charlesworth explores “bears,” or larger men, in gay culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are between four and six stages to coming out as gay, according to people who study these sorts of things. Many assume all gay people will first be confused about their sexuality, figure out they’re gay, accept that they are and finally, take pride in it. But for many gay men, including the photographer Alan Charlesworth, there’s another step of confusion thrown into the mix.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Brotherhood of the Bears', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Men and Boys', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T09:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Moskowitz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moskowitz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15141073-bc44-55ba-85ca-9a3495f09fb7', 'word_count': 726, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15141073-bc44-55ba-85ca-9a3495f09fb7'}, {'abstract': '6 Q’s About the News | What did Neil Armstrong accomplish in his lifetime?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/farewell-to-neil-armstrong/', 'snippet': '6 Q’s About the News | What did Neil Armstrong accomplish in his lifetime?', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHAT did Neil Armstrong, who died on Saturday, accomplish in his lifetime?For WHAT is he most remembered? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Farewell to Neil Armstrong', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T09:03:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shannon Doyne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shannon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doyne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/859ba87c-bc24-583e-a766-38713751ddf2', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/859ba87c-bc24-583e-a766-38713751ddf2'}, {'abstract': 'Pakistan’s Supreme Court on Monday delayed pressing contempt charges against Prime Minister Raja Pervez Ashraf, a move some analysts saw as a softening of the court’s posture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/28/world/asia/pakistani-prime-minister-wins-time-in-conflict-with-court.html', 'snippet': 'Pakistan’s Supreme Court on Monday delayed pressing contempt charges against Prime Minister Raja Pervez Ashraf, a move some analysts saw as a softening of the court’s posture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ISLAMABAD, Pakistan — In a rare conciliatory move toward the civilian-led government, Pakistan’s Supreme Court on Monday delayed until at least Sept. 18 the pressing of contempt charges against Prime Minister Raja Pervez Ashraf that could lead to his dismissal from office. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Charges Against Pakistani Prime Minister Are Delayed', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pakistan: Charges Against Premier Are Delayed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ashraf, Raja Pervez', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T09:14:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Salman Masood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Salman', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Masood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1edd42b0-11e7-5326-865f-a961ded3c6b8', 'word_count': 510, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1edd42b0-11e7-5326-865f-a961ded3c6b8'}, {'abstract': 'This week’s summer reading contest winner analyzes the reasons women and minority superheroes are missing from best-selling comics.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/student-contest-winner-missing-superheroes/', 'snippet': 'This week’s summer reading contest winner analyzes the reasons women and minority superheroes are missing from best-selling comics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We’re sad to announce that, with more than 1,500 comments posted since we began this contest in June, we’ve reached the last week, and our final winner. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Student Contest Winner | Missing Superheroes', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T09:15:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8bdb7c5c-e628-57ee-8f39-9d215b691cbb', 'word_count': 665, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8bdb7c5c-e628-57ee-8f39-9d215b691cbb'}, {'abstract': 'The scientist Michael Mann has threatened to sue National Review for defamation, setting up the potential for an interesting jury trial on climate change and the hockey stick graph.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/a-scopes-monkey-trial-for-climate-science/', 'snippet': 'The scientist Michael Mann has threatened to sue National Review for defamation, setting up the potential for an interesting jury trial on climate change and the hockey stick graph.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eighty-seven years ago, people and organizations who believed in freedom of scientific inquiry arranged for a test case of Tennessee’s law against teaching the theory of evolution. The result was theater so cogent that it was later distilled as the play “Inherit the Wind.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Climate Change, a Possible Trial Could Echo the Scopes Monkey Case', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mann, Michael E', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Review', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tennessee', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Inherit the Wind (Movie)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T10:00:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicity Barringer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicity', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barringer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9851177f-73ad-5064-8ebb-c87cd8e85d59', 'word_count': 625, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9851177f-73ad-5064-8ebb-c87cd8e85d59'}, {'abstract': 'Outsourcing continues to grow and hampers job creation in the United States, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/our-dis-integrated-economy/', 'snippet': 'Outsourcing continues to grow and hampers job creation in the United States, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nancy Folbre is an economics professor at the University of Massachusetts, Amherst.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Our Dis-Integrated Economy', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Outsourcing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN SOCIOLOGICAL ASSN', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T10:00:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nancy Folbre', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Folbre', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/07e9a0f5-65ec-590b-9c6c-0f26d0b81c60', 'word_count': 615, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07e9a0f5-65ec-590b-9c6c-0f26d0b81c60'}, {'abstract': 'Highlight matches at the United States Open.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/what-to-watch-on-monday-2/', 'snippet': 'Highlight matches at the United States Open.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was 2006 when James Blake last contended for a United States Open title, but he remains a class act and a fan favorite. Now 32, Blake missed the Australian Open this year and suffered first-round losses at the French Open and Wimbledon. He needed a wild card to get into the United States Open main draw, as his singles ranking sagged to 115.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What to Watch on Monday', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blake, James', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kvitova, Petra', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robson, Laura', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stosur, Samantha', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T10:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aron Pilhofer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pilhofer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c34a1ac2-ff16-5df4-bbed-c15fffc550ae', 'word_count': 693, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c34a1ac2-ff16-5df4-bbed-c15fffc550ae'}, {'abstract': 'On their last day of surveys in Samoa, researchers observe humpback whales in the early part of the winter breeding season.', 'web_url': 'https://scientistatwork.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/humpback-whales-off-upolu/', 'snippet': 'On their last day of surveys in Samoa, researchers observe humpback whales in the early part of the winter breeding season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Scott Baker, associate director of the Marine Mammal Institute at Oregon State University, writes from Samoa, where he studies the formation of local communities among dolphins and their genetic isolation from one another. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Slowing to a Whale’s Pace in Samoa', 'kicker': 'Scientist at Work', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Whales and Whaling', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oregon State University', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Samoa', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T10:07:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/db51a226-36f6-5d4a-bf24-4adf1d8ed6ec', 'word_count': 697, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db51a226-36f6-5d4a-bf24-4adf1d8ed6ec'}, {'abstract': 'The Seattle Seahawks named their starting quarterback, and it was the talented but unpardonably short player who slipped into the third round of the draft.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/russell-wilsons-victory-for-short-people/', 'snippet': 'The Seattle Seahawks named their starting quarterback, and it was the talented but unpardonably short player who slipped into the third round of the draft.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I don’t know the measurables for Matt Waldman’s head — I don’t want it to inflate — but here’s a hat tip for his film work on behalf of the Fifth Down and his defense of Wisconsin quarterback Russell Wilson leading up to the draft. On Sunday, the Seattle Seahawks officially named Wilson — the unpardonably short player whom N.F.L. talent evaluators allowed to slip into the third round — as their starting quarterback. (He’s 5-11.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Russell Wilson’s Victory for ‘Short People’', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Russell', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Seattle Seahawks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T10:11:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3f67eb9-9ffd-5784-bb24-ea8bbf219eee', 'word_count': 486, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3f67eb9-9ffd-5784-bb24-ea8bbf219eee'}, {'abstract': 'The Jets’ offense failed to score a touchdown for the third preseason game in a row. But how much does it matter?', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/how-worried-should-the-jets-be/', 'snippet': 'The Jets’ offense failed to score a touchdown for the third preseason game in a row. But how much does it matter?', 'lead_paragraph': '“We want to see the offense score. Am I frustrated? No. It’s the preseason right now.”Jets cornerback Darrelle Revis, after the Jets’ offense struggled again in a loss to Carolina on Sunday night. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Worried Should the Jets Be?', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T10:48:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/45fcccee-e786-56e5-b973-d51a85292e14', 'word_count': 302, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45fcccee-e786-56e5-b973-d51a85292e14'}, {'abstract': 'There’s simply not enough talent here for the new coach Mike Mularkey to turn things around right away.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/jacksonville-jaguars-2012-n-f-l-season-preview/', 'snippet': 'There’s simply not enough talent here for the new coach Mike Mularkey to turn things around right away.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Andy Benoit has been previewing all N.F.L. 32 teams. This week, he analyzes the A.F.C. South, and the Jaguars are first up. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jacksonville Jaguars, 2012 N.F.L. Season Preview', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Jacksonville Jaguars', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T11:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Benoit', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benoit', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2265d3aa-53d8-5487-82a7-a8d5c4512542', 'word_count': 2701, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2265d3aa-53d8-5487-82a7-a8d5c4512542'}, {'abstract': 'Plus: waiting for the Fed, marginal tax rates through the years, tweets of the week — and how many people are you expecting at your funeral?', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/this-week-in-small-business-trash-talk/', 'snippet': 'Plus: waiting for the Fed, marginal tax rates through the years, tweets of the week — and how many people are you expecting at your funeral?', 'lead_paragraph': 'What’s affecting me, my clients and other small-business owners this week.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Week in Small Business: Trash Talk', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Entrepreneurship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T11:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gene Marks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0651671-e415-5304-807d-79a3159e2ff6', 'word_count': 1243, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0651671-e415-5304-807d-79a3159e2ff6'}, {'abstract': 'If the fake-news media outlet The Onion is a parody of American life, Fognews is the romantic ideal of Russian life.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/the-russian-fake-news-media-outlet-fognews-presents-reality-as-it-should-be/', 'snippet': 'If the fake-news media outlet The Onion is a parody of American life, Fognews is the romantic ideal of Russian life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — The best news in Russia these days comes from www.fognews.ru. It’s a news agency — sort of — whose home page warns readers that the news is “new things we have made up.” It does so in Latin (nоvае rеs а nоbis соnfiсtае), though, so most people probably don’t notice.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fog of News', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pussy Riot', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T11:02:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Masha Gessen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Masha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gessen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5d6007e-0920-5588-9dce-1584deb9f0bf', 'word_count': 663, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5d6007e-0920-5588-9dce-1584deb9f0bf'}, {'abstract': 'Make iTunes automatically convert the songs on a CD and eject the disc when it finishes.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/tip-of-the-week-streamlined-cd-ripping-in-itunes/', 'snippet': 'Make iTunes automatically convert the songs on a CD and eject the disc when it finishes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Buying music online may have replaced the act of purchasing compact discs for many people. For those with a closet full of old CDs still waiting to get converted to digital files in iTunes, there is a program setting that can speed up the chore slightly — by making iTunes automatically eject a disc once the songs have been converted to digital files on the computer.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tip of the Week: Streamlined CD-Ripping in iTunes', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Compact Discs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'iTunes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T11:02:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3afcf724-cc26-51b3-8c14-b6526d1f8076', 'word_count': 248, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3afcf724-cc26-51b3-8c14-b6526d1f8076'}, {'abstract': 'The tracking forecasts reached a consensus that the storm would land overnight Tuesday somewhere around southeastern Louisiana as a Category 2 hurricane.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/28/us/tropical-storm-gains-strength-moving-toward-coast.html', 'snippet': 'The tracking forecasts reached a consensus that the storm would land overnight Tuesday somewhere around southeastern Louisiana as a Category 2 hurricane.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW ORLEANS — Huge and slow, Tropical Storm Isaac lumbered up 