ke the spotlight away from the convention to nominate Mitt Romney. If Isaac hits the Gulf Coast states it could become a breaking news event that pulls attention away from the convention. Under one unpleasant scenario, Jim Rutenberg and Michael D. Shear write, the networks could go to a split-screen, pairing', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Isaac Crashes G.O.P. Convention, and Whither ‘Nightline’?', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T13:44:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d9f1a75-d1e4-538f-84c0-8159ebe1c96c', 'word_count': 597, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d9f1a75-d1e4-538f-84c0-8159ebe1c96c'}, {'abstract': 'I.B.M. has agreed to acquire Kenexa, a maker of recruitment software, for $1.3 billion in cash, or $46 a share.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/ibm-snaps-up-kenexa/', 'snippet': 'I.B.M. has agreed to acquire Kenexa, a maker of recruitment software, for $1.3 billion in cash, or $46 a share.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For enterprise software businesses with a social spin, valuations are rising.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'I.B.M. Snaps Up Kenexa', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Enterprise Computing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Inter