': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Barclays Center (NYC)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T14:18:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth A. Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/785e1cd2-145f-5594-87de-b02a85bd92a3', 'word_count': 1123, 'uri': 'nyt://article/785e1cd2-145f-5594-87de-b02a85bd92a3'}, {'abstract': 'TV networks are moving their anchors into position in New Orleans while still trying to cover the Republican convention in Tampa.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/with-isaac-bearing-down-networks-weigh-their-options/', 'snippet': 'TV networks are moving their anchors into position in New Orleans while still trying to cover the Republican convention in Tampa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Put yourself in the shoes of a network news president for a moment. There’s a tropical storm bearing down on the Gulf Coast, reviving memories of Hurricane Katrina seven years ago. But there’s also a political convention beginning in Tampa, Fla., representing the start of a two-month sprint to Election Day. Where do you send the symbols of your news division, your news anchors?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'With Isaac Bearing Down, Networks Weigh Their Options', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricanes and Tropical Storms', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Republican National Convention', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cooper, Anderson', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sawyer, Diane', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Shepard', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Brian', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ABC INC', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corporation', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS News', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CNN', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox News Channel', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T14:18:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3340d78-f604-52fd-9e25-c7c990c955a7', 'word_count': 680, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3340d78-f604-52fd-9e25-c7c990c955a7'}, {'abstract': 'The closing chapter of the presidential race will be highlighted by a handful of marquee events that could change the dynamic of the contest.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/key-dates-between-now-and-election-day/', 'snippet': 'The closing chapter of the presidential race will be highlighted by a handful of marquee events that could change the dynamic of the contest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama and Mitt Romney have 71 days of campaigning from Monday until the election. The closing chapter of the race will be highlighted by a handful of marquee events that could change the dynamic of the contest. The political conventions will give way to a burst of campaigning in September, followed by the closely watched debates in October. While the election is not until Nov. 6, tens of millions of voters will not wait. Early voting begins in less than a month in Iowa, and four years ago, 88 percent of the electorate in the battleground state of Colorado cast their ballots before Election Day. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Key Dates Between Now and Election Day', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T14:32:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Zeleny', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zeleny', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0bd1b904-a781-5af6-a330-416a46f2b160', 'word_count': 174, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0bd1b904-a781-5af6-a330-416a46f2b160'}, {'abstract': 'Rustic free-form pies with a nutty but delicate whole-wheat crust showcase the season’s sweet, juicy stone fruits.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/health/nutrition/dessert-galette-pastry.html', 'snippet': 'Rustic free-form pies with a nutty but delicate whole-wheat crust showcase the season’s sweet, juicy stone fruits.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As long as there are still peaches, plums, apricots, berries and nectarines to be had, I’m buying them up and making pies and galettes. A galette is a free-form pie, more rustic than a tart. Although they’re usually made with classic buttery pie dough or puff pastry, I’ve been working at developing a dough recipe that is delicate and tasty but not too rich. I decided that a yeasted dough could work, and came up with a formula that yields enough for two galettes but has only 60 grams of butter (about 4 tablespoons). The flavor is nutty and rich because of the whole-wheat flour, but the dough isn’t heavy. The trick is to roll it very thin, then freeze it right away so that it doesn’t continue to rise and become too bready, and also so that it’s easy to work with when you are ready to assemble the tart. The dough works beautifully for these free-form galettes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/28/science/27recipehealth/27recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 377, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/28/science/27recipehealth/27recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 377}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/28/science/27recipehealth/27recipehealth-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 643, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/28/science/27recipehealth/27recipehealth-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1285, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/28/science/27recipehealth/27recipehealth-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/28/science/27recipehealth/27recipehealth-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/28/science/27recipehealth/27recipehealth-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'he