appealed to voters both together and separately.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2012/08/26/us/politics/0825wip.html', 'snippet': 'While volunteers and campaign workers readied Tampa for the Republican National Convention, President Obama campaigned in Ohio and Nevada and Mitt Romney and Paul D. Ryan appealed to voters both together and separately.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While volunteers and campaign workers readied Tampa for the Republican National Convention, President Obama campaigned in Ohio and Nevada and Mitt Romney and Paul D. Ryan appealed to voters both together and separately.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/us/politics/0825wip-slide-XV8Y/0825wip-slide-XV8Y-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/us/politics/0825wip-slide-XV8Y/0825wip-slide-XV8Y-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/us/politics/0825wip-slide-XV8Y/0825wip-slide-XV8Y-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/26/us/politics/0825wip-slide-XV8Y/0825wip-slide-XV8Y-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/26/us/politics/0825wip-slide-XV8Y/0825wip-slide-XV8Y-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'This Week in Politics Pictures: Aug. 20-26', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Paul D Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Republican National Convention', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T16:01:59+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/51482519-6e02-5e17-b910-47c1736f9c61', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/51482519-6e02-5e17-b910-47c1736f9c61'}, {'abstract': 'Nars celebrates the artist and his many muses in a 29-piece offering with never-before-seen applications of Warhol’s works.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/vain-glorious-the-andy-warhol-collection-from-nars/', 'snippet': 'Nars celebrates the artist and his many muses in a 29-piece offering with never-before-seen applications of Warhol’s works.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Andy Warhol once proclaimed that “an artist is somebody who produces things that people don’t need to have,” a statement exemplified by his namesake foundation, which, in recent years, has allowed the artist’s work to be paired with everything from retail marketing initiatives (Barneys) to limited-edition luxury goods, ranging from accessories (Philip Treacy) to wine (Dom Perignon) and even skateboards (Alien Workshop). Noticeably missing from the portfolio, however, has been a beauty collaboration. Enter the Nars autumn/winter 2012 color collection.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vain Glorious | The Andy Warhol Collection From Nars', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T16:05:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lily Nima', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nima', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/85e7b127-4e72-5e85-a1f6-ec4f626e979f', 'word_count': 264, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85e7b127-4e72-5e85-a1f6-ec4f626e979f'}, {'abstract': 'History sometimes has a wicked sense of humor: German judges are telling Jews that circumcision might not be legal in their country.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/when-german-judges-ban-circumcision/', 'snippet': 'History sometimes has a wicked sense of humor: German judges are telling Jews that circumcision might not be legal in their country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TEL AVIV — It appears Judaism is under attack in Western Europe. Following a ruling by a local German court this spring that banned circumcisions for infringing a child’s “fundamental right to bodily integrity,” a criminal complaint was filed last week against a rabbi [paid content] in Germany for performing that sacred Jewish ritual. It still isn’t clear whether the complaint should have been pursued at all — the ruling and the rabbi depend on different jurisdictions. But this attack on freedom of religious practice has already sparked quite a bit of outrage.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nip and Tuck?', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Circumcision', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion-State Relations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T16:07:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shmuel Rosner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shmuel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/40bdd0d6-68f6-5c7d-9a19-d6123ef239f0', 'word_count': 671, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40bdd0d6-68f6-5c7d-9a19-d6123ef239f0'}, {'abstract': 'Despite decades of immunological research and a recent surge of interest in the bacterial garden of the human gut, diarrhea remains the most unpredictable travel-related illness.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/when-the-mango-bites-back/', 'snippet': 'Despite decades of immunological research and a recent surge of interest in the bacterial garden of the human gut, diarrhea remains the most unpredictable travel-related illness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW DELHI — Accepting a just-picked mango from a stranger in Lodi Gardens and then putting it directly into my mouth — skin and all — was stupid. I admit that.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/28/science/28HARR_SPAN/28HARR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/28/science/28HARR_SPAN/28HARR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xla