 earliest defensive tackle Chris Canty and tight end Travis Beckum can be activated is Oct. 16.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/giants-place-canty-and-beckum-on-pup-list/', 'snippet': 'The earliest defensive tackle Chris Canty and tight end Travis Beckum can be activated is Oct. 16.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EAST RUTHERFORD, N.J. – Defensive tackle Chris Canty, who underwent knee surgery in the off-season and was unable to practice this summer, was the most prominent among 15 players removed from the Giants’ roster Monday in order to reach the N.F.L.’s required limit of 75 players.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Giants Place Canty and Beckum on PUP List', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Canty, Chris', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Giants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T16:18:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tom Pedulla', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pedulla', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f16671ae-baf4-5fcd-8c70-f978959581cc', 'word_count': 198, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f16671ae-baf4-5fcd-8c70-f978959581cc'}, {'abstract': 'An unusual intersection of legal issues, local politics and weather has allowed Occupy Central to defy the authorities longer than similar movements elsewhere.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/28/business/global/occupy-hong-kong-protesters-defy-court-order-to-leave.html', 'snippet': 'An unusual intersection of legal issues, local politics and weather has allowed Occupy Central to defy the authorities longer than similar movements elsewhere.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — Occupy Wall Street and the movements it inspired in cities around the world last autumn have largely disappeared, brushed aside by battalions of police officers, but a handful of activists still cling to a site in the heart of Hong Kong’s central financial district, and on Monday evening they defied a court-imposed deadline for them to leave.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/28/business/global/28occupy/28occupy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/28/business/global/28occupy/28occupy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/28/business/global/28occupy/28occupy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/28/business/global/28occupy/28occupy-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/28/business/global/28occupy/28occupy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/28/business/global/28occupy/28occupy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/28/business/global/28occupy/28occupy-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Occupy Hong Kong Holdouts Defy Order to Leave Despite Effort by HSBC', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Occupy Hong Kong Holdouts Defy Order to Leave Despite Effort by HSBC', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evictions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hong Kong', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'HSBC Holdings PLC', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T16:20:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Keith Bradsher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Keith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bradsher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d90bd388-d5d8-5bbb-a4fa-7b7ede5ab152', 'word_count': 1129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d90bd388-d5d8-5bbb-a4fa-7b7ede5ab152'}, {'abstract': 'Young urban workers in the Philippines are helping to give the country its best prospects in decades, economists say.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/28/business/global/philippine-economy-set-to-become-asias-newest-bright-spot.html', 'snippet': 'Young urban workers in the Philippines are helping to give the country its best prospects in decades, economists say.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MANILA — In the upscale business district of Manila, a midweek crowd spills out into the street. The New York-themed Borough restaurant is pulsating to the beat of a Bon Jovi song, while young, hip Filipinos take shots of tequila from a passing tray and sing in unison.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/28/business/global/28philecon/28philecon-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/28/business/global/28philecon/28philecon-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/28/business/global/28philecon/28philecon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/28/business/global/28philecon/28philecon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/28/business/global/28philecon/28philecon-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Youthful Populace Helps Make the Philippines an Economic Bright Spot in Asia', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Youthful Populace Helps Make the Philippines an Economic Bright Spot in Asia', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Philippines', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Population', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Outsourcing', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T16:26:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Whaley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Whaley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/69b073eb-3d6d-5641-9d8c-68164f25b4bf', 'word_count': 1163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69b073eb-3d6d-5641-9d8c-68164f25b4bf'}, {'abstract': 'As the Union retreated from Virginia, Lincoln faced the biggest crisis of his administration.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/washington-in-disarray/', 'snippet': 'As the Union retreated from Virginia, Lincoln faced the biggest crisis of his administration.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Capt. Charles Francis Adams Jr., grandson and great-grandson of presidents and son of the current minister to Britain, arrived in Washington on Aug. 27, 1862. His regiment, the 1st Massachusetts Cavalry, had been hastily snatched from its operations on the South Carolina coast and shipped north to meet the emergency created by Gen. Robert E. Lee’s stunning counteroffensive after the failure of the Union’s Peninsula campaign. Adams knew the capital city well, and was appalled by its “atmosphere of treason, jealousy, and dissension” — more than usual, given the recriminations pouring forth as federal forces retreated.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Washington in Disarray', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ADAMS, CHARLES FRANCIS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McClellan, George B', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Army of the Potomac', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Virginia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T16:30:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Slotkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Slotkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f47062af-0dec-549c-91a3-dbe7010d4391', 'word_count': 1529, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f47062af-0dec-549c-91a3-dbe7010d4391'}, {'abstract': 'In his new book, David K. Randall talks to sleep experts of all kinds for a thoroughly enjoyable overview of a familiar yet remarkably foreign terrain.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/28/health/dreamland-review-exploring-the-mysteries-of-sleep.html', 'snippet': 'In his new book, David K. Randall talks to sleep experts of all kinds for a thoroughly enjoyable overview of a familiar yet remarkably foreign terrain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You may dye your gray hairs and Botox your wrinkles, but you will never sleep the sleep of your youth again. Not without serious illness or a powerful blow to the brain, that is. One of the many underappreciated facts David K. Randall puts forth in his perfectly paced tour of the subject is that adults are insomniacs because nature made them that way.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/28/science/28ZUGE/28ZUGE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 678, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/0