ng.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This sounds like common sense: If you work in a nursing home, you wash your hands when you start your shift and again before you leave. You wash your hands (or, in some cases, use an alcohol-based antimicrobial) before and after any direct contact with residents. Before you help someone with tooth-brushing, bathing, eating or using the toilet. Before and after handling a catheter or taking a finger-stick blood sample or changing a dressing. Or handling used bed linens. Or blowing your own nose.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Dirty Little Secret of Nursing Homes', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hygiene and Cleanliness', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nursing Homes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Pittsburgh', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Michigan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pennsylvania', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T17:19:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula Span', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Span', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd92e1c0-486e-5483-ba92-7089749d1018', 'word_count': 664, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd92e1c0-486e-5483-ba92-7089749d1018'}, {'abstract': 'Is a commercial about responding to a hoax actually a hoax itself?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/business/media/q-and-a-with-stuart-elliott.html', 'snippet': 'Is a commercial about responding to a hoax actually a hoax itself?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stuart Elliott, the advertising columnist, answers quest