me': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claudia Dreifus', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claudia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dreifus', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ded2fef-b39a-5755-8bc1-fff94b5d739e', 'word_count': 1258, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ded2fef-b39a-5755-8bc1-fff94b5d739e'}, {'abstract': 'Explaining the Summer Fiction Series on the One-Page Magazine.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/the-story-behind-my-very-very-short-stories/', 'snippet': 'Explaining the Summer Fiction Series on the One-Page Magazine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Certain writers (Ernest Hemingway, Lydia Davis) are — among their other literary talents — considered masters of the form known as flash fiction. I’m not. In fact, my last novel clocked in at 555 pages. Even so, because it sounded fun, I agreed last spring to write a bunch of really short stories for the One-Page Magazine. I knew that their appearance might perplex readers (fiction in The New York Times?), so my editor and I agreed that each one would run with the label “Summer Fiction Series.” Confusion nevertheless ensued.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Story Behind My Very, Very Short Stories', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T18:30:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Curtis Sittenfeld', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Curtis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sittenfeld', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88d06a6a-2fe3-55f9-b6b1-fe08a5f8427