/dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/far-from-wall-st-and-silicon-valley-a-focus-on-family-ties/', 'snippet': 'David Einhorn is the largest investor in a fund run by his brother and father. Their firm, Capital Midwest Fund, aims to tap the Midwestern work ethic and has ambitions of changing the way early stage investments are run.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MILWAUKEE — When the hedge fund manager David Einhorn was just another investment analyst in the mid-1990s, his family gave him $500,000 to get his fund, Greenlight Capital, off the ground. Now that he is a billionaire after a career of doing battle with large corporations, he has returned the favor.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Far From Wall Street and Silicon Valley, a Focus on Family Ties', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Far From Wall St. and Silicon Valley, a Focus on Family Ties', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Venture Capital', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Einhorn, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Greenlight Capital Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Milwaukee (Wis)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T22:29:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6390aaf6-7f90-5957-95f7-8883985bb954', 'word_count': 1130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6390aaf6-7f90-5957-95f7-8883985bb954'}, {'abstract': 'Protests get off to a slow start, just like the convention, in Tampa.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/protests-muted-as-convention-gears-up/', 'snippet': 'Protests get off to a slow start, just like the convention, in Tampa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TAMPA, Fla. — The storm that caused Republican officials to cancel the bulk of the first day of their convention is also dampening the protests, as fewer demonstrators than were expected turned out for the first day of rallies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Protests Muted as Convention Gears Up', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Republican National Convention', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Human Rights Campaign', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tampa (Fla)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T22:39:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Colin Moynihan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Colin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moynihan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/387c77aa-ae6d-58e9-87cd-bafd7c607f17', 'word_count': 691, 'uri': 'nyt://article/387c77aa-ae6d-58e9-87cd-bafd7c607f17'}, {'abstract': 'Mitchell Krueger, the highest-ranked American junior player, has decided to skip college and turn professional after the United States Open.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/top-american-junior-will-turn-pro-after-u-s-open/', 'snippet': 'Mitchell Krueger, the highest-ranked American junior player, has decided to skip college and turn professional after the United States Open.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Mitchell Krueger of Aledo, Tex., has decided to skip college and turn professional after the United States Open junior tournament, which begins next weekend.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Top American Junior Will Turn Pro After U.S. Open', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T22:54:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5f50e88-a4fe-5f6b-9961-a5dde1069867', 'word_count': 255, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5f50e88-a4fe-5f6b-9961-a5dde1069867'}, {'abstract': 'The police say they believe a 17-year-old, Akbar Johns, fatally shot Ronald Wallace just after midnight Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/photo-released-for-suspect-in-13-year-olds-killing/', 'snippet': 'The police say they believe a 17-year-old, Akbar Johns, fatally shot Ronald Wallace just after midnight Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Three days after a 13-year-old boy was killed in Brownsville, Brooklyn, the police released a photo on Monday of a 17-year-old who they say they believe is the gunman. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Photo Released for Suspect in 13-Year-Old’s Killing', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brownsville (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T22:55:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a017f12d-7668-51bf-b7cc-cc471ed7039d', 'word_count': 152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a017f12d-7668-51bf-b7cc-cc471ed7039d'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Butcher oversaw the bank’s international expansion and its merger with Chemical Banking after succeeding David Rockefeller Sr. as chairman.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/08/27/willard-c-butcher-former-chief-of-chase-manhattan-dies-at-85/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Butcher oversaw the bank’s international expansion and its merger with Chemical Banking after succeeding David Rockefeller Sr. as chairman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Willard C. Butcher, the former chairman and chief executive of the Chase Manhattan Corporation, who led the bank during a period of international growth in the 1980s, died on Saturday at his home in Hobe Sound, Fla. He was 85.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Willard C. Butcher, Former Chief of Chase Manhattan, Dies at 85', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Willard Butcher, 85; Led Chase Manhattan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Butcher, Willard Carlisle', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'JPMorgan Chase & Company', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-27T23:03:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Lattman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1754c5a8-2fae-578f-9d01-d0affeb06526', 'word_count': 591, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1754c5a8-2fae-578f-9d01-d0affeb06526'}, {'abstract': 'Nine American service members were disciplined on Monday for the burning of Korans and the posting of a video showing Marines urinating on dead insurgents, events that prompted outrage in Afghanistan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/28/world/asia/us-military-disciplines-9-for-koran-burning-and-incendiary-video.html', 'snippet': 'Nine American service members were disciplined on Monday for the burning of Korans and the posting of a video showing Marines urinating on dead insurgents, events that prompted outrage in Afghanistan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — American military authorities disciplined nine service members on Monday for two episodes this year — the burning of Korans and the posting of a video showing Marines urinating on dead insurgents — that prompted nationwide outrage in Afghanistan. The punishments did not include criminal charges or jail time, and they fell short of what President Hamid Karzai of Afghanistan had demanded.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. Military Disciplines 9 Over Video and Koran Burning', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.S. Military Disciplines 9 Over Video and Koran Burning', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Marine Corps', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Koran', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afgh