b_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/28/us/texas-appeals-court-declines-to-rule-on-forced-shaving-of-defendant.html', 'snippet': 'The highest military appeals court ruled on Monday that it was premature for them to decide whether Maj. Nidal Malik Hasan could have his beard forcibly shaved before the start of his trial.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The highest military appeals court ruled on Monday that it was premature for them to decide whether Maj. Nidal Malik Hasan, the Army psychiatrist accused in a shooting rampage at an Army base in Texas in 2009, could have his beard forcibly shaved before the start of his trial. Major Hasan told his lawyers that he began growing a beard because of his Muslim faith. But the judge overseeing his pretrial hearings at Fort Hood said that Major Hasan was disrupting the proceedings and violating Army regulations, and that he would be forcibly shaved if he did not do so voluntarily. Major Hasan’s lawyers appealed, but on Monday the United States Court of Appeals for the Armed Forces said that since the judge had not issued a definitive order for a forcible shaving, it was too early for them to decide. The court said that if such an order were issued, Major Hasan could appeal to a lower Army appeals court. But the court appeared to agree with Major Hasan’s lawyers on elements of the case, asking the judge to address, if an order for shaving was given, why a forcible shaving was the “least restrictive means” of intruding on his religious liberties. With the court’s stay on the proceedings lifted, neither a hearing date nor a trial date had yet been scheduled.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Texas: Appeals Court Declines to Rule on Forced Shaving of Defendant', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Southwest', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Texas: Appeals Court Declines To Rule On Forced Shaving of Defendant', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 