, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-28T12:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Times Topics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip B. Corbett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9986403-3139-5fc8-accb-02066c20fb5d', 'word_count': 1342, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9986403-3139-5fc8-accb-02066c20fb5d'}, {'abstract': 'John Hillcoat’s “Lawless,” with Shia LaBeouf, is set in Virginia’s moonshine country during the Prohibition era.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/29/movies/lawless-with-shia-labeouf-a-film-by-john-hillcoat.html', 'snippet': 'John Hillcoat’s “Lawless,” with Shia LaBeouf, is set in Virginia’s moonshine country during the Prohibition era.', 'lead_paragraph': 'John Hillcoat’s new movie, “Lawless” — written by Nick Cave — is based on a true-story novel called “The Wettest County in the World,” by Matt Bondurant. The area in question is Franklin County, Va., and in 1931, when most of the action in the film takes place, it was a bloody paradise of bootleggers. This picturesque corner of Appalachia has now provided a bonanza for dialect coaches and their charges, who set the hills alive with a symphony of dropped consonants and attenuated vowels almost as violent as the gunfire that periodically erupts. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/27/movies/lawless-span/lawless-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/27/movies/lawless-span/lawless-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None,