club capital of America (there are 50).', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/28/strip-club-workers-can-tell-youre-from-the-convention-arent-you/', 'snippet': 'The host city of the Republican National Convention is the strip club capital of America (there are 50).', 'lead_paragraph': 'TAMPA, Fla. — “You’re from the convention, aren’t you?” asks Rico, the security guard stationed outside of one of Tampa’s hottest strip clubs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Strip Club Workers Can Tell, ‘You’re From the Convention, Aren’t You?’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Republican National Convention', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tampa (Fla)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-28T16:04:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Barbaro and Ashley Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barbaro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f933f0b5-58a2-5b5c-8cc9-011a73db03d2', 'word_count': 296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f933f0b5-58a2-5b5c-8cc9-011a73db03d2'}, {'abstract': 'Seeking out paintings at yard sales and thrift shops, an art critic observes, is often worth the effort.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/29/arts/design/flea-market-paintings-as-art-discoveries.html', 'snippet': 'Seeking out paintings at yard sales and thrift shops, an art critic observes, is often worth the effort.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like busmen, critics often have job-related holidays, leisure time activities that feed into, spill over from — and, with luck, provide respite from — more diligent pursuits in their supposed areas of expertise. One of mine comes remarkably close. In summer I like to browse yard sales, thrift shops and, lately, flea markets, looking for paintings that speak to me. If the price is right (it usually is), I buy them and take them home.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/29/arts/LIFE/LIFE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/29/arts/LIFE/LIFE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/29/arts/LIFE/LIFE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/29/arts/LIFE/LIFE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1369, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/29/arts/LIFE/LIFE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/29/arts/LIFE/LIFE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/29/arts/LIFE/LIFE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Finding Something Worthy in Every Find', 'kicker': 'Still Life', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Finding Something Worthy in Every Find', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flea Markets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Thrift Shops', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-28T16:08:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c6afaeb8-5707-5329-9b85-a88b43e31d58', 'word_count': 1258, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c6afaeb8-5707-5329-9b85-a88b43e31d58'}, {'abstract': 'The deal highlights the growing business of internal investigations into possible wrongdoing at corporations and other institutions, an increasingly lucrative area for law firms.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/08/28/pepper-hamilton-to-acquire-louis-freehs-law-firm-and-investigative-group/', 'snippet': 'The deal highlights the growing business of internal investigations into possible wrongdoing at corporations and other institutions, an increasingly lucrative area for law firms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pepper Hamilton, the corporate law firm, has acquired the investigative group and legal practice run by Louis J. Freeh, the former director of the Federal Bureau of Investigation.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pepper Hamilton to Acquire Louis Freeh’s Firms', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pepper Hamilton to Acquire Louis Freeh’s Firms', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Freeh, Louis J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-28T16:10:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Lattman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6d1e67a-012a-5add-ad0d-47319bfbc354', 'word_count': 622, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6d1e67a-012a-5add-ad0d-47319bfbc354'}, {'abstract': 'Or do his gains within the G.O.P. reflect partisan incentives rather than principled shifts?', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/28/is-ron-paul-winning-the-future/', 'snippet': 'Or do his gains within the G.O.P. reflect partisan incentives rather than principled shifts?', 'lead_paragraph': 'With no actual convention to cover here in Tampa these last two days, the press has gravitated toward what you might call para-convention events: Newt Gingrich’s week-long “Newt University” tutorial in conservative ideas, for instance, and Ron Paul’s “We Are the Future” rally for his army of supporters, held Sunday at the University of South Florida. About Gingrich, at this point, there is little of interest to be said, but the boasting title of Paul’s rally has at least some claim to be the truth, given how well the libertarian congressman performed with younger voters in the primary season. Writing for the American Conservative, Jim Antle makes a plausible case that Paul has moved the Republican Party in his direction, and might move it further still: From the party-wide enthusiasm for critiques of the Federal Reserve to the reappearance of “bona-fide noninterventionists” in Republican House contests to the emergence of Kentucky Senator Rand Paul as a more mainstream advocate for his father’s ideas, Antle writes, “the rapid progress” of Paulism “in a short period of time is undeniable.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is Ron Paul Winning the Future?', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-28T16:26:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/915be143-3434-52c2-be16-a40d666924bb', 'word_count': 405, 'uri': 'nyt://article/915be143-3434-52c2-be16-a40d666924bb'}, {'abstract': 'How junk mail could put the Postal Service back on track. | Apple sets the stage for a war with Google. | Solving the mystery of a bourbon-loving fungus.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/business/100000001745784/business-day-live-august-28-2012.html', 'snippet': 'How junk mail could put the Postal Service back on track. | Apple sets the stage for a war with Google. | Solving the mystery of a bourbon-loving fungus.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How junk mail could put the Postal Service back on track. | Apple sets the stage for a war with Google. | Solving the mystery of a bourbon-loving fungus.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/28/business/video-bdl-120828/video-bdl-120828-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/28/business/video-bdl-120828/video-bdl-120828-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'ur