William K. Rashbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Danny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hakim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bb03e7e1-9495-593b-a82d-ceef19e53fb0', 'word_count': 1447, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bb03e7e1-9495-593b-a82d-ceef19e53fb0'}, {'abstract': 'The brand’s sharp new black shoe with gothic ruthenium spikes is a must-have for fall.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/28/the-get-valentinos-noir-capsule-flats/', 'snippet': 'The brand’s sharp new black shoe with gothic ruthenium spikes is a must-have for fall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The designers Maria Grazia Chiuri and Pierpaolo Piccioli have done it again, this time by giving Valentino’s beloved studded accessories a tough new look for their Noir Capsule, which stars materials like python, crocodile and patent leather in all-black with gothic ruthenium spikes (instead of the usual rocker-y silver ones). A favorite from the group and a must-have for fall is this strappy patent-leather flat ($895). The black on black is such a striking contrast to the pretty-lady shape. Lookin’ sharp!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Get | Valentino’s Noir Capsule Flats', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-28T18:30:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rae Boxer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rae', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Boxer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d59efc51-0ffb-5da6-8164-dabb5f484ca4', 'word_count': 84, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d59efc51-0ffb-5da6-8164-dabb5f484ca4'}, {'abstract': 'Kyle Wittels pulls together various artisanal vendors for a market in TriBeCa.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/29/dining/food-court-for-high-end-provisions-to-open-in-tribeca.html', 'snippet': 'Kyle Wittels pulls together various artisanal vendors for a market in TriBeCa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When he set out to open a market, Kyle Wittels, an interior designer, knew his limitations. “I wasn’t a retailer,” he said. “I knew I had to get professionals.” So that’s just what he did. All Good Things is a no-frills, floor-through affair in the heart of TriBeCa with counters for different artisanal vendors, like a food court for provisioning. He has lured Orwasher’s for bread, Blue Marble Ice Cream and Nunu Chocolates from Brooklyn, Dickson’s Farmstand Meats from Chelsea Market, Blue Bottle Coffee, the florist Polux Fleuriste and, from Sag Harbor, Cavaniola’s cheeses, condiments and cured meats. The chef Ryan Tate, formerly at Savoy, will run the produce and seafood stands. Mr. Tate will also be the chef for the intimate downstairs restaurant, Le Restaurant, to open in mid-October with a $70 to $80 prix fixe menu with no choices that will change every day. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/29/dining/29STUFF_FOODCOURTSPAN/29STUFF_FOODCOURTSPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/29/dining/29STUFF_FOODCOURTSPAN/29STUFF_FOODCOURTSPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/29/dining/29STUFF_FOODCOURTSPAN/29STUFF3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/29/dining/29STUFF_FOODCOURTSPAN/29STUFF3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/29/dining/29STUFF_FOODCOURTSPAN/29STUFF_FOODCOURTSPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/29/dining/29STUFF_FOODCOURTSPAN/29STUFF_FOODCOURTSPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Artisanal Food Court in TriBeCa', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Artisanal Food Court in TriBeCa', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'All Good Things (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wittels, Kyle', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'TriBeCa (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-28T18:37:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/095685ba-828e-5357-94bf-e28521e24fe9', 'word_count': 169, 'uri': 'nyt://article/095685ba-828e-5357-94bf-e28521e24fe9'}, {'abstract': 'Isaac’s rain could help blunt this year’s epic drought, but too late for many crops.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/28/hurricane-isaac-is-a-rainmaker-for-a-parched-heartland/', 'snippet': 'Isaac’s rain could help blunt this year’s epic drought, but too late for many crops.', 'lead_paragraph': '[Sept. 2, 4:32 p.m. | Update | Heavy rains from the storm formerly known as Isaac have indeed been welcomed in a broad stretch of the drought-stricken Midwest, Jack Healy reports in The Times.]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hurricane Isaac is a Rainmaker for a Parched Heartland', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drought', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricanes and Tropical Storms', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Weather Service', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Louisiana', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-28T18:39:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9ac3e85-77cd-59f6-8381-47625b0f2e6b', 'word_count': 80, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9ac3e85-77cd-59f6-8381-47625b0f2e6b'}, {'abstract': 'The company’s two offerings are called Bittersweet Vermouth and Petal and Thorn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/29/dining/imbue-of-oregon-offers-specialized-aperitifs.html', 'snippet': 'The company’s two offerings are called Bittersweet Vermouth and Petal and Thorn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The business of making spirits in America is constantly finding new niches. The latest are aperitif concoctions, like vermouth, that are inspired by venerable European models. Imbue, a fledgling Oregon company owned by three entrepreneurs, all of whom have worked in wineries, breweries or restaurants, has introduced a vermouth it rightly calls bittersweet. Instead of the austere herbaceousness of a classic French dry vermouth, it has a mellower flavor, without being sweet. Though the company says it was designed to go with new American gins, it seems better suited for what is called a perfect manhattan, which is made with half sweet, half dry vermouth. It’s also elegant on its own, over ice. An aperitif wine called Petal and Thorn is pale rose, fragrant with orange blossom and mimosa up front, but grips with a bitter finish. Serve it on the rocks as you might Lillet. Both products are made from Oregon pinot gris wines that are distilled and blended with eau de vie and botanicals.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/29/dining/29STUFF_SPIRITS/29STUFF1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 469, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/29/dining/29STUFF_SPIRITS/29STUFF1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 937, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/29/dining/29STUFF_SPIRITS/29STUFF_SPIRITS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/29/dining/29STUFF_SPIRITS/29STUFF_SPIRITS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Aperitifs From Oregon Vineyards', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Aperitifs From Oregon Vineyards', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [