News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3e35448-9c22-51c4-a2a9-dd884aa4f8b4', 'word_count': 876, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3e35448-9c22-51c4-a2a9-dd884aa4f8b4'}, {'abstract': 'Pro-choice Republicans are struggling for inclusion.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/28/whats-left-of-the-big-tent/', 'snippet': 'Pro-choice Republicans are struggling for inclusion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TAMPA, Fla.– The word “abortion” first appeared in a Republican platform in 1976, when the Party hadn’t yet made up its mind on reproductive freedom. The platform acknowledged that “the question of abortion is one of the most difficult and controversial of our time.” The 1976 G.O.P. favored “a continuance of the public dialogue on abortion,” which it called a “moral and personal issue,” while expressing support for “those who seek enactment of a constitutional amendment to restore protection of the right to life for unborn children.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s Left of the Big Tent', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ABORTION', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Republican National Convention', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Straight, Candace', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-28T21:10:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Juliet Lapidos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Juliet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lapidos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/20e01399-1795-516b-a56e-1ff0844dcf41', 'word_count': 489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20e01399-1795-516b-a56e-1ff0844dcf41'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Smith is the chief executive of Prudential Real Estate Investors, which 