es on Tuesday that it would lay off 14 percent of them, or about 160 people over the next seven months, starting with 80 right away. Instead of trying to keep his e-mail to the staff a secret, though, Rob Glaser, the founder of RealNetworks and its interim chief executive, cut and pasted it onto his Facebook page.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/28/technology/28bits-glaser-pic/28bits-glaser-pic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 833, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/28/technology/28bits-glaser-pic/28bits-glaser-pic-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1666, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Chief Executives Share Company News on Facebook', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executives and Management (Theory)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Glaser, Rob', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hastings, Reed', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Netflix Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'RealNetworks Inc', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-29T00:36:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Wingfield', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wingfield', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/478ad18a-96ff-5e5d-84d8-b5f00aadedac', 'word_count': 382, 'uri': 'nyt://article/478ad18a-96ff-5e5d-84d8-b5f00aadedac'}, {'abstract': 'Two Chinese-American brothers’ odyssey to retrieve their parents’ bodies and gain compensation for a deadly high-speed railroad crash highlighted the workings of an unbending government agency in China.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/29/world/asia/in-china-sons-fight-railways-ministry-over-crash.html', 'snippet': 'Two Chinese-American brothers’ odyssey to retrieve their parents’ bodies and gain compensation for a deadly high-speed railroad crash highlighted the workings of an unbending government agency in China.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SHANGHAI — Henry Cao has stark memories of the moment the high-speed train he was riding rear-ended another last summer in the eastern city of Wenzhou: the pleasantly hypnotic rocking that gave way to a jolt he likened to an earthquake, followed by blackness and the sensation of falling as the car plummeted 100 feet off a viaduct. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/29/world/TRAIN-1/TRAIN-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 345, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/29/world/TRAIN-1/TRAIN-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 345}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/29/world/TRAIN-1/TRAIN-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/29/world/TRAIN-1/TRAIN-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0