, 'lastname': 'Robertson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Kim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Severson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8b8595ba-2d38-574f-90b6-36234a5c0856', 'word_count': 1264, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8b8595ba-2d38-574f-90b6-36234a5c0856'}, {'abstract': 'The event designer for the Venice Film Festival shares his favorite places with T.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/29/the-insider-matteo-corvino/', 'snippet': 'The event designer for the Venice Film Festival shares his favorite places with T.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Behind the scenes, the event designer Matteo Corvino sees to it that Venice’s high-society parties like the Venice Biennales and the annual Film Festival, which starts today, are tricked out with the most lavish decorations, props, cutlery, crystal and flowers within his reach. And his reach is vast. He is the man that Chanel calls to build runway designs for their cruise collection shows on Venice’s Lido. Private dinner parties for Jeff Koons fall under his order, and the biggest galas in town, like those at La Fenice Opera House or Save Venice Inc., seem all the better because of his creative flair. For this year’s Film Festival, Corvino is hosting a party for the V-A-C Foundation (guests will include François Pinault, the designer Pierre Cardin and Francesco Bonami, the head curator of the Whitney Biennial). Here the Venetian insider shares his favorite places with T.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Insider | Matteo Corvino', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Harry's Bar", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Venice (Italy)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-29T11:56:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rocky Casale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rocky', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Casale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f048a69e-eade-50c1-bc16-0cedd2138791', 'word_count': 949, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f048a69e-eade-50c1-bc16-0cedd2138791'}, {'abstract': 'The documentarian Errol Morris has written a book about Jeffrey MacDonald, in prison for the 1970 murder of his wife and children.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/books/errol-morris-takes-on-macdonald-murder-case.html', 'snippet': 'The documentarian Errol Morris has written a book about Jeffrey MacDonald, in prison for the 1970 murder of his wife and children.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DID Jeffrey MacDonald, an Army doctor and Green Beret stationed at Fort Bragg, stab and bludgeon his family to death early on the morning of Feb. 17, 1970?', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/arts/02MORRIS2/02MORRIS2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 395, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/02/arts/02MORRIS2/02MORRIS2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 395}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/arts/02MORRIS2/02MORRIS2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 780, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/arts/02MORRIS