{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Myers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7204df9-3049-5376-a429-2ee4abfb07a1', 'word_count': 1162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7204df9-3049-5376-a429-2ee4abfb07a1'}, {'abstract': 'The Department of Homeland security suspended immigration enforcement in the Hurricane Isaac disaster area.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/29/the-hurricane-and-the-undocumented/', 'snippet': 'The Department of Homeland security suspended immigration enforcement in the Hurricane Isaac disaster area.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Hurricane Isaac continues its agonizingly slow crawl over southeast Louisiana, it is exposing one of the regrettable consequences of a broken immigration system – a shadow population of the undocumented who can’t or won’t seek emergency help. The authorities and immigrant advocates have learned from prior storms that fear can have a crippling effect on people’s willingness to flee a disaster zone. That is why they have urged the Department of Homeland Security not just to suspend its immigration enforcement during this emergency, but to make that policy highly public and official, so that families will not hesitate to enter public shelters or to just get in the car and go.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Hurricane and the Undocumented', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Isaac (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Illegal Immigrants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-29T21:35:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lawrence Downes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lawrence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Downes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b936fb0e-a9c6-5275-a1b3-1666479378c7', 'word_count': 448, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b936fb0e-a9c6-5275-a1b3-1666479378c7'}, {'abstract': 'The strange trashing of a not-great but pretty good speech.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/29/on-chris-christies-flop/', 'snippet': 'The strange trashing of a not-great but pretty good speech.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I wrote my Campaign Stops column\xa0today about Ann Romney’s convention speech, but I should say something about Chris Christie’s address, given how quickly it’s been branded as a solipsistic flop. There is, of course, no real way to disprove the conventional wisdom in a case like this: Nobody (to my knowledge)\xa0conducts insta-polls of the TV audience’s reaction to a keynote speech, so if the chattering and political classes agree that Christie laid an egg, then that’s how his remarks will be remembered. But it’s worth noting that the indictment against the New Jersey governor is … distinctly underwhelming. He stands accused of talking too much about himself and not enough about Mitt Romney (or maybe not enough about Barack Obama, depending on which critique you read), and generally of failing to deliver the kind of tour de force that reporters are accustomed to seeing in his YouTube clips. But as even the big Politico piece calling the speech a “flop” allows, the most famous keynotes\xa0— Mario Cuomo in 1984 and Barack Obama in 2004\xa0— were less about touting the nominee than laying out a broader vision of what the party stood for, and that was pretty clearly what Christie was trying to do as well. And I don’t think he did it all that badly. He made conservatism seem attractive, caricatured liberalism effectively, recast Romney in his own truthtelling image without mentioning of the details that might complicate that recasting, and generally came across as what he is: A tough-talking, charismatic politician, and an effective spokesman for the G.O.P.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Chris Christie’s “Flop”', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-29T21:35:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3be90057-e949-5cfe-8dd2-28d3e0a160b8', 'word_count': 678, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3be90057-e949-5cfe-8dd2-28d3e0a160b8'}, {'abstract': 'Flim-flam indeed.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/29/the-snooker-factor/', 'snippet': 'Flim-flam indeed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kudos to William Saletan for admitting that Paul Ryan snookered him:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Snooker Factor', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-29T21:36:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f81f1e0-9c56-506b-a88b-cd689f05188d', 'word_count': 105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f81f1e0-9c56-506b-a88b-cd689f05188d'}, {'abstract': 'After Wednesday night’s game at D.C. United, the Red Bulls are idle in Major League Soccer until facing Columbus on Sept. 15. That respit