'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Lasky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lasky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/ebc4fa51-97db-5aaa-9d63-07c13f98300a', 'word_count': 864, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ebc4fa51-97db-5aaa-9d63-07c13f98300a'}, {'abstract': 'The Chinese decorative art shop has moved into the 1st Dibs space at the New York Design Center.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/30/garden/pagoda-red-comes-to-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'The Chinese decorative art shop has moved into the 1st Dibs space at the New York Design Center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After 15 years in Chicago, Pagoda Red has opened a showroom on the 1st Dibs floor of the New York Design Center, adding Asian flair to the predominantly midcentury modernist mix. “People who aren’t familiar with Chinese furniture think of highly ornate lacquered pieces, but those were for export,” said the owner, Betsy Nathan, of her offerings, 19th-century Chinese furniture and decorative arts as well as new work by contemporary Chinese designers. “The pieces the Chinese designed and built for themselves are very simple, more reserved and more along the Ming principles.”', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/30/garden/30OPEN1/30CURR1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/30/garden/30OPEN1/30CURR1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1536, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/30/garde