746068/the-whiskey-fungus-mystery.html', 'snippet': 'Melena Ryzik talks to residents around Louisville, Ky., who filed a lawsuit to stem the growth of a black mold that was recently linked to whiskey production.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Melena Ryzik talks to residents around Louisville, Ky., who filed a lawsuit to stem the growth of a black mold that was recently linked to whiskey production.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/28/us/video-whiskeyfungus/video-whiskeyfungus-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/08/28/us/video-whiskeyfungus/video-whiskeyfungus-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/28/us/video-whiskeyfungus/video-whiskeyfungus-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/28/us/video-whiskeyfungus/video-whiskeyfungus-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fighting the Whiskey Fungus', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALCOHOLIC BEVERAGES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kentucky', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mold', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Air Pollution', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T07:04:47+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'Nadia Sussman, Channon Hodge and Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nadia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sussman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Channon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodge', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/de85256a-7e56-592a-ae19-7c3075cf8717', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/de85256a-7e56-592a-ae19-7c3075cf8717'}, {'abstract': 'The conventional Onam celebrations, which revolved around the legend of King Mahabali, have given way to high commercialization over the years.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/30/in-kerala-feasting-splurging-and-mollywood-usher-in-onam/', 'snippet': 'The conventional Onam celebrations, which revolved around the legend of King Mahabali, have given way to high commercialization over the years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Visiting Kerala this week is like visiting New York during Christmas week, except for the scale. Both places have the festive atmosphere, illumination, feasting everywhere, high alcoholic consumption and crass commercialization, including a grand shopping festival. None of these have anything to do with the traditional Onam festival, but care is taken to do all these in the name of Mahabali, the legendary ruler of prehistoric times. His majestic and well-fed figure juts out of every hoarding like Santa Claus in the West.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Kerala, Feasting, Splurging and Mollywood Usher in Onam', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kerala (India)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T07:21:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By T.P. Sreenivasan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'T.', 'middlename': 'P.', 'lastname': 'Sreenivasan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/99e595a3-3b7a-5768-ac49-6843b94f24eb', 'word_count': 921, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99e595a3-3b7a-5768-ac49-6843b94f24eb'}, {'abstract': 'Among the surveys that came out on Wednesday, one in Ohio had encouraging news for Barack Obama, and another, in Nevada, was slightly favorable for Mitt Romney.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/30/aug-29-so-much-depends-upon-ohio/', 'snippet': 'Among the surveys that came out on Wednesday, one in Ohio had encouraging news for Barack Obama, and another, in Nevada, was slightly favorable for Mitt Romney.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Polls that are published during the party conventions can be anticlimactic, representing old news since they won’t fully reflect the effects of the convention bounce. And none of the surveys that came out on Wednesday were that newsworthy to begin with – although there was one, in Ohio, that had encouraging news for Barack Obama, and another, in Nevada, that was slightly favorable for Mitt Romney.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Aug. 29: So Much Depends Upon Ohio', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ohio', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T07:54:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/74cc6fc2-c89f-5704-b450-f55a2c7a9626', 'word_count': 699, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74cc6fc2-c89f-5704-b450-f55a2c7a9626'}, {'abstract': 'Can you choose the correct spelling of the word that completes the phrase that means “total freedom”?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/30/test-yourself-english-aug-30-2012/', 'snippet': 'Can you choose the correct spelling of the word that completes the phrase that means “total freedom”?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The question was written by Shannon Doyne. It comes from the article “A Very Long Journey Was Very Swift.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | English, Aug. 30, 2012', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T08:26:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb26b19d-0fcf-5453-ba07-843bd40ff4ad', 'word_count': 47, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb26b19d-0fcf-5453-ba07-843bd40ff4ad'}, {'abstract': 'Across the political spectrum, more voices are calling for limits on banks’ size, and no persuasive evidence demonstrates that this would be a bad thing, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/30/why-are-the-big-banks-suddenly-afraid/', 'snippet': 'Across the political spectrum, more voices are calling for limits on banks’ size, and no persuasive evidence demonstrates that this would be a bad thing, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Simon Johnson is the Ronald A. Kurtz Professor of Entrepreneurship at the M.I.T. Sloan School of Management and co-author of “White House Burning: The Founding Fathers, Our National Debt, and Why It Matters to You.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Are the Big Banks Suddenly Afraid?', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Sherrod', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Financial Services Roundtable', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T09:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Simon Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Simon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/037e964a-18aa-5986-aeb5-d57299ac2517', 'word_count': 1197, 'uri': 'nyt://article/037e964a-18aa-5986-aeb5-d57299ac2517'}, {'abstract': 'Marc McAndrews spent five years photographing the legal brothels of 11 Nevada counties, finding less in the way of salaciousness and more in the way of capitalism, commerce and community.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/30/a-fresh-take-on-the-oldest-profession/', 'snippet': 'Marc McAndrews spent five years photographing the legal brothels of 11 Nevada counties, finding less in the way of salaciousness and more in the way of capitalism, commerce and community.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With sweaty palms, a heavy step and a stutter, Marc McAndrews entered a brothel in Elko, Nev., without much of a pitch or a plan. Facing the line of half-naked sex workers, he had to choose one and pose a question that made him blush.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Fresh Take on the Oldest Profession', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Phot