dia', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'Channon Hodge and Stephanie Rosenbloom', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Channon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodge', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Stephanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenbloom', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/eaef9299-d9a0-5631-8370-0754a4413937', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/eaef9299-d9a0-5631-8370-0754a4413937'}, {'abstract': 'From the French electronics company Archos comes the 101XS, a 10.1-inch tablet that weighs 21 ounces and is 0.31 inches thick. The tablet’s innovative feature is the Coverboard, an integrated keyboard that doubles as a cover.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/30/a-tablet-that-moves-closer-to-becoming-a-laptop/', 'snippet': 'From the French electronics company Archos comes the 101XS, a 10.1-inch tablet that weighs 21 ounces and is 0.31 inches thick. The tablet’s innovative feature is the Coverboard, an integrated keyboard that doubles as a cover.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The French electronics company Archos is hoping to bolster its presence in the United States with a new line of Android tablets that include an integrated keyboard. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Tablet That Moves Closer to Becoming a Laptop', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Android (Operating System)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tablet Computers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ARCHOS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T17:58:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Schmidt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schmidt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d4b3eba-f741-5697-9304-bf2206529b72', 'word_count': 332, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d4b3eba-f741-5697-9304-bf2206529b72'}, {'abstract': 'If your singing in the shower requires accompaniment, you can use the iShower wireless speaker to bring your Bluetooth music player along.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/30/a-music-player-for-soapy-singers/', 'snippet': 'If your singing in the shower requires accompaniment, you can use the iShower wireless speaker to bring your Bluetooth music player along.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If your singing in the shower requires accompaniment, you can use the iShower wireless speaker to bring music from your Bluetooth-equipped phone or music player along.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Music Player for Soapy Singers', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T17:59:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roy Furchgott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Furchgott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c492a56-47b9-5050-806f-41ca6523fb34', 'word_count': 267, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c492a56-47b9-5050-806f-41ca6523fb34'}, {'abstract': 'Notes on Paul Ryan’s convention speech.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/30/the-ryan-record-and-the-ryan-speech/', 'snippet': 'Notes on Paul Ryan’s convention speech.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two weeks ago, I wrote a trio of posts analyzing Paul Ryan’s record as a policy entrepreneur in the Obama era. My overarching argument was that he deserves substantial credit for pushing his party toward substance and away from the pathologies of the post-Bush moment; that he deserves credit, as well, for putting forward his own ideas and proposals on issues where he hadn’t yet brought the entire party on board; and that he deserves particular credit for his willingness to offer up multiple iterations of his signature entitlement reform idea, tweaking and rewriting and partnering with Democrats in an effort to make his proposals more plausible and marketable.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Ryan Record and the Ryan Speech', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T18:01:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7fd5564c-38ad-5982-a6d5-b50b3765efcb', 'word_count': 753, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7fd5564c-38ad-5982-a6d5-b50b3765efcb'}, {'abstract': 'Video proof that she’s hilarious.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/30/a-tig-notaro-primer/', 'snippet': 'Video proof that she’s hilarious.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this week’s Talk, the comedian Tig Notaro recounts\xa0how she lost her mother, was given a diagnosis of breast cancer and broke up with her girlfriend in quick succession — and how she turned the worst four months of her life into material for a show at the Largo in Los Angeles last month.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Tig Notaro Primer', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Notaro, Tig', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T18:04:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jillian Dunham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jillian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dunham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/17a379ec-9430-5ebb-9c59-e6842ba8ab68', 'word_count': 153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17a379ec-9430-5ebb-9c59-e6842ba8ab68'}, {'abstract': 'This capital city, marbled by a complex past, is looking to the future, targeting the title of European Capital of Culture 2019.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/travel/36-hours-in-sofia-bulgaria.html', 'snippet': 'This capital city, marbled by a complex past, is looking to the future, targeting the title of European Capital of Culture 2019.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FIRST came the Thracians, about 2,700 years ago, followed by the Romans, the Byzantine Empire, the Ottoman Turks and then the Communists. These days — more than two decades after the fall of the Iron Curtain, and five years after joining the European Union — the Bulgarian capital is finally coming into its own. In place of the old Lenin monument, a statue of Sofia’s patron saint now stands tall — a dark princess that somehow embodies the city’s East-meets-West, old-meets-new allure. Under her gaze, a youthful, creative energy displays itself as Sofia angles for the title of European Capital of Culture 2019.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/travel/02HOURS_SPAN/02HOURS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/02/travel/02HOURS_SPAN/02HOURS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/travel/02HOURS_SPAN/02HOURS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 687, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/travel/02HOURS_SPAN/02HOURS1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1374, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/travel/02HOURS_SPAN/02HOURS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/02/travel/02HOURS_SPAN/02HOURS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '36 Hours in Sofia, Bulgaria', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '36 Hours: Sofia, Bulgaria', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bulgaria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sofia (Bulgaria)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T18:09:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Ajudua', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ajudua', 'qualif