rd_count': 791, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c0adf42a-2335-575a-b804-5d3aba970fbe'}, {'abstract': 'Welcome to the Obama campaign’s mobile war room, a 30-person pop-up shop in a storefront one block beyond the security perimeter of convention.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/30/in-tampa-the-obama-campaign-has-its-own-war-room/', 'snippet': 'Welcome to the Obama campaign’s mobile war room, a 30-person pop-up shop in a storefront one block beyond the security perimeter of convention.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TAMPA, Fla. — The awning over the door says “American Institute of Architects.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Tampa, the Obama Campaign Has Its Own War Room', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T18:34:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Trip Gabriel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Trip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gabriel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8068819b-9073-5520-a4e4-ac079bb21584', 'word_count': 654, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8068819b-9073-5520-a4e4-ac079bb21584'}, {'abstract': 'Early apples are even earlier this year, but less red and crunchy.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/30/apples-are-earlier-but-not-as-red-or-crunchy/', 'snippet': 'Early apples are even earlier this year, but less red and crunchy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Apples are in the Greenmarket earlier, thanks to the longer, warmer summer. But the fruit is likely to have less crunch and skins colored rose, peach or lemon, rather than red. The heat affects color and texture, but not flavor.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Apples Are Earlier, But Not as Red or Crunchy', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T18:37:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cathy Barrow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cathy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barrow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e22ee750-5dd2-539f-87b7-127195f41c5d', 'word_count': 311, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e22ee750-5dd2-539f-87b7-127195f41c5d'}, {'abstract': 'But the gay advocacy group also labeled one broadcaster, CBS, as “failing” and called for more ethnic and cultural diversity in the portrayals of gay characters.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/30/glaad-gives-networks-passing-grade-for-tv-portrayals-of-gays/', 'snippet': 'But the gay advocacy group also labeled one broadcaster, CBS, as “failing” and called for more ethnic and cultural diversity in the portrayals of gay characters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most of the broadcast networks are satisfactorily portraying gays and lesbians on television, a leading media advocacy group said in a report released on Thursday.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'GLAAD Gives Networks Passing Grade for TV Portrayals of Gays', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Glaad Grades Networks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gay and Lesbian Alliance Against Defamation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T18:46:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/be31573e-6dda-54bd-b950-a8bfcac20092', 'word_count': 256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be31573e-6dda-54bd-b950-a8bfcac20092'}, {'abstract': 'Aa Amazon prepares to freshen its line of tablet devices next week, it revealed that its original Kindle Fire seemed to have done less impressively than many were assuming. Next week’s upgrade will face heightened competition from Google and, of course, Apple.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/30/amazon-hints-about-fires-sales-success/', 'snippet': 'Aa Amazon prepares to freshen its line of tablet devices next week, it revealed that its original Kindle Fire seemed to have done less impressively than many were assuming. Next week’s upgrade will face heightened competition from Google and, of c...', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the eve of its introduction of new tablets next Thursday, Amazon is putting out of print the original Kindle Fire it introduced a year ago.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/09/blogs/bitpix-amazon-kindel-fire/bitpix-amazon-kindel-fire-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 771, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/12/09/blogs/bitpix-amazon-kindel-fire/bitpix-amazon-kindel-fire-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/12/09/blogs/bitpix-amazon-kindel-fire/bitpix-amazon-kindel-fire-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Amazon Hints About Fire’s Sales Success', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Amazon Kindle', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Books and Readers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tablet Computers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMAZON.COM INC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T18:53:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Streitfeld', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Streitfeld', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/412cea42-b0e4-5062-bbf9-79ad1921dbe0', 'word_count': 328, 'uri': 'nyt://article/412cea42-b0e4-5062-bbf9-79ad1921dbe0'}, {'abstract': 'We want to see you at your most adorable — or, better, most awkward. We’ll start.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/30/send-us-your-first-day-of-school-photos/', 'snippet': 'We want to see you at your most adorable — or, better, most awkward. We’ll start.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is something amazing and painful about revisiting your first-day-of-school photos. It’s not just the parade of bad clothes and worse hairstyles. It’s the fact that those clothes and hairstyles were chosen so carefully, so deliberately, in an effort to make you look your absolute best for your first new-year impression. Like the engagement ring flashed ostentatiously at a college reunion, those loafers or baby-doll dresses or No Fear shirts were your way to signal to your classmates how far you’d come since they saw you last and how this year would be different. To see that striving and posturing laid bare with the curse of hindsight can be a trying exercise in humility.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Send Us Your First-Day-of-School Photos', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T18:59:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Samantha Henig', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Samantha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Henig', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/84722b1d-f0b1-5c08-a506-73d7a3982fdf', 'word_count': 361, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84722b1d-f0b1-5c08-a506-73d7a3982fdf'}, {'abstract': 'Iran has installed most of the centrifuges it needs to complete an underground site for the production of nuclear fuel, a report by the International Atomic Energy Agency said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/31/world/middleeast/nuclear-inspectors-confirm-iranian-progress.html', 'snippet': 'Iran has installed most of the centrifuges it needs to complete an underground site for the production of nuclear fuel, a report by the International Atomic Energy Agency said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Iran has installed three-quarters of the nuclear centrifuges it needs to complete a site deep underground for the production of nuclear fuel, international inspectors reported Thursday, a finding that led the White House to warn that “the window that is open now to resolve this diplomatically will not remain open indefinitely.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inspectors Confirm New Work by Iran at Secure Nuclear Site', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline':