agreement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The N.H.L. Players’ Association will respond on Friday to the league’s latest offer for a new collective bargaining agreement, the union said in a statement Thursday, just days ahead of a Sept. 15 lockout promised by N.H.L. Commissioner Gary Bettman if a new agreement is not in place.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'N.H.L. Union Plans Response to League Proposal', 'kicker': 'Slap Shot', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Players to Answer N.H.L.’s Offer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bettman, Gary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Hockey League', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "National Hockey League Players' Assn", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T20:56:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Z. Klein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': 'Z.', 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4e72356-886c-557b-b894-2d36cd863b0b', 'word_count': 470, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4e72356-886c-557b-b894-2d36cd863b0b'}, {'abstract': 'For the magazine’s Food & Drink issue, I’ve decided to travel to California’s San Joaquin Valley, the most important food center in the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://bittman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/30/you-sent-me-packing-to-california/', 'snippet': 'For the magazine’s Food & Drink issue, I’ve decided to travel to California’s San Joaquin Valley, the most important food center in the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thanks to all for your suggestions for where I should visit for the magazine’s Food & Drink issue. After much consideration and brain-racking, and reading through hundreds of your e-mails and comments, I’ve decided to travel to California’s San Joaquin Valley next week; I could think of no place more exotic — or at least foreign — to which I might do a little justice in the short time I have. Many of you asked that I stay in America rather than venture abroad. The valley is the most important food center in the United States (and among the most important in the world), and it gets short shrift, largely because we tend to see it as industrial, monolithic, ugly and uninteresting. Though it has elements of all of those things (except the uninteresting), it has the opposites as well. I’m hoping to take a look at both extremes as well as the middle, through people, the land, the crops and animals, and of course through cooking.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'You Sent Me Packing … to California', 'kicker': 'Mark Bittman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T20:58:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Bittman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bittman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/69f4cfe6-c63b-58fa-8c7a-489dfbb88686', 'word_count': 167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69f4cfe6-c63b-58fa-8c7a-489dfbb88686'}, {'abstract': 'Republicans charted a course of denial and obstruction from the day Mr. Obama was inaugurated.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/31/opinion/the-hidden-subject-in-tampa.html', 'snippet': 'Republicans charted a course of denial and obstruction from the day Mr. Obama was inaugurated.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mitt Romney wrapped the most important speech of his life, for Thursday night’s session of his convention, around an extraordinary reinvention of history — that his party rallied behind President Obama when he won in 2008, hoping that he would succeed. “That president was not the choice of our party,” he said. “We are a good and generous people who are united by so much more than divides us.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mr. Romney Reinvents History', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mr. Romney Reinvents History', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Republican National Convention', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T20:58:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ae05275-a6c4-5717-97a3-e9530153ddfd', 'word_count': 807, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ae05275-a6c4-5717-97a3-e9530153ddfd'}, {'abstract': 'To rid Shaoyang of its budgetary and quality-of-life problems, 1,000 neighborhood watch committee members were given the power to issue tickets for traffic violations and other minor infractions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/31/world/asia/chinese-city-gives-retirees-license-to-ticket.html', 'snippet': 'To rid Shaoyang of its budgetary and quality-of-life problems, 1,000 neighborhood watch committee members were given the power to issue tickets for traffic violations and other minor infractions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — Imagine what might happen if civilians were given the power to issue tickets for minor traffic violations and other quality-of-life infractions — and keep 80 percent of the fines. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chinese City’s Retirees Given a License to Ticket', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chinese City’s Retirees Given a License to Ticket', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Traffic and Parking Violations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T20:59:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Jacobs and Adam Century', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Century', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/946c101d-6e72-5884-a3a8-5f772836bec4', 'word_count': 607, 'uri': 'nyt://article/946c101d-6e72-5884-a3a8-5f772836bec4'}, {'abstract': 'Jars, whole and in shards, made by the slave David Drake, a 19th-century potter in Edgefield, S.C., are turning up in private collections and digs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/31/arts/design/pottery-by-david-drake-a-slave-craftsman-in-edgefield-sc.html', 'snippet': 'Jars, whole and in shards, made by the slave David Drake, a 19th-century potter in Edgefield, S.C., are turning up in private collections and digs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Newly discovered vessels by David Drake, an enslaved 19th-century potter in Edgefield, S.C., keep emerging, either whole or in shards.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/31/arts/31ANTIQUES1/0831ANTIQUES1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 679, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/31/arts/31ANTIQUES1/0831ANTIQUES1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1359, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/31/arts/31ANTIQUES1/31ANTIQUES1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/08/31/arts/31ANTIQUES1/31ANTIQUES1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/31/arts/31ANTIQUES1/31ANTIQUES1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Slave Potter’s Presence Emerges in Fragments', 'kicker': 'Antiques', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Slave Potter’s Presence Emerges in Fragments', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ceramics and Pottery', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTIQUES', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walther, Artur', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T21:03:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eve M. Kahn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5d722ff-0533-54a0-a7a4-70ad7fc39d48', 'word_count': 1128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5d722ff-0533-54a0-a7a4-70ad7fc39d48'}, {'abstract': 'Chris Bauer and Jim Francescon were looking for a two-bedroom to share for up to $3,500 a month.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/realestate/the-hunt-river-to-river-views-on-a-budget.html', 'snippet': 'Chris Bauer and Jim Francescon were looking for a two-bedroom to share for up to $3,500 a month.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN the spring, after four years of college and two years working in Denver, Chris Bauer landed a job in New York. So he packed up, left much of his stuff at his parents’ house in the Boston suburbs, and started work.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/realestate/02HUNT4_SPAN/02HUNT4_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/02/realestate/02HUNT4_SPAN/02HUNT4_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/realestate/02HUNT4_SPAN/HUNT4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1001, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/realestate/02HUNT4_SPAN/HUNT4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2001, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'River-to-River Views, on a Budget', 'kicker': 'The Hunt', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'River-to-River Views, on a Budget', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Renting and Leasing (Real Estate)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T21:05:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joyce Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joyce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8b4d99a-8028-5cd3-b94d-6d84ee970f5e', 'word_count': 1121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8b4d99a-8028-5cd3-b94d-6d84ee970f5e'}, {'abstract': '“Ornette: Made in America” is Shirley Clarke’s kaleidoscopic film portrait of the saxophonist and composer Ornette Coleman.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/31/arts/music/ornette-coleman-profile-ornette-made-in-america.html', 'snippet': '“Ornette: Made in America” is Shirley Clarke’s kaleidoscopic film portrait of the saxophonist and composer Ornette Coleman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not far into “Ornette: Made in America,” Shirley Clarke’s kaleidoscopic film portrait of the saxophonist and composer Ornette Coleman, is a scene in which Mr. Coleman sits on the porch of his childhood home in Fort Worth. He’s talking with his son, Denardo, about the imperative of original artistic expression and how it survives both the barbs of ungenerous critics and the bile of envious peers. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/31/arts/31ORNETTE1/0831ORNETTE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 700, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/08/31/arts/31ORNETTE1/0831ORNETTE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1400, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Freewheeling Musician Gets Free-Form Tribute', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Freewheeling Musician Gets Free-Form Tribute', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clarke, Shirley', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fort Worth Symphony Orchestra', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Ornette: Made in America (Movie)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coleman, Ornette', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T21:07:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37193e9a-c1cb-5f9c-a516-6346bbcca03e', 'word_count': 669, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37193e9a-c1cb-5f9c-a516-6346bbcca03e'}, {'abstract': 'A century ago Columbia Heights was “the most fashionable street in Brooklyn,” and it remains highly desirable today.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/realestate/brooklyn-heights-streetscapes-still-in-fashion-a-century-later.html', 'snippet': 'A century ago Columbia Heights was “the most fashionable street in Brooklyn,” and it remains highly desirable today.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE soft sea breezes make the Brooklyn Heights Promenade, overlooking New York Harbor, a transcendent summer sunset stroll. But get there an hour early for a sultry saunter down the flanking street, Columbia Heights, especially the block between Clark and Pierrepont Streets. A century ago this dead end was “the most fashionable street in Brooklyn,” and houses with water views now trade for $10 million.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/realestate/02STREETS2_SPAN/02STREETS2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/02/realestate/02STREETS2_SPAN/02STREETS2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/realestate/02STREETS2_SPAN/streets-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 820, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/realestate/02STREETS2_SPAN/streets-2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1639, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/realestate/02STREETS2_SPAN/02STREETS2_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/02/realestate/02STREETS2_SPAN/02STREETS2_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Still in Fashion, a Century Later', 'kicker': 'Streetscapes | Brooklyn Heights', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Still in Fashion, a Century Later', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Heights (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T21:08:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Gray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b650b63-0c35-52e2-89f1-0f85a1701b97', 'word_count': 895, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b650b63-0c35-52e2-89f1-0f85a1701b97'}, {'abstract': 'The Here and Now Labor Day Festival at Bargemusic includes composers and performers of many stripes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/08/31/arts/music/here-and-now-labor-day-festival-at-bargemusic.html', 'snippet': 'The Here and Now Labor Day Festival at Bargemusic includes composers and performers of many stripes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bargemusic’s Here and Now Labor Day Festival is a motley but spirited new-music celebration that brings together composers of all stripes and draws some of the biggest crowds the barge gets all season. Listeners don’t always know what they are in for. Though an overall schedule is published on the barge’s Web site, on each of the festival’s four nights — it runs through Sunday — the performers and works are juggled anew for each program.', 'prin