_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alison Gregor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gregor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9adb1afe-66e6-5689-8eff-898179287097', 'word_count': 1037, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9adb1afe-66e6-5689-8eff-898179287097'}, {'abstract': 'Hours before Mitt Romney’s speech at the Republican National Convention, his campaign introduced a Web site on Thursday — business.mittromney.com — devoted almost entirely on his years at the investment firm Bain Capital.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/08/30/on-eve-of-romneys-big-speech-a-focus-on-bain/', 'snippet': 'Hours before Mitt Romney’s speech at the Republican National Convention, his campaign introduced a Web site on Thursday — business.mittromney.com — devoted almost entirely on his years at the investment firm Bain Capital.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Mitt Romney prepares to take the stage at the Republican National Convention on Thursday night to make his case for the presidency, his record at Bain Capital continues to be a focus — some might say the focus — of both his supporters and detractors.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Before Romney’s Big Speech, a Focus on Bain', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Republican National Convention', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bain Capital', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-30T21:34:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Lattman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad2497b6-d468-55d1-9474-78dd9321bc92', 'word_count': 472, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad2497b6-d468-55d1-9474-78dd9321bc92'}, {'abstract': 'Judith Martin has become recognized as America’s most entertaining and informative arbiter of etiquette.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'Judith Martin has become recognized as America’s most entertaining and informative arbiter of etiquette.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the years, Judith Martin has become recognized as America’s most entertaining and informative arbiter of etiquette, and was even recognized as such with the National Humanities Medal. Born, as her Miss Manners Web site has it, “a perfect lady in an imperfect society,” she continues to contend, through her books and her newspaper and Internet columns, with “the gamut of social discourse and recourse” — an activity that also deeply engaged the novelist Edith Wharton, whose fictional portrait by Jennie Fields she reviews this week.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-upfront/0902-upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 697, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-upfront/0902-upfront-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1395, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'lega