f64-c22e-5471-bc54-b257a76ea798', 'word_count': 228, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08551f64-c22e-5471-bc54-b257a76ea798'}, {'abstract': 'A reader responds to a recent review of Chris Hedges and Joe Sacco’s “Days of Destruction, Days of Revolt.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/books/review/righteous-anger.html', 'snippet': 'A reader responds to a recent review of Chris Hedges and Joe Sacco’s “Days of Destruction, Days of Revolt.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Righteous Anger', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Righteous Anger', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-31T14:33:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d92bdbe-237b-5fb8-b49d-58a59cb05fd9', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d92bdbe-237b-5fb8-b49d-58a59cb05fd9'}, {'abstract': 'A reader responds to a recent review of Alix Ohlin’s fiction.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/books/review/alternative-readings.html', 'snippet': 'A reader responds to a recent review of Alix Ohlin’s fiction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Alternative Readings', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Alternative Readings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-31T14:33:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/69bd437a-06f8-536a-9a8b-af902f4c646f', 'word_count': 222, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69bd437a-06f8-536a-9a8b-af902f4c646f'}, {'abstract': 'In Ruth Rendell’s novel “The St. Zita Society,” characters are undone by their own obsessions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/books/review/the-st-zita-society-by-ruth-rendell-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'In Ruth Rendell’s novel “The St. Zita Society,” characters are undone by their own obsessions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m well aware of her reputation as a good citizen and a responsible member of Parliament, but trust me — you don’t want to live in Ruth Rendell’s neighborhood. Like her other urban teardowns, “Portobello” and “Tigerlily’s Orchids,” THE ST. ZITA SOCIETY (Scribner, $26) takes up residence in a picturesque London street and ever so slowly and delicately eviscerates the pretentious upper-middle-class residents behind its pseudo-Georgian facades. And lest anyone accuse this most cynical of satirists of sentimentalizing the lower orders, she turns and takes a bite out of these minions for adopting their masters’ shabby values.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Crime/0902-Crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 992, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Crime/0902-Crime-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1984, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Crime/0902-Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Crime/0902-Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Crime/0902-Crime-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bad Neighborhood', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bad Neighborhood', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perry, Anne', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Penny, Louise', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rendell, Ruth', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Arsenault, Emily', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-31T14:34:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8d9461c-7dd0-5222-a0f9-92bb217f322d', 'word_count': 902, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8d9461c-7dd0-5222-a0f9-92bb217f322d'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEX AND GOD AT YALE: Porn, Political Correctness, and a Good Education Gone Bad,  by Nathan Harden. (Thomas Dunne/St. Martin’s, $25.99.) Harden’s censure stems from his shock at what he saw as an undergraduate during “Sex Week.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-31T14:34:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2291e39-760e-539c-a5f6-ab05ca918fa9', 'word_count': 280, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2291e39-760e-539c-a5f6-ab05ca918fa9'}, {'abstract': 'Required reading.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/magazine/the-burning-book.html', 'snippet': 'Required reading.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My mother died 10 years ago. Her sister (my aunt) had been taking care of her and found a diary she kept during the last months of her life. My aunt gave it to me (I am the oldest child in our family). The diary shows a side of my mother I’d never seen before — angry, fearful, self-recriminatory. It’s upsetting to read. I suspect that the steroidal medications she was taking may have influenced her feelings, because she was not an angry person. I have never told my four younger siblings of its existence, but now — 10 years later — I wonder if I made the right decision. Should I burn it? NAME WITHHELD PORTLAND, ORE.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/magazine/02ethicist/02ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 402, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/magazine/02ethicist/02ethicist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 402, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/magazine/02ethicist/02ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/02/magazine/02ethicist/02ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/magazine/02ethicist/02ethicist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Burning Book', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Burning Book', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-31T14:34:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chuck Klosterman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chuck', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klosterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/476144d1-8942-5d48-bfc0-2841dcab1fa8', 'word_count': 511, 'uri': 'nyt://article/476144d1-8942-5d48-bfc0-2841dcab1fa8'}, {'abstract': 'How a Japanese sandal got Westernized — and supersized.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/magazine/who-made-that-flip-flop.html', 'snippet': 'How a Japanese sandal got Westernized — and supersized.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I do not know how you feel about the Japanese,” wrote a journalist named Elliott Chaze in 1952, “but I do like their feet.” Seven years after World War II, many Americans still regarded the Japanese as enemies — and distrusted anything to do with the country. Yet a rubber version of a Japanese sandal called the zori had become a popular item in U.S. drugstores. “Our women have commenced wearing thonged sandals,” Chaze announced, noting that “obscene picture-books” from Asia eroticized the feet. Japanese women, he believed, revealed themselves in the curl of their toes. And now, thanks to the rubber zoris, American women would too.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/magazine/02wmt/02wmt-articleLarge-v5.jpg', 'height': 405, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/02/magazine/02wmt/02wmt-articleLarge-v5.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 405}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/magazine/02wmt/mag-02WMT-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 731, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/magazine/02wmt/mag-02WMT-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1463, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/magazine/02wmt/02wmt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/02/magazine/02wmt/02wmt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'image