2-08-31T14:35:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/00f28259-b798-54f7-87ff-e42b6bd4c01c', 'word_count': 677, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00f28259-b798-54f7-87ff-e42b6bd4c01c'}, {'abstract': 'My cousin’s turn to an unusual hobby in the wake of disaster.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/magazine/looking-for-meaning-in-the-detritus-of-hurricane-katrina.html', 'snippet': 'My cousin’s turn to an unusual hobby in the wake of disaster.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The strangest package I’ve ever received came in the mail one fall day in 2006, from my cousin Jim in New Orleans. Inside was a rickety white bathroom cabinet with two doors, one drawer and a new set of brass pulls. I could see that my cousin had given it a fresh coat of paint, but when I bumped it with my knee, the wood gave way like moist cardboard and my nose filled with the smell of swamp. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/magazine/02lives/mag-02Lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 487, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/magazine/02lives/mag-02Lives-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 487, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/magazine/02lives/02lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/02/magazine/02lives/02lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/magazine/02lives/02lives-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Looking for Meaning in the Detritus of Hurricane Katrina', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Salvage Operation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Orleans (La)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'HURRICANE KATRINA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-31T14:35:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Gravois', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gravois', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de5c181d-7cb3-53e7-a693-652516865ecf', 'word_count': 770, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de5c181d-7cb3-53e7-a693-652516865ecf'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE REAL ROMNEY,  by Michael Kranish and Scott Helman. (Harper, $15.99.) Mitt Romney — private-sector multimillionaire, former Massachusetts governor and Republican presidential candidate — remains an enigma to many Americans. This balanced, informative biography, by two Boston Globe journalists, traces the prominent role Romney’s ancestors have played in Mormon history, deconstructs his leadership style and assesses his changing views on an array of social issues.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-31T14:35:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/db0253fe-d68e-561c-a17c-30688729a803', 'word_count': 492, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db0253fe-d68e-561c-a17c-30688729a803'}, {'abstract': 'George Orwell’s diaries covered everything from his strong feelings about World War II to his careful counting of eggs laid by Moroccan hens.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/books/review/george-orwells-diaries.html', 'snippet': 'George Orwell’s diaries covered everything from his strong feelings about World War II to his careful counting of eggs laid by Moroccan hens.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Christopher Hitchens’s introduction to George Orwell’s “Diaries,” among the last things he wrote before his death, is meant as a tribute to one of the writers he most admired, but it can also be taken as a warning. “Read with care, these diaries . . . can greatly enrich our understanding of how Orwell transmitted the raw material of everyday experience into some of his best-known novels and polemics.” Read with care? What is Hitchens trying to tell us with that phrase? A few sentences later we have: “This diary is not by any means a ‘straight’ guide or a trove of clues and cross-references.” About the creative process that went into constructing one of the novels, Hitchens says Orwell “gives us little or no insight.” Most tellingly, he refers to the entries as “meticulous and occasionally laborious jottings.” Read with care, Hitchens’s introduction alerts Orwell devotees that they should not expect the same pleasures from this book that they get from other of his writings.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02gewen-190/02gewen-190-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 770, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02gewen-190/02gewen-190-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 770}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02gewen-190/02gewen-190-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 798, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02gewen-190/02gewen-190-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1205, 'width': 939, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02gewen-190/02gewen-190-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02gewen-190/02gewen-190-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02gewen-190/02gewen-190-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Patriotism and Poultry', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Staying Power', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Orwell, George', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davison, Peter', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-31T14:36:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Barry Gewen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Barry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gewen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/40ca909d-8e05-542d-8722-1fd4e22cd550', 'word_count': 1794, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40ca909d-8e05-542d-8722-1fd4e22cd550'}, {'abstract': 'Alice Mattison’s novel follows the friendship of two men through the greater part of a century.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/books/review/when-we-argued-all-night-by-alice-mattison.html', 'snippet': 'Alice Mattison’s novel follows the friendship of two men through the greater part of a century.', 'lead_paragraph': '“For crying out loud!” “For Christ’s sake.” “Don’t give me that.” “I’ve never heard anything so stupid in my life.” “What the hell did you do that for?” “Enough already.” “Crazy dame.” The barbed expressions scattered liberally throughout “When We Argued All Night,” Alice Mattison’s fine novel of a friendship that lasts more than half a century, were for a long time the sonic wallpaper of life in certain quarters of New York City. During the years in which the novel unfolds, from 1936 to the beginning of the new millennium, being a New Yorker meant something specific and unfakable, and Mattison captures that essence of city life, beginning with how New Yorkers talked and then moving on to the small details that speak volumes, like this one, which comes at the beginning of the description of a dinner party: “They had no hall closet.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02RUSSO/02RUSSO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 915, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02RUSSO/02RUSSO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1771, 'width': 1982, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02RUSSO/02RUSSO-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02RUSSO/02RUSSO-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02RUSSO/02RUSSO-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Taking Sides', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Taking Sides', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mattison, Alice', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-31T14:38:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Russo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Russo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/39ae54a9-710b-5ff1-ac0d-fda04587ef96', 'word_count': 848, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39ae54a9-710b-5ff1-ac0d-fda04587ef96'}, {'abstract': 'A young American woman’s experience in 1960s Saudi Arabia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/books/review/in-the-kingdom-of-men-by-kim-barnes.html', 'snippet': 'A young American woman’s experience in 1960s Saudi Arabia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It takes guts to title a novel after a line from the Bible — “the Most High rules in the kingdom of men” — and then to begin Chapter 1 with possibly the most famous biblical reference available: “In the beginning.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02lapidos/02lapidos-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 695, 'width': 451, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02lapidos/02lapidos-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 695, 'width': 451, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02lapidos/02lapidos-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02lapidos/02lapidos-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02lapidos/02lapidos-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Accidental Tourist', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Accidental Tourist', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None},