eview', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9960689-c491-55f7-8a94-4c233591f798', 'word_count': 592, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9960689-c491-55f7-8a94-4c233591f798'}, {'abstract': 'Jennie Fields reimagines Edith Wharton’s Gilded Age world, and the love affair that threatened her closest friendship.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/books/review/the-age-of-desire-by-jennie-fields.html', 'snippet': 'Jennie Fields reimagines Edith Wharton’s Gilded Age world, and the love affair that threatened her closest friendship.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is impossible to libel the dead; legal protection of reputation stops at the grave. But is it possible to embarrass the dead?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/MARTIN/MARTIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 895, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/MARTIN/MARTIN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1791, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/MARTIN/MARTIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/MARTIN/MARTIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/MARTIN/MARTIN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Edith Wharton’s Awakening', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Edith Wharton’s Awakening', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fields, Jennie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wharton, Edith', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-31T14:41:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judith Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/265b0108-5ff3-504a-8a66-8500de0e04f7', 'word_count': 1145, 'uri': 'nyt://article/265b0108-5ff3-504a-8a66-8500de0e04f7'}, {'abstract': 'Was the forgotten British seafarer Robert Knox the inspiration for “Robinson Crusoe”?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/books/review/crusoe-by-katherine-frank.html', 'snippet': 'Was the forgotten British seafarer Robert Knox the inspiration for “Robinson Crusoe”?', 'lead_paragraph': '“Each historical era and every culture has appropriated ‘Crusoe’ for its own purposes,” Katherine Frank announces at the start of her ambitious new book about “Robinson Crusoe,” Daniel Defoe and the obscure Englishman who might have been a significant inspiration for one of literature’s most famous characters. Before setting out her case for an unexpected original behind the original, Frank catalogs Crusoe’s latter-day incarnations, which have included “a hero of Romantic individualism, of Victorian Empire or 20th-century capitalism, an explorer, an inventor, the embodiment of radical or conservative ideologies, an evangelist, a prophet of positive psychology and the gospel of prosperity, even an antihero.” Surveying this long and varied afterlife, she declares, “You name it and someone has probably thought and said Crusoe has been it. That’s his secret: Crusoe is Anyone and Everyone. He is you and he is me.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Boyagoda/0902-Boyagoda-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Boyagoda/0902-Boyagoda-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 397}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Boyagoda/0902-Boyagoda-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Boyagoda/0902-Boyagoda-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1356, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Boyagoda/0902-Boyagoda-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Boyagoda/0902-Boyagoda-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Boyagoda/0902-Boyagoda-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Castaway', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Castaway', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Knox, Robert', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Defoe, Daniel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frank, Katherine', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-31T14:41:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Boyagoda', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Boyagoda', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f4e62cd-6a90-5d06-8f3f-06bcca94732f', 'word_count': 953, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f4e62cd-6a90-5d06-8f3f-06bcca94732f'}, {'abstract': 'A war-scarred heroine finds solace in Japanese artistic traditions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/books/review/the-garden-of-evening-mists-by-tan-twan-eng.html', 'snippet': 'A war-scarred heroine finds solace in Japanese artistic traditions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IF you’ve never seen one, it’s almost impossible to capture the mesmerizing allure of a classic Japanese garden — and even standing for the first time in front of a bed of raked gravel can be a challenge. No vivid colors. No sweeping borders. No topiary animals. No shooting fountains. No fun, it would seem. Still, the traditional Japanese garden, esoteric as it is, has an ancient and undeniable appeal. It’s about secrets, perspectives, initiation, memory and time. It may take ages for a Japanese garden to come to maturity, to say nothing of the gardener. And yet, for all its mystery, the Japanese garden reveals itself as a capacious symbol of the human soul, replete with exactly the kinds of “borrowed landscapes” we live with. But we call them our personal histories.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Browning/0902-Browning-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 255, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Browning/0902-Browning-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 255}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Browning/0902-Browning-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 436, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Browning/0902-Browning-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 871, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Browning/0902-Browning-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Browning/0902-Browning-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Browning/0902-Browning-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Making Arrangements', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Making Arrangements', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tan Twan Eng', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-31T14:42:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dominique Browning', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dominique', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Browning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/347a27dd-4b58-5341-8992-8d8e478578f2', 'word_count': 705, 'uri': 'nyt://article/347a27dd-4b58-5341-8992-8d8e478578f2'}, {'abstract': 'Two New Yorkers with a video camera roam the city asking people for their ideas — any ideas — in the hope that someone might someday make use of them. Or not.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/31/ideas/', 'snippet': 'Two New Yorkers with a video camera roam the city asking people for their ideas — any ideas — in the hope that someone might someday make use of them. Or not.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pacing on the High Line one recent afternoon, Kevin Boyle and Rick Horan brandished signs that read, in bold block letters, “Ideas Wanted.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Project to Set Ideas Free', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On New York’s Walkways, a Mission to Set Unused Ideas Free', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boyle, Kevin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-31T14:46:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anne Barnard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1dfbb899-c2a7-515b-a90c-8bfc6522cd1b', 'word_count': 968, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1dfbb899-c2a7-515b-a90c-8bfc6522cd1b'}, {'abstract': 'The New York Times critics share their thoughts on “The Flying Swords of Dragon Gate,” “For a Good Time, Call … ” and “Side by Side.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/31/this-weeks-movies-august-31/', 'snippet': 'The New York Times critics share their thoughts on “The Flying Swords of Dragon Gate,” “For a Good Time, Call … ” and “Side by Side.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In This Week’s Movies, the New York Times critics share their thoughts on “The Flying Swords of Dragon Gate,” “For a Good Time, Call … ” and “Side by Side.” Full reviews can be found here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Week’s Movies: August 31', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-31T14:57:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9b7f73c-e9ee-5dec-b641-9b67c9882367', 'word_count': 36, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9b7f73c-e9ee-5dec-b641-9b67c9882367'}, {'abstract': 'Critics of women’s tennis say the game is now nothing but brute force. A tour of the courts at the United States Open was a first step in trying to determine whether the allegation has merit.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/31/is-the-womens-game-boring-an-investigation/', 'snippet': 'Critics of women’s tennis say the game is now nothing but brute force. A tour of the courts at the United States Open was a first step in trying to determine whether the allegation has merit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To attend Wednesday’s United States Open was to feel fortunate: the sky was bright blue, insolently cloudless over the sprawling acreage of the Billie Jean King National Tennis Center, and the swampland humidity had lifted. There was a fresh bounce in the steps of the players on the outer courts where I moved from match to match.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is the Women’s Game Boring? An Investigation', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-31T14:59:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By SAM TANENHAUS', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'TANENHAUS', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/14aa5623-6fbb-579a-8843-b65b9036e761', 'word_count': 925, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14aa5623-6fbb-579a-8843-b65b9036e761'}, {'abstract': 'A foe of fracking splits with allies over the urgency factor.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/31/the-inevitable-slow-path-to-fracking-in-new-york/', 'snippet': 'A foe of fracking splits with allies over the urgency factor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chip Northrup, a former oil and gas investor from Texas who now splits his time between Dallas and upstate New York, is an articulate and energetic opponent of hydraulic fracturing, or fracking, for the natural gas locked in deep shale layers in the state. Read his output here to get his views. But he splits with many other fracking foes in the region on a critical point – urgency.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Inevitable Slow Path to New York Shale Gas', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hydraulic Fracturing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Natural Gas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shale', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fox, Josh', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google+', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-31T14:59:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7dd48b08-7eec-55f0-abcb-16b4b2c883ec', 'word_count': 638, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7dd48b08-7eec-55f0-abcb-16b4b2c883ec'}, {'abstract': 'Confidant of Narendra Modi gets 28-year prison term for her role in 2002 riots.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/31/stiff-sentence-for-former-gujarat-minister/', 'snippet': 'Confidant of Narendra Modi gets 28-year prison term for her role in 2002 riots.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW DELHI, India – A top lieutenant of one India’s most powerful politicians was sentenced to 28 years in prison Friday for her role in a deadly attack that killed at least 94 people during the 2002 Gujarat riots.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stiff Sentence for Former Gujarat Minister', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'India: 32 Sentenced Over Deadly Gujarat Riots in 2002', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hinduism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kodnani, Mayaben', 'rank': 3