ue Vila-Matas’s publisher-hero is on a mission.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/books/review/dublinesque-by-enrique-vila-matas.html', 'snippet': 'Enrique Vila-Matas’s publisher-hero is on a mission.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lying ill in a hospital, an aging, once successful publisher has a vivid dream. He is in Dublin, a city he has never visited but in his dream knows intimately. After a long walk, he enters a pub and orders a drink. His wife catches him, and the two end up sitting on a sidewalk, sobbing. The dream haunts the publisher, and two years later, he decides to make a trip to Dublin — “to feel a little busier in his retirement.” He rounds up three of his former writers and whisks them off to the cemetery where Paddy Dignam was buried in “Ulysses,” to stage a half-serious funeral for “the age of print.” There, he discovers that he’s being shadowed by a dead ringer for the young Samuel Beckett.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Nolan/0902-Nolan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 628, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Nolan/0902-Nolan-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1257, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Nolan/0902-Nolan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Nolan/0902-Nolan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/0902-Nolan/0902-Nolan-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Written Off', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Written Off', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vila-Matas, Enrique', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-31T15:08:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Nolan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nolan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/090db091-6a6b-5aa3-a297-08018beb1390', 'word_count': 654, 'uri': 'nyt://article/090db091-6a6b-5aa3-a297-08018beb1390'}, {'abstract': 'In Bernhard Schlink’s stories, diffident men grapple with the small delusions and half-truths of the everyday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/books/review/summer-lies-by-bernhard-schlink.html', 'snippet': 'In Bernhard Schlink’s stories, diffident men grapple with the small delusions and half-truths of the everyday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since the success of “The Reader,” Bernhard Schlink has been firmly established as the voice of German guilt and conscience. Not all of his characters are as complicit as Nazi prison guards or the people who unwittingly love them, but it’s difficult for this German writer to use his country as a neutral background for private lives. With his mournful, meditative new collection of stories, translated (like “The Reader”) by Carol Brown Janeway, Schlink comes closer than ever to putting the war behind him. His characters could live anywhere, and in fact many do — in Amsterdam, in New York. They’re still haunted by the past, but the failures they confront are smaller, quieter and adamantly personal.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02ZEIDNER/02ZEIDNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 395, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02ZEIDNER/02ZEIDNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 395}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02ZEIDNER/02ZEIDNER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 731, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02ZEIDNER/02ZEIDNER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 731, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02ZEIDNER/02ZEIDNER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02ZEIDNER/02ZEIDNER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/02/books/review/02ZEIDNER/02ZEIDNER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 128, 'width': 129, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Lonely Middle', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Lonely Middle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schlink, Bernhard', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {