nization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/273a5d94-7c4c-5207-a322-55f3014427ab', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/273a5d94-7c4c-5207-a322-55f3014427ab'}, {'abstract': 'Who actually has the most power to create jobs today – the president, or the Fed chairman? The answer to that question tells us whether we now live in the age of democracy, or the age of the central bank.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/08/31/presidential-policy-vs-fed-policy-on-jobs/', 'snippet': 'Who actually has the most power to create jobs today – the president, or the Fed chairman? The answer to that question tells us whether we now live in the age of democracy, or the age of the central bank.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mitt Romney said Thursday night that, if elected president, he would create millions of jobs. On Friday morning, the Federal Reserve chairman, Ben S. Bernanke, said that the Fed’s policies may have helped create millions of jobs since the financial crisis.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Presidential Policy vs. Fed Policy on Jobs', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-31T19:55:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Eavis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eavis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc539d53-2152-502a-850f-960cfb142d11', 'word_count': 664, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc539d53-2152-502a-850f-960cfb142d11'}, {'abstract': 'They have more in common than you think.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/31/quiz-michael-jackson-or-karen-carpenter/', 'snippet': 'They have more in common than you think.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this week’s Riff, Rob Hoerburger writes that Michael Jackson’s “Thriller” — which turns 30 this year — “had a first-rate video in which Jackson came off like a musical James Bond, sexy, sly and licensed to dance.” But before Jackson’s success with “Thriller,” his 1979 album, “Off the Wall,” was already a hit, with four top-12 singles, a feat surpassed at the time only by “A Song For You” by the Carpenters.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Quiz: Michael Jackson or Karen Carpenter?', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-08-31T19:57:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Hoerburger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoerburger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15c465b8-fe3b-5cd6-95e4-aae0aad10de4', 'word_count': 244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15c465b8-fe3b-5cd6-95e4-aae0aad10de4'}, {'abstract': 'Some tennis players burn out, others fade away, and some, like Andy Roddick, get to choose how they retire, and when.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/01/sports/tennis/andy-roddick-retires-on-his-own-terms-harvey-araton.html', 'snippet': 'Some tennis players burn out, others fade away, and some, like Andy Roddick, get to choose how they retire, and when.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There was no general manager to summon Andy Roddick into his office to solemnly say, “Son, I’m afraid we’re not going to pick up your option.” No one was about to give him a nudge out the door. Retirement for a professional tennis player is all personal reflection, that elusive intersection of heart and mind, full understanding that the time has come. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/01/sports/subARATON/subARATON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 828, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/01/sports/subARATON/subARATON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1790, 'width': 1447, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/01/sports/subARATON/subARATON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/01/sports/subARATON/subARATON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/01/sports/subARATON/subARATON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ready to Retire, Work Yet to Finish', 'kicker': 'On Tennis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'He&#8217;s Close', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roddick, Andy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-31T20:04:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Harvey Araton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Harvey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Araton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/739bb76a-fcc1-5b17-b988-6000aa7e9138', 'word_count': 1009, 'uri': 'nyt://article/739bb76a-fcc1-5b17-b988-6000aa7e9138'}, {'abstract': 'An 1890s five-and-a-half-story town house with a classic limestone facade features a rooftop garden with Central Park views.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/31/big-ticket-sold-for-21-million-2/', 'snippet': 'An 1890s five-and-a-half-story town house with a classic limestone facade features a rooftop garden with Central Park views.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A five-and-a-half-story town house at 26 East 73rd Street, between Fifth and Madison Avenues, sold for $21 million, the biggest sale of the week, according to city property records.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Big Ticket | Sold for $21 Million', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Big Ticket: $21,000,000', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brown Harris Stevens', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Sotheby's International Realty", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Upper East Side (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-31T20:07:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michelle Higgins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michelle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Higgins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bfa827e5-fc3f-5bc7-a59a-254586949aa9', 'word_count': 313, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bfa827e5-fc3f-5bc7-a59a-254586949aa9'}, {'abstract': 'The latest volume of “Soul Eater” hits the list at No. 1.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/31/graphic-books-best-sellers-six-new-arrivals-on-the-manga-list/', 'snippet': 'The latest volume of “Soul Eater” hits the list at No. 1.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our manga best-seller list has six new entries this week. They tell stories about life, death, dreams — and high school. At No. 1 on the list is volume 10 of “Soul Eater,” about an arms expert who wants to hone the ultimate weapon for Death. At No. 2 is “Tenjo Tenge,” which sounds like “Fight Club” set in a high school where all the students are brawlers. Volume 13 of “The Melancholy of Haruhi Suzumiya,” at No. 7, is about a school club interested in the paranormal and a club member who has the power to destroy the universe.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Graphic Books Best Sellers: Six New Arrivals on the Manga List', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graphic Novels', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-08-31T20:09:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Gene Gustines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': 'Gene', 'lastname': 'Gustines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3aadf1e0-86ac-57b6-a5ae-42f582613907', 'word_count': 192, 'uri